\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 This is Russia living the Kremlin's alternate reality\b0\par\par\uc2 1,775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 "We have to erase those symbols, the V and the Z, and whoever sticks them on our vehicles should be put in jail. A totalitarian regime is never as firm as it was before it collapsed." Over the past year Russian citizens have suffered the consequences of the Kremlin's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hundreds of thousands fled Russia to avoid being mobilized. Those who stayed behind live in a bearable parallel reality following government measures that have so far prevented economic collapse. And those who protest are repressed. In Russia, allegations like the above against the Kremlin can only be heard in court, during trials against activists, opponents or simple citizens accused of discrediting the armed forces.\par \par \uc2 The above words were uttered by journalist Maria Ponomarenko last week in her last word before judges in Barnaul (Altai krai) who sentenced her to six years in prison for a post she published on Telegram on March 17, 2022 saying that Russian forces had shelled the Mariupol theater, where hundreds of civilians had taken refuge. The \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  had denied having anything to do with it and blamed the shelling on Ukrainian "nationalists."\par \par \uc2 The law against "fake news" about the army and the crackdown has reduced opposition to almost zero.Vladimir Putin signed the law against spreading "false news" about the army shortly after sending troops to Ukraine. Since then that rule, which can punish the offender with up to 15 years in prison, has conditioned Russian opposition to the Kremlin's plans in Ukraine and reduced it to almost zero.\par \par \uc2 Pavel Chikov\uc2 , head of the Agora NGO, counted 180 criminal charges brought in this case as of December. This week, Alexander Bastrikin, head of the \uc2 Russian Investigative Committee\uc2  under the Kremlin, told the Tass news agency that to date "152 criminal cases have been opened, in which 136 people were prosecuted. Fifty-three criminal cases have been brought to court, 16 sentences have already been handed down."\par \par \uc2 It has affected both prominent political opposition figures (\uc2 Ilya Yashin\uc2 , sentenced to 8.5 years in prison) and activists (artist Sasha Skochilenko may spend 10 years in prison if convicted for changing supermarket price tags in St. Petersburg to peace messages) or simple citizens (in Arkhangelsk, university student Olesia Krivtsova is under house arrest for a message on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  about the explosion of the Crimean bridge in October). To citizens who have stayed in Russia and others who have left the country: recently well-known journalists Alexander Nevzarov and Veronika Belotserkhovskaya have been sentenced in absentia to 9 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 All these cases are an unmistakable message to those who in the first months of the special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  , the euphemism used officially in Russia, were ready to show their faces and demonstrate. "From the very beginning we carried out protests against the war as an organization. In accordance with the new laws that reduce freedom, we were forced to stop carrying them out," explained Oleg Orlov, of the historical outlawed \uc2 NGO Memorial\uc2 , winner of the Nobel Peace Prize 2022, to La Vanguardia a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 Today only a vague memory remains of protests that the enormous police repression ended up blurring. From time to time, one can still see in Moscow a green ribbon that someone forgot to hang in a window or on a tree branch. Few know that for a few days it was used as a symbol of a resistance that today is totally silent.\par \par \uc2 Silent are also the few spontaneous signs of rejection, as when anonymous citizens left flowers next to a statue of the Ukrainian poet Lesya Ukrainka after a Russian attack on the city of Dnipro this January. And the police continue to arrest anyone who still dares to step forward. According to the NGO OVD-Info, more than 19,500 people have been arrested since February 24, 2022 for demonstrating against the war and military mobilization.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  forecasts that Russia will grow by 0.3% in 2023 and the government believes it will emerge from recession this year.Every once in a while little glimmers of hope appear. \uc2 Grigory Yavlinsky\uc2 , founder of the liberal Yabloko, the only opposition party still active, wrote a few weeks ago an article in the magazine Novaya Rasskaz-Gazeta , one of the many banned or blocked media in Russia. In it, he insisted on achieving peace, the goal set by his party.\par \par \uc2 Peace "in practice can only be implemented if at least Putin, Zelenski, Biden and the leaders of the \uc2 EU\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  want to do it." But he regrets that "to date all parties intend to continue hostilities on a large scale, wrongly counting on a military victory, which in modern conditions is unattainable for anyone," wrote the veteran politician. And he ended with an emotional "Reflect! Stop!".\par \par \uc2 During the first Cold War, crossing the Iron Curtain was accompanied by a strange sensation of having arrived in a parallel world, where many things were familiar but totally different. Something similar is gradually happening in today's Russia. Except for the regions close to the Ukrainian border (Belgorod, Kursk, Bryansk), Russian cities give the impression that life goes on as if thousands of people were not dying a thousand kilometers away.\par \par \uc2 But there are disturbing signs that everything is changing. On their way to work, many Muscovites still stop by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to pick up their morning coffee or a \uc2 Dunkin Donuts\uc2  coffee shop to complete a quick breakfast. The problem is that they are now called Stars Coffee and Donutto. You look up and on the office building where there used to be a billboard advertising \uc2 Ikea\uc2  or Decathlon, now a huge Z and other patriotic messages are drawn.\par \par \uc2 After February 24, 2022, the Moscow we knew is turning into a parallel Moscow, as the authorities try not to make the changes too noticeable. It is part of the process of adapting to the sanctions and the departure of more than a thousand Western companies.\par \par \uc2 In the grocery stores there is no more \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , Pepsi or Sprite, to name the best known. The factories of these and other brands have been sold to Russian entrepreneurs who continue to produce the same products under different names. The \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's chain was bought by a Russian entrepreneur and renamed Sabroso y Punto. \uc2 Ikea\uc2  has just announced the sale of its four factories.\par \par \uc2 Despite the war earthquake, the population that was not affected by the military mobilization in October has hardly felt a tremor. Why?\par \par \uc2 The explanation may lie in the fact that the catastrophic forecasts that many analysts and observers made about the Russian economy under the pressure of sanctions have not come true. Last March, \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts expected an 8% drop in GDP, annual inflation of over 20% and an average annual rate of 110 rubles to the dollar. But none of these things happened. There was no banking crisis, because the Kremlin and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took drastic measures to plug any holes and prevent the outflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the country.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the year, Putin announced a 2.5% drop in GDP, much lower than the best forecasts of months earlier.Russia has also been helped by its main economic resource: hydrocarbon exports. Together they allowed the ruble to recover from the March slump and inflation to be contained. In the first six months of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Russia made a piggy bank of $158 billion from the sale of energy products, according to the Energy and Clean Air Research Center, Finland. Moscow found in Asian markets, especially China and India, an alternative to the loss of customers in Europe.\par \par \uc2 In the first months of the conflict, the sanctions hardly affected hydrocarbon exports, explained Oleg Itzhoki, a professor at the University of California in Los Angeles, in Meduza. Only the US imposed an embargo on Russian oil, but supplies there were small, while the \uc2 European Union\uc2  continued to buy oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the year, Putin announced a 2.5% drop in GDP. Much lower than the best forecasts months earlier. Moreover, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has predicted that this year Russia may even have growth. In January it stated that the Russian economy will grow by 0.3%, when its previous forecasts indicated a fall of 2.3%. By 2024 he forecasts growth of 2.1%. Deduction in Moscow: Russia has resisted.\par \par \uc2 With these figures \uc2 Andrei Belousov\uc2 , Russian deputy prime minister, said two weeks ago that Russia can emerge from recession this year 2023. And the Ministry of Economic Development said in a statement that "the Russian economy is confidently overcoming the barriers of sanctions from hostile countries."\par \par \uc2 Another reason why many Russians have not noticed the change too much is that many Western products, in theory affected by sanctions, are still coming into the country. This is due to the so-called "parallel imports" allowed by the government.\par \par \uc2 In the context of the fight against intellectual piracy, in 2002 Russia banned products from entering the country without a license or permission from the manufacturer or rights owner. That ended last year, when Moscow decided that it was no longer illegal to import through third countries - such as Kazakhstan - a long list of products, including iPhones, cars or furs.\par \par \uc2 Will Russia continue to resist and will many Russians continue to live in this sort of alternative limbo?\par \par \uc2 The situation may start to change. On December 5, the \uc2 EU\uc2  implemented the full embargo on Russian oil, and since this February 5, on petroleum products. Also on December 5, the \uc2 G-7\uc2  "price ceiling" began to operate, which set at $60 per barrel the maximum price at which ships and insurers can transport Russian oil. As a result, the Kremlin's coffers began to feel the pressure. In January, the fiscal deficit soared and state revenues from energy sales fell by 46%.\par \par \uc2 More than 19,500 people have been arrested for protesting against the war, according to the NGO OVD-Info.Another challenge Russia will have to face is the fall in consumption, exacerbated by uncertainty. In a December report Natalia Orlova, chief economist at \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2 , predicted that the economy will shrink by 6.5% in 2023 due to a slowdown in consumption, lower investment and the loss of export potential.\par \par \uc2 But forecasts are just that, forecasts sometimes influenced by our own desires. With or without them, this year Russia has definitely moved away from the West and, as in the first cold war, is already walking in parallel.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230226ej2q003kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
