\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine finally in \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? The US 'theory of victory\b0\par\par\uc2 4,715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In his 2015 book, Conflict in Ukraine: The Unwinding of the Post-Cold War Order, co-written with fellow international relations specialist Eugene Rumer, Rajan Menon discussed one of the possible future scenarios of the Donbas war, which began in 2014: the ceasefire, he wrote, "breaks down and the conflict in eastern \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  escalates again and leads to direct Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the Donetsk and Lugansk regions; Russia launches a full-scale invasion of Ukraine and occupies significant parts along the right bank of the Dnieper River, possibly including Kiev, leaving a truncated independent Ukrainian state."\par \par \uc2 Despite explicitly contemplating this scenario eight years ago, Rajan Menon, professor emeritus of international relations at City College of New York, senior fellow at the Saltzman Institute of War and Peace Studies, \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , and director of the Grand Strategy program at the Defense Priorities think tank, acknowledges that last year's full-scale invasion surprised him. By the scale of the gamble and by the scale of the response, as if we had been catapulted into one of those epic conflagrations you only read about in history books.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with Menon, who has just returned from his second visit to war-torn Ukraine, about the prospects for the conflict and the role played by the United States - the main material and diplomatic driver of the Ukrainian war effort. We asked him about Biden's plans, European defense, the roots of the Russian invasion and the role of China. From a, as he says, "realistic" and "measured" perspective. The interview has been slightly edited for clarity.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION. First of all, I'd like to clarify something about the think tank you work for, whose mission, expressed on the front page of its website, is to promote a U.S. foreign policy based on diplomacy and temperance. What is the philosophy of international relations that underlies Defense Priorities?\par \par \uc2 ANSWER. It is a center that accommodates realism and restraint. That is, a policy that understands what the vital national security interests of the United States are, but without necessarily having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere in the world, stretching our capabilities too far and reducing our ability to solve our domestic problems. Part of this philosophy was born as a result of the disastrous wars in Iraq and Afghanistan. But also to solve the problems that accumulate at home. You live in New York and you see what's going on in the streets.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, if you look at places like Europe, and you add up their GDP, their technology and their population, the idea that they can't organize their own defense against Russia is simply impossible to believe. People say that's a lot of countries and how are you going to organize them. But look at what the \uc2 European Union\uc2  has done. Who would have thought, 20 years ago, that there would be a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most countries, or the abolition of travel restrictions. So it can be done. Only Europe doesn't have the incentive to do it, as long as the US remains its protector. And the US, even though it wants to be the protector, complains. But part of the US position in the world is because it provides security for Europe, Japan, South Korea and Australia. In other words: all the great economic power centers of the world, except China and perhaps India.\par \par \uc2 P. It's a paradoxical relationship. Presidents as different as Barack Obama, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have been asking European partners for years to raise their defense spending, since only 10 of the 30 \uc2 NATO\uc2  members reach the agreed 2% of GDP. The United States wants Europe to defend itself so that it can devote its resources to China, to the Pacific. However, the Americans are also pressuring European nations not to do business with the Chinese, and there is a perception, especially on the left, that Washington is using the war in Ukraine to secure its political and military influence in Europe.\par \par \uc2 A. That's very interesting. The complaints about Europe date back even to the time of John F. Kennedy in the 1960s. That the Europeans were not doing enough to defend themselves and that they had to be made to do so. But at the moment when, in Europe, someone like \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  is talking about strategic autonomy, that's not allowed. So we want them to spend more money on defense within \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but we're not comfortable with them going beyond that. And we're tolerating a lot of underspending on defense. If you look at the richest country in Europe, Germany, the reports of the parliamentary committee on the armed forces paint a picture of the German military that is shocking. I have a hard time understanding it. So the U.S. complains, but then sends its secretary of state, almost as if he were a Roman proconsul visiting the provinces of the empire, and I think we like that. We like going to Japan, we like going to Korea.\par \par \uc2 Regarding competition with China, the interesting thing is to look at new industrial policies. We've always talked, in general, about how much the U.S. likes the free trade system and dislikes protectionism. But if you look at agriculture, for example, it is heavily subsidized. Not as much as the \uc2 EU\uc2 's Common Agricultural Policy, at least for now. But essentially, we're making adjustments to produce in the U.S. as much as possible. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's latest law, the Inflation Reduction Act, and others, give a lot of incentives to produce here, also for foreign companies. So Europe and Japan see this and worry about job losses. In this initiative there is also the need to prevent the leakage of sensitive technologies to China. So Europe is in a difficult situation: on the one hand, the Europeans understand that these leaks have to be prevented; on the other hand, it is a protectionist policy that causes tensions. It is important to remember that the U.S. has been committed to free trade since 1945. But throughout the 19th century it was an enormously protectionist country.\par \par \uc2 P. I wanted to ask you about the reasons behind the Russian decision to invade. The realist school tends to place part of the responsibility on the US, which would have overreached itself and behaved arrogantly, getting involved in Ukraine with promises and military investments, knowing, as the former ambassador to Moscow, William Burns, now director of the CIA, said, that Ukraine for the Russians was the "most vivid of red lines". The other side, which we can call idealistic, explains the aggression as a consequence merely of Russian traumas, of their revanchism and their apocryphal understanding of Ukraine. What is your position?\par \par \uc2 A. It is a mixture. I don't quite fit into either camp. Let me briefly present the landscape of the debate. On the one hand, you have people like John Mearsheimer of the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  who say that the invasion is entirely due to \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion. The idea is that because the 2008 NATO summit in Bucharest opened the door to Ukraine, Russia concluded that Ukraine was going to be a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member. And this is a special country for Russia. They are geographically tied. Putin faced, as John says, an existential threat. On the other hand, to mention two names, you have Michael McFaul and \uc2 Anne Applebaum\uc2 , who say that the invasion has absolutely nothing to do with \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion. That Putin wants to control as many ex-Soviet countries as possible, in particular Ukraine, whose independence he cannot accept. These two extremes end up nullifying the possibility of standing in the middle.\par \par \uc2 My position is this: from the moment \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion was shuffled around in the 1990s, and then made a reality, I always thought it was a terrible idea. Because, if you put yourself in Russia's position, whether you're a leftist, liberal or conservative, and you see a Cold War alliance parading to your borders, even if the United States tells you that the Cold War is over and Russia is democratizing, and so on, the question remains: why are you moving the alliance to our borders? And the idea that this enlargement was harmless is absurd. Imagine for a moment that the U.S. loses the Cold War and the USSR places Mexico in the Warsaw Pact. We would not accept it either way. This is where I differ with Applebaum and company. I think telling the story of the invasion of Ukraine without mentioning \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 But I also differ with John Mearsheimer. In 2008 \uc2 NATO\uc2  opened the door to Ukraine, but in order to accept it, the first thing to do is to draw up a Membership Action Plan. 14 years later, Ukraine had not been given such a plan. So it was clear that, for whatever reason, the unanimity required to accept this new member did not exist. I don't buy the argument that Putin invaded a country larger than Spain because of its imminent \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership.\par \par \uc2 Another thing to keep in mind is that the expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  has shifted, little by little, the balance of power within the organization away from countries like Spain, France and Germany, towards countries like Poland and the Baltics, which have a very different, very complicated history with Russia, with many conflicts. So I think the harder line towards Russia, in a way, is a consequence of \uc2 NATO\uc2 's expansion into these countries. There is also the argument that these countries wanted to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , that they kept asking for it and that's why we had to accept them. But a military alliance is not a book club. A military alliance is serious, because it involves going to war to protect the other members.\par \par \uc2 P. Be that as it may, the damage has already been done. What do you think of the Biden Administration's policy towards Ukraine after the invasion began?\par \par \uc2 A. For me, especially after going to Ukraine and seeing the incredible damage, which I don't mind having seen on television or in the newspapers before, because it is shocking, I am not opposed to providing Ukraine with the means to defend itself. But I think that difficult decisions will have to be made. For example, let's say the war evolves and the Ukrainian Army is in a position to enter Crimea, will the West be united in its support for Ukraine, or will it be considered too dangerous? Because, if Putin is left with nothing, the war could spiral out of control. We would need a deal that would leave him something, so the Crimea issue would have to be fine-tuned as a problem that would be solved by future generations. Ukraine has not been given a blank check, because it has not been given certain weapons, such as ATACMS tactical missiles or fighter jets. It is being supported, but without allowing the war to escalate. The rule is to give weapons, but not to reach Russian territory.\par \par \uc2 P. The United States was absolutely right with the prediction of invasion, and with months to spare. But it also made a mistake. It thought that Ukraine would be quickly defeated by Russia. Washington was preparing to arm a Ukrainian resistance, not to support a full-fledged war effort, as turned out to be the case. Interestingly, it was the same mistake Russia made: the notion that the Ukrainians didn't stand a chance against the Russian tank roll.\par \par \uc2 A. The Russians have made many mistakes. When Nazi Germany invaded the Soviet Union in World War II, the Russians demonstrated an impressive capacity for sacrifice to defend their country. What did they expect would happen when they invaded Ukraine? That the Ukrainians would not defend themselves? Ukrainian soldiers told me the same thing many times: for us this is no ordinary war. If we lose this war, we lose a country. Are there any Russian soldiers who believe that, if they lose this war, they would lose Russia? No. There is an asymmetry in combat morale. That was one mistake. Another mistake has to do with the training provided by \uc2 NATO\uc2  to the Ukrainian Army since 2014. 60,000 Ukrainian soldiers received training and the Ukrainian armed forces have been adopting \uc2 NATO\uc2  military practices. In addition, they have been fighting in two separatist provinces. So the Ukrainian Army had changed a lot. I don't think Putin took it seriously. But neither did we. The CIA thought the Ukrainian resistance would collapse immediately. Honestly, I thought so too, because, if you look at any measure of power, like population, tanks, aircraft or artillery, Russia had a huge advantage. But it's not just that the Ukrainians have higher morale, it's that the Russian Army didn't turn out to be what was expected.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian soldiers told me the same thing many times: this is no ordinary war. If we lose this war, we lose a country\par \par \uc2 . P. You have mentioned in your articles that peace negotiations, for now, are impossible, because neither Ukraine, nor Russia, have incentives to sit down and negotiate. Both believe that they can improve their position in the spring or summer, that is why neither country seriously offers to mediate between them, because they know it would fail. On the other hand, we are getting curious signals, like that anonymous White House source who told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  that, essentially, Ukraine had this spring to carry out its counter-offensives, but then American support would probably cease and they would have to sit down and talk. What do you think the United States will do? What is its next step?\par \par \uc2 A. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  article came up at an event we hosted at Defense Priorities. In my view, it has received a lot of attention precisely because it is an exception. If you read most of the statements from the Biden Administration, they don't say they will give all the aid to Ukraine all at once, but they do say they will help Ukraine get the territories back, although without mentioning to what extent [Menon is referring to the possibility of Ukraine getting the entire territory back, up to the 1991 borders, including Crimea, or the territory from before February 24, 2022]. So I wouldn't read too much into it. It's not consistent with what the administration has been saying. And, if you compare it to other articles, you'll see that those articles say the opposite. I think what it suggests, probably, is that there's a debate within the Biden administration about how far they're willing to go.\par \par \uc2 P. Certainly, but it's also true that while the Biden Administration presents a consistent message, sometimes it leaks to the media what looks like a knock on the Ukrainians. For example, when it was leaked that Biden had raised his voice to the president, \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2 , during a telephone conversation, and told him to show a little gratitude for the aid. A way, perhaps, to remind the Ukrainians that American aid is not unconditional.\par \par \uc2 A. It's the oldest game in Washington and I'm sure in Madrid as well. To have in the government someone with a different opinion who wants it publicized. I don't know what exactly is going on. What we have to do is look at concrete actions, and what we see is $27 billion in U.S. military assistance. And, if you add in European, it's $7 billion or $8 billion. We're talking about 40% of the Russian defense budget last year. If we had sat down to talk a year and a half ago, and asked ourselves whether there was any chance of an all-out war between Ukraine and Russia, we probably would have said no. And, if there was such a war, and we had wondered whether the US and \uc2 NATO\uc2  would act in unison and send some $40 billion in military aid, we would have thought it was highly unlikely because of the risk of escalation. This has been a war of surprises for me. So much so that I am very reluctant to make predictions, because I have seen things that I thought would never happen.\par \par \uc2 Now people say Putin is an expansionist and an imperialist. He is an expansionist, but he has never done anything like this. A full-scale invasion of a large country. Georgia in 2008 was very different. In Syria, it was mostly the air force. In 2014, in Donbas and Crimea, it was provinces very close to Russia. What is happening now is an extraordinary step. How do you explain it? There is a theory going around that, during the covid, Putin was reading a lot about the conquests of Peter the Great and Catherine the Great, and all these nationalist philosophers like Ivan Ilyin. Some kind of Russian fascist. Who knows. But I didn't expect Putin to do something like this, even when he was amassing his troops on the border. I thought it was a bullying tactic to force the US to stall \uc2 NATO\uc2  involvement in Ukraine. That was my view.\par \par \uc2 P. A year after the start of the full-scale war, there is a possibility that is sounding more and more in military and geopolitical circles: that of ending up with a territorially diminished Ukraine, but integrated, at last and as a reward for what it has suffered, into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Or with some other security guarantee. It would also be the only way to make possible its reconstruction, which promises to be very costly, without the risk of Russia attacking again in the future. Figures of more than $300 billion are being bandied about. That is 50% more than the Ukrainian GDP in 2021.\par \par \uc2 A. Now there is even talk of a trillion [trillion] dollars. A gigantic amount.\par \par \uc2 Q. How do you see the option of a Ukraine in \uc2 NATO\uc2  as an outcome of this war or as a kind of compensation for the loss of territories?\par \par \uc2 A. I can certainly imagine a peace, even though Zelensky says he wants everything back, including Crimea, in which Ukraine is territorially diminished: perhaps preserving 85% of its territory [that is currently under its control], but not all of it. \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion is a complicated issue, because the alliance would possibly be divided. The eastern flank, plus the United Kingdom and the United States, would say yes, after all that Russia has done, and Russia has shown its true colors, Ukraine should be admitted to \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The question is: would Hungary, France and Germany think the same? I am not so sure. Look at what has happened with the Turks regarding the admission of Finland and Sweden. So it is not clear that Ukraine can join.\par \par \uc2 If you are in Ukraine and you say this, they get angry, because.There it is almost a religious question. They believe that, after all that has happened, joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is a birthright. But even the \uc2 EU\uc2  has been very careful in its statements. Also, if Ukraine joins the \uc2 EU\uc2 , the \uc2 EU\uc2  would have to bear most of the cost of reconstruction. We are talking about an awful lot of money.\par \par \uc2 "For every artillery shell Ukraine fires, the Russians fire ten. But they can't take it, because they are too badly commanded"\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a second possibility, although the\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians refuse to mention it because they don't want anyone to think that there are better options than \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and that is a different security guarantee. But a security guarantee would be worthless if it doesn't include the United States. So, if you have the UK, the Baltics and the US, maybe they'll be happy with second prize. It would be interesting to see what happens. The other possibility is a neutral Ukraine, which is what the Russians will demand, and I think the Ukrainians will put up a lot of resistance.\par \par \uc2 There are many things we cannot predict. Some will depend on what happens in the next year. And I also don't exclude the possibility that the war will continue. If the Ukrainians regain a lot of territory, they will be in a position of strength, vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis Russia and vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the West. One thing to watch in the next three months is the Russian offensive. From what we can see they are attacking all along the front, from north of Lugansk to the south, but they are not doing well. They have not been able to take Bakhmut. They have been trying to take Bathmut since the end of August. A town that before the war had 70,000 inhabitants and now has 7,000, and they can't, despite having many advantages. For every artillery shell Ukraine fires, the Russians fire ten. But they can't take it, because they are too badly commanded.\par \par \uc2 P. As you watch this war, don't you worry that we might be missing something? Ukraine has a lot of activists, young, English-speaking, charismatic, social media-savvy people who are often the fixers who guide foreign reporters and who are active on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Zelensky's government has many such profiles and has led an iron grip on content. There is no doubt that pro-Russian views have greatly diminished since 2014, and it would not be correct to speak of a divided country, especially now. But, according to the director of the Kiev Sociological Institute, Anton Hrushevsky, there were around 30% of pro-Russian voters in Ukraine at the beginning of 2022. Probably many of them changed their minds soon after, but it is still a significant number.\par \par \uc2 A. That's an excellent question. As you said, the Ukraine narrative has been very well coordinated and orchestrated by Zelensky. And I'm not saying it's propaganda, but they have a point of view. And they've been very efficient promoting it in Western countries. They use social media and they do an excellent job. So in the West there is huge support for this narrative, which is backed by the U.S. foreign policy establishment, and by major newspapers. Even the British Guardian, a reference of the left, supports it.\par \par \uc2 It is true that there have been divisions in the country. Before 2014, it was not clear that there was broad support for joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The country was divided. The west and the center were more inclined to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the EU than the east and the south. But I think the war has changed a lot of things. All this talk by Putin that the war is to protect Russian speakers from genocide is absurd. The Russian-speaking parts of Ukraine have suffered the most destruction. Look at Mariupol, for example. The war has changed a lot of things. More and more Ukrainians, even if they are Russian speakers or ethnic Russians, identify themselves as Ukrainian citizens. There is a sense of a consolidating national identity. The Ukrainian language is increasingly used in cities like Kiev. It is encouraged by the government. And resistance to joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  is now much lower. The problem is not in Ukraine, the problem is to see to what extent \uc2 NATO\uc2  is ready to open its doors.\par \par \uc2 P. Speaking of narratives, a question about the tone of the debate. I'm going to read you this that realist thinker Stephen Walt, professor of international relations at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 , wrote recently in Foreign Policy: "The public debate over Ukraine has been extraordinarily vituperative, with warmongering commentators competing with each other to see who expressed the most support for Kiev, while dismissing alternative perspectives as na\u239\'00\'EFve, immoral, pro-Russian, or worse." As a realist, do you recognize this sentiment?\par \par \uc2 A. Personally, I find no difficulty in expressing my views, but there is no doubt that people critical of mainstream narratives, say, John Mearsheimer or the late \uc2 Stephen Cohen\uc2  of Princeton, have been vilified. They've been called all sorts of things, almost treated as Putin sympathizers. And I think that's a very unfortunate thing, because you can debate someone on the assumption that, if that person disagrees with what you think, it's because they work for a foreign government. I find it in very bad taste and social media has exacerbated the problem. All you have to do is sit in front of your computer, type something without caring, and make it go viral. But people with alternative views to those of the establishment, like John Mearsheimer, have not been quiet. Is it harder for them to be heard in the mainstream media? Absolutely. When was the last time you read a piece by John in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  or The \uc2 Washington Post\par \par \uc2 ? P. The New Yorker did two interviews with him and in both cases he got a good one.\par \par \uc2 Right, right. And the guy who did the interviews, Isaac Chotiner, is known to be a suck-up. But John is a different guy. He almost likes controversy, he loves to be in the middle of it. But yes, I think the debate has degenerated because there are so many passionate beliefs. The \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion people believe that the invasion justifies their position. So when John Mearsheimer comes along and reminds us that \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion would lead to war and that Ukraine would be destroyed by Putin, the insults get very harsh. And the more nuanced part of the debate is drowned out between these two extremes. Yes, I think Steve Walt is right.\par \par \uc2 P. We can't end without asking you about Taiwan. Despite the many differences, the case of Russia and Ukraine, in which a revanchist power wants to annex by force a neighboring country that it does not recognize and considers a province, has led many analysts to draw parallels with the case of China and Taiwan. To what extent do you think it is useful to conflate the two disputes?\par \par \uc2 A. Some people who show almost unconditional support for Ukraine make exactly that argument: that the current war goes beyond Ukraine because, depending on how we act, the Chinese will gauge our resolve and conclude that they may or may not attack Taiwan. I hear the same thing all the time. The credibility argument. You have to do something in this conflict because your credibility is at stake. My sense is that the Chinese are much smarter than this. They are not going to think about invading Taiwan based on what the Americans have done in Ukraine. They are going to look at the local circumstances. What capacity do we have to defeat Taiwan? What could the Americans do? Can we defeat them? If not, can we raise the cost so high as to deter them from defending Taiwan? What will be the effect on the global economy? Because this is no longer Mao's China. China is deeply connected to the global economy. An attack on Taiwan would make the markets go crazy. I think they will look at local circumstances. I don't think Ukraine is the element that will make them make a decision. But, of course, if you're someone who considers Ukraine a sacred cause, you'll conflate it with Taiwan and China. I'm not persuaded by that argument.\par \par \uc2 In his book published in 2015, Conflict in Ukraine: The Unwinding of the Post-Cold War Order, written four-handed with fellow international relations specialist Eugene Rumer, Rajan Menon shuffled through one of the possible future scenarios of the Donbas war that began in 2014: the ceasefire, he wrote, "breaks down and the conflict in eastern \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  escalates again and leads to direct Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the Donetsk and Lugansk regions; Russia launches a full-scale invasion of Ukraine and occupies significant parts along the right bank of the Dnieper River, possibly including Kiev, leaving a truncated independent Ukrainian state."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230219ej2j002ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
