\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine-Russia war: Latest news of the invasion, live\b0\par\par\uc2 7,965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Update narration 20:00 ???? Putin tells Xi he wants to increase their military cooperation. In a conversation with China's president, the Kremlin chief accuses the West of "unprecedented pressure and provocations" 19:48 Stoltenberg calls for more weaponry for Ukraine NATO Secretary General Jens Stoltenberg called on alliance member states to supply more weapons to Ukraine, according to an interview published Friday. "I call on allies to do more. It is in our security interest to make sure that Ukraine prevails and that (Russian President Vladimir) Putin does not win," Stoltenberg told German news agency DPA. Stoltenberg noted that it is perhaps even more important that Ukraine receives sufficient ammunition for the systems already in place, adding that the need for ammunition and spare parts is "enormous.\par \par \uc2  "19:36 Putin hails "best relations in history" between China and Russia Russian President Vladimir Putin on Friday called relations with China the "best in history" in a videoconference conversation with his Asian country's counterpart Xi Jinping. "Our relations worthily stand every test, demonstrate maturity and stability and continue to expand dynamically," the Kremlin chief told Xi. According to Putin, Moscow-Beijing relations are "the best in history and a model of cooperation between great powers in the 21st century." "With you we equally see the causes, the march and logic of the transformations of the global geopolitical landscape," he added 19:24 US exempts Russian oil refined in other countries from price cap The \uc2 US Treasury Department\uc2  has indicated that the price cap agreed by the international coalition formed by the G7 countries, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and Australia will not apply when Russian crude oil has been substantially transformed in a jurisdiction other than the Russian Federation by one of the refining processes covered. "Once Russian petroleum products or Russian oil is substantially transformed in a jurisdiction other than the Russian Federation, they are no longer considered to originate in the Russian Federation and, therefore, the price cap no longer applies," the \uc2 Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) \uc2 has explained in preliminary guidance on the implementation of the price cap on Russian petroleum products 19:12 The awkward anniversary (for Russia) of the Soviet Union, by Gonzalo Aragon\u233\'00\'E9s If we were living inside a uchronic novel emerged from such magnificent pens as those of Philip K. Dick or Harry Turtledove, Moscow would be celebrating today. There would be parades, flags, I don't know about tanks, missiles or cosmonauts. But for sure there would be many posters with factory workers and peasants marching magnificent in the hope of building a new country, the one that the Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin founded on December 30, 1922. But we live in the midst of reality, with Russia bogged down in its conflict with Ukraine, isolated by Western sanctions and seeking commercial niche in Asia for its gas and oil. Too many worries for the Kremlin to think about celebrating the 100th anniversary of the birth 100 years ago of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics 19:00 Ukraine receives another 5,000 diesel generators A new shipment of 5,000 diesel generators has arrived in Ukraine this month and is expected to receive around 8,000 more next month with the start of the year. Despite this, the nation's needs are much greater due to Russia's repeated airstrikes on its energy infrastructure, according to the \uc2 Ukrainian Infrastructure Ministry\uc2 . Russia continues to insist on shelling Ukraine's power grid, a method it has followed in recent weeks of war and is expected to maintain 18:48 UN calls for education so that children do not make mistakes like Ukraine's war UN High Commissioner for Human Rights Volker T\u252\'00\'FCrk on Friday raised a child-centered reflection and called on societies to "open their eyes to the mistakes of the past" so that they are able to write a different history, one of hope and unity. "We can inspire them to write a story of hope and unity, and instill in them a commitment to create a better world," he said on the eve of the end of 2022, a year that far from marking a return to normality after the pandemic, has been one of war in Ukraine, and humanitarian and food crises in various parts of the world. T\u252\'00\'FCrk considered that it is time to "celebrate diversity, convinced that we are stronger together than apart" 18:36 Ukraine hopes to regain sovereignty over Crimea next year The permanent representative of the Ukrainian Presidency for Crimea, Tamila Tasheva, hopes that by 2023 Ukraine's sovereignty over all its occupied territories, including the peninsula illegally annexed in 2014, will be restored. Tasheva, head of the Crimean Platform, stresses that all expectations for 2023 are exclusively related to the Russian-initiated war, which has resulted in thousands of deaths of children, civilians and soldiers. "We hope that all this will end next year with Ukraine's victory," she told EFE, adding, "We are looking forward to returning to Crimea, our home. "18:24 Russia, from Yeltsin's corruption to Putin's iron fist "After Yeltsinism comes Putinism, which is similar, but worse." This is how the Russian publication Novaya Gazeta - the publication closed down by the Kremlin of the ill-fated Anna Politkovskaya - saw the surprising replacement of Boris Yeltsin as President of the country by a Vladimir Putin practically unknown to public opinion four months earlier, when the still President elevated him to Prime Minister thanks to his practically plenipotentiary powers. It was December 31, 1999, and Yeltsin, physically very deteriorated and besieged by the corruption scandals that had affected his entire family, decided by surprise to leave power. Tied up and, on this occasion, really well tied up in the hands of a dolphin whom he had raised to the Executive from the direction of the Russian secret services. 18:12 Latvia wants Russia to be more isolated in 2023 Latvia aims in 2023 to bring about Russia's further international isolation and eventually the adoption of new sanctions for the invasion of Ukraine, in a year that will be dominated by "three interconnected crises", security, energy and economic. Latvian Foreign Minister Edgars Rink?vi?s has assured in a foreign policy statement that his country will focus its foreign action on consolidating support for Ukraine, "which will include military, financial and humanitarian assistance", achieving further international isolation of Russia and strengthening Latvia's security with an increased NATO presence on Latvian soil. Rink?vi?s specified that in relation to Ukraine Riga will support the reconstruction of the country and its reforms by means of instruments of development cooperation, as well as participation in the military mission of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU) and assistance in the training of Ukrainian soldiers 18:00 Algeria stands as non-aligned gas exporter in Ukraine war Algerian President Abdelmadjid Tebboune assured that if France asked him to increase gas exports they would do so, as was done with Italy, and defended Algiers remaining non-aligned in the war in Ukraine. In an interview published today by the French daily Le Figaro, Tebboune said that when Algeria "has surpluses" it makes the rest benefit. "The \uc2 European Union\uc2  and France are not adversaries (of Algeria). We maintain good neighborly relations. Therefore, if France asked us to increase gas exports, we would do so. It has not yet done so. Italy has asked for it," he said 17:48 Ukraine hopes to enter 2023 with light problems solved Ukraine is preparing to welcome the New Year amid blackouts and Russian aerial bombardment of its civilian infrastructure, while the front seems to be stabilizing for the time being. "Our goal is that on New Year's Eve all Ukrainians (...) will have light and I think we will achieve it," said Kyrylo Timoshenko, spokesman for the Ukrainian Presidency. At the same time, operators are working against the clock to repair damaged infrastructure and allow electricity to reach as many Ukrainian households as possible during the holiday season. Ukrenergo, the operator of Ukraine's electricity distribution network, said today that "the deficit of the electricity system is at the same level as before the tenth massive Russian missile attack", which took place yesterday, Thursday 17:36 Ukraine denounces a "massive" attack with more than 120 missiles on Kyiv and other cities in the country Russia today launched more than 120 missiles on Ukraine, said Mikhail Podolyak, advisor to the head of the Ukrainian President's Office. "29.12.22. All over Ukraine more than 120 missiles fired by the 'evil Russian world' to destroy critical infrastructure and massively kill civilians," Podolyak wrote in a message on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  picked up by the Ukrinform agency. He added, sarcastically, to be "waiting for further suggestions from peacemakers on peaceful settlement, security guarantees for Russia and inconveniences of provocations. "17:24 U.S., UK continue to bolster Ukraine The United States announced last week nearly $2 billion in additional military aid, including the Patriot Air Defense System, which provides protection against aircraft, cruisers and ballistic missiles. Britain said Friday it delivered to Ukraine more than 1,000 metal detectors and 100 bomb-disposal kits to help clear minefields. Defense Minister Ben Wallace said Thursday that Britain would allocate 2.3 billion pounds ($2.800 million) to Ukraine in military aid in 2023, matching the amount it provided this year 17:12 Ukraine accuses Iran of supplying drones to Russia Kyrylo Tymoshenko, deputy head of Ukraine's presidential administration, said Friday that at least three people had been killed in fresh Russian shelling in a border area in the northern Chernihiv region and in the eastern region of Kharkov. The Ukrainian military said Moscow's forces also shelled 20 settlements around the shelled town of Bakhmut in eastern Ukraine, where the fiercest fighting is raging, and more than 25 settlements in the Kherson and Zaporiyia regions. Tehran continues to reject Kyiv's accusations as it faces international sanctions over its ties with the Kremlin 17:00 Ukraine says it will continue to shoot down Russian drones and missiles Ukrainian Defense Minister Oleksii Reznikov said Friday that Ukraine will continue to shoot down Russian drones, missiles, planes and helicopters over Ukrainian territory a day after a new massive attack by Russian forces. "We have shot down, are shooting down and will shoot down Russian drones, missiles, planes and helicopters in our skies," for which Ukraine will use everything from old MANPADS man-portable air defense systems to modern "Patriot" systems, he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , adding, "We protect our own land." The Ukrainian Air Force specified today that in the massive attack yesterday by Russian troops it managed to shoot down 58 missiles, four reconnaissance drones and a helicopter 16:48 Putin tells Xi he wants to increase their military cooperation Isolated from Western countries after sending the army to Ukraine, Putin has this year taken his political alliances and economic interests to other latitudes, preferably Asia, and especially China. The Russian president spoke this Friday by videoconference with Chinese President Xi Jinping, to whom he showed his desire to increase military cooperation between the two countries. In the little more than twenty minutes that were made public, the head of the Kremlin reiterated that cooperation between Russia and China on the world stage contributes to the formation of a just world order. Putin has repeated this idea before, at several summits in which both leaders have participated 16:36 Stoltenberg talks about a "peaceful solution" between Russia and Ukraine NATO Secretary General Jens Stoltenberg has explained that once Putin realizes that he cannot subjugate Ukraine, there could be a "negotiated peaceful solution that ensures that Ukraine prevails as an independent democratic state." The recent Ukrainian attacks on military targets in Russia are legitimate as "all countries have the right to defend themselves," the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  chief said. Stoltenberg has therefore pointed to the need to continue arming Ukraine and increase supplies for the country's defense against Russian encroachment 16:24 Ukraine manages to fend off a new wave of Russian drones Ukraine's defenses shot down the fourth wave of drones sent the previous day, and a district administrative building was damaged in Kyiv, according to local authorities and Ukraine's military command in the east. It was part of one of the most intense missile and drone strikes of the war. Russian shelling killed two people and wounded two more in the Kharkov region, according to the Ukrainian president's office The president said the heaviest fighting is taking place near Bajmut and Soledar, and he expects more attacks before New Year's Eve 16:12 Russian soccer to study whether to quit UEFA The Union of Russian Football (UFR) will set up a working group to study whether to quit \uc2 UEFA\uc2  in favor of the \uc2 Asian Football Confederation (AFC) \uc2 because of the exclusion of the Russian national team and clubs due to the military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , local press reports Friday. The news was reported by sports dailies Sport Express and Championat, citing their own sources, and later confirmed by UFR president Alexandr Diukov. The UFR previously postponed the vote on whether to resign from \uc2 UEFA\uc2  due to the need for further consultations on Russia's potential AFC membership 16:00 Tatars want their 'president ' If any Russian region could say no to Moscow in the midst of the conflict in Ukraine, it could be none other than the autonomous republic of Tatarstan. Until the last moment, its institutions have refused to comply with the latest demand to submit to the Kremlin: to strip their leader of the title of president. In the end, the local parliament had to give in, but found a solution to maintain its own identity. The next leader of Tatarstan will be called rais, which in the Arab republics stands for the same as president, but also has roots in the Tatar tradition itself 15:48 International observers denounce Russian war crimes in Ukraine Ten months after the Russian invasion of Ukraine, overwhelming evidence is mounting that the Kremlin's troops have waged an all-out war, disregarding international laws governing the treatment of civilians and conduct on the battlefield. Ukraine is investigating more than 58,000 possible Russian war crimes: killings, kidnappings, indiscriminate shelling and sexual assaults. Reports by The Associated Press and "Frontline," recorded in a public database, have independently verified more than 600 incidents that appear to violate the laws of war. Some of those attacks were massacres that killed dozens or hundreds of civilians and, taken together, could account for thousands of individual war crimes 15:36 Beijing speaks of "crisis" in Ukraine after months of neutrality The report on the meeting between Russian President Vladimir Putin and Chinese leader Xi Jinping has been treated by Chinese state broadcaster CCTV as a meeting to address the "crisis" in Ukraine. The term marks a departure from China's usual references to the "Ukraine situation," and the change may reflect growing Chinese concern about the direction of the conflict. "Xi Jinping emphasized that China has noted that Russia has never refused to resolve the conflict through diplomatic negotiations, for which (China) expresses its appreciation," CCTV has reported 15:24 Zelenski warns that Russia still plans to capture all of Donetsk Ukraine's President Volodimir Zelenski has warned a few hours ago that Russia has not abandoned plans to capture all of Donetsk, aiming to achieve victory by New Year's Day. Zelenski has also warned Ukrainians that there could be another widespread air strike: "There are two days left in this year. Perhaps the enemy will once again try to make us celebrate the New Year in the dark. Perhaps, the occupants plan to make us suffer with the next attacks on our cities," he explained. The Ukrainian president expressed confidence in the country's resistance: "No matter what they plan, we know one thing about ourselves: we will survive. We will. We will drive them out. There is no doubt about that. And they will be punished for this terrible war. "15:12 Belarus considers it "unlikely" that the Ukrainian missile entered its territory by accident The secretary of the Belarusian Security Council stated on Friday that it is "unlikely" that a Ukrainian air defense missile shot down on Thursday had entered Belarusian airspace by accident. According to Minsk, there was most likely some "intention" behind its launch, the Russian state news agency RIA reported, based on an interview the secretary gave to state mediaRussian Sputnik Belarus. The \uc2 Belarusian Defense Ministry\uc2  said Thursday that its air defense forces had shot down a Ukrainian S-300 surface-to-air missile near the town of Harbacha in the Brest region, about 15 km from the Belarus-Ukraine border 15:00 The United States defends its global role and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the balance of the year Support for Ukraine vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis Russia, rivalry with China and sanctions against Iran have focused this 2022 the foreign policy of Joe Biden's administration in the United States, which is trying to regain the role of global leader that it considers lost during the term of his predecessor, Donald Trump. "The world does not organize itself. If the United States is not leading and organized, then someone else will do it or no one else will do it. Neither of these two options are good for us," summarized Secretary of State Antony Blinken at a press conference to take stock of the year. The U.S. response has been to arm the Kyiv government to defend itself, which has enabled Ukraine to resist the invasion and facilitated an image unthinkable months ago: the December visit to Washington of Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelensky 14:48 Russia expects diesel exports to increase ahead of EU sanctions Russia expects to increase its diesel exports next month ahead of the start of \uc2 European Union\uc2  sanctions on crude oil in February. Fuel shipments from Russian ports in the Baltic and Black Seas will rise to 2.68 million tons in January, an 8% increase over flows forecast in December and the highest export rate since at least January 2020, the article said, citing industry data. Russian cargo plans only include exports of diesel supplied to the country's ports by pipeline and some additional smaller volumes that can be transported by rail. The Primorsk data also include planned exports of 120,000 tons of diesel from Belarus, the information adds 14:36 The awkward anniversary (for Russia) of the Soviet Union, by Gonzalo Aragon\u233\'00\'E9s If we were living inside a uchronic novel from such magnificent pens as those of Philip K. Dick or Harry Turtledove, Moscow would be celebrating today. There would be parades, flags, I don't know about tanks, missiles or cosmonauts. But for sure there would be many posters with factory workers and peasants marching magnificent in the hope of building a new country, the one that the Bolshevik leader Vladimir Lenin founded on December 30, 1922. But we live in the midst of reality, with Russia bogged down in its conflict with Ukraine, isolated by Western sanctions and seeking commercial niche in Asia for its gas and oil. Too many worries for the Kremlin to think about celebrating the 100th anniversary of the birth 100 years ago of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics 14:24 Russian ruble manages to recover to close the year higher The ruble rallied Friday in its final session of a volatile year, but has maintained the path of heavy losses in December after fears about the impact of a Western oil price cap on Russia's export revenues dominated trading this month. The ruble has lost around 11% against the dollar since a price cap on Russian oil exports came into effect on December 5, although analysts said the technical impact will be felt most strongly in January-February. After falling to a record low of 120 to the dollar in March, shortly after Russia shook markets by sending tens of thousands of troops to Ukraine for the War, the ruble spent most of the year as the world's best performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosted by capital controls and a collapse in imports 14:12 Russia resorts to "kamikaze" drones again to bomb Ukraine Russia has launched 16 "kamikaze" drones against Ukraine tonight, according to the Kyiv military after the airstrike alert at 2 a.m. All of them have been shot down by air defenses, while Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski has explained that air commandos in central, southern, eastern and western Ukraine repelled in the last hours 54 Russian missiles and 11 drones. More than 18 residential buildings and 10 critical infrastructure facilities were destroyed in Thursday's attacks, according to a statement from the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2  14:00 Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez regrets the inflation caused by the war in Ukraine The President of the Government and leader of the PSOE, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, admits that "much remains to be done to continue reducing inflation still at high levels" although he assures, "with all due prudence", that the measures implemented have worked to improve the situation in Spain. This is stated in a letter sent to PSOE militants in which S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, in his capacity as secretary general of the party, points out that inflation in Spain stands at 6.8%, "well below the European average". It reflects the data known at the end of November, just the day on which the INE has published the December advance which places inflation at 5.8%. The head of the Executive ensures that the context marked by the "overcoming" of the pandemic of covid-19 the "consequences of this serious crisis are profound and have a huge global reach" arising from the war over the invasion of Ukraine by Russia 13:48 So Christmas is lived in Russia, in full war in Ukraine Russians take refuge in Christmas shopping despite the high prices and the flight of many Western brands, to forget for a while of the Russian military campaign in neighboring Ukraine. "In the last few days the number of customers has grown a lot, they come for gifts for children," Alexandr, a clerk at the Mir Kubikov toy store network that has come to supplant the Danish Lego group, which last July announced its withdrawal from Russia, told EFE. However, although the store offers brands such as MORK, Reobrix, LOZ, Sembo and others of Chinese or Russian production, more than half of the offer is still Lego, with the only difference being that the prices, which were never low, now touch 13 stars:36 Kuleba calls for pulling the Russians "into their borders and locking them up" Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dmytro Kuleba expressed his conviction in an interview published today by "RBK Ukraina" that Russia should be locked up in its borders to extinguish aggression and make its expansion impossible. According to Kuleba, "they should be put in their borders and locked up" and "let them live in their autarchy, under their father king, but not attack anyone". We must manage to agree with the thesis that "Russia is what it is, and our task is not to turn an orc into an elf, but to extinguish this aggression, and to create conditions in which the expansion of Russia would be impossible, that is, from an authoritarian and aggressive country it should become only an authoritarian and conservative country," he specified 13:23 Are we heading towards an era of wars? by Yuval Harari In February 2022 I was shocked by Vladimir Putin's attempt to conquer Ukraine. So destructive were the foreseeable repercussions of such an eventuality for Russia itself and for all of humanity that it had seemed an improbable move even for a megalomaniacal and ruthless being. However, the Russian autocrat chose to put an end in February to the most peaceful era in human history and to push humanity into a new era of war that could be worse than anything we have seen so far. In fact, it could threaten the very survival of our species 13:11 Russian military reports launching overnight "massive attack" on Ukraine's energy network \uc2 Russia's Defense Ministry\uc2  disclosed Friday that it had carried out a "massive attack" on Ukraine's energy and industrial infrastructure on Dec. 29 using high-precision weapons, Interfax reported. It said the attacks had disrupted the production and repair of military equipment and the movement of reserve troops. Yesterday Russia massively shelled Ukraine with 120 missiles, an attack denounced by Kyiv because of its magnitude 12:59 Russia Gravely Concerned About Ukrainian Missile Downed Over Belarus The Kremlin said Friday it was extremely concerned about a Ukrainian anti-aircraft missile that was shot down after flying into the airspace of its close ally Belarus on Thursday. The \uc2 Belarusian Defense Ministry\uc2  said Thursday that its air defense forces shot down a Ukrainian S-300 surface-to-air missile near the village of Harbacha in the Brest region, about 15 km (9 miles) from the Belarus-Ukraine border. It occurred as Russia fired dozens of missiles at Ukrainian towns in one of the largest waves of attacks of the conflict. "This is an incident that causes great concern, not only for us, but also for our Belarusian partners," Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov told reporters Friday 12:47 Putin and Xi Jinping discuss Ukraine war President Vladimir Putin spoke Friday with Chinese leader Xi Jinping amid signs of Beijing's impatience over the broader political and economic impact of Russia's invasion of Ukraine. Their year-end video call, the first talks since Xi and Putin met in person in Uzbekistan in September, underscores Moscow's growing dependence on Beijing. Putin called Xi "dear friend" during a televised portion of the meeting and the Chinese leader responded in kind. Xi also thanked Putin for sending a congratulatory message after a congress of China's ruling Communist Party in October that gave him a third term in power, which defies precedent. China ready to expand "strategic partnership," Xi said 12:35 Kuleba thanks unprecedented aid to Ukraine, calls for more weapons Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dmytro Kuleba thanked in an interview published today by "RBK Ukraina" for the unprecedented support Ukraine has received in armaments, while expressing confidence in being able to count on longer-range ballistic missiles and modern drones. "The assistance with arms and ammunition that countries from all over the world have provided us for ten months is unprecedented in terms of volumes, nomenclature and number of countries involved in this process," he said. He also recalled that this week, in his speech to the Verkhovna Rada, Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelensky emphasized this "historic support" and expressed his gratitude for it. "Of course, we are at war and we always need more, and we always ask for more, but we should remember this simple fact: we are receiving the greatest support any country has ever received, at least in the 21st century," he has added. 12:24 Inflation and the economic impact of the war, challenges for the Baltics in 2023 Controlling inflation and managing the economic consequences of the war in Ukraine are the most prominent challenges in 2023 for the Baltic countries, close to a conflict that has had a major impact on all areas of life in the three states. Latvia's economy has entered a moderate recession "and that will continue during the first half of the year" because of the reduction of the population's purchasing power due to inflation, the more restrictive monetary policy of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and difficulties for the export sector, according to the analysis of Morten Hansen, head of the Economics department of the Stockholm School of Economics in Riga (SSE Riga). 12:12 Putin explains that Russia is now one of China's top oil and gas suppliers Russian President Vladimir Putin said today, Friday, that Russia has become one of China's top oil and gas suppliers, with 13.8 billion cubic meters of gas shipped to China through the Siberian Power pipeline in the first 11 months of 2022. Speaking at the start of a video conference with Chinese President Xi Jinping, Putin said, "According to this year's results, Russia has become one of the leaders in oil exports to China." Russia has thus overtaken Saudi Arabia as China's main crude oil supplier last month. Putin further added that Russia was China's second-largest supplier of pipeline gas and fourth-largest supplier of liquefied natural gas (LNG). He has also said that in December shipments had exceeded daily contractual obligations by 18%. Russian energy exports to China have risen sharply since Moscow sent its armed forces into Ukraine on February 24, after which Western countries imposed unprecedented sanctions on Russia. 12:02 Putin has expressed condolences over Pele's death, highlighting his unique talent Russian President Vladimir Putin today, Friday, expressed condolences over the death of Edson Arantes do Nascimento 'Pele', who died at the age of 82, and wanted to highlight his unique talent for which millions of people all over the world, including in Russia, became fond of soccer. "Edson Arantes do Nascimento is a distinguished son of the Brazilian people. Thanks to his unique talent and bewitching game, soccer became the favorite sport of millions of people around the world, including in Russia," the Kremlin chief said in a telegram to Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro. Putin has added that he was lucky enough to meet Pele personally the last time he was in Russia in 2017. "I will forever keep the best memory of him," he has concluded. 11:50 Serbia raises its army's alert to the maximum because of the tension in Kosovo Serbian President Aleksandar Vucic on Tuesday described as "difficult and dynamic" his contacts with the international community to try to defuse the situation in Kosovo, and as "hysterical" the pressures for the Kosovo Serbs to lift the road blockade they maintain in the north of the former Serbian province that became independent in 2008. "We must advocate for the interests of the Serb people (of Kosovo), they are not on the barricades for nothing," Vucic told reporters. 11:36 Latvia will favor in 2023 further international isolation of Russia Latvia aims in 2023 to favor further international isolation of Russia and eventually the adoption of new sanctions for the invasion of Ukraine, in a year that will be dominated by "three interconnected crises", security, energy and economic. Latvian Foreign Minister Edgars Rink?vi?s assured in a foreign policy statement that his country will focus its foreign action on consolidating support for Ukraine, "which will include military, financial and humanitarian aid", achieving greater international isolation of Russia and reinforcing Latvia's security with an increased NATO presence on Latvian soil. Rink?vi?s specified that in relation to Ukraine Riga will support the reconstruction of the country and its reforms by means of instruments of development cooperation, as well as participation in the military mission of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU) and assistance in the training of Ukrainian soldiers. 11:21 Putin says relations with China are the "best in history" Russian President Vladimir Putin today, Friday, called relations with China the "best in history" in a videoconference conversation with his counterpart from the Asian country, Xi Jinping. "Our relations worthily withstand all tests, demonstrate maturity and stability and continue to expand dynamically," the Kremlin chief told Xi. According to Putin, Moscow-Beijing relations are a "model of cooperation between great powers in the 21st century." "With you we equally see the causes, the course and logic of the transformations of the global geopolitical landscape," he added. The Russian leader also stressed that a "special" place in the relations is occupied by the military-technical cooperation between the two countries. According to him, such cooperation "contributes to ensuring the security of our countries and maintaining stability in key regions." "We intend to strengthen cooperation between the armed forces of Russia and China," Putin assured. As for economic relations, he has said that despite the external conjuncture "illegitimate restrictions and direct blackmail by some countries of the West, Russia and China have managed to ensure record rhythms of trade exchange." 11:05 Putin says Russia expects state visit from Xi in spring Russian President Vladimir Putin said today, Friday, that Chinese President Xi Jinping is expected to pay a state visit to Russia in spring 2023. Introducing a video conference between the two leaders broadcast on Russian state television, Putin said that the importance of relations between Moscow and Beijing is increasing as a stabilizing factor, adding that his goal is to deepen military cooperation between the two countries. 10:52 Russia at historic lows in public perception Russia's public image suffered a significant decline following its invasion of Ukraine, dropping 16 points in its positive influence internationally and leaving it at historic lows, according to Ipsos research for the 'Halifax International Security Forum 2022'. Before the invasion of Ukraine, almost half (45%) of people worldwide thought that Russia would have a positive influence internationally, but today that percentage drops 16 points to 29%, according to the survey of 33 markets conducted by Ipsos on its Global Advisor online platform. Russia's public image suffers a significant decline immediately after its invasion of Ukraine almost nine years ago.months. As Ipsos explained, as the invasion drags on, "expectations that Russia will have a positive influence on world affairs has been diminishing" 10:38 Ukraine denounces a "massive" attack with more than 120 missiles on Kyiv and other cities in the country Russia today launched more than 120 missiles on Ukraine, said Mikhail Podolyak, advisor to the head of the Ukrainian President's Office. "29.12.22. All over Ukraine more than 120 missiles fired by the 'evil Russian world' to destroy critical infrastructure and massively kill civilians," Podolyak wrote in a message on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  picked up by the Ukrinform agency. He added, sarcastically, to be "waiting for further suggestions from peacemakers on peaceful settlement, security guarantees for Russia and inconveniences of provocations. "10:25 Kyiv warns that countries that remain neutral are supporting Russia Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dmytro Kuleba today appealed to the international community after the new massive missile attack by Russian forces and said that defending a neutral stance is tantamount to taking sides with Russia. "Senseless barbarism. These are the only words that come to mind as Russia launches another wave of missiles against peaceful Ukrainian cities on New Year's Eve. There can be no 'neutrality' in the face of such massive war crimes. Pretending to be 'neutral' is tantamount to siding with Russia," he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , quoted by "Ukrainka Pravda". For his part, Ukrainian Prime Minister Denys Schmyhal noted in a message on Telegram picked up by the Ukrinform agency that "in its tenth large-scale attack, Russia is trying to deprive Ukrainians of light before the New Year." 10:11 Zelensky: "Russia has less and less missiles and is sinking into a dead end" The President of Ukraine, Volodymir Zelensky, has assured, after the new massive attack suffered today, that Russia has "less and less missiles" and that with these aggressions it is sinking into "a dead end". "With each of these missile attacks, Russia is only sinking deeper and deeper into a dead end. They have fewer and fewer missiles. Instead, the status of the world's largest terrorist will have consequences for Russia and its citizens for a long time to come. And every rocket only confirms that all this must end with a court," he has stated in his usual evening address. Russia on Thursday launched a new massive attack on Ukraine's energy infrastructure that has left at least 3 wounded in Kyiv and 90 percent of the city of Lviv without power. According to Zelenski, the Ukrainian armed forces shot down 54 missiles and 11 attack drones, although some bombs were successful and hit their targets. 09:55 Ukrainian Air Force has shot down 16 "Shahed" drones overnight The Ukrainian Air Force reported today, Friday, the downing last night of a total of 16 kamikaze drones attacking the country from the southeast and north. "On the night of December 29-30, the enemy attacked Ukraine with Iranian-made 'Shahed-131/136' kamikaze drones. In total, 16 'Shaheds' were launched, which attacked Ukraine from the southeastern and northern directions," according to the message on Telegram. It adds that to overcome Ukraine's air defense system, the Russian military has tried to use the Dnipro riverbed. "The 16 kamikaze drones were destroyed by the forces and means of the 'East' and 'Center' air commands, as well as by the air defense units of other components of the Ukrainian Defense Forces," he has specified. 09:41 Are we heading towards an era of wars? by Yuval Harari In February 2022 I was shocked by Vladimir Putin's attempt to conquer Ukraine. So destructive were the foreseeable repercussions of such an eventuality for Russia itself and for all of humanity that it had seemed an improbable move even for a megalomaniacal and ruthless being. However, the Russian autocrat chose to put an end in February to the most peaceful era in human history and to push humanity into a new era of war that could be worse than anything we have seen so far. In fact, it could threaten the very survival of our species 09:27 Russian ruble strengthens against dollar as volatile year ends The ruble has strengthened today Friday, heading towards the 71 mark against the dollar as a highly volatile 2022 comes to an end, with the last month of trading dominated by fears over the impact of a Western oil price cap on Russia's export revenues. The ruble has lost about 13% against the dollar since the price cap on Russian oil exports came into effect on Dec. 5, although analysts have said the technical impact would be felt most strongly in January-February. 09:13 Stoltenberg calls for more arms for Ukraine NATO Secretary General Jens Stoltenberg has called on alliance member states to supply more weapons to Ukraine, according to an interview published today, Friday. "I call on allies to do more. It is in our security interest to make sure that Ukraine prevails and that (Russian President Vladimir) Putin does not win," Stoltenberg has told German news agency DPA. Stoltenberg has also noted that it is perhaps even more important that Ukraine receives sufficient ammunition for the systems already in place, adding that the need for ammunition and spare parts is "enormous." Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelensky already called last week in a speech to a group of Western leaders for a wide range of weapons and air defense systems, with which to help efforts to counter the Russian invasion. 08:59 Ukraine moves forward with "de-Russification" The statue of Russian Empress Catherine II has been removed from the Ukrainian city of Odessa as part of the "de-Russification" of public space in Ukraine. "I thank the residents of Odessa, who expressed their position that the Russian imperial heritage has no place in modern, legal and democratic Ukraine," the head of the local regional military administration Maksym Marchenko said today, calling the removal of the statue a "historic event." According to local photographers Kostiantin and Vlada Liberov, the statue was removed "in a civilized manner" after a process that lasted almost the whole day. It will now be handed over to a local museum for preservation 08:46 U.S. aid to Ukraine set to exceed $100 billion Since the war began, Capitol Hill has given the green light to three aid packages to Kyiv in March, May and September, amounting to $67.3 billion in total. And it is about to approve the fourth, which will total up to $111 billion The assistance, promoted by the Biden Administration, is intended to cover military aid, both financial and in the form of arms shipments. But also humanitarian assistance, economic support to the Ukrainian government or government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  related to managing the effects of the Ukrainian conflict, such as support to the energy sector to alleviate the costs associated with the war. 08:33 How Russia and Ukraine are facing the end of the year Ukraine is preparing for a New Year's holiday season marked by blackouts and Russian aerial bombardments against its civilian infrastructure, while the front seems to be stabilizing for the time being. For their part, Russians are taking refuge in Christmas shopping despite high prices and the flight of many Western brands, to forget for a while about the military campaign in neighboring Ukraine. 08:19 Sirens sound in Kyiv as its governor warns of drone strikes Kyiv residents were urged to head for bomb shelters early today (Friday) as sirens blared across the city, a day after Russia carried out the biggest airstrike since the war began in February. Shortly after 2 a.m., Kyiv City Hall issued an alert on its channel on the Telegram messaging app about the anti-aircraft sirens, asking its residents to head for shelters. Olekskiy Kuleba, governor of Kyiv region, has said on Telegram that a "drone attack" was taking place. 08:01 Brent crude oil down 1.67% to $82.26 The price of a barrel of Brent crude oil for delivery in February ended the day on the London futures market at $82.26, down 1.67% from the end of the previous session. The North Sea crude, the European benchmark, ended the day on the International Exchange Futures with a decrease of 1.4 dollars with respect to the last negotiation, when it closed at 83.66 dollars. The Brent quotation continues to be conditioned by the situation in Ukraine, after Russia has refused to work on a "peace plan" if Kyiv does not assume the annexation of the regions of Lugansk, Zaporiyia, Kherson and Donetsk that Putin proclaimed in September. In addition, Russian President Vladimir Putin has banned the export of oil and oil products to countries that apply a cap on the price of Russian crude. 07:47 Putin's man in the Donbass summers in a mansion on the Costa Brava Putin's man in the Donbass, the architect of the annexation referendums, has one foot in Palamos. He owns a mansion overlooking the sea in Cala dels Corbs, in an area of great beauty where four houses were built in the late 1970s. Sergey Kirienko, a senior Kremlin official, acquired one in December 2013 through a front man, Robert Gndolian, a close friend of his son, Russian investigative media Sobesednik (Interlocutor) and Vazhnie istorii (Important Stories) 07 have reported:34 Belarus demands Ukrainian ambassador give explanation for missile downed on its territory Belarus summoned Ukraine's ambassador after it said it had shot down a Ukrainian air defense missile over its territory, the \uc2 Belarusian Foreign Ministry\uc2  said. "We have demanded that Ukraine conduct a thorough investigation into all the circumstances of the missile launch, bring those responsible to justice and take comprehensive measures to prevent a repetition of such incidents in the future. Which could have catastrophic consequences for everyone." said ministry spokesman Anatoly Glaz. The \uc2 Belarusian Defense Ministry\uc2  said its air defenses had shot down a Ukrainian S-300 missile Thursday morning, during one of Russia's biggest missile strikes against Ukraine since the start of the war 07:21 Zelenski: "Russia has fewer and fewer missiles and is sinking into a dead end" Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelenski has assured, after the new massive attack suffered today, that Russia has "fewer and fewer missiles" and that with these aggressions it is sinking into "a dead end". "With each of these missile attacks, Russia is only sinking deeper and deeper into a dead end. They have fewer and fewer missiles. Instead, the status of the world's largest terrorist will have consequences for Russia and its citizens for a long time to come. And every rocket only confirms that all this must end with a court," he has stated in his usual evening address. Russia on Thursday launched a new massive attack on Ukraine's energy infrastructure that has left at least 3 wounded in Kyiv and 90 percent of the city of Lviv without power. According to Zelenski, the Ukrainian armed forces shot down 54 missiles and 11 attack drones, although some bombs were successful and hit their targets. 07:08 Russia may allow Kazakh oil to pass through to Germany The Russian government on Thursday expressed readiness to approve the transit of Kazakh oil through the Druzhba pipeline bound for Germany. "Yes, that issue was discussed. We see it as something normal," Alexandr Novak, Russian Deputy Prime Minister, told local media. Novak said the government would give the go-ahead for "part of the Kazakh oil" to be shipped from the Baltic port of Ust-Luga to the Druzhba, whose northern branch crosses Belarus and Poland before reaching German territory. As it became known today, Kazakh operator \uc2 KazTransOil\uc2  asked Russian pipeline monopoly \uc2 Transneft\uc2  for authorization to pump 1.2 million tons in 2023 through the Druzhba to Germany 06:57 Ukraine denounces "massive" attack with more than 120 missiles on Kyiv and other cities in the country Russia today launched more than 120 missiles on Ukraine, Mikhail Podolyak, advisor to the head of the Ukrainian President's Office, said. "29.12.22. All over Ukraine more than 120 missiles fired by the 'evil Russian world' to destroy critical infrastructure and massively kill civilians," Podolyak wrote in a message on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  picked up by the Ukrinform agency. He added, sarcastically, to be "waiting for further suggestions from peacemakers on peaceful settlement, security guarantees for Russia and inconveniences of provocations. "06:48 Russia's gas supplies to Europe continue to shrink Russia's \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's pipeline gas exports to Europe will shrink further next year after reaching a post-Soviet record low in 2022, which could put pressure on state coffers from 2023 onwards, according to analysts and industry experts. Kremlin-controlled \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  said Wednesday that its exports outside the former Soviet Union will reach 100.9 billion cubic meters (bcm) this year. "I assume exports to Europe and Turkey will fall from 175 billion cubic meters in 2021 to 85 billion cubic meters in 2022 and 65.billion cubic meters in 2023," Ron Smith of Moscow-based brokerage BCS said Thursday 06:39 Borrell denounces Russia's "massive and senseless" missile attack on Ukraine European Union (EU) High Representative for Foreign Affairs Josep Borrell denounced Thursday that Russia has carried out "another massive and senseless" missile attack on Ukraine, which has "deliberately" killed civilians and destroyed infrastructure such as medical facilities. "Another massive and senseless Russian missile attack against Ukraine, indiscriminately destroying infrastructure and medical facilities, deliberately targeting and killing civilians," Borrell said in a message through his profile on the social network \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . The head of EU diplomacy stressed that "there will be no impunity for Russian war crimes" and that the EU will continue its support for Ukraine. Russia today launched more than 120 missiles over Ukraine, said Mikhail Podolyak, advisor to the head of the Ukrainian President's Office 06:30 War Ukraine - Russia, live | The latest news on the conflict Good morning! Welcome to the live monitoring of the war between Ukraine and Russia. From now on and in the next few hours we will inform you about all the news related to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221230eicu00hpj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
