\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Czechia At 'AA-' FC; 'AA' LC; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 4,677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2022\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 14 Oct 2022 16:07 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Czechia At 'AA-' FC; 'AA' LC; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We expect that a sharp slowdown in the eurozone, reduced Russian \par energy supplies to Europe, and high inflation will weigh on the Czech \par Republic's (Czechia's) economic activity in 2023. \par      -- We now project Czechia's inflation will average about 17% in 2022, and \par we expect the economy to stagnate in 2023. \par      -- Despite uncertainty over the evolution of the conflict in Ukraine, \par including heightened risks to Europe's energy supply, we believe Czechia's low \par government debt, solid external financial profile, and strengthening natural \par gas reserve levels provide buffers that can help mitigate these risks. \par      -- We have affirmed our unsolicited 'AA-/A-1+' foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par 'AA/A-1+' local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on the Czech Republic and maintained the \par stable outlook. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Oct. 14, 2022, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'AA-/A-1+' unsolicited long- \par and short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings and its 'AA/A-1+' \par unsolicited long- and short-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on \par the Czech Republic (Czechia). The outlook on both long-term ratings remains \par stable. The transfer and convertibility assessment is 'AA+'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our view that the buffers provided by Czechia's \par solid government and external balance sheets, including still sizable foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, are able to mitigate the adverse macroeconomic impact \par stemming from the Russia-Ukraine conflict. \par  \par Downside scenario \par The ratings could come under pressure if: \par      -- The negative impact of the conflict in Ukraine proves more severe than \par we currently expect, hurting economic output while causing budget deficits and \par government debt to soar above our current expectations. \par      -- The supply of energy to Czechia becomes meaningfully constrained, \par preventing the country from sustaining industrial production and leading to a \par much weaker medium-term growth outlook. \par      -- If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech National Bank (CNB)  \f28 \uc2 \f2 overextends its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , materially reducing its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and prompting \par concerns about the credibility of the monetary regime and the effectiveness of \par its execution. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could raise the ratings if, once the effects of the conflict subside, \par Czechia's income levels improve to those of similarly rated sovereigns \par globally. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The Russia-Ukraine conflict and the associated shock of stagflation is \par exerting pressure on Czechia's open and industrialized economy through \par multiple channels, such as trade, inflation, energy supply concerns, \par tightening monetary conditions, and weakening business and consumer confidence \par levels. \par  \par Domestic demand has held firmly through the first half of 2022, providing \par support to the annual growth forecast, but we anticipate that very high \par inflation will eat into households' purchasing power in the second half of the \par year, thus negatively affecting private consumption and investment. \par  \par We expect Czechia's full-year real GDP growth will reach 2.5% in 2022 but will \par stagnate in 2023, compared with our previous projections of 1.8% and 4.0%, \par respectively. We have also raised our annual average inflation forecast to \par 16.8% in 2022 and 8.0% in 2023, reflecting global energy and food price \par pressures, a very tight labor market, supply-side bottlenecks, and loosening \par fiscal policies. \par  \par Czech inflation, as measured by consumer price inflation, reached a multi-year \par high of 18% year over year in September 2022 and has prompted assertive \par monetary policy actions from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  . So far in 2022, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has hiked its \par policy rate four times from 3.75% at the end of 2021 to the current level of \par 7%, with the policy rate kept stable over the past two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings. \par  \par Additionally, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intermittently in the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par market since March this year in support of the koruna. While the total amount \par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not been disclosed, we observe a sizable 22% decline (in \par U.S. dollar terms) in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves since March 31, \par 2022, which stood at US$135.9 billion on Sept. 31, 2022. Despite this \par decrease, we note that the reserves remain very high by international \par standards, at about 47% of GDP or 8.4 months of imports. \par  \par Based on our current macroeconomic assumptions, we think the Czech economy \par will maintain its resilience to the current shock. We base this view on what \par we regard as Czechia's solid economic fundamentals and the temporarily \par improving energy supply outlook on the back of government efforts to secure \par non-Russian natural gas resources, alongside its ample fiscal headroom and \par still sizable foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve buffers. More broadly, our assessment \par of the country's strong institutional arrangements and high degree of monetary \par flexibility underpin our view of its credit quality. That said, the country \par reports lower income levels than most sovereigns rated in the 'AA' category, \par which weighs on the ratings. \par  \par If the situation in Ukraine broadened to a conflict between \f28 \uc2 \f2 NATO\f28 \uc2 \f2   and Russia, \par it would likely lead to substantial downside pressures to the base-case \par economic, balance-of-payments, and fiscal projections published in this \par report, with likely adverse impact on the sovereign creditworthiness. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: High inflation and falling external demand \par will hurt economic growth in 2023 \par      -- We currently expect real GDP growth to stagnate in 2023, as domestic \par consumption stalls and external demand drops from a broad-based eurozone \par slowdown. \par      -- We expect energy supplies to remain sufficient through the upcoming \par winter because gas reserves stand almost 90% full, with additional security \par from a leased Dutch liquid natural gas (LNG) terminal. \par      -- The new government will introduce windfall taxation on utilities and \par banks to finance the fiscal costs related to a price cap on energy. \par  \par Czechia's economic growth has shown resilience through the first half of this \par year, with year-on-year GDP growth at 3.7% in the second quarter providing a \par solid carryover into the full-year outturn. Behind the headline numbers, a \par substantial part of the strong output was attributable to solid fixed capital \par formation and households tapping into their high savings to support a \par post-pandemic consumption rebound. \par  \par We expect contraction of output over the second half of the year as external \par demand and household consumption cool because of the continued increase in the \par cost of living, especially energy prices, and by a tighter monetary policy. We \par project Czechia's real economic growth will slow to 0.15% in 2023 with \par additional significant downside related to the further development of the \par conflict in Ukraine and the direct and indirect repercussions it has on the \par whole of Europe's economy. \par  \par As a small and open economy, Czechia's economic development will be hit by \par lower business activity at its main European partners, particularly Germany, \par which receives over 30% of Czech goods exports. We now forecast the euro area \par will expand by just 0.3% in 2023 and Germany will post a mild full-year \par recession of 0.3% next year. \par  \par Although Czechia's direct trade exposure is limited, with Ukraine, Russia, and \par Belarus representing a combined 3% of exports and imports, its heavy reliance \par on Russian gas makes it vulnerable to exogenous volatility. Specifically, \par Czechia imports 90% of gas and about 50% of oil from Russia, therefore being \par heavily reliant on energy imported from Russia. Natural gas represents 20% of \par the country's total energy mix, with coal representing 35%, oil 25%, nuclear \par 17%, and minor portions of renewables making up the rest. We note that the \par Czech authorities have re-commissioned previously closed coal plants to secure \par the domestic energy supply. \par  \par Energy supply concerns are very significant. Any abrupt shortages to natural \par gas or oil supplies would be felt mainly in Czechia's industrial sector and \par materially impact economic activity. Currently, Czechia's gas storage levels \par stand at about 90%, which we believe should cover the upcoming winter months. \par In addition, the authorities have leased a shared LNG terminal in the \par Netherlands that is estimated to reduce the reliance on Russian gas by up to \par one-third. \par  \par In terms of oil imports, Czechia has received a temporary exemption for oil \par imports from Russia through the Druzhba pipeline until alternative sources are \par put in place. We understand that the Czech authorities are exploring \par possibilities of increasing the capacity of the Transalpine pipeline, which \par today supplies about 50% of Czechia's oil imports, to remove its dependance on \par Russian hydrocarbons. In the event of disruption to the Druzhba oil flows, we \par understand that the state's strategic oil reserves would suffice for a 90-day \par respite until alternatives sources came on stream. \par  \par While event risks to energy supply remain, we do not project the current \par environment will have a permanent negative effect on the Czech economy. For \par now, the country's labor market seems hardly affected. The unemployment rate \par remains around 2.5%, one of the lowest levels in the EU. We do not anticipate \par that in the near term, and in absence of a more abrupt reduction in energy \par supplies, the cooling economic environment will result in any material \par increase in unemployment; but rather help fill the high level of vacancies. \par Furthermore, the inflow of over 400,000 Ukrainian refugees, per October 2023, \par will inevitably result in a boost to exports (Ukrainian refugees are \par statistically considered nonresidents) and government spending, as well as \par provide some relief for the very tight Czech labor market. \par  \par We believe the increased inflow of additional EU funds from the next EU \par Multiannual Financing Framework 2021-2027, as well as the Next Generation EU \par Funds (NGEU), will support economic activity. Total EU fund absorption will \par likely exceed 2% of GDP this year and next, given the additional NGEU funds. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14 Oct 2022 16:07 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Czechia At 'AA-' FC; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The incumbent government, consisting of two electoral alliances with inherent \par and substantial ideological differences, has remained united and navigated \par various instances of friction throughout the past year. It has recently been \par under pressure to produce mitigating measures that alleviate cost-of-living \par pressures on households from the soaring energy prices, and in September the \par government announced a comprehensive price-capping scheme that will cost an \par estimated 2% of GDP, which the government intends to finance through windfall \par taxation imposed on utilities and banks. \par  \par Overall, we view Czechia's institutional capabilities as a credit strength. \par Over the past few years, Czech governments have established a strong record of \par taking a firm pro-EU stance and pro-business policies, while generally \par maintaining fiscal prudence. Importantly, the independence and effectiveness \par of key political and economic institutions, such as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  , are not impeded. \par We do not anticipate any near-term challenges to this institutional setup. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: High inflation has prompted a significant \par monetary response from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   in recent months \par      -- Rising energy and food prices are exacerbating already elevated \par inflation, and we expect inflation to average about 17% this year. \par      -- In response, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has hiked its policy rate to 7% currently from a \par low of 0.25% in May 2021, complemented by meaningful foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in support of the koruna. \par      -- The government will tap into windfall revenue to help finance \par energy-related extraordinary fiscal support measures over 2022-2024. \par  \par Despite additional tax revenue on the back of higher nominal economic growth, \par we expect general government deficits will remain elevated over 2022-2023. In \par September 2022, the government announced a Czech koruna 130 billion (2% of \par GDP) fiscal package, comprising predominantly a capping scheme on energy \par prices with the intention to alleviate pressures on households and small \par businesses. The majority of this scheme will be financed by using dividends \par from state companies, as well as income from planned windfall taxes on utility \par entities and banking sector and from emission allowances. The windfall tax on \par utilities will be complementary to the revenue generated from the European \par wide price cap of EUR180 per megawatt hour on wind, solar, and nuclear power \par generation. Any revenue above that will be taken by the government. \par  \par We forecast Czechia's fiscal deficit at 4.4% of GDP in 2022 and expect a \par similar level for 2023. Our budgetary projections incorporate multiple revenue \par and spending-side pressures and are still subject to risks. For example, \par refugee-related spending could increase significantly as could military \par spending in light of the commitment to raise the related spending to 2% of GDP \par (1% of GDP in 2020). In addition, the indexation of wages and pensions in line \par inflation will put additional pressure on spending. Hence, we believe that the \par government's budgetary consolidation will be more gradual and delayed compared \par with our previous assessment. \par  \par Czechia's relatively low stock of government debt will help the government \par digest the comparatively elevated budget deficits. We forecast the stock of \par government debt will remain stable at around 40% of GDP between 2022 and 2025. \par We project net government debt at about 33% of GDP in 2025, which ranks as one \par of the lowest in the EU. \par  \par We expect the Czech government to continue to finance itself predominantly in \par the domestic market. Despite the uptick in government borrowing costs on the \par back of higher interest rates at home and abroad, the favorable maturity and \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  composition of the debt stock will allow rising interest rates to \par only gradually re-price into the government's interest bill. The average tenor \par of the state's treasury debt is about 6.5 years and the share of foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated debt remains a modest 15% of the total. Furthermore, \par strong nominal growth will sustain government tax revenue over 2022-2023. We \par expect the share of interest payments in general government revenue to be \par broadly stable during our forecast horizon through 2025, at about 2.5%. \par  \par Inflation has increased rapidly over 2022 and stood at 18.0% in September, up \par from 17.2% in August. We expect full-year a harmonized index of consumer \par prices of 16.8% for 2022. Inflation in Czechia was already elevated before the \par Ukraine conflict, partly due to its tight labor market, and reaching 10% year \par on year already in February. Inflation in the country has therefore exceeded \par that of most Central and Eastern European countries for some time. \par  \par The high inflation, combined with external factors forcing many prices up, has \par created a difficult policy environment for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which has an inflation \par target of 2%. To curb inflation and anchor expectations regarding future \par inflation, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   frontloaded its policy tightening to the first half of \par 2022, hiking rates earlier and more rapidly than most other central banks in \par the region. Currently, the main policy rate stands at 7%, having been hiked \par four times in 2022 from 3.75% in December 2021, and compared with 0.25% in May \par 2021. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has kept rates stable over the past two meetings and future rate \par hikes will likely be tied to a situation where wages shoot up materially more \par than \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   expectations of nominal wage growth of 4.5% for this year, in line \par with the actual developments through the second quarter. \par  \par In addition to rate hikes, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intermittently in the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since March, during a period of heightened exchange rate \par volatility following the onset of the Russia-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conflict. While the total \par amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not been disclosed, we observe a 22% decline (in \par U.S. dollar terms) in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves between March and \par September this year. We consider accentuated use of non-conventional monetary \par policy tools and the magnitude of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as limiting the free \par floating nature of the koruna. That said, we maintain our assessment of the \par strong credibility of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  's monetary policy regime. We expect the \par country's flexible exchange rate will help contain the build-up of external \par imbalances and, in tandem with still sizable official reserves (equivalent to \par 47% of GDP), continue to serve as a buffer against external shocks. \par  \par We expect the current account to swing into a sizable deficit of 4.6% of GDP \par this year, driven by a deterioration in the terms of trade. We project the \par current account to remain in deficit over the next few years, based on a \par sluggish recovery in exports, along with imports related to EU-funded \par projects. Moreover, we forecast the terms of trade will gradually improve from \par 2024, which will help to narrow the current account deficit through 2025. \par  \par We consider Czechia's external profile as a rating strength. We project a \par temporary dent to the country's net creditor position in 2022-2023 due to the \par reduction in public external assets, namely its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, but \par we expect liquid external assets will again exceed external debt in the outer \par years of our forecast. This is because we expect capital account inflows, \par including \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   funds and net foreign direct investment, to exceed current \par account deficits over the 2024-2025, thereby curbing external leverage and \par replenishing foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves over the next few years. Nevertheless, \par downside risks to our assessment of the external position could rise if the \par economy's external financial position increased, for example, through rising \par external leverage or rising external financing needs. \par  \par The banking sector remains predominately foreign owned, profitable, \par well-capitalized, and funded by domestic deposits. In our view, it poses a \par very limited contingent liability risk for public finances, particularly in \par light of the shift from a bail-out to an effective bail-in regime for the \par majority of banks in the country. We consider that the Czech regulator \par effectively monitors commercial banks under a fairly advanced regulatory \par framework and that it has enough power and independence to enforce regulations \par and implement macroprudential policies. In our view, foreign strategic \par shareholders will remain committed to supporting their Czech subsidiaries if \par needed, thus being a backstop for Czech banks if there are material shocks. \par  \par The Czech banking sector performed strongly in 2021, and we expect resilience \par in 2022. While we expect Czech banks to benefit from higher interest income on \par repricing of assets and new loans, debt repayment capacities of households and \par corporates will be tested and ultimately have an impact on credit losses in \par the next 12 months. Similarly, the Russia-Ukraine conflict will likely lead to \par a temporary increase in credit losses because of adverse secondary effects on \par the corporate sector through supply-chain bottlenecks and spikes in commodity \par prices. \par  \par That said, the increase in policy rates by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CNB\f28 \uc2 \f2   will translate into higher \par margins for the banking sector and help banks maintain solid profitability, in \par our view, even in the face of recently announced windfall taxation by the \par Czech government. We assume banks to preserve sound profitability, strong \par capitalization, and self-funded natures with high liquidity buffers. Tighter \par financial conditions on capital markets have not hit Czech banks because they \par are mainly funded with loyal customers' deposits. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011