\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Oil prices fall to pre-Ukraine invasion levels\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 ? Financial markets resent geopolitical tensions in Asia. Electronic screen with the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  index in Hong Kong.Afp Photo Clara Zepeda Peri\u243\'00\'F3dico La JornadaFriday, August 5, 2022, p. 24Withthe high risk of recession and galloping inflation in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , driven by energy prices, the price of WTI oil fell 2.3 percent and closed at 88.5 dollars per barrel, below 90 dollars for the first time since February, the month in which Russia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 .Brent, meanwhile, fell 3.18 percent to 93.7 dollars per barrel and the Mexican blend fell to 84.20 dollars, the lowest level since February 10.\par \par \uc2 Oil's losses accelerated on Wednesday after U.S. data showed crude and gasoline stockpiles rose.\par \par \uc2 Natural gas futures contracts for September lost 11 cents to 8.12 dollars, and gasoline futures expiring in the same month lost 11 cents to 2.79 dollars a gallon.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar registered significant weakness against its main international crosses, amid geopolitical tensions in Asia, bad economic news in Europe and awaiting employment data in\par \par \uc2 the United States.Due to the above, the peso closed Thursday with an appreciation of 0.59 percent against its US counterpart, equivalent to 12.08 cents, to end at 20.3413 units per dollar spot (wholesale). According to data from the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 , this is the second consecutive gain of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar; the peso traded between a high of 20.3910 and a low of 20.2940 units.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, the DXY, which measures the performance of the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of six international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , registered a 0.73 percent drop to 105.597 units.\par \par \uc2 The market anticipates that 250 thousand jobs will be created in July, but a lower than expected job creation could cause greater risk aversion, as it would imply a deterioration of the labor market after two quarterly GDP contractions.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street closed mixed: the Nasdaq ended higher, with a gain of 0.41 percent, at 12,420.58 points, while the Dow Jones fell 0.26 percent to 32,726 units and the S&P 500 increased only 0.08 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Mexican Stock Exchange (BMV) lost 0.16% to close at 46,916.17 units, affected by the drop in the price of Banco del Baj\u237\'00\'EDo (2.59%), Pe\u241\'00\'F1oles (2.40) and Grupo M\u233\'00\'E9xico (2.05) shares.\par \par \uc2 The Bank ofEngland\par \par \uc2 on Thursday made the highest interest rate increase in 27 years, in order to counteract inflation that is expected to exceed 13 percent annually in October, when the United Kingdom will enter a recession that will last until the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee raised the bank rate by half a point, from 1.25 to 1.75 percent. This is the largest increase since 1995 and brings it to the highest level in 13 years.\par \par \uc2 This is the sixth consecutive increase in the rate since December last year, in order to combat rising inflation in the country, which already reached 9.4 percent annually in June, a record in 40 years and more than four times the 2 percent target set by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , which has triggered strikes, amid the rise in the cost of living, particularly for households with fewer resources.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts that inflation will reach 13.3 percent in October, the highest level since 1980, mainly due to the rise in energy prices following the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 With information from Reuters and Afp\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220805ei85004a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
