\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 At least three killed in 13-missile attack in central Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 4,350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 KeymomentsKey moments4h agoAt least 358 minors killed in Ukraine since start of conflict17h agoWhat happened in the last hours EL PA\u205\'00\'CDS offers free of charge the latest news on the conflict in Ukraine as a public service. If you want to support our journalism, subscribe.\par \par \uc2 At least three people have been killed in a Russian attack with 13 missiles on a military airfield and railroad network in Kirovograd province in central Ukraine, local governor Andriy Raikovich said Saturday. In a televised interview, Raikovich detailed that at least nine Ukrainian soldiers have been injured and that the provincial capital, Kropivnitsky, is currently without electricity due to infrastructure damage. Ukrainian authorities have also denounced a Russian missile attack on the Black Sea port of Odessa, a day after Kiev and Moscow signed two separate agreements with Turkey - with \uc2 UN\uc2  mediation and supervision - to resume grain exports from Ukraine. "The enemy has attacked the port of Odessa with cruise missiles. The air defense forces fired two missiles that hit the port infrastructure," the Southern Ukrainian Military Command has warned in a message on the Telegram network.\par \par \uc2 Latest videos of the invasionKey dates of the conflict: when and how it started. New publications Expansion | Ukraine asks \uc2 UN\uc2  to hold Russia to agreement to unblock grainTheUkrainian Foreign Ministry on Saturday asked the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  and Turkey to hold Russia to its commitments made yesterday with the signing of an agreement to unblock grain stored in Ukrainian ports. Kiev has denounced this Saturday a missile attack on the port of Odessa, in the south of the country.\par \par \uc2 The spokesman for the Ukrainian administration in Odessa, Serhi Bratchuk, has assured the portal New Voice of Ukraine that two other missiles were intercepted by the Ukrainian Army's air defense systems. All the projectiles were Kalibr model, used by the Russian forces, the administrator detailed. Earlier, MP Oleksiy Goncharenko had reported that there had been six explosions in the city, as well as the start of a fire in the port.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Ukrainian Foreign Ministry\uc2  has condemned the attack. "Russia has taken less than 24 hours to launch a missile attack on the port of Odessa, reneging on its promises and undermining its commitments to the \uc2 UN\uc2  and Turkey under the Istanbul agreement," ministry spokesman \uc2 Oleg Nikolenko\uc2  lamented on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account. "In case of non-compliance, Russia will bear the full responsibility for the global food crisis," he lamented. &lpar;Agencies&rpar;\par \par \uc2 Kiev denounces Russian missile attack on Odessa port, a day after signing deal to unblock Ukrainian grainUkrainehas denounced on Saturday that Russia has attacked the Black Sea port of Odessa with missiles, a day after Kiev and Moscow signed two separate agreements with Turkey - with \uc2 UN\uc2  mediation and supervision - to resume grain exports from Ukraine\par \par \uc2 ."The enemy has attacked the port of Odessa with cruise missiles. The air defense forces fired two missiles, which hit the port's infrastructure," the Military Command of Southern Ukraine has warned in a message on the Telegram network.(Reuters)\par \par \uc2 Orb\u225\'00\'E1n calls for new European war strategy after 'failure' ofsanctionsHungarian Prime Minister \uc2 Viktor Orb\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2  said on Saturday that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  needs a new strategy to react to the war in Ukraine, as the sanctions adopted against Moscow "have not worked," he said in a speech in Romania. &lpar;Reuters&rpar;\par \par \uc2 Borrell: "Spain is in a very good position to help supply gas to the EU "The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's High Representative for Foreign Affairs, \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , advocates that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  should prepare "as soon as possible" for a possible cut-off of Russian gas, with savings in consumption, and considers that Spain is in a "very good" position to help supply that energy from other suppliers\par \par \uc2 ."We can't suddenly say, 'Oh, they've cut off our gas!' We have to take measures and those measures are savings measures, but there are measures to increase supply and Spain is in a very good position to help Europe have more gas that is not Russian gas," Borrell said in a telephone interview with Efe. The also Vice President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  responded in this way when asked about the opposition of Spain and other countries of the community club to the proposal to cut 15% of gas consumption until next spring, which was presented on Wednesday by the President of the Community Executive, \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , and which will be discussed by the countries in the extraordinary Energy Council next Tuesday in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 Borrell pointed out that 37% of the natural gas liquefaction capacities and 40% of the storage of this gas in the EU are in Spain, which long ago began to import from Algeria as opposed to other countries that "relied solely and exclusively on Russian gas".\par \par \uc2 At least three people were killed in aRussian\par \par \uc2 missile attack in central UkraineAtleast three people were killed Saturday in a Russian attack with 13 missiles in Kirovograd province in central Ukraine, local governor Andriy Raikovich said. They were two security guards at a power substation and a Ukrainian soldier, Raikovich said in a televised interview. Nine other servicemen were wounded, he added.\par \par \uc2 The provincial capital, Kropivnitsky, is currently without electricity, according to the governor. Initial reports of the attack suggest that the missiles hit a military airfield and the railroad network. &lpar;Reuters&rpar;\par \par \uc2 At least 358 minors killed in Ukraine since start of conflict, says country's prosecutor's officeTheUkrainian prosecutor's office for minors said Saturday that at least 358 children have been killed and around 683 injured in attacks by Russia since the invasion of Ukraine began in late February\par \par \uc2 .Most of the child casualties, counting killed and wounded, have been recorded in Donetsk region with 361, Kharkov with 194, the capital Kiev region with 116, Chernihiv (68), Lugansk region (54), Kherson (54), Mikolaiv (48), Zaporiyia (40), Sumy (17), and Yitomir (15).\par \par \uc2 Some 5,110 Ukrainian civilians have been killed and nearly 6,800 injured as a result of the Russian invasion, according to the latest updated balance sheet of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights, published last Monday. The \uc2 UN\uc2  has recorded, in the same balance sheet, 346 children killed and 547 wounded since the beginning of the war.\par \par \uc2 However,both the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  and the Ukrainian Prosecutor's Office fear that these data are actually lower than the real figures given the difficulty of collecting information during the conflict. (EP)\par \par \uc2 Erdogan, the balancing act between Kiev and Moscow, achieves a diplomatic victory with the Ukrainian grain agreementTheagreement to unblock the export of grain from Ukraine, signed this Friday in Istanbul, has allowed the Turkish President, \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 , to insist that the diplomatic route is the best way to stop the conflict between Russia and Ukraine. But beyond the negotiating table, the Turkish leader is scoring a victory on the international stage as a mediator. After the Russian invasion last February, and while bombs were falling on Ukrainian cities, Turkey postulated itself as mediator between the two actors: Kiev's friend and Moscow's partner. Both turn out to be, for Turkey, of paramount importance. And the conflict between the two has shown that Ankara does not want to tip the balance in favor of either. In fact, while the international community was closing in on Russia and supporting sanctions packages against the Kremlin, Turkey remained neutral and did not follow any guidelines for punishing Moscow. But neither did it forget Ukraine, to which the Turkish arms industry has sold the Bayraktar drones, of utmost importance for Kiev's military objectives and Ankara's diplomatic weapon.\par \par \uc2 Immersed in a galloping economic crisis, with the lira vanishing day by day and inflation continuing to escalate, Turkey continues to seek friends abroad and wants to assert itself on the international scene. \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2 , \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General, has highlighted and thanked Turkey for its role in the achievement of the agreement - in the signing of which he participated - which will allow the distribution of basic foodstuffs to millions of people all over the world. Erdogan, on the other hand, has boasted about hosting the event and recalled that after four months of work -since the first meeting promoted by Ankara- the negotiations are beginning to yield positive results.\par \par \uc2 By Albert NayaIn\par \par \uc2 the image, by Umit Bektas\par \par \uc2 for Reuters, Erdogan, on Friday, during the signing ceremony in Istanbul of the agreement to resume the export of grain through the Black Sea.Read the full story here\par \par \uc2 Russian troops' supply lines west of the Dnieper River are in danger, says UKInthe last 48 hours, heavy fighting has been raging in the Kherson province of southern Ukraine, where Ukrainian forces are continuing their offensive against Russian occupation, the \uc2 UK Ministry of Defense\uc2  highlights Saturday. Supply routes for Kremlin troops west of the Dnieper River are increasingly at risk, adds the latest British intelligence report.\par \par \uc2 Difficulties in fulfilling deal releasing Ukrainian grain: sailing around mines and distrust of RussiaTwoseparate but symmetrical agreements with Turkey - one signed by Russia and the other by Ukraine, mediated and monitored by the \uc2 UN\uc2  - are the hope for unlocking millions of tons of Ukrainian grain, trapped since the Russian invasion last February by the blockade of Ukrainian ports. After weeks of diplomatic negotiations, the pact signed Friday in Istanbul, which envisages an almost cinematic deployment to get the wheat out, provides for the arrival of cargo ships to Ukrainian ports through "safe channels" identified by the Kiev authorities to avoid mines in their territorial waters, guided by Ukrainian pilots, without military escort, but supervised beforehand by Turkish and \uc2 UN\uc2  teams to ensure they are not carrying weapons into Ukraine. Moscow has sought to downplay the role of the agreement in alleviating the food crisis aggravated by Russian aggression and its export policies. The Kremlin insists that the problem, which could escalate into a severe famine, has been caused by Western sanctions against Russia over the invasion.\par \par \uc2 The aim of the deal, which would open a path for significant volumes of commercial food exports from three key Ukrainian ports - Odessa, Chernomorsk and Yuzhny - is to enable Ukraine to export around five million tons of grain per month, a volume similar to that before the full-scale war launched by Russia five months ago, which blocked Ukrainian ports leaving tens of millions of tons of grain in silos and clogged the global supply chain. In addition, Ukrainian authorities have accused Moscow of stealing grain in the territories it has occupied - as an investigation by EL PA\u205\'00\'CDS showed - and of deliberately bombing Ukrainian fields to set them on fire and thus fuel the global crisis, with great destabilizing potential.\par \par \uc2 By Mar\u237\'00\'EDa R. Sahuquillo and Javier G. CuestaIn\par \par \uc2 the picture, by Luis de Vega\par \par \uc2 , an engineer next to one of the conveyor belts of the Mlybor granary, in Chernihiv, northern Ukraine, last month.Read the full story here\par \par \uc2 Ukraine denounces 13-missile attack in central UkraineAtleast 13 Russian missiles hit a military airfield and railroad network in Kirovograd province in central Ukraine on Saturday, local governor Andriy Raikovich said in a message posted on the Telegram network. Raikovich claims that the attack has caused dead and wounded, without detailing how many.(Reuters)\par \par \uc2 Putin awakens identity sentiment in UkraineUkraineis a young state that, since it gained its independence 30 years ago, coexists with a neighbor that denies its existence. This neighbor, Russia, has wanted to militarily subdue the former Soviet republic since 2014. Ukrainian national sentiment has been growing exponentially since \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered that year the annexation of Crimea and promoted the uprising of the Donbas separatists &lpar;in the east&rpar;. The invasion that began last February has further boosted the population's affinity for a Ukrainian identity, both in the western provinces and in those closest to Russian culture, those in the east.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Wilson, professor of Ukrainian Studies at \uc2 University College London\uc2 , wrote in the latest edition of his acclaimed The ukrainians, unexpected nation &lpar;2015, Yale University Press&rpar; that Ukrainian national identity is still in the process of construction after centuries of subjection to outside powers-Poland, the Austro-Hungarian Empire, tsarist Russia and the Soviet Union. "Nations are formed by circumstances and opportunities," Wilson wrote. "Ukrainians like to talk about a 'national idea.' Concepts like 'nation' belong to the realm of political and cultural imagination," this scholar reflected. Putin, according to his thesis, has been the determining circumstance in building the new Ukrainian citizen.\par \par \uc2 By Cristian Segura\par \par \uc2 &lpar;Special Envoy&rpar;In the image, by Luis de Vega\par \par \uc2 , a boy with a Ukrainian flag in the center of Lviv, in March.Read the full story here\par \par \uc2 Macron and Al Sisi discuss Ukraine, Middle East and IranThePresidents of France and Egypt, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  and Abdelfatah al Sisi, today discussed the threat to food security created by the war in Ukraine, the situation in the Middle East and the Iranian nuclear program. The two leaders met today at the Elysee, without making statements, in their first meeting after Macron's re-election last April. The consequences of the war in Ukraine, especially the threat to international food security, were among the main issues they discussed, the Elysee said in a statement. Egypt is a grain-importing country, so it has been hit hard by the international price increases of recent months.\par \par \uc2 After Macron has recently received in Paris the Israeli prime minister and the \uc2 Palestinian National Authority\uc2 , the situation in the Middle East was another topic discussed. The two presidents also discussed the Iranian nuclear program, about which the director general of the \uc2 UN\uc2  atomic agency, \uc2 Rafael Grossi\uc2 , was "alarmed" this week by Tehran's recent claims about its progress towards the possibility of building nuclear weapons. Cooperation in the fight against terrorism and the situation in Libya were other topics discussed. On Libya, Macron and Al Sisi agreed to continue working to restore the unity and sovereignty of that country, neighboring Egypt, after the long period of instability and civil war it has suffered.\par \par \uc2 EFE agency reports.\par \par \uc2 Zelenski, on the agreement to export grain: "It is possible to avoid a global catastrophe, a famine that could lead to political chaos "The Ukrainian President, \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 , expressed his satisfaction for the agreement reached this Friday in Istanbul, since it could unblock the export of grain from the country, stranded in the ports due to the war. "It is possible to avoid a global catastrophe, a famine that could lead to political chaos in many countries of the world, particularly in the countries that help us," he said. In his address via social networks tonight, Zelenski indicated that about 20 million tons from last year's harvest and also from this fiscal year's harvest could be exported. "These are revenues for farmers, for the entire agricultural sector and for the national budget. These are jobs," he said. "There is a possibility to reduce the severity of the food crisis caused by Russia," Zelenski stressed.\par \par \uc2 The agreement reached today will allow exporting from three Ukrainian ports - Odessa, Pivdennyi and Chornomorsk - about 22 million tons of wheat, corn and other grain stored in silos, which could not be sold due to the Russian naval blockade. Russia and Ukraine are world grain exporters and together, they sell a third of the world's wheat supply. This is the first major agreement between the two warring countries since the Russian invasion of Ukraine on February 24 and is expected to ease a crisis forged by the Russian naval blockade of Ukraine and Western sanctions on Russia, which have also driven up fertilizer and energy prices.\par \par \uc2 EFE agency reports.\par \par \uc2 Ensuring pricestabilityInflation is too high. Russia's war in Ukraine has pushed up the cost of energy and agricultural products. Shortages of materials, equipment and labor resulting from the pandemic are also contributing to higher prices, which is hurting citizens and businesses across the euro zone, especially those with low incomes.\par \par \uc2 Much of this inflation is due to factors that central banks cannot control, but what we can do is ensure that inflation does not become persistent. This is what could happen if price increases spread throughout the economy and people start to expect higher inflation in the future. In such a case, wage and price spirals of the kind that have historically led to inflation spiraling out of control could be generated.\par \par \uc2 An opinion of \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , president of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You can read the full article here.\par \par \uc2 Lithuania reintroduces rail transit of goodsa KaliningradLithuaniaon Friday resumed allowing unrestricted transit of non-military goods by rail between Russia and its Kaliningrad enclave bordering Poland and the Baltic country. Vilnius thus complies with the protocol agreed by the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , which states that sanctions only apply for goods transported by road.\par \par \uc2 Vilnius had been preventing the transit of several sanctioned goods between Russia and its enclave since June, which aroused the ire of Moscow, which accused Lithuania of breaching the treaties signed between the two countries regulating transit to and from Kaliningrad. Moscow can now return to transporting cement, timber and alcohol to its territory. According to Russia's TASS news agency, the Kaliningrad regional government has 60 concrete wagons ready to be transported.\par \par \uc2 However, yesterday, Thursday, Lithuanian Transport Minister \uc2 Marius Skuodis\uc2  claimed that "transit will not be the same as it was before." "We have certain limits on how much cargo can be transported, and clearly we can say what cannot be transported in general. The change is obvious, no matter what anyone says." (Reuters)\par \par \uc2 Zelenski says Ukraine has $10 billion worth of grain instorageUkrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  said Friday that Ukraine has $10 billion worth of grain in storage and will also be able to sell this summer's harvest after signing an agreement in Istanbul with the \uc2 UN\uc2  and Turkey &lpar;and, indirectly, with Russia&rpar; to be able to export grain to the rest of the world through its ports\par \par \uc2 .In his usual daily televised address, Zelenski has indicated that Russia may try to discredit international and Ukrainian efforts through "provocations" but that the improved export capacity is "another demonstration that Ukraine can resist war." "It is important that we control our territorial waters," he has considered. &lpar;Reuters&rpar;\par \par \uc2 Russia opens 25 files a day for criticizing UkraineinvasionRussian police have opened more than 3,300 files for "discrediting" the military since the start of the invasion of Ukraine, 25 a day on average, according to Russian NGO Setevye Svobody (Freedom Network). The organization, which legally assists victims of political repression in Russia, said it had calculated the figure between March 4 and July 14, based on \uc2 Russian Interior Ministry\uc2  data. "On average, Russian police issue 35 files for discrediting the army every working day," the NGO said via its Telegram account.\par \par \uc2 The offense, which came into force in March to silence criticism of the offensive in Ukraine, carries fines of up to 100,000 rubles (\u8364\'20\'AC1,700) for an individual and up to 1 million rubles for a legal entity. According to the specialized NGO OVD-Info, about 200 people who have denounced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  were prosecuted in Russia in criminal cases, which carry very harsh prison sentences. On July 8, a Moscow city councilor, Alexei Gorinov, was sentenced to seven years in prison for "spreading false information" about the Russian military after publicly condemning the attack on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 US announces new $270 million military package to UkraineThePentagon on Friday announced a new $270 million military aid package to Ukraine. Of that figure, $100 million will be earmarked for the purchase of an additional 580 Phoenix Ghost drones. This class of unmanned vehicles can fly for six hours and is designed for ground targets with unreinforced armor. More than a hundred Phoenix Ghost drones were sent to Ukraine last April for use in the battle for the Donbas region. In addition, four more HIMARS multiple missile launcher systems will be sent, bringing the total delivered to Ukraine to 20.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon spokesman John Kirby said that the U.S. Defense Department is conducting "preliminary explorations" into the possibility of delivering fighter jets to Ukraine, an option so far ruled out by Washington in view of the possibility of an escalation of the direct conflict by Russia. However, he has indicated, if it were to happen, "it would not be executed in the short term."\par \par \uc2 &lpar;Reuters&rpar;\par \par \uc2 What has happened in the last few hours On the 149th day of the war launched by Russia against Ukraine, these are the key facts as of 20.05 this Friday, July 22:\par \par \uc2 Ukraine and Russia agree to unblock Ukrainian grain exports\par \par \uc2 . Ukraine and Russia have taken a step on Friday that may mean the end of the blockade on Ukrainian grain exports. The two have agreed to create a Black Sea corridor, now guarded by Russian frigates, to get grain trapped in ports to global markets without being attacked by Russian frigates. The pact, partially reached last week, was signed Friday in Istanbul with the mediation of Turkey and the \uc2 UN\uc2 . Its text establishes that Ukraine will be able to recover the departure of its ships to the sea under the protection of its frigates and thus unblock up to 22 million tons of grain.The \uc2 IAEA\uc2  considers the situation at the Zaporiyia nuclear power plant to be "increasingly alarming"\par \par \uc2 . The \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ) said in a statement released Friday that the latest available information on the situation at the Zaporiyia nuclear power plant in southeastern Ukraine is "increasingly alarming" and has again called for a mission by its international organization to verify the situation at the plant. Zaporiyia is the largest nuclear power plant in Europe, with six nuclear reactors totaling 5,700 megawatts of power.Zelenski compares Putin to "a python": "He thought we were just another bunny rabbit.But we are not and he can't swallow us."\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  has reiterated that his country seeks first to recover the territories occupied by Russia since 2014 and which it considers as its own before opening any peace negotiations with the Kremlin because, in his view, a ceasefire now "would only prolong the war." Zelenski has compared Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as "a python," gobbling up territories in Georgia and Moldova and Ukraine itself before setting his sights on conquering the country in February. "They thought we were just another bunny rabbit," he has claimed. "But we're not a bunny and it turns out they can't swallow us - and, in fact, they run the risk of us tearing them apart."\uc2 Gazprom\uc2  warns that the turbine that would restore gas flow through NordStream is still in Germany\par \par \uc2 . Russian gas company \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  said Friday that the turbine at the Portovaya compressor station, whose repair has become a diplomatic crisis between Moscow, Berlin and Ottawa, is still in Germany in the absence of permits to allow its return to Russia. The gas company, which in principle requires the turbine to fully restore the flow of hydrocarbon through the NordStream 1 pipeline, has stated that the contract it has with the German company \uc2 Siemens Energy\uc2  makes the latter responsible for obtaining all the appropriate \uc2 permits.Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extends veto on circulation of dollars and euros beyond September\par \par \uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will extend beyond September 9 its veto on the cash delivery of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially dollars and euros. At the beginning of the offensive, the regulator had banned the sale of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to individuals and had limited to $10,000 the maximum amount that could be withdrawn from accounts that had been deposited before March 9. The rest, including what was deposited later, would be converted to rubles. "There is no reason now to expect that the flow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cash to our country will improve," the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  (BCR), Elvira Nabiulina, explained at a press conference. "So in September we will be forced to extend the restrictions."In the photo by Dmytro Smolienko for Reuters, the Zaporiyia nuclear power plant as seen from Nikopol, on the other side of the Dnieper River.\par \par \uc2 Zelenski compares Putin to "a python": "He thought we were just another bunny rabbit. But we are not and he cannot swallow us. "Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  has reiterated that his country first seeks to regain the territories occupied by Russia since 2014 and which it considers as its own before opening any peace negotiations with the Kremlin because, in his view, a ceasefire now "would only prolong the war." "Our society believes that all territories should be liberated first, and only then should we negotiate what to do," he said in an interview published Friday by the Wall Street Journal. "Freezing the conflict with Russia means a pause that will give Russia a pause to rest."\par \par \uc2 Zelenski has likened Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as "a python," gobbling up territories in Georgia and Moldova and Ukraine itself before setting his sights on conquering the country in February. "They thought we were just another bunny rabbit," Zelenski has claimed. "But we are not a bunny and it turns out that you can't swallow us - and, in fact, you run the risk of us tearing you apart."\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president has indicated that the arrival of HIMARS multiple missile launcher systems from the West has made a material difference on the battlefield. According to Zelenski, at the beginning of the major offensive in Donbas in May and June, Ukraine was losing between 100 and 200 soldiers a day. Today, that figure is down to around 30 killed and 250 wounded per day.\par \par \uc2 However,Zelenski has stated that the weaponry arriving is still far less than his country needs to reverse the trend and win the war, and that even more urgent than reinforcing these systems are anti-aircraft systems that can prevent long-range missile attacks. (Reuters)\par \par \uc2 12345 View full liveFollowall the international information on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , or in our weekly newsletter\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220723ei7n00674\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
