\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The problem with the current Russian sanctions regime\b0\par\par\uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Five months have passed since Europe and the United States imposed harsh economic and financial sanctions on Russia, a G20 country that was the world's 11th largest economy on the eve of its invasion of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the sanctions have been gradually tightened in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  months, the debate over their effectiveness, the broader implications of the war for global markets and the global economy, and what the West's next steps should be, is intense.\par \par \uc2 On the first question, while the sanctions have been less effective than Europe and the United States expected, they are also proving more onerous than the Kremlin claims. \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects GDP to contract by 8-10% this year, while other forecasts predict a larger drop, along with more lasting damage to growth potential. Imports and exports have been severely disrupted, and foreign investment inflows have come to a virtual halt. Shortages are multiplying, pushing inflation up. At this point, the country no longer has a properly functioning \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The sanctions would have been much tougher if the West had not opted for a carve-out of the Russian energy sector, and if many more countries had joined the United States and Europe in the effort. Since that did not happen, Russia has not felt as much pressure as it would have. Moreover, it has been able to continue to trade through various side and back doors that are likely to become increasingly important as long as the sanctions regime, as currently designed, continues.\par \par \uc2 However, it is only a matter of time before the Russian economy takes a bigger hit. Inventories of imported goods - including many critical technological and industrial inputs - are declining rapidly, and many sectors are becoming less resilient. The cumulative damage to the Russian economy will be significant and long-lasting, a fact that has not yet been fully captured by consensus medium-term forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The second issue concerns the overall effects of the war and the sanctions regime. Most observers agree that the Russian invasion has increased not only energy but also food insecurity, highlighting the consequences of the disruption of Ukrainian agricultural exports. However, there remains much debate about the West's use of the nuclear economic sanctions option: the restrictions imposed on the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on Russia's use of the international payments system.\par \par \uc2 These curbs are far more intrusive than the usual combination of restrictions on sanctioned government and private sector trade and financial transactions by individuals. However, because they are not subject to internationally agreed standards, guidelines or controls, they fall outside the purview of the relevant global governance bodies, such as the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 , the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In times of war, this kind of oversight might seem like a detail. But some worry that sanctions could significantly reduce the role of the dollar as the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the role of the U.S. financial system as the main global intermediary for other countries' savings and investments. After all, there is no doubt that a growing number of countries now feel more vulnerable to the reach of U.S. sanctions.\par \par \uc2 But it is impossible to replace something with nothing, which means that there will be no significant loss of the dollar or U.S. financial primacy in the foreseeable future. Rather, the sanctions will give further impetus to the gradual process of global economic fragmentation, which was also fueled a few years ago by the tariffs imposed by the Trump administration. More countries now have even more reason to seek greater financial resilience and inherently inefficient forms of self-insurance.\par \par \uc2 This brings us to the third debate. With no end to the war in sight, what should the West do next? Fearing the implications for energy prices and gas supplies to Europe, many in the West are tempted to call for a moratorium on any new sanctions, or even to make further exceptions. Others, however, favor additional measures to hold Russia accountable for its indiscriminate attacks on the Ukrainian civilian population.\par \par \uc2 In any case, maintaining the current sanctions regime is not without its problems, due to the dual objectives of pressuring Russia and limiting economic disruption in Europe. Moreover, as \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  recently said, it gives the feeling that Russia is "blackmailing" Europe with the threat of disrupting gas supplies at any time. No wonder the Commission is urging member countries to reduce consumption by 15%.\par \par \uc2 With the current sanctions regime, the West risks falling between two horses. While easing sanctions could help alleviate concerns about Europe's economic prospects, this option is not viable given the atrocities being committed by Russian forces in Ukraine. But if the West is serious about pressuring Russia through truly crippling economic and financial sanctions, it needs to make a U-turn and remove the energy cuts.\par \par \uc2 Doing so would undoubtedly have a serious short-term economic impact on European economies and the rest of the world, amplifying the "small fires everywhere" syndrome I warned about in May. It is therefore critical that governments use the fiscal space available to them to provide targeted support to vulnerable segments of the population, as well as to fragile countries; and multilateral agencies should support developing countries through aid and a more \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt relief framework. If done well, this option would yield better results in the medium and long term than the current strategy.\par \par \uc2 If pursued, it risks the worst of all possible worlds. It is not enough to deter Russia from continuing its illegal war; it is fueling further fragmentation of the international monetary system; and it is not even protecting Europe from a winter gas disruption.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220722ei7m008lm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
