\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  raises rates to 25% in first  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\uc2  since Russian invasion\b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The board of the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  has decided on Thursday to raise the benchmark interest rate to 25% per annum from the 10% at which it had kept the price of money anchored since before the start of the Russian invasion at the end of last February.\par \par \uc2 The institution has explained that its decision is aimed at protecting households' hryvnia income and savings, increasing the attractiveness of assets denominated in the Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reducing pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and thus improving the institution's ability to maintain exchange rate stability and curb inflationary processes during the war.\par \par \uc2 "The development of inflation requires the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  to return to active interest rate policy to prevent further deterioration of inflation expectations and dollarization of the economy," the institution has noted.\par \par \uc2 Thus, it has pointed out that, after considering several scenarios, it decided to increase the rate by 15 percentage points, thinking that a slight increase "would not have had a significant influence on the financial and economic system", as the monetary transmission mechanism has a limited effect in wartime, while it would have given rise to expectations of further rate hikes, in addition to the fact that, to revive interest in hryvnia assets, their yield must exceed the expected inflation rates.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2  has reported an increase in the scope of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defense of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting that it raised \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales from a monthly average of $2 billion (\u8364\'20\'AC1.87 billion) in March-April to $3.4 billion (\u8364\'20\'AC3.18 billion) in May.\par \par \uc2 The governor of the \uc2 National Bank of Ukraine\uc2 , \uc2 Kyrylo Shevchenko\uc2 , has defended the bank's decision not to take any monetary policy measures so far as he believes that due to "strong psychological pressure caused by the war,changing rates would not be enough to stabilize expectations and accumulate and hold assets in hryvnias."\par \par \uc2 To this end, in the first months of the war, the bank directed its monetary policy measures primarily to ensuring the uninterrupted functioning of the banking system and payments in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220602ei6200er1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
