\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Putin hikes Russian pensions, plays down Ukraine impact on economy\b0\par\par\uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2022\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Siluanov estimate of cost of measures, paragraph 13)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 25 (Reuters) - President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered 10% rises on Wednesday in pensions and the minimum wage to cushion Russians from inflation, but denied the country's economic problems were all linked to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 With annual inflation near 18% last month, the Kremlin leader acknowledged that 2022 would be a "difficult" year for the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 "When I say 'difficult', it doesn't mean all these difficulties are connected to the special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Putin told a televised meeting of the State Council in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 "Because in countries that aren't conducting any \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  - say, overseas, in North America, in Europe - inflation is comparable and, if you look at the structure of their economies, even more than ours."\par \par \uc2 His comments ignored the fact that rising inflation in Western economies is in part a direct consequence of Russia's war in Ukraine, which has driven up prices for energy and food around the world.\par \par \uc2 The pension increase comes into effect from June 1, while the minimum wage hike kicks in on July 1. Analysts said the steps would not prevent a sharp fall in real incomes.\par \par \uc2 Putin -- whose approval rating has jumped more than 10 points since the start of the Ukraine campaign to 82%, according to the independent Levada Centre's April poll -- pledged in March to reduce poverty and inequality this year despite crippling Western sanctions and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Russian economy has been rocked by an unprecedented barrage of Western sanctions imposed over his decision to send troops into Ukraine on Feb. 24, with consumer prices soaring and foreign companies quitting Russia en masse as trade becomes near-impossible.\par \par \uc2 The Research and Expert Review Institute of the bank VEB said the increase in social payments would slow but not prevent a decline in Russians' real incomes, wages and pensions - after inflation is taken into account.\par \par \uc2 Even with a 10% increase in the minimum wage and retirement pensions, VEB expects Russians' real disposable incomes to fall by 7.5% and real wages to drop nearly 6% this year. VEB also expects poverty to rise to 12.6% this year from 11% in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Russia's minimum wage currently stands at 13,890 roubles ($250) a month, while the average retirement pension amounts to 18,521 roubles per month.\par \par \uc2 The increase in wages and pensions may add to the inflation pressures that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to cap with an emergency rate hike to 20% in late February, as the rouble's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  value plunged. It has cut its rate twice since then as the rouble has recovered.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  said the measures would cost the federal budget around 600 billion roubles ($10.5 billion) this year and about 1 trillion roubles in 2023.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to hold an out-of-schedule policy meeting, where analysts expect it to slash the benchmark interest rate from 14% back towards the 9.5% where it stood before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Reuters; editing by Mark Trevelyan and Kevin Liffey) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-5-25T17:01:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220525ei5p036w5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
