\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russia sanctioned? The global economic dimension of the conflict in Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 1,762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Last February 24, Russia launched a "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , escalating a conflict that quickly unleashed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the "West", under the leadership and coordination of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . On the same day, the Ukrainian government called for economic sanctions that would come only a few days later.Instrumented by the United States and Europe, they were applied with many hesitations and tensions, due to the fear that they would produce a "boomerang effect" on the countries of the old continent, mainly in the supply of raw materials from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Five days after the start of the large-scale armed conflict, on March 1, the USTreasury Department announced a ban on all transactions with the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the \uc2 European Union\uc2  officially announced a list of Russian banks to be excluded from the SWIFT financial communications system. VTB Capital Bank PJSC, \uc2 Bank Rossiya\uc2 , Bank Otkritie, \uc2 Novikombank\uc2 , Promsvyazbank PJSC, \uc2 Sovcombank PJSC\uc2  and the Russian Development Bank - VEB.RF appeared on the list. This last entity had been named by the Western powers as the "nuclear option" of the financial sanctions (El Cronista, 01/03/2022).\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the freezing of the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assets abroad was established. As a consequence, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummeted nearly 30% against the US dollar. In this scenario, the French Minister of Economy, \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2 , stated: "We are waging a total economic and financial war against Russia, Putin and his government, and let us be clear, the Russian people will also pay the consequences". The Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodomir Zelensky\uc2 , celebrated the decision by stating that the financial sanctions carried out meant the "disconnection from global financial civilization" (\uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , 04/03/2022).\par \par \uc2 While the Russian Federation received sanctions of all kinds, the Republic of Ukraine placed new bonds on the markets to finance the war and received immediate assistance of US$ 1.4 billion from the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . In other words, the economic and financial assistance to the Caucasus country were the first maneuvers of the "West" to sustain the conflict and project it over time. The first assistance was economic, the second of mercenaries ("volunteers") and, finally, arms. Three staggered contributions in a conventional war in which Germany and the United States, key players in the leadership of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , do not wish, for various reasons, to enter directly, that is to say, with "their own troops".\par \par \uc2 The financial war is not only decreed by governments.The economic "sanctions" did not come only from the hand of the States. Dozens of global companies from all sectors of the economy, mainly of U.S. and European origin, officially ceased their activities in Russia. Among them, the technological giants \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , as well as some of the main global energy companies, such as \uc2 Shell\uc2 , or automobile manufacturers such as \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Ford\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , among many others.\par \par \uc2 In the financial sphere, \uc2 Visa\uc2 , \uc2 Mastercard\uc2  and \uc2 American Express\uc2  announced the suspension of their business in Russia. Their objective was to prevent the normal functioning of the payment systems, both in the internal and external economy. The Financial Times defined this situation as "a full-scale financial war of unprecedented nature and scope" (FT, 04/03/2022). This was a major blow on the Russian payments market. In 2020, 74% of credit card transactions in Russia were made through \uc2 Visa\uc2  and \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, Russia did not sit idly by. In response to the various sanctions, Moscow decreed a plan to protect its economy that included, among other things, a ban on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transfers abroad in an amount exceeding $10,000, the suspension of the activities of the Moscow stock exchange and the obligation for Russian exporters to convert 80% of their revenues earned in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into rubles as of January 1, 2022. Days later, the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned the sale of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its citizens and, faced with the blocking of credit cards, began working with other banks to issue cards under its own branded network, Mir, which as of March 22 already had 75 million debit cards throughout Russia. In addition, UnionPay, a Chinese company, partnered with 30 local Russian banks and issued more than 4 million cards as of March 6 (La Rep\u250\'00\'FAblica de Colombia, 22/03/2022).\par \par \uc2 The giants of the global financial network also joined the pressures. More than 26 foreign asset management firms operating in Russia froze funds with significant exposure to that country. Notable sanctions included \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , \uc2 Amundi Asset Management\uc2 , \uc2 Ubs Investment Bank\uc2 , Bnp Paribas, \uc2 Abrdn PLC\uc2 , \uc2 Schroders\uc2  and \uc2 Pictet Asset Management\uc2 , among others. (FT, 04/03/2022)\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has become a stage on the global chessboard, where the big players are playing, and where the battle for who will impose the rules of the game in trade, finance and technological development in these times is also being debated.\par \par \uc2 Russia in the face of sanctionsDespite the catastrophic results that were announced, the reality continues to indicate that Russia can still resist the pressures, demonstrating its capacity to anticipate and respond. It can be assumed that the Russian government foresaw the possibility of financial sanctions in its decision to move forward with the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  deployed over Ukraine, a territory long targeted by \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces and their allies.\par \par \uc2 There are two noteworthy facts regarding the sanctions. Firstly, the disconnection from the SWIFT system is not total, since it continues to allow the payment of hydrocarbons (gas and oil), the key supplies that Europe still needs, despite having announced a gradual "disconnection", which allows Russia to continue to receive income from its main export resources.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the sanctions are not new. The financial exclusion falls on "some banks", which had already been sanctioned, mainly during the Crimean conflict. Already in 2019, faced with threats of disconnection from SWIFT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  reported the development of an alternative platform, the SPFS, and joining the China International Payment System (CIPS). Although both systems are not sufficiently developed to face SWIFT, it is what has allowed Russia to sustain the flow of its domestic transactions and to China, its great economic, political and strategic ally, thus avoiding the collapse of its domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the Russian Federation was able to meet its outstanding foreign debt payments, initially in rubles, which earned it a "debt default" rating from rating agencies such as Standard & Poors. Eventually, it also managed to make its scheduled payments in dollars.\par \par \uc2 On April 18, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  went so far as to affirm that "the strategy of the economic blitzkrieg (of the West) has failed", and reported that the balance of payments in the first quarter stood at a "historical maximum" of US$ 58,200. The situation is stabilizing", added the president (\u193\'00\'C1mbito, 22/04/2022).\par \par \uc2 In the same sense, this week \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported that the ruble is the best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world so far in 2022, with a growth of 11% against the US dollar, which places it at higher levels than those recorded prior to the war.\par \par \uc2 A few days ago, the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  came out to deny a report by Bloomberg Agency that stated that the Russian Gross Domestic Product (GDP) would fall by 12% during 2022, according to the Ministry. At the end of April, the estimates of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  were published, which foresees a contraction of between 8% and 10% this year, due to the fact that "the external environment of the Russian economy remains challenging and significantly limits economic activity", but added that it will return to "rapid growth in 2023, thanks to a structural economic transformation". At the same time, the monetary entity cut the benchmark interest rate from 17% to 14% as "risks to prices and financial stability ceased to increase", giving a sign of strength to its economy (\uc2 Deutsche Welle\uc2 , 04/29/2022).\par \par \uc2 For its part, the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's World Economic Outlook report announced that global production will slow down this year to 3.6%, as opposed to an initial forecast in January of 4.4%, i.e. 1.2% more. Pierre-Olivier Gourinchas, \uc2 IMF\uc2  chief economist, said that "the world economic outlook has been seriously affected, largely due to the Russian invasion of Ukraine" (La Naci\u243\'00\'F3n, 19/04/2022). It would seem, then, that the economic war against Russia has had enormous effects. However, most of them end up hitting other territories, due to their growing impact on international trade and the worsening of the energy and food crisis. Argentina is no exception.\par \par \uc2 War as a manifestation of a structural crisisOn March 24, \uc2 Larry Fink\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , the world's largest asset manager, stated that "the Russian invasion of Ukraine has put an end to the globalization we have experienced over the past three decades. Businesses and governments will also look more broadly at their dependencies on other nations," and warned that "the potential impact of the Ukraine war will be seen in the acceleration of digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " (\uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , 3/24/2022).\par \par \uc2 The battery of "Western" sanctions on Russia also set off alarm bells in Beijing. China met on April 22 with major banks to try to protect their assets from U.S. and European sanctions. "President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's administration has maintained staunch support for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  throughout the crisis, but Chinese banks and companies remain wary of doing any business with Russian entities that could trigger U.S. sanctions," said the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 . The British media added that this meeting "included officials from \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry, as well as executives from dozens of local and international lenders, such as \uc2 HSBC\uc2 " (\uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , 1/05/2022).\par \par \uc2 The structural crisis that the economic system is going through has a long way to go, and the war conflict can only be considered as "the continuation of the pandemic, by other means". The acceleration of organic transformations, which are giving birth to a new phase in the world economic system, are unfolding a deeper war, for the imposition and conformation of a new world order that is advancing, worryingly, towards a strategic-military moment.\par \par \uc2 The full digitalization of the economy, the relocation of production centers, the reconfiguration of supply chains, the imposition of an energy transition that reduces dependence on resources geographically located in other nations, are some of the transformations in full swing. In this scenario, disputes are intensifying and, as Machiavelli's old premise says, the end seems to justify all means.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220513ei5d005w0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
