\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Tough economic war between the West and Russia enters a new phase\b0\par\par\uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 While Russia continues its military offensive in Ukraine, another war, of a financial nature, is developing between Russia and the West.\par \par \uc2 This Monday, \uc2 European Union\uc2  energy ministers are meeting to discuss what to do with Russia: European gas companies are debating whether to give in to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or face an eventual supply cut-off, as happened last week with Poland and Bulgaria.\par \par \uc2 In March, Putin decreed that "hostile countries" - among them all those of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  - pay for their gas imports in rubles, instead of euros or dollars, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  used for most contracts. The measure is a sort of revenge against Western sanctions, since the mechanism involves the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , punished by the United States and Europe. It also allows Putin to raise the price of the ruble by creating more demand for the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Russia-Ukraine War, LIVE, Day 68: Kiev sank with drones two Russian ships in the Black Sea\par \par \uc2 Russia-\b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  War: Putin threatens blitzkrieg attacks on countries that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the invasion.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly, it is a political test for the unity of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  as, in the short term some countries are more dependent on Russian gas than others and there is no united front on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Russia proposes that companies open two accounts at \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  (excluded from the financial sanctions), one in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and one in rubles: companies should deposit their payments in the original \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the bank would be responsible for converting it into rubles, via the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The payment is only considered done when the conversion into rubles is completed.\par \par \uc2 Poland and Bulgaria refused to comply with the decree. Both account for about 8% of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's Russian gas imports, and the countries do not believe that the supply cut will have a major impact on the population. Poland, for example, has gas reserves at 76% and with the northern summer approaching, consumption is expected to drop. In fact, both countries were planning not to renew their contracts with \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  when they expire at the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 But not all European countries depend on Russian gas to the same extent: between 40% and 50% of Italy's and Germany's gas imports, respectively, come from Russia. Germany, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's largest economy, has refused to make the payment in rubles but says it can pay \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  in euros without violating economic sanctions imposed by the bloc.\par \par \uc2 Italy's \uc2 Eni\uc2  said it had not yet opened a ruble account - although it is preparing to do so, according to Bloomberg - and said it will continue to make payments in euros, according to the contract, and observing all international sanctions.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's executive body, warned companies that giving in to Russian conditionalities could imply a "violation of EU sanctions and therefore a high risk for companies".\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine: Biden called for $33 billion in aid for Kiev, while Russia warns about testing his patience\par \par \uc2 The next few days will be decisive as companies will have to start making April payments.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russia avoided defaulting on its sovereign debt, but at a cost. The \uc2 Russian Finance Ministry\uc2  said it made payments of u$s 564.8 million and u$s 84.4 million for two bonds maturing in 2022 and 2042.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official confirmed that the payments had been made and said the source of the funds was unclear.\par \par \uc2 As Russia Threatens World War III, U.S. and Europe Send More Weapons to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Four days after Russia launched the invasion of Ukraine, the U.S. froze Russian reserves in that country (about u$s 630 billion) and banned U.S. entities from doing business with the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Then, the Biden Administration went a step further and revoked a special exception for Russia to use US banks to pay its Western creditors, leaving the country closer to default.\par \par \uc2 Concern grew in early April, when \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  refused to process the payment of two bonds for US$649.2 million, after consultation with the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , sources familiar with the transaction said.\par \par \uc2 Following the blockade, Russia said it paid its creditors the amount owed, but in rubles, because sanctions prevented it from meeting its obligations. However, both bonds were denominated in dollars and both rating agencies and the Credit Derivatives Determination Committee (CDDC) said that payment in rubles could be considered a default.\par \par \uc2 Russia finally made the payments through the UK branch of Citibank, just before the grace period expired next Wednesday. But although it avoided what could have been a historic default - the last time it defaulted was in 1918 - Russia had to wire the money to London from its domestic reserves, which was precisely what the Western sanctions sought: to force the Kremlin and drain its resources to finance the invasion.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220502ei5200888\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
