\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine - Russia war, live: breaking news on the invasion\b0\par\par\uc2 12,244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 ???? Keys of the day:\par \par \uc2 - Russia begins cutting off gas to Europe by suspending shipments to Bulgaria and Poland.\par \par \uc2 - Fighting continues with heavy shelling in Kherson and nearby Izium area\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 UN\uc2  assures to be "preparing" the evacuation of Mariupol\par \par \uc2 - Shelling continues in the city of Kharkiv and the blockade and shelling of the Azovstal plant near the port of Mariupol\par \par \uc2 - Guterres arrived in Kyiv on peace mission to meet with Zelenski after seeing Putin\par \par \uc2 21:00 Ukraine goes a step further and launches several attacks inside Russian territory, two small towns in Donbass will decide the fate of the Russian offensive.\par \par \uc2 20:46 Ukraine calls shelling on Kyiv a "barbaric act" Ukraine has called the Russian shelling on Kyiv a "heinous act of barbarism", amid the visit of \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2  to the Ukrainian capital. "With this heinous act of barbarism, Russia once again demonstrates its attitude towards Ukraine, Europe and the world," Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 20:35 Biden launches $33 billion package for "continued aid" to Ukraine in arms and money The U.S. president has proposed to Congress to approve a $33 billion package to arm Ukraine "without interruption" and help its citizens and government weather the Russian invasion. La Vanguardia's Washington correspondent, Fernando Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, details Biden's proposal and its possible political consequences.\par \par \uc2 20:22 ENLARGEMENT | Two missiles hit Kyiv during Guterres' visit Two projectiles fired by Russia have hit Kyiv during the visit of \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2 , as reported by the capital's own mayor, \uc2 Vitali Klitchko\uc2 . "Tonight, the enemy fired on Kyiv. There have been two hits in the Shevchenkivski district. All emergency services are on the spot. Information about possible casualties is being clarified," the ex-boxer wrote on his Telegram channel. For his part, President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 's advisor Mikhail Podoliak remarked that the shelling took place during Guterres' visit: "The day before he was sitting at a long table in the Kremlin and today there are explosions over his head. A postcard from Moscow?" he wrote on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 20:08 Shelling continues in Kyiv Despite the apparent withdrawal of Russian troops, two explosions have rocked a central district of the city of Kyiv, Mayor \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2  has claimed. The attack comes on the same day that \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  visited the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 19:53 Zelenski confident of "successful outcome" in attempt to unblock Azovstal Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  has assured that he is confident of a "successful outcome" in the attempt to unblock the besieged Azovstal metallurgical plant in the port city of Mariupol, where some 1,000 civilians and 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers are sheltering. "I believe that a successful outcome is possible in the unblocking" of Azovstal in which Ukraine is working together with the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  and the \uc2 International Committee of the Red Cross\uc2 , Zelenski said at a press conference after meeting with \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 19:40 Guterres says \uc2 UN\uc2  is doing "everything possible" to evacuate Mariupol \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  has assured that the \uc2 UN\uc2  is doing "everything possible" to evacuate civilians from the Azovstal metallurgical plant in the Ukrainian city of Mariupol, where about 1,000 people still remain refugees together with about 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers. "We are doing everything possible to make it a reality. My first and only priority is the people who are suffering and have to be rescued," said the \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General at a press conference after meeting with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 19:29 Russia calls the US initiative to include the country on the list of sponsors of terrorism "idiotic" \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  has called the \uc2 US government\uc2 's initiative to include the country on the list of sponsors of terrorism "idiotic". In her own words, it is a measure "of an idiotic nature" which will bring, moreover, its "consequences" and against which Russia will take measures, TASS news agency noted. "All the measures they take, even if they are idiotic, will be analyzed and monitored. They will be followed up and, of course, will not go unanswered, this must be understood," she has said. Zakharova has warned that the international community has not yet addressed the legal issues of U.S. actions in countries such as Syria, Libya or Irakh, so she has assured that if Washington continues to take measures against Moscow because the alleged crimes committed against Ukrainians could have repercussions in the United States.\par \par \uc2 19:14 Bulgaria will repair damaged Ukrainian military equipment Bulgaria has announced that it will take over the repair of Ukrainian military equipment damaged in combat in the war against Russia. This was made public by Bulgarian Prime Minister \uc2 Kiril Petkov\uc2  after a meeting with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  in Kyiv. "Specialized companies of works in the military sphere will repair heavy Ukrainian military equipment. I will submit to the Cabinet of Ministers such a request and I think it will be approved by the Parliament next week as military technical assistance to Ukraine," Petkov said. In addition to repairing an unspecified amount of military equipment, the Balkan country will offer Ukraine the Black Sea port of Varna for export of agricultural products. During his visit, the Bulgarian prime minister thanked Zelensky for the leadership shown by Zelensky during the Russian invasion. "Ukraine will win this war and all democracies are behind you, there is no doubt that this is the only way out," Petkov has declared to the Ukrainian president. The announcement comes hours after Russia cut off gas supplies to Bulgaria for non-payment of amounts in rubles.\par \par \uc2 19:00 \uc2 UN\uc2  confirms the death of some 2,830 civilians in Ukraine The UN Office for Human Rights has already counted 2,829 civilians killed in Ukraine as a result of Russia's military offensive. Within this figure, 205 children stand out, although it assumes that the real figure would be "considerably higher". The \uc2 UN\uc2  has also recorded 3,180 wounded, including 303 minors, according to a count which places most of the victims in territories controlled by forces loyal to the Ukrainian government. Moreover, the High Commissioner for Human Rights has assured that it still lacks more information on some areas where there is still "intense" fighting and includes in this group the area of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 18:46 Peace talks stall for almost a month It has been almost a month since the last face-to-face meeting between Russian and Ukrainian negotiators. Although talks between experts from both countries continued telematically after the meeting in Istanbul last March 29, the sides still have not brought their positions closer and today Moscow assured that it has not yet received Ukraine's response on its latest proposals to reach a possible peace agreement. "This morning, at the time of preparing the press conference, the Russian side had not received a reply," said \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2 . Zakharova was referring to Russian counter-proposals sent to Kyiv after studying its version of a possible agreement between the parties. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  has repeatedly insisted on the need for a direct meeting with his Russian counterpart, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , because contacts through mediators and third countries will not produce the "desired result".\par \par \uc2 18:34 Ukrainian justice says it has identified "more than 8,000 cases" of alleged war crimes Ukrainian investigators say they have identified "more than 8,000 cases" of alleged war crimes since the beginning of the Russian invasion, Ukrainian Prosecutor General Iryna Venediktova has said. "We have more than 8,000 cases," she said in an interview with German media outlet \uc2 Deutsche Welle\uc2 . "We are actually talking about 8,600 cases that only concern war crimes and more than 4,000 cases that are related to war crimes," Venediktova said. These alleged war crimes include "killings of civilians, shelling of civilian infrastructure, torture" and "sexual crimes" reported in the "occupied territory of Ukraine", according to the judge.\par \par \uc2 18:24 Russia continues to advance into the Donbass Russian troops continue their advance into the Donbass from Kharkiv, a region considered key to being able to outflank Ukrainian positions in northern Donetsk, while consolidating their control over the south of the country, bordering the annexed Crimean peninsula. According to the Ukrainian General Staff, the Russian army has spent the last hours concentrating troops and weapons around Izium, while intensifying attacks to definitively control that area. In addition, Moscow has reportedly moved airborne units and up to 500 military equipment to Izium, which is located 70 kilometers from Kramatorsk, a Ukrainian military stronghold in the Donbass.\par \par \uc2 18:12 War in Ukraine fuels right-wing extremists in the Netherlands Dutch intelligence services have warned that Russia's war in Ukraine is fueling "the narrative of right-wing and anti-government extremists" in the Netherlands. Director of the General Intelligence and Security Service, Erik Akerboom, has said that the war in Ukraine is being seen "becoming part of the narrative of right-wing extremists and anti-government extremists," which is also fueling polarization in the Netherlands. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's narrative about the war he launched in Ukraine at the end of February "fits" with the ideas already held by some radical groups and "that inspires to be against the government, in this against the institutions," he said.\par \par \uc2 18:00 OSCE announces the closure of its special mission in Ukraine The OSCE Chairman-in-Office, the Pole, Zbigniew Rau, has announced the end of the Special Monitoring Mission in eastern Ukraine, which had been in \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2014. The decision follows the lack of consensus at the OSCE Permanent Council on March 31, 2022 to extend the mission's mandate. All decisions of the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe, which has 57 partner states from Europe, Central Asia and North America, must be made with full consensus among its members. "It is not an easy decision to make. We have explored all possible options through political dialogue with the participating states to achieve the renewal of the SMM's mandate, but Russia's position left us no choice but to take steps to close the mission," Rau said. In a statement, the Pole assured that the SMM has played a "crucial role" in providing objective information on the ground, facilitating the ceasefire and working to alleviate the effects of the conflict on the civilian population."\par \par \uc2 17:44 Biden proposes $33 billion more in aid to Ukraine U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has asked the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  for $33 billion in additional aid to Ukraine, of which more than $20 billion will go to militarily support Kyiv's fight against the Russian invasion. "As long as the assaults and atrocities continue, we will continue to help Ukraine defend itself against Russian aggression," Biden said in a speech from the White House.\par \par \uc2 17:34 Gas cut-off to Poland and Bulgaria complicate Western sanctions Russian gas company \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's decision to cut off supplies to Poland and Bulgaria for not paying for deliveries in rubles has tangled up the complex framework of EU sanctions, which are intended to punish Moscow economically but also leave open a way for the \uc2 EU\uc2  to buy hydrocarbons from Russia. "The Commission is, as a matter of principle, always in contact with the authorities of the Member States" to "ensure that gas deliveries to Europe continue," said the \uc2 EU\uc2  executive's chief spokesman, Eric Mamer. The supply cuts to Warsaw and Sofia are the first practical application of the decree approved at the end of March by the Kremlin, according to which European gas importers must open two accounts in the Russian bank \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  before May 20, one in euros and the other in rubles, to make their purchases of the hydrocarbon. According to this mechanism designed by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's regime, the purchase does not formally end until the euro deposit has been converted into rubles, but this process, which is "entirely in the hands of the Russian authorities, involves the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2 , whose transactions are prohibited under European sanctions.\par \par \uc2 17:17 Sanchez expresses his support to Bulgaria in the face of Russia's "unacceptable blackmail" with the gas cut The President of the Government, Pedro Sanchez, has expressed Spain's support to the President of Bulgaria, \uc2 Rumen Radev\uc2 , in the face of Russia's "unacceptable blackmail" against his country, whose gas supply was cut off yesterday. S\u225\'00\'E1nchez met with Radev at the Moncloa Palace. As indicated by the President of the Government on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he took the opportunity to convey to the Bulgarian leader "Spain's support in the face of the unacceptable Russian blackmail of suspending gas supplies". In S\u225\'00\'E1nchez's opinion, "the effects of Putin's attack on Ukraine redouble the importance of the already excellent Spanish-Bulgarian relations".\par \par \uc2 17:02 Kyiv still not safe Kyiv authorities have reported that they still cannot guarantee the safety of the residents waiting to return, even after Russian forces withdrew from the area. There are more than 200 buildings in the city damaged by shelling, with reconstruction potentially costing around $100 million. "We don't know how long the war will last and how much it will cost in the coming weeks, maybe months, maybe years," former boxer and Kyiv mayor \uc2 Vitali Klitschko\uc2  has maintained. Since Russian forces withdrew from northern Ukraine, many residents have begun to return to Kyiv, even as the city continues to be the target of sporadic missile attacks. The capital remains barricaded, some bridges remain impassable, and the metro runs only until 8 p.m., so its stations can serve as air-raid shelters during regular attack alerts.\par \par \uc2 16:47 Why Transnistria may be relevant in the Russia-Ukraine war. Ukraine has recently accused Moscow of trying to drag the border region of Transnistria, a self-proclaimed breakaway republic of Moldova, into the war against Kyiv. La Vanguardia's journalist explains why this region may be key in the Ukrainian war.\par \par \uc2 16:35 Transnistria could prove key in Ukraine war Recent infrastructure explosions in the pro-Russian region of Transnistria, officially still part of Moldova, have triggered rumors that the Kremlin could launch a new offensive here. However, Moldova has not increased security measures in the area. Transnistrian authorities have accused Ukraine of perpetrating "terrorist attacks," something the Kyiv government denounces as an excuse to justify entering the war. An offensive from Transnistria, where Moscow has had troops deployed for decades, would facilitate the seizure of the strategic Ukrainian city of Odessa and the connection of the already occupied territories in southern Ukraine with the regions already controlled by Russia in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 16:23 Germany and Japan to strengthen security and energy collaboration vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis Russia The leaders of Germany and Japan, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  and \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 , have pledged to strengthen their security collaboration vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis Russia, as well as to reduce their energy dependence on Moscow. Scholz and Kishida met in Tokyo during the German Chancellor's two-day visit. In doing so, the two leaders wanted to send "a signal of their political commitment" and "shared democratic values". The two leaders pledged to work hand in hand to "take firm steps" in response to Russia, which willincludes the implementation of the successive rounds of sanctions coordinated with the \uc2 G7\uc2 , as well as "additional measures," Scholz said at a joint press conference.\par \par \uc2 16:11 Chernobyl has elevated, but not "dangerous" levels of radioactivity One month after the Russian occupation of the Chernobyl nuclear plant, the \uc2 UN\uc2  nuclear agency (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ) has analyzed samples in the "exclusion zone" revealing that radiation levels are not "dangerous" despite being elevated. The fear of an accident that could release nuclear material due to the beginning of the war in Ukraine that led the Russian army to occupy Chernobyl finally did not go further, as nuclear experts have confirmed. The agency has also denounced the excavations in the area carried out by Russian troops, something that "they would never recommend to anyone".\par \par \uc2 16:03 EU warns that buying Russian gas in rubles violates sanctions against Russia European Union authorities have called on governments and companies in the bloc on Thursday not to buy Russian gas supplies in rubles, as Moscow is now demanding, as they would be in violation of sanctions against Russia. From Brussels they defend that if the purchase is made with the Russian national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this would favor the weakened \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is one of the big targets of international sanctions due to the war in Ukraine. Energy companies such as Italy's \uc2 Eni SpA\uc2  have already given in to pressure from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to purchase in rubles directly from the supplier Gazprombank JSC .\par \par \uc2 15:54 Mariupol port could be back in \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May The plans of pro-Russian Donetsk separatists in Mariupol envisage the return to \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Ukrainian city's port in May to support the Russian effort in the Ukrainian war. This has been advanced by the governor of the separatist republic of Donetsk, \uc2 Denis Pushilin\uc2 , who explains that they plan that "already in May the first cargo will be shipped from the port of Mariupol". On the other hand, Puhilin has made known that the Russian forces that have stormed the city are at "inventory stage, cleaning of the territory, preparation of regulations so that the port can fully function"\par \par \uc2 15:46 Margarita Robles promises that Russia "will not win this cruel war " The Spanish Minister of Defense, Margarita Robles, on Thursday attended a meeting with the Spanish military stationed at the Adazi base in Latvia, where she promised that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  "will not win this cruel war" in Ukraine. This is how forceful Robles has been with the 500 Spanish soldiers who are on the eastern flank of \uc2 NATO\uc2  in the face of the Russian threat led by a Putin whom they will not let "get away with it," according to the head of defense. "It is important that in such difficult times we tell the world that Spain is committed to peace, wants peace, is not going to leave Ukraine and the Ukrainian people alone," Robles remarked\par \par \uc2 15:37 Russia insists again with another offensive in the Donbass The Russian Armed Forces have increased the pressure on Ukrainian defenders in the Donbass region, the main battlefront of the Ukrainian war, which continues to keep both armies in contention. The latest report from the Ukrainian General Staff has also made it known that the country's western border with Moldova is being reinforced, as the threat of an entry into the Transnistrian war is growing. From Kyiv they describe that "Russian invaders are increasing the pace of the offensive \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In almost all directions, Russian troops are intensifying their war activity."\par \par \uc2 15:29 Pedro Sanchez warns that "doing nothing" for Ukraine would be "the end of Europe " The Spanish Prime Minister, Pedro Sanchez, has called on the international community to continue supporting Ukraine, because otherwise it would be "the end of Europe". "Doing nothing and letting Putin sweep Ukraine, would be the end of Europe", has considered Sanchez, who has stressed the need to invest in military equipment because security "does not come for free". For the president, the war in Ukraine is "Putin's war" and he points out this stage as a key moment in which it is necessary to "make the right decisions because they will shape the future".\par \par \uc2 15:20 German Chancellor announces preparations for possible Russian gas cut-off The threat of gas supply disruption from Russia has been hovering over Germany since the beginning of the Ukrainian war, and on Thursday Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  announced that preparations have begun in case such a cut-off happens. Scholz acknowledged from Tokyo, where he met with Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 , that it will be difficult for Germany to eliminate gas imports from Russia. "Any disruption has an impact on the economy, the government is very clear about that," warned the chancellor, who has justified that "that's why it is one of the things that was not immediately included in the sanctions."\par \par \uc2 15:12 Spain sends the ship Ysabel to Poland with the largest shipment of military supplies for Ukraine 200 tons of ammunition, military equipment and weapons and 40 combat-ready trucks have arrived this Thursday in Poland by the Spanish ship Ysabel, sent to Eastern Europe to support Ukraine in the war. The Ministry of Defense has informed of the massive shipment that the Ukrainian army will receive in the coming hours to reinforce itself in the face of persistent Russian attacks throughout the country. The supplies were a personal promise by Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez to his counterpart \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 , whom he visited in Kyiv last week\par \par \uc2 15:03 ???? 100,000 inhabitants are "in danger of death" in Mariupol The razed city of Mariupol is still hosting 100,000 inhabitants who are "in danger of death" from the war, the city council of the stricken Ukrainian town warned Thursday. The mayor of Mariupol, Vadim Boichenko, has denounced the "unhealthy" conditions in which the residents who still resist in the city live and who must face diseases such as "cholera, dysentery and Escherichia coli". On the other hand, Boichenko has regretted that the Russian army does not try to help the civilians who still remain in the locality and assures that "the occupants cannot provide food, water and medicines, or they are simply not interested".\par \par \uc2 14:54 Russian economy stagnates growing by 1.6% due to Ukraine war sanctions The Ministry of Economic Development today released data confirming that the Russian economy is suffering from the numerous international sanctions in the wake of the Ukraine war and that they have already blocked its growth to only 1.6% compared to last year. According to the figures, the noticeable drop in Russian economic growth is a far cry from the high rates of January this 2022, with a 5.8% rise and even February, when 4.3% year-on-year GDP was recorded. The manufacturing, food, chemical and steel industries have been particularly hard hit by the rain of international punishments in the wake of the war in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 14:45 Biden to compensate Ukraine with confiscated Russian oligarchs' funds U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has launched an initiative in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  to have the seized assets of Russian oligarchs sympathetic to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  used as aid to Ukraine. The measure will be accompanied by actions to facilitate the application of sanctions against Russian elites resident or with bank accounts in the United States for the war in Ukraine. On the other hand, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's confiscatory capacity against oligarchs will also be expanded, adding more assets within the list of possible assets to be seized\par \par \uc2 14:36 NATO pledges to support Ukraine in war, even if it lasts for years Kyiv on Thursday received indefinite support from \uc2 NATO\uc2  in its war against Russia after the alliance's secretary general, \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 , guaranteed that they will stand by Ukraine. "We need to be prepared for the long term.... There is absolutely the possibility that this war will drag on and last for months and years," admitted a Stoltenberg who wants to reaffirm \uc2 NATO\uc2 's position side by side with the Ukrainians. This assistance will take the form of arms shipments from the alliance for the defenders in the face of a Russian invasion, which, although long-lasting, will meet with \uc2 NATO\uc2 's obstacle, according to Stoltenberg\par \par \uc2 14:27 U. S. to fight Russian Internet censorship The White House announced this morning that it has launched a campaign, supported by more than 50 countries, to promote a "democratic" Internet in Russia against repression of "free speech." According to the U.S. Administration, they have noticed "an increase in digital authoritarianism" by the Kremlin, so they will seek to quell it in the context of the war in Ukraine. The main objective is to cut off Russian disinformation campaigns spreading fake news about the invasion on Ukrainian territory or cyber attack on other countries\par \par \uc2 14:18 Spain could join the center for sending arms to Ukraine created by the United States The Spanish Ministry of Defense has revealed that Spain is considering joining the EUCOM Control Center of Ukraine (ECCU), a hub that centralizes the sending of arms to Ukraine. The Chief of Defence Staff (Jemad), Admiral General Teodoro L\u243\'00\'F3pez Calder\u243\'00\'F3n, participated in the multinational meeting at the Ramstein air base, where the creation of this support group for the war in Ukraine was announced. Although Spain has already supplied the Ukrainian army with military equipment and ammunition, it has not yet officially joined this body\par \par \uc2 14:09 Russia warns the West: "I do not advise you to test our patience any further " \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  has addressed Western powers and \uc2 NATO\uc2  on Thursday, whom she accuses of "openly calling on Kyiv to attack Russia." Zakharova criticized the West for supplying \uc2 NATO\uc2  weapons for the Ukrainian war and issued a serious warning: "I do not advise you to test our patience any further". The incumbent has insisted that "Kyiv and Western capitals should take seriously the statement of the Ministry of Defense that further incitement of Ukraine to attack Russian territory will undoubtedly lead to a harsh response from Russia."\par \par \uc2 14:01 Finland one step away from joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  The war in Ukraine has led Finland to consider joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  "very soon," the country's Prime Minister \uc2 Sanna Marin\uc2  has just announced, acknowledging the change in international policy due to the Russian threat. "We have a modern army and we can work with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , as we have done for decades in joint exercises," remarked Marin, who assured that Finland would be a great ally because it would contribute "to the security of the entire alliance." Earlier Thursday, \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  made it clear that both Sweden and Finland could join the alliance "very quickly"\par \par \uc2 13:52 Ukraine strikes back on Russian territory Since the beginning of the Ukrainian war two months ago now, we have not yet seen a Ukrainian attack on points so far inside the Russian Federation, with shelling at points 300 kilometers inland. This is the big change that has been taking place in recent hours by the Ukrainian army, and Xavier Xas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s tells us first hand from Kramatorsk, in eastern Ukraine. In fact, the governors of Belgorod, Kursk and Veronezh, regions of Russia, denounced in the last hours attacks that until now had not happened on the part of the Ukrainian forces\par \par \uc2 13:43 Ukraine opens the door to attack Russian territory because it has the "right" to do so Ukrainian Presidential Advisor Mikhail Podoliak warned this morning that the country's Armed Forces have the "right" to attack Russian military targets on foreign territory. "Russia has attacked and is killing civilians, Ukraine will defend itself in any way, including attacks on bases and warehouses of the murderers," Podoliak said. In addition, he believes that "the world recognizes this duty" for Ukraine to take the war to the Russian Federation as a response of the invasion\par \par \uc2 13:34 Putin and Erdogan have been talking about the US-Russia prisoner exchange The Turkish presidency has revealed Thursday that Turkish President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  and his Russian counterpart \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  have been talking about the US-Russia prisoner exchange. They are Russian pilot \uc2 Konstantin Yaroshenko\uc2  and one-time U.S. Marine Trevor Reed, who have become part of the negotiations between the two world powers. Turkey's MIT intelligence agency was key to solving this barter, a fact that Putin has thanked in person and from Ankara is seen as part of the mediation between Moscow and Kyiv over the war in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 13:26 Russia halts evacuation of Azovstal factory The governor of the pro-Russian separatist Donetsk region, Pavlo Kyrylenko, has assured moments ago that Russia will not allow evacuation of Ukrainian soldiers wounded at the Azovstal steel plant in Mariupol. The steel plant has become the symbol of the Ukrainian war and local resistance after fighters and civilians have been besieged for days by the Russian army waiting for a way out. Kyrylenki, on the other hand, has detailed that some 370,000 inhabitants are still living in Ukrainian-controlled parts of Donetsk.\par \par \uc2 13:23 ???? War Ukraine | Latest News | Donetsk separatist leader says Russia will not allow evacuation of wounded Ukrainian soldiers from Azovstal steel plant in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 13:14 No EU country will pay for Russian gas in rubles The \uc2 European Commission\uc2  announced Thursday that all EU countries have agreed that they will not pay Russia directly in rubles for gas supplies, following the measure announced by the Kremlin. On the other hand, from Brussels have indicated that May 20 is the deadline for the next payments on the purchase of Russian gas, in the magnifying glass of Europe since the beginning of the war in Ukraine. "What we do know, and on this there is consensus on the part of all member states, is that none is willing to pay," the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  reported\par \par \uc2 13:05 Ukraine will not suffer gas and electricity shortages, for the time being The Ukrainian Parliament has made it known on Thursday that the country has enough gas and electricity to not suffer shortages for the time being, although it will be more difficult to cover them in late autumn. This is the conclusion of the study made by the head of the Energy and Utilities Commission of the Verkhovna Rada, Andriy Herus, who has ruled out problems in supply for the time being, despite the war in Ukraine. "Today, if we talk about gas volumes, we have enough. We consume less gas than is produced and even today gas is pumped to subway storage facilities," Herus stressed\par \par \uc2 12:57 Russia warns that shipments of heavy weapons to Ukraine "threaten the security" of Europe The recent approval of the German parliament to send heavy weapons to the war in Ukraine has already received a response from the Kremlin, from where it has been warned that "filling Ukraine and other countries with weapons are actions that of the continent and provoke instability." This has been expressed this Thursday by the spokesman of the Russian Presidency, \uc2 Dmitri Peskov\uc2 , who has already warned that these shipments coming from the West will be "legitimate targets" of the Russian army. On the other hand, Peskov recalled that "the decision of the United States to supply the aforementioned 'Afghan' helicopters to Ukraine grossly violates international law and the corresponding Russian-American contracts."\par \par \uc2 12:48 Ukraine War | Transnistria Analysis The separatist region of Transnistria, located as a strip of territory in eastern Moldova next to the western Ukrainian border, is gaining prominence in recent days for its possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Ukrainian war. With more than half a million inhabitants, Transnistria still holds large amounts of Soviet-era weaponry and military detachments of between 1,500 and 2,000 Russian soldiers. The main fear now is that under the pretext of alleged terrorist attacks of Ukrainian authorship inthe country, the territory is involved in the war in Ukraine to attack from the west towards Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 12:39 Pedro Sanchez criticizes "Russian blackmail" with gas and shows his support to Bulgaria The Spanish Prime Minister, Pedro Sanchez, has regretted the "unacceptable Russian blackmail" to suspend gas supplies to Bulgaria and has conveyed his support to his counterpart \uc2 Rumen Radev\uc2 . In a meeting that Sanchez had today with the Bulgarian leader, both presidents have discussed in depth the war in Ukraine and the consequences for the rest of Europe that it is having. Both Sanchez and Radev have talked especially about Russia's decision to block gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria for refusing to pay in rubles\par \par \uc2 12:30 Russia warns that attacks in Transnistria could "involve" it in Ukraine war Recent terrorist attacks reported by authorities in the separatist republic of Transnistria, located in present-day Moldova, could bring this territory into the Ukrainian war. "We strongly condemn attempts to involve Transnistria in the events in Ukraine," Kremlin Foreign Ministry spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  announced Thursday. Instead, from Kyiv there is talk of a "false flag" \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which Moscow seeks to involve Transnistria in the invasion of Ukraine after a staged\par \par \uc2 12:22 ????? The war in Ukraine and Syria, two battlefields that Russia has connected The complexity of the war in Ukraine has led the Kremlin to put General Alexander Dvornikov, nicknamed 'the Butcher of Syria' for his \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the civil war-torn country, in command of the second phase of the invasion. In his analysis of the impact of the Ukrainian war on the Middle East, Jordi Joan Ba\u241\'00\'F1os tells us from Istanbul how the Russian attack on its neighboring country is affecting "Syria, first of all," as Defense expert Merve Seren herself narrates. "There, Russia's role will be reconsidered by the other actors, as evidenced by Turkey's decision to close its airspace to Russian civilian and military flights bound for Syria," warns Seren\par \par \uc2 12:13 Mariupol could be evacuated soon, according to UN UN Humanitarian Coordinator in Ukraine Osnat Lubrani has announced moments ago that she is heading "to Zaporiyia to prepare for the expected evacuation of Mariupol". Lubrani informed that \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  has instructed her to travel to southern Ukraine to speed up negotiations for the rescue of Ukrainians besieged in Mariupol. The survivors are still holed up in the Azovstal steelworks, a symbol of the Ukrainian war, where they have endured another night of shelling by the Russian Armed Forces while awaiting evacuation\par \par \uc2 12:09 ???? Ukrainian war | Latest news | \uc2 UN\uc2  says it is "preparing" the evacuation of Mariupol\par \par \uc2 12:06 The Ukrainian army denounces that Russia has used "phosphorus bombs" on Mariupol The Russian Armed Forces have bombed with about fifty air strikes the devastated city of Mariupol using "a large number of phosphorus bombs", the Ukrainian army warned on Thursday. The denunciation has come from the besieged city itself, where, Captain Sviatoslav Palamar has reported via Telegram "a huge amount of phosphorus bombs: 50 air strikes, missiles, artillery and everything that a barbarian can use against Humanity". Therefore, the military has called for "forceful actions to be taken to lift the blockade or evacuate" the city\par \par \uc2 11:57 \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General asks Russia to "collaborate" to clarify possible war crimes in Ukraine \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2 , the top representative of the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 , on Thursday addressed the Kremlin to be ready to "accept and collaborate with the international criminal court" in the case of war crimes in the Ukrainian war. Guterres has visited the mass grave in Bucha, where the \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary general has been blunt: "When I see this horrific site, I feel how important investigation and accountability is." Guterres' request came hours before meeting this afternoon with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenski\uc2 , whom he will visit after visiting his Russian counterpart \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 11:49 German parliament gives green light to send heavy weapons to Ukraine Germany's doubts about sending heavy weapons to Ukraine are over: The Bundestag has just passed a motion to supply the Ukrainian army with rocket launchers, anti-aircraft missiles, anti-tank mines or machine guns. The agreement between the three parties in the government of German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  and the main opposition formation have jointly agreed on this measure to support in the Ukrainian war. This is not the first time Germany has provided top-level military equipment for Ukraine, as just this week it approved the delivery of 50 anti-aircraft tanks\par \par \uc2 11:40 Russia intensifies its strategy to cut off Ukraine's exports by shelling The Russian Armed Forces are insisting on a bombing campaign on railway bridges connecting to the Danube so that Ukraine cannot carry out its export of grain and metallurgical products to Western Europe. As analyzed by Felix Flores in La Vanguardia, the campaign orchestrated by the Kremlin has concentrated these last hours on destroying infrastructures such as the railway bridge in the south of Odessa. These actions shift Russian actions further southwest of Ukraine, which links the country with the rest of the continent.\par \par \uc2 11:32 UK pledges to "defend the sovereign nation" of Ukraine "for as long as it takes" The \uc2 British government\uc2  has again come out in favor of the Ukrainian resistance against Russia in the war, assuring that it will help "defend its sovereign nation for as long as it takes". These are the words of the Minister of Defense, Ben Wallace, who warned that "if Russia continues to indiscriminately bombard people from the air", then the West will "respond more to the requests of the Ukrainians to defend themselves, and sometimes this will include planes and tanks". Despite his commitment to Ukraine, Wallace also wanted to clarify that the UK is not seeking a further escalation of the war: "What we have always been careful about is making sure that we calibrate the type of weaponry that we send," he remarked,\par \par \uc2 11:24 ???? War Ukraine : Russia denounces "war crimes" in Kherson due to Ukrainian missile shelling The \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  on Thursday denounced a Ukrainian missile launch against residential neighborhoods and the central area of Kherson, located in southern Ukraine and currently under Russian control. According to General Igor Konashenkov, this shelling by the Ukrainian armed forces is "a war crime and a flagrant violation of international humanitarian law". As he narrates in the war report, Konashenkov describes "a massive attack with Tochka-U ballistic missiles and high-powered multiple launcher rockets on residential neighborhoods and the center of Kherson."\par \par \uc2 11:16 Finland and Sweden could join \uc2 NATO\uc2  "quickly," says alliance secretary general \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  today unveiled that Finland and Sweden could "quickly" join the transatlantic alliance and would be "very welcome." The growing hostility in Eastern Europe with Russia could lead the Scandinavian countries to enter the US-led conglomerate, and for the time being Stoltenberg already has an appointment to speak with the Finnish president. "I'm sure there are ways to bridge that interim period in a way that is good enough and works for both Finland and Sweden," the \uc2 NATO\uc2  chief stressed\par \par \uc2 11:08 Almost half of Mariupol is razed to the ground by the war The city of Mariupol, a symbol of Ukrainian resistance for weeks now in the face of the Russian invasion, has reached a point of destruction that stands at 43% of buildings damaged by the Kremlin-ordered attacks. The revealing infographic brought to us by Laura Arag\u243\'00\'F3 shows to what extent the artillery, missile launches and tank barrages launched by Russia on Mariupol have affected the surface of the town. On the other hand, the Kharkiv war hell in Ukraine has also been represented by the indiscriminate destruction in the neighborhood of Satilvka, 40% of which has been razed to the ground.\par \par \uc2 11:00 Albares sees "unacceptable the use of gas as a political weapon" and calls on the EU not to fall into Putin's "trap" The Minister of Foreign Affairs, \uc2 European Union\uc2  and Cooperation, \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Manuel Albares\uc2 , has branded as "unacceptable" the use of gas as a "political weapon" by Russia after it has cut off supplies to Poland and Bulgaria, while warning that the \uc2 EU\uc2  must not fall into this "trap" and must remain united. "The use of gas as a political weapon is completely unacceptable and this must be made clear," Albares said at a press conference from Vilnius together with his Lithuanian counterpart, \uc2 Gabrielius Landsbergis\uc2 , stressing that "this is the Spanish position" and also the European one, as the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula Von der Leyen\uc2 , made clear yesterday. The fact that Russia uses gas as a way to "strike and threaten countries as can be done with military personnel" must have a "European response". "We must maintain unity," the minister stressed, as did his Lithuanian counterpart, arguing that "this is the best defense to support Ukraine and defend the EU and European values."\par \par \uc2 10:52 UK says Putin could "become a cancerous tumor" in Ukraine UK Defense Minister Ben Wallace said Thursday that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  could "try to consolidate" the developments in Ukraine and "become a cancerous tumor" in the country, for which he called for the withdrawal of Russian troops from the entire Ukrainian territory. Wallace has indicated in declarations to the British television channel Sky News that the Russian leader is "an ethnic nationalist" and has stressed that Putin "has always had the ambition" to invade Ukraine. "Obviously there are Russian-speaking areas that he potentially perceives as the Nazis felt during World War II, as if this population was his possession," he explained.\par \par \uc2 10:45 Ukraine goes a step further and launches several attacks inside Russian territory, reports Xavier Xas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s, special envoy of La Vanguardia to Kramatorks The war took a very significant leap yesterday. Ukraine launched several attacks on Russian territory, one of them more than 300 kilometers from its border, while Russia cut off gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria, its most forceful response so far to the economic sanctions weighing on it since the beginning of the invasion. The escalation of the war seems unstoppable and, at the same time, the economic consequences for the \uc2 European Union\uc2  may be much harsher than expected. Without Russian energy, it will not be able to grow enough to overcome the recession of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 10:37 Guterres urges Russia to cooperate in war crimes investigation \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary-General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  on Thursday stressed the importance of the investigation into the possible commission of war crimes in the municipality of Bucha during the Russian occupation and urged Moscow to accept and cooperate with that investigation. "I appeal to the Russian Federation to accept and cooperate with the international criminal court," Guterres said during his visit to the mass grave in Bucha, where he has expressed his "full support" for the ICC investigation. "When we talk about war crimes we cannot forget that the worst crime is war alone," lamented the \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary general, on a visit to Ukraine to meet with President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 10:30 ???? War Ukraine | Russia says destroyed two ammunition depots in Ukraine overnight \uc2 Russia's Defense Ministry\uc2  said Thursday that Russian missiles had struck four Ukrainian military targets overnight, destroying two missile and ammunition depots near the settlements of Barvinkove and Ivanivka in the east of the country. It also said the Russian military had shot down a Ukrainian Su-24 aircraft near Luhansk.\par \par \uc2 10:22 \uc2 Von der Leyen\uc2  warns companies that if they pay in rubles for Russian gas they will violate EU sanctions \uc2 Von der Leyen\uc2  warns companies that if they pay in rubles for Russian gas they will violate EU sanctions \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , on Wednesday warned companies that buy gas from Russia that if they give in to the Kremlin's "blackmail" of paying in rubles for the supply when it is not foreseen in their purchase contracts, it will mean a violation of the sanctions that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  imposes on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's regime, which could have legal consequences for these companies. In a statement to the press following Moscow's decision to cut off energy supplies to Poland and Bulgaria for refusing to pay in rubles, the head of the \uc2 EU\uc2  executive assured that this challenge will have an "immediate, united and coordinated" response from the EU, while pointing out that the bloc is working "intensively" on the next package of coercive measures, without clarifying the timetable or content.\par \par \uc2 10:15 ???? War Ukraine : Lugansk separatist authorities denounce two civilians killed by Ukrainian Army attacks The authorities of the self-proclaimed Lugansk People's Republic, located in eastern Ukraine, have denounced on Thursday the death of two civilians, including a child, due to attacks carried out during the last day by the Ukrainian Army against the area. Thus, they have detailed that the second victim is a 95-year-old woman resident in Pervomaisk, where two residential buildings, an educational center and a kindergarten have also been damaged, according to the Russian news agency TASS. Separately, the self-proclaimed Donetsk People's Republic has said that one civilian has been injured in recent hours due to attacks by the Ukrainian Armed Forces.\par \par \uc2 10:07 The Russian-occupied Ukrainian province of Kherson will start using the ruble from May 1 The Russian-imposed military authorities in the southern Ukrainian province of Kherson have assured that the area will start using the ruble from May 1, a day after Moscow claimed to control the entire region as part of its invasion of Ukraine. The deputy chairman of the Kherson Military and Civil Administration, Kiril Stremusov, told the Russian news agency RIA Novosti that there will be a four-month transition in which the ruble and the hryvna, the Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will be used at the same time. He also stressed that Kherson oblast will not return to control of "Nazi Ukraine." "It is impossible. The Kherson region will develop economically. Kiev will not be able to continue imposing its Nazi policies on our land, aimed at destroying the people and their identity," he added.\par \par \uc2 10:00 Hungary confirms it will pay Russia in rubles for oil and gas imports The Hungarian government has confirmed that it will use the payment system put in place by Russia to pay for oil and gas imports in rubles, following the battery of sanctions imposed against Moscow for its military invasion of Ukraine. Hungarian Foreign Minister \uc2 Peter Szijjarto\uc2  defended Budapest's decision in an interview with U.S. television network CNN and stressed that "85 percent of the gas supply" and "65 percent of the oil supply" comes from Russia. "Why? It is determined by infrastructure. It is not for fun, we have not chosen this situation," he stressed, before stressing that there are no alternative routes or sources that would allow Hungary to stop importing energy from Russia.\par \par \uc2 09:52 ???? War Ukraine : Russia increases its offensive and concentrates forces in Izium to attack Donbass The Russian Army has been concentrating troops and weapons in the area of Izium, near the Donbass region, bordering Russia, and has intensified its offensive to control this pro-Russian area and southern Ukraine. The General Staff of the Ukrainian Armed Forces reported on Thursday on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  about the situation on the different fronts where it is facing the Russian Army. According to the Ukrainian military part, Russian forces have moved airborne units and up to 500 units of military equipment to the city of Izium to strengthen their offensive capabilities. "It is noted," it adds, "that themain efforts of Russian troops are focused on Izium. The enemy is trying to launch an offensive in the directions of Sulyhivka.\par \par \uc2 09:45 Zelensky: Russia uses gas and any other goods as a weapon to blackmail Russia considers gas and any other goods it can export as a weapon and will use them to blackmail Europe, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  said tonight in a video message reproduced by Ukrinform agency. "The sooner Europe admits that it is inadmissible to depend on Russia in trade, the sooner it will be possible to ensure stability on European markets," the Ukrainian leader added.\par \par \uc2 09:37 Ukraine war: Ukraine estimates its post-war reconstruction at $600 billion Ukraine's Prime Minister Denis Shmigal has put the country's reconstruction after the end of the war with Russia at $600 billion and has asked the international community for a fund to cope with what is to come in order to modernize the state for its entry into the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . "We estimate that a large-scale reconstruction will cost around $600 billion," Shmigal said in an article for The Economist in which he not only mentioned damage to infrastructure, but also to industry and the environment. "The Ukrainian government has already set up a long-term recovery fund. In our opinion, it can be a universal solution that will ensure reconstruction. It will reduce logistical and administrative costs, and we encourage our international partners to direct all resources through this fund," he encouraged.\par \par \uc2 09:30 Zelenski insists to \uc2 Von der Leyen\uc2  that the new package of sanctions on Russia include oil embargo Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  on Wednesday again asked \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  that the new package of sanctions on Russia include oil embargo. In a message via his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  profile, Zelenski told that he had a conversation with the president of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , whom he thanked for Brussels' decision to abolish tariffs on imports of Ukrainian products. "We are grateful to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  for deciding to abolish tariffs and quotas on Ukrainian industrial goods and foodstuffs," Zelenski wrote, who also let it be known that other issues of support and collaboration have been addressed through the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.\par \par \uc2 09:22 Lugansk separatist authorities denounce two civilians killed by Ukrainian Army attacks The authorities of the self-proclaimed Lugansk People's Republic, located in eastern Ukraine, have denounced on Thursday the death of two civilians, including a child, due to the attacks executed during the last day by the Ukrainian Army against the area. Thus, they have detailed that the second victim is a 95-year-old woman resident in Pervomaisk, where two residential buildings, an educational center and a kindergarten have also been damaged, according to the Russian news agency TASS. Separately, the self-proclaimed Donetsk People's Republic said that one civilian has been injured in recent hours due to attacks by the Ukrainian Armed Forces.\par \par \uc2 09:15 Putin wants to hold on to Ukraine like a "cancerous tumor" Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may try to consolidate what he has in Ukraine and entrench himself, like a "cancerous tumor" inside the country, British Defense Secretary Ben Wallace said Thursday. "You can see from his current statements that, almost in desperation, he is trying to amplify the situation with threats or, indeed, possible false flags or attacks," he told Sky News. "I think it's certainly the case that Putin, having failed in almost all of his objectives, may seek to consolidate what he has, sort of fortify and entrench himself as he did in 2014. Just be a kind of cancerous tumor inside the country in Ukraine and make it very difficult for people to move them out of those fortified positions."\par \par \uc2 09:07 About 20 Russian Navy ships are in the Black Sea, UK says About 20 Russian Navy ships are currently in the Black Sea \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  area, including submarines, according to the latest intelligence report released Thursday by the \uc2 British Ministry of Defense\uc2 . The Bosphorus Strait remains closed to all non-Turkish warships, making it impossible for Russia to replace its Moskva cruiser in the Black Sea, the report adds. Despite the losses of the Saratov landing ship and the Moskva cruiser, Russia's Black Sea fleet retains the capability to attack Ukrainian and coastal targets, according to the British information.\par \par \uc2 09:00 Servicemen besieged in Mariupol ask for evacuation like 1940 Dunkirk Servicemen who remain besieged by Russian troops at the Azovstal steel plant in the Ukrainian cost city of Mariupol have asked to be evacuated following the "extraction" procedure used at Dunkirk (France) in 1940 in World War II. \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dynamo, also known as the Dunkirk miracle or Dunkirk evacuation, was an evacuation \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Allied troops on French territory in World War II that saved thousands of soldiers. The appeal was made by the commander of the 36th Marine Brigade of the Ukrainian Armed Forces (FAU), Serhiy Volynsky, one of about half a thousand Ukrainian servicemen who remain encircled in the facilities of the Azovstal plant. "We have been fighting a complete encirclement for 62 days, now we are at the Azovstal plant, performing tasks together with units of the Azov regiment and other units that have been encircled due to hostilities in Mariupol," Volynsky said in a video message posted on his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page and reported by Interfax-Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 08:52 Putin defends Russia's right to recognize Donbass as the West recognized Kosovo, Gonzalo Aragon\u233\'00\'E9s reports from Moscow While \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  explained his concern about the humanitarian situation in Ukraine and in the city of Mariupol, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Tuesday preferred to take the beginning of his conversation to Kosovo to compare this case with that of the pro-Russian entities in eastern Donbass in eastern Ukraine. After meeting with Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2 , Guterres was received by Putin in the Kremlin. The meeting lasted an hour and only the first parts were made public, through images on the Russiya 24 TV channel.\par \par \uc2 08:45 Borrell: "Europe's dependence on Russian gas has gone too far" European Union High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2  said Wednesday that the bloc must reduce dependence on Russian gas and oil, after the Kremlin cut off supplies to Poland and Bulgaria over their refusal to pay in rubles. "Our dependence on Russian gas and oil has undoubtedly gone too far (...) This latest decision by Russia will only accelerate Europe's conversion to green energies that do not create dependencies and do not harm the climate," said Borrell, who is on an official visit to Chile. Poland and Bulgaria are the first countries to which Russia, which represents for both of them their main gas supplier, is cutting gas supplies, although it is maintaining them for the rest of Europe. So far, it is Moscow's harshest reaction to the measures imposed by the West to the freezing of the reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  and the disconnection of several Russian banks from the SWIFT international interbank system.\par \par \uc2 08:37 Russia-Ukraine War | The Russian invasion of Ukraine began on February 24 and has reduced towns and cities to rubble and forced more than 5 million people to flee abroad. Western countries have responded with sanctions and weapons for Ukraine to fight a war that has raised fears of a wider conflict in the West, unthinkable for decades. Russia calls its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "special \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to disarm \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect it from fascists. Ukraine and the West say it is a false pretext for an unprovoked war of aggression by President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 08:30 Putin warns West of withering retaliation as sanctions punish Russia Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  warned that he will retaliate witheringly if other countries interfere in Ukraine, as European leaders accused Russia of "blackmail" over its gas supply cuts. Russia has called on the United States to stop sending arms to Ukraine, claiming large Western shipments of weaponry were exacerbating the conflict.In a speech to parliamentarians in St. Petersburg on Wednesday, Putin said the West wanted to cut Russia into different pieces and accused it of pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  into conflict with Russia. "If someone intends to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the ongoing events from outside and create strategic threats to Russia that are unacceptable to us, he should know that our retaliatory strikes will be withering like lightning," Putin said, according to a video of his speech provided by Russian media. "We have all the tools for this, things that no one else can boast of having now. And it's not about boasting, we will use them if necessary. And I want everyone to know that."\par \par \uc2 08:22 US House passes bill urging sale of seized Russian yachts The \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2  on Wednesday overwhelmingly passed a bill, urging US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to sell assets of Russian oligarchs affected by sanctions and use the funds to provide more military and humanitarian aid to Ukraine. The legislation is non-binding but its passage with 417 votes in favor and 8 against reflects a desire for the president to take a more aggressive stance toward Russia, as reported by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . The bill's passage comes a day after Attorney General Merrick B. Garland told the Senate that the administration would ask Congress for greater authority to seize and liquidate Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 08:15 Shockwave of Ukraine war reshapes Middle East, Jordi Joan Ba\u241\'00\'F1os reports from Istanbul Despite the sparse use of aviation in Ukraine, to minimize civilian casualties, the devastation of Mariupol has made comparison with Aleppo inevitable. The largest Syrian city, which Russian bombers razed to the ground to subdue the insurrection, as US bombers raided Raqa. General Alexander Dvornikov, the butcher of Syria , has been put in command of the second phase of the Russian "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine. So the ghosts of Syria are already haunting Ukraine, while less attention is paid to how the war is reshaping the East.\par \par \uc2 08:07 Japan protests expulsion of Japanese diplomats from Russia The Government of Japan today transferred its protests to Moscow over the expulsion of eight Japanese diplomats from Russian territory, a move taken in retaliation for Tokyo's similar decision earlier in the wake of the invasion of Ukraine. "We cannot accept in any way the decision announced by Russia," said today at a press conference the spokesman of the Japanese Executive, \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2 , who also pointed out that Moscow bears "full responsibility" for the deterioration of bilateral relations. The \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  announced the day before that it was requiring eight Japanese diplomats to leave the country until May 10, "guided by the principle of reciprocity".\par \par \uc2 08:00 Zelensky: Russia uses gas and any other goods as a weapon to blackmail Russia considers gas and any other goods it can export as a weapon and will use them to blackmail Europe, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  said tonight in a video message reproduced by Ukrinform agency. "The sooner Europe admits that it is inadmissible to depend on Russia in trade, the sooner it will be possible to ensure stability on European markets," the Ukrainian leader added. He recalled that "this week, Russia's leadership launched a new series of energy blackmails to the Europeans. The decision to cut off gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria is another argument in favor of the fact that no one in Europe can hope to maintain normal economic cooperation with Russia." Moscow "considers not only gas, but any commercial activity as a weapon. It is just waiting for the moment when one or another area of trade can be used to blackmail the Europeans politically. Or to strengthen Russia's military machine, which aims to (destabilize) a united Europe," Zelensky stressed.\par \par \uc2 07:52 Australia issues over 7,000 visas to displaced Ukrainians The Australian Government on Thursday revealed that over 7,000 visas have been issued to Ukrainian citizens and over 3,000 arrivals have been recorded since Russia's invasion of Ukraine began. Of the 7,000 visas granted, most have been temporary visas that are valid for three years and allow work, study and access to basic services. "The Ministry of Home Affairs is progressing visa applications for Ukrainian citizens as a priority, particularly for those with a connection to Australia," a Home Office spokesman has said, adding that hundreds of visas have been given to Ukrainians now in other countries outside Ukraine, as reported by Australian television station SBS News.\par \par \uc2 07:45 Canada unanimously approves calling Russia's invasion of Ukraine "genocide" The Canadian House of Commons on Wednesday unanimously passed a motion recognizing the Russian invasion of Ukraine as an act of "genocide." The resolution has been tabled by MP, Heather McPherson, is non-binding and does not require the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  to take any action, as reported by CBC News. The motion has noted that the killing of Ukrainian civilians, desecration of corpses, forcible transfer of Ukrainian children to Russian territory, and torture and rape by Russian soldiers constitute genocide.\par \par \uc2 07:37 U.S. Evaluates Suspending Tariffs on Ukrainian Steel The Biden Administration is evaluating suspending tariffs on Ukrainian steel imports, people familiar with the matter said, a largely symbolic show of support for the war-torn nation. The move to suspend the duties is not imminent and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has not received the proposal, the people said. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  imposed a 25% tariff on all steel imports, and the justification was that he was doing so for national security reasons. Some countries, including Japan, the United Kingdom and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , have negotiated agreements with the United States to reduce the tariffs. A National Security Council spokeswoman said no decision has been made. A Commerce Department spokeswoman did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 07:30 Ukraine puts Russian damage at $90 billion Russia has caused Ukraine infrastructure damage valued at about $90 billion (more than 85 billion euros) since the March 24 invasion, the Kiev government said Wednesday.Ukrainian Infrastructure Minister \uc2 Oleksandr Kubrakov\uc2  told the meeting of the Congress of Local and Regional Authorities, which was attended by President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2 , that the damage to infrastructure as a result of the war launched by Russia amounted to almost $90 billion.\par \par \uc2 07:22 Robles visits today the 500 Spanish military deployed in Latvia to deter Russian threat on the border Defense Minister Margarita Robles travels this Thursday to Latvia to visit the 500 Spanish military deployed on \uc2 NATO\uc2 's eastern border to deter any threat from Russia. Spain has been participating since 2017 in this Atlantic Alliance \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was designed with the objective of maintaining a permanent presence in Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania and Poland as a deterrence and defense measure. The objective, as defined by \uc2 NATO\uc2 , is to "prevent potential conflicts, protect allies and preserve peace and stability beyond the Alliance's borders." However, the mission has become particularly relevant following Russia's invasion of Ukraine, after which the Alliance decided to strengthen protection and deterrence in the countries on its eastern flank.\par \par \uc2 07:15 Transnistria opens a new hotbed of tension on Ukraine's borders, reports La Vanguardia Moldovan President \uc2 Maia Sandu\uc2  warned yesterday that attacks in the self-proclaimed republic of Transnistria, bordering southwestern Ukraine and backed by Russia, are an attempt to increase tension on the part of "pro-war factions". The Security Council of the separatist region yesterday denounced several "terrorist attacks" in less than 24 hours against government facilities and telecommunication antennas. "There were three terrorist attacks in Transnistria. They fired grenade launchers at the building of the Ministry of State Security, there were two explosions at the broadcasting center in the village of Maiak and they also attacked a military unit in Parcani," the press service of the region's president , Vadim Krasnoselski, said on his Telegram channel.\par \par \uc2 07:07 Borrell asks \uc2 EU\uc2  to bet on green energies after new Russian "aggression" The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy, \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , on Wednesday described as "aggression" Russia's decision to cut gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria and asked the EU bloc to move towards green energies. Borrell, who is on an official visit to Chile, said that European dependence on Russian gas and oil "has gone too far" and that Moscow's announcement "will only accelerate Europe's conversion to green energies that do not create dependencies and do not harm the climate". "What Russia is doing today is turning a dependency into an act of aggression. We have the means to confront it and we will confront this new act of aggression jointly and in solidarity. Europeans can be sure of them," he added. Russia's state-owned \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  announced on Wednesday the total interruption of its exports to Poland and Bulgaria because of their refusal to pay in rubles, a demand from Moscow rejected by most of the countries of the European bloc.\par \par \uc2 07:00 UK calls for providing fighter jets to Ukraine The UK and other Western powers should provide weapons, tanks and fighter jets to Ukraine as part of long-term military support, British Foreign Secretary \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  said Wednesday. In a speech at Mansion House, the mayor's residence in the City of London (financial center), Truss said the West "must be prepared for the long term and redouble our support" for the country so that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  cannot find a place to fund this "terrible war." "Heavy weapons, tanks, aircraft: deepen our inventories, increase production. We need to do all this," said the minister, who considered that "the war in Ukraine is our war, it is everyone's war, because the victory of Ukraine is a strategic imperative for all of us." Western sanctions, she stressed, have already caused Russia to face its first debt default in a century.\par \par \uc2 06:52 World discusses how to ensure justice for atrocities in Ukraine Countries, international organizations and human rights bodies discussed at the \uc2 UN\uc2  on Wednesday how to ensure accountability for the grave atrocities being committed in Ukraine, a war that will be a litmus test for international justice, they warned. Meeting in an informal session, the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  heard from, among others, \uc2 UN\uc2  High Commissioner for Human Rights \uc2 Michelle Bachelet\uc2 , \uc2 International Criminal Court (ICC) \uc2 Chief Prosecutor Karim Khan, Ukrainian Prosecutor General Iryna Venediktova, and organizations such as \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  (\uc2 HRW\uc2 ). They all insisted that the crimes being witnessed in Ukraine cannot go unpunished if the world is to maintain a glimmer of justice for the future.\par \par \uc2 06:45 Europe prepares for the shutdown of the Russian gas tap, reports Esther Herrera from Brussels Russia has taken the step that so many in Europe feared. \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  has cut off gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria after these two countries refused to pay in rubles. Reactions have been swift, in the midst of the debate about a future embargo on Russian-origin fossil fuels. "\uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's announcement that it will unilaterally suspend gas deliveries to customers in Europe is another attempt by Russia to use gas as an instrument of blackmail," said \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 . "This is another provocation by the Kremlin, but it is not a surprise," she added.\par \par \uc2 06:37 Russian journalist detained for reporting attack on Mariupol theater Journalist Maria Ponomarenko of the RusNews news portal was detained for reporting the "alleged Russian airstrike" on March against the Drama Theater in the Ukrainian city of Mariupol where hundreds of civilians were hiding, Sputnik agency reported Wednesday. A statement released by the joint press service of the St. Petersburg courts, quoted by the Russian agency, adds that "the Oktyabrsky district court ruled on the imposition of the preventive measure in the form of detention on Maria Ponomarenko." The arrested woman is accused of publicly spreading false information about the use of the Armed Forces of the Russian Federation, adds the note published by Sputnik. Ponomarenko, according to the statement, posted the information on her Telegram channel, was arrested last April 24 and will remain under arrest until next June 22.\par \par \uc2 06:30 Putin promises to respond "ultra-rapidly" to anyone who tries to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Ukrainian conflict Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Wednesday promised to respond "ultra-rapidly" to anyone who tries to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Ukrainian conflict from outside and creates "a threat" against the Eurasian country. "If someone intends to interfere in the ongoing events (the invasion) from outside and create a nature of strategic threats, they should know that our response to counterattacks will be ultra-fast," Putin has said at a meeting with lawmakers, Russian news agency Interfax has reported. Putin has added that Russia has "all the tools" to respond. "Those that no one can now boast about. And we are not going to boast. We will use them if necessary and I want everyone to know them," he has emphasized.\par \par \uc2 06:22 Turkey confident of a meeting between Putin and Zelenski in the "coming days" Turkey's Defense Minister \uc2 Hulusi Akar\uc2  on Wednesday expressed confidence in holding a meeting between the presidents of Russia \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Ukraine \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  in the "coming days." "We hope that, despite some difficulties, the two leaders will be able to meet in the coming days," Akar moved, after participating in the meeting held at the German Ramstein air base, and hosted by the United States, in which Western defense ministers have participated to discuss military assistance to Kyiv. "Turkey continues to contribute, doing everything necessary, including playing a mediating role, so that the humanitarian situation in Ukraine does not deteriorate and a ceasefire is reached as soon as possible," the Turkish minister stressed, Russian TASS news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 06:15 This is how Ukrainian artillerymen are resisting Russian attacks on the Kharkiv front, reports Xavier Xas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s from Kharkiv Dimitri lives on the eastern front of Kharkiv, where the asphalt and the chimneys of the industrial estates give way to the wide, unpopulated plain, sown with wheat that is now beginning to sprout. His house is simple, one-story with a gable roof. It has a beautiful garden with almond trees in bloom and tulips that grow from bulbs planted many years ago. The chickens roam freely, as crazy as ever. The fence that guarded them was blown up at the beginning of the invasion and it is useless to repair it.\par \par \uc2 06:07 Zelenski insists to \uc2 Von der Leyen\uc2  that the new package of sanctions on Russia should include the oil embargo Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  on Wednesday again asked the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  that the new package of sanctions on Russia should include the oil embargo. In a message via his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  profile, Zelenski told that he had a conversation with the president of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , whom he thanked for Brussels' decision to abolish tariffs on imports of Ukrainian products. "We are grateful to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  for deciding to abolish tariffs and quotas on Ukrainian industrial goods and foodstuffs," Zelenski wrote, who also let it be known that other issues of support and collaboration have been addressed through the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.\par \par \uc2 06:00 Ukraine puts its post-war reconstruction at $600 billion Ukraine's Prime Minister Denis Shmigal has put the country's reconstruction after the end of the war with Russia at $600 billion and has asked the international community for a fund with which to cope with what is to come with a view to modernizing the state for its entry into the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . "We estimate that a large-scale reconstruction will cost around $600 billion," Shmigal said in an article for The Economist in which he not only mentioned damage to infrastructure, but also to industry and the environment. "The Ukrainian government has already set up a long-term recovery fund. In our opinion, it can be a universal solution that will ensure reconstruction. It will reduce logistical and administrative costs, and we encourage our international partners to direct all resources through this fund," he encouraged.\par \par \uc2 05:55 Putin defends Russia's right to recognize Donbass as the West recognized Kosovo, reports Gonzalo Aragon\u233\'00\'E9s from Moscow While \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  explained his concern about the humanitarian situation in Ukraine and in the city of Mari\u250\'00\'FApol, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Tuesday preferred to take the beginning of his conversation to Kosovo to compare this case with that of the pro-Russian entities in eastern Donbass, in eastern Ukraine. After meeting with Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2 , Guterres was received by Putin in the Kremlin. The meeting lasted an hour and only the first parts were made public, through images on the Russiya 24 television channel.\par \par \uc2 05:48 Guterres arrives in Ukraine to meet with Zelenski after visiting Putin in Moscow \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  arrived Wednesday afternoon in Ukraine to meet with its president, \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 , after visiting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Moscow, in the context of the Russian invasion of its neighboring country. As Guterres has moved through his account on the social network \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he seeks to expand humanitarian support to Ukraine and ensure the evacuation of civilians from conflict zones. "The sooner this war ends, the better for Ukraine, Russia and the world," he added. As reported by Guterres' spokesman \uc2 Stephane Dujarric\uc2  on Friday, the \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary general will meet with Zelenski and Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dimitro Kuleba on Thursday. The Ukrainian president openly criticized Russia as the first leg of Guterres' trip in the midst of the armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 05:45 Russia-Ukraine war | Latest news from the conflict and Putin, live. Good morning and welcome to La Vanguardia for a new day of coverage of all the information related to the war in Ukraine, which is now in its 64th day. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  warned Wednesday that Moscow will respond with "lightning strikes" in case of strategic interference in Ukraine, as the Russian Army destroyed a large consignment of Western-supplied weaponry in the south of the country. "If someone, I insist, sets out to interfere in the ongoing events and creates inadmissible strategic threats to Russia, they should know that our response strikes will be lightning, swift," the Russian leader assured during an address to the Council of Parliamentarians in St. Petersburg.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220429ei4s001p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
