\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms United Kingdom 'AA/A-1+' Ratings: Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 4,278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par 22:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 22 Apr 2022 16:29 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms United Kingdom 'AA/A-1+' Ratings: Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We forecast the U.K.'s inflation will average 6.3% in 2022, the \par highest since 1991, with risks that it could turn out even higher. \par      -- Upward price pressures, aggravated by the fallout from Russia's \par military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , are driving real incomes lower. We have \par revised our GDP growth forecast down to 3.5% for 2022, from the 4.6% we \par projected in December 2021. \par      -- Inflation is also fueling a rise in the government's interest \par expenditure but public finances are strengthening overall, with net general \par government debt projected to fall to 92% of GDP by 2025 from 96% at the end of \par 2021. \par      -- In our view, the U.K. benefits from strong institutional settings and \par a highly effective and credible monetary policy. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) is \par in a rate-tightening cycle and we expect inflation will gradually be brought \par under control by mid-2023. \par      -- We affirmed our 'AA/A-1+'sovereign credit ratings on the U.K. The \par outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On April 22, 2022, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'AA/A-1+' unsolicited long- \par and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the U.K. \par The outlook is stable. \par  \par At the same time, we affirmed our 'AA/A-1+' long- and short-term issuer credit \par ratings on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2   and our 'AA' issue ratings on the debt programs of Network \par Rail Infrastructure Finance PLC, The Channel Tunnel Rail Link Section 1 \par Finance PLC, Affordable Housing Finance PLC, and London Continental Railways \par PLC. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects balanced risks to the U.K.'s creditworthiness over \par the next 24 months. \par  \par Downside scenario \par The ratings could come under pressure if economic growth is significantly \par weaker than we anticipate, challenging fiscal consolidation. This could \par happen, for instance, if the fallout from Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  proved more substantial for the U.K., ultimately leading to \par weaker revenue performance or pressure to increase spending to offset the \par rising cost of living. Ratings downside could also emerge if foreign financing \par for the U.K.'s large external deficit diminished, potentially weighing on the \par country's economic prospects. \par  \par Upside scenario \par Ratings upside could arise if the persistent uncertainty regarding the U.K.'s \par relations with the EU subsided. Upward ratings pressure could also build if \par the government's fiscal performance and debt reduction prove stronger than we \par anticipate. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Our ratings on the U.K. are supported by its high income levels; its large, \par diversified economy and financial sector; its developed product and labor \par markets; the strength and independence of its key institutions; and the \par sterling's reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  status. \par  \par The ratings are constrained by the government's large debt stock, although the \par overall debt profile benefits from one of the longest average maturities among \par advanced economies, and the fact that almost all the debt is denominated in \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The ratings are also constrained by still lingering \par uncertainties regarding the U.K.'s relations with its key trading partner, the \par EU, given ongoing disagreements over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Northern Ireland \par Protocol. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: The U.K.'s "cost-of-living crisis" is \par being aggravated by the fallout from Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as inflation accelerates \par      -- Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is aggravating the \par U.K.'s cost-of-living crisis as energy price rises fuel already-high inflation. \par      -- We expect this will weigh on domestic consumption and we have revised \par our headline growth forecast down to 3.5% from the 4.6% we projected in \par December 2021. \par      -- The domestic political scene will likely face several recurring \par challenges, including the implementation of the Northern Ireland Protocol and \par Scotland's continuing demands for a second independence referendum. \par  \par We consider that the economic outlook for the U.K. has weakened following \par Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Direct trade exposure to \par Russia, Belarus, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is limited given that the three countries \par together account for around 1% of U.K. exports. Nevertheless, as a net energy \par importer, the U.K. remains exposed to the conflict's impact on oil and gas \par prices, which had already risen and recently accelerated further, fueling \par headline inflation. In March 2022, inflation measured 7% year-on-year, a \par 30-year high for the U.K. In response, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2   embarked on a rate-rising \par cycle. It has undertaken three interest rate hikes since December 2021, with \par further tightening likely in the coming months. \par  \par The current rapid pace of inflation is aggravating the U.K.'s cost-of-living \par crisis, which had already been unfolding before the recent military \par escalation. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Office for Budget Responsibility\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the U.K.'s independent \par fiscal body, is projecting that real disposable incomes will fall by 2.2% this \par financial year (2022/2023)--the largest annual decline since records began. In \par our view, this will pressure domestic consumption. Additionally, the \par uncertainty around the duration and the outcome of the conflict in Ukraine is \par likely to adversely affect investments as well as export performance via \par second-round effects on the growth outlook for the EU, the U.K.'s key trading \par partner. \par  \par We have revised down our growth forecast for the U.K. to 3.5% for 2022, from \par the 4.6% projection we published in December. This comparatively high headline \par growth, however, masks a substantial growth carryover effect from 2021 of \par 2.6%. Effectively, we therefore project sequential growth to be 1% this year \par and believe that a recession could happen, but only if additional downside \par shocks materialize. These could include, for instance, outright cuts of \par Russian gas supplies to Europe, more acute direct supply chain challenges, or \par a significant deterioration in global financing conditions. Nevertheless, in \par our baseline scenario for now we forecast growth will remain positive. \par  \par There are several factors underpinning our growth forecast. First, the U.K. \par economy entered the latest shock from a comparatively strong position. In \par January 2022, output in real terms had fully recovered to pre-pandemic levels \par and the government subsequently removed all remaining domestic- and \par foreign-travel COVID-19-related restrictions in February. Although case \par numbers have fluctuated and hospitalizations have recently risen, we do not \par expect a sizable economic drag beyond temporary staff absences in some \par sectors. We expect that high vaccination levels will reduce the likelihood of \par the government introducing social distancing restrictions again, which should \par support economic activity. Additionally, economic agents have been \par increasingly adapting to COVID-19 over the last two years, so subsequent waves \par of the virus are unlikely to disturb economic activity as much. \par  \par The labor market is also strengthening. The Coronavirus Job Retention Scheme, \par also known as furlough, has successfully preserved employment. The scheme \par ended in September 2021 with unemployment averaging 4.5% in 2021, a limited \par rise from the pre-pandemic 3.8% seen in 2019. The pre-pandemic unemployment \par rate was regained in February 2022. Additional household savings were \par accumulated through the lockdowns and social-distancing restrictions. These \par savings could be deployed, at least by some households, to smooth consumption \par in the face of elevated inflation. \par  \par The long-term economic impact of Brexit remains difficult to quantify, in our \par view. Since the transition period ended at the end of 2020, the U.K. has \par effectively left the single market and has been trading with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   under a \par new Trade and Cooperation Agreement signed in late 2020. While the new \par arrangement is purportedly tariff- and quota-free, tariffs are applicable in \par some circumstances--for example if a certain proportion of a product's added \par value originates outside the U.K. and the EU. Moreover, businesses now face \par additional costs and frictions where none existed previously, for example \par relating to documentation and certification. The trade agreement between the \par U.K. and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   does not cover the U.K. service sector's automatic access to \par the EU single market. \par  \par U.K. trade also appears to have recently fared worse than peers. For instance, \par following a significant export contraction by 13% in real terms in 2020, the \par U.K.'s real exports contracted by a further 1.3% in 2021. This contrasts with \par the outcomes registered in Germany, France, and Italy, for example, all of \par which saw a notable rebound in exports last year. While it is difficult to \par ascertain to what degree Brexit contributed to this, particularly given the \par impact of the pandemic, it was likely among the factors underpinning this \par weaker dynamic. \par  \par Since the transition period ended, the Northern Ireland Protocol has also \par remained a source of tension between the U.K. and the EU. To preserve the 1998 \par Good Friday Agreement and ensure no hard border or additional checks on the \par island of Ireland, Northern Ireland remained within the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  's single market for \par goods, under the Protocol, consequently creating a de facto customs border in \par the Irish Sea. This led to additional requirements and checks on goods being \par shipped from elsewhere in the U.K. It also raised concerns in the business \par community, as well as opposition from Northern Ireland's Democratic Unionist \par Party. Northern Ireland's first minister resigned in February 2022 in protest \par against the continued implementation of the protocol. The U.K. is requesting a \par comprehensive renegotiation of the protocol and has threatened to unilaterally \par suspend parts of the agreement otherwise. This could lead the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   to retaliate \par and impose restrictions. \par  \par Despite ongoing Brexit-related uncertainties, we view the U.K.'s institutional \par arrangements as a key sovereign rating support. The U.K.'s political system is \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 22 Apr 2022 16:29 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms United Kingdom -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par characterized by a system of checks and balances between government bodies, \par free press, open public debate, and the effective functioning of key economic \par institutions, including the Treasury and the BoE. \par  \par In May 2021, the U.K. held local elections that delivered a strong result for \par the Scottish National Party (SNP), falling just short of a majority in \f28 \uc2 \f2 the \par Scottish Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Subsequently, the SNP and the Scottish Greens entered a \par new power-sharing arrangement. The agreement between the two parties contains \par a commitment to hold another referendum on Scottish independence within the \par next five years, and preferably within the first half of the new Scottish \par Parliament's term. Although prime minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boris Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2   has said he will not \par allow such a referendum, we expect uncertainties to remain elevated and shaped \par by prevailing public opinion on the subject in Scotland. Over the last two \par years, support for independence has oscillated around 45%-55%, according to \par opinion polls. \par  \par Flexibility and Performance profile: The upswing in inflation is driving a \par rise in government interest spending, but the overall fiscal picture is \par improving \par      -- We expect inflation will average 6.3% this year, the highest in 30 \par years. \par      -- Elevated inflation is contributing to higher recorded government \par interest expenditure as principal is adjusted upward for retail-price-index \par (RPI) linked gilts. Nevertheless, overall fiscal performance is on an \par improving trend and we expect general government deficits to reduce to 2.5% of \par GDP by 2024. \par      -- The \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2   has hiked rates three times since December 2021 and we expect \par it will succeed in bringing inflation back to the 2% target by the second half \par of 2023. \par  \par The U.K.'s fiscal performance slightly exceeded our expectations in 2021. We \par estimate the general government deficit was 8.2% of GDP last year, compared to \par the 12.9% of GDP deficit recorded in 2020. Revenue performance has been strong \par with central government tax receipts up almost 15% year-on-year across a broad \par range of categories. Income and capital gains taxes (+16.5%), corporate taxes \par (+21%), and so-called self-assessed income taxes (+35%) all performed strongly \par and notably exceeded pre-pandemic levels, following the drop in 2020. \par  \par In parallel, spending remained almost flat in nominal terms in 2021 as the \par government withdrew previous pandemic-related support schemes. \par  \par Stronger budgetary performance provided the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.K. government\f28 \uc2 \f2   with some \par additional headroom for ad-hoc policy measures to partially soften the impact \par of the aforementioned cost-of-living crisis. To this end, in February 2022 the \par chancellor unveiled a GBP9 billion (0.4% of GDP) package to cushion the \par consequences for households of increased energy prices, which rose in April \par after the energy sector regulator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ofgem\f28 \uc2 \f2   increased the price cap by 54%. \par Further support measures worth an additional GBP9 billion (as estimated by the \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 OBR\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) were subsequently introduced by the government in the Spring Statement. \par The main policy initiatives are a temporary cut in the fuel duty and an \par increase in the threshold from which national insurance contributions are to \par be paid. \par  \par We forecast that the U.K. will register a further improvement in budgetary \par performance this year with the headline deficit falling to 5% of GDP. Deficits \par should keep falling toward 2.5% of GDP by 2024-2025. Nevertheless, we consider \par that fiscal performance could turn out weaker, with the government potentially \par being under pressure to do more to offset the decline in disposable incomes, \par particularly with a general election scheduled for 2024. \par  \par In our view, even with continuously moderating deficits and net general \par government debt returning to a downward trend, the fiscal impact of the \par pandemic will take years to undo. We now expect that net general government \par debt will decline to 92% of GDP by 2025 from 96% last year, but still notably \par higher than the pre-pandemic 80%. Despite having the longest debt maturity \par profile of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  , we note the high sensitivity of U.K. public finances to \par inflation and changes in interest rates. We project that in 2022 interest \par spending will rise to 8.0% of revenues from 6.8% in 2021 (4.8% in 2020), \par mostly as a result of elevated inflation feeding into upward adjustments of \par principal on RPI-linked gilts, which amount to around 25% of government debt \par stock. \par  \par As inflation comes down closer to target, we expect the government's nominal \par accrued interest expenditure will fall again, even with recent and forthcoming \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2   rate hikes factored in. After these hikes, we expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \par continue with its gradual approach of policy normalization in the medium term. \par  \par One of the U.K. economy's most significant macroeconomic features is its \par current account deficit. At an annual average of close to $90 billion (3% of \par GDP) over the last five years, it is the second largest in the world in \par absolute terms, after that of the U.S. Since peaking at 5.3% of GDP in 2016, \par the U.K.'s current account deficit has been narrowing, partly on the back of a \par narrowing income deficit, reflecting lower global interest rates. Given the \par size and cross-border exposures of the U.K.'s large and profitable financial \par sector, the economy's gross external financing needs remain elevated. We \par estimate that, for 2022, short-term external debt by remaining maturity totals \par $10.5 trillion (3x the U.K.'s GDP), dwarfing nearly all other major economies \par except the U.S. \par  \par Nevertheless, we do not expect the U.K. to face problems rolling over this \par elevated stock of short-term external debt. In the past, nonresidents have \par been consistently willing to fund the current account deficits and the foreign \par debt falling due. The sterling has also maintained its reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par status. According to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  , as of end-2021, claims in sterling constituted \par 4.5% of the total global allocation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. \par  \par The U.K. banking system fared well in the pandemic, and banks are now looking \par to put the pandemic behind them. 2021 annual results for the largest banks \par were exceptional, in our view, flattered by provision releases that will not \par be repeated. That said, despite a weakening economic outlook, 2022 should be a \par solid year for U.K. banks. Interest income is turning a corner and should grow \par in 2022 as interest rates continue to climb amid an inflationary environment. \par Also, impairment charges for the year are likely to be better than before the \par pandemic, costs are likely to grow in a controlled way, with efficiency gains \par partly mitigating rising inflation and technology investments, and the U.K.'s \par medium-term economic picture is generally stable (see "Banking Industry \par Country Risk Assessment: United Kingdom," published Dec. 9, 2021). \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Guarantee Criteria, Oct. 21, 2016 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, April 11, 2022; a free interactive version \par is available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, April 7, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, April 5, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, April 5, 2022 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 12, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and expl