\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine - Russia war, live | Latest news from Mariupol\b0\par\par\uc2 6,251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The key points of the day\par \par \uc2 - Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  claims that Russia has started "the great battle" for Donbass.\par \par \uc2 - Pedro Sanchez will travel to Kyiv in the coming days and will meet with Zelenski\par \par \uc2 -The Ukrainian army in Mariupol asks the Pope for help to evacuate civilians.\par \par \uc2 - A total of 2,072 civilians, 169 of them children, have died since February 24 due to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 - Ukraine has already filled out the form to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - A Russian town near the Ukrainian border has been attacked by Ukrainian forces, according to the governor of Belgorod.\par \par \uc2 - Russia gives another ultimatum to Ukrainian forces in Mariupol to surrender.\par \par \uc2 14:12 Brussels working on sanctions on Russian oil as part of its sixth package against MoscowThe\par \par \uc2 European Commission\uc2  explained Tuesday that it is working on an embargo on Russian oil imports as part of the \uc2 EU\uc2 's sixth package of sanctions against Russia and in response to the announcement of Moscow's offensive in Donbas. 14:06 Ukraine says it has so far located 420 bodies of civilians in Bucha\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian authorities said Tuesday that they have already located a total of 420 bodies of citizens of Bucha, a locality in Kyiv region where Russian troops perpetrated a massacre of civilians before withdrawing, according to the Ukrainian government. "The police in Kyiv region continue to work. As of Saturday night, more than 420 bodies were found, only in Bucha," regional police chief Andriy Nebytov has said, his press service reports on its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page. 13:59 China says it will "strengthen" its "coordination" with Russia The Chinese government has said it will "strengthen" its "strategic coordination" with Russia regardless of "the international situation", with a view to "safeguarding the common interests of both sides". Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister \uc2 Le Yucheng\uc2  met with Russian Ambassador to China Andrey Denisov on Monday, according to a \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry\uc2  statement released Monday, which specified that the aim was to "exchange views on Beijing-Moscow relations and the international and regional situation."\par \par \uc2 13:51 Russia affirms that the Army has started "the next phase" of the "special \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " against Ukraine Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergei Lavrov\uc2  affirmed on Tuesday that the Russian Army has started "the next phase" of the "\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " launched on February 24 against Ukraine, before stressing that the objective is "the total liberation of the Donetsk and Luhansk republics". Lavrov further pointed out that the war "is rooted in the desire of the United States and the West to lead the world." "They want to show the world that there will be no multipolarity and created a launching pad against us on our border," he has argued, referring to Ukraine. "They sent weapons to Ukraine," he has trenched.\par \par \uc2 13:43 About 70,000 people remain trapped in Luhansk, local authorities say\par \par \uc2 The head of the Luhansk regional military administration, \uc2 Serhii Haidai\uc2 , claims that about 70,000 people remain trapped in the eastern Ukrainian region, where the major Russian offensive has begun, President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  confirmed on Monday. Before the conflict, which started on February 24, the region had about 350,000 inhabitants. 13:35 Morawiecki calls from Lviv to intensify aid to Ukraine Polish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki visited the Ukrainian city of Lviv on Tuesday, where he urged the international community to "put calculators aside" and intensify its aid to Ukraine. Morawiecki warned that the evidence of Russian "barbarism" in Ukraine is also "a call to action" for Western governments, from which Poland has been calling for stronger measures against Russia in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 13:28 \uc2 WHO\uc2  brings attacks on health facilities in Ukraine to 147\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 World Health Organization (WHO)\uc2 , \uc2 Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus\uc2 , on Tuesday condemned attacks in Ukraine on health facilities in the country. "\uc2 WHO\uc2  strongly condemns the increase in attacks on health facilities in Ukraine. 13:18 Poland announces it has capacity to receive and treat some 10,000 wounded Ukrainian soldiers Poland's health service has the capacity to treat at least 10,000 wounded Ukrainian soldiers, the Polish prime minister said Tuesday. His statements coincide with Russia's new offensive in eastern Ukraine. Mateusz Morawiecki told reporters during a visit to the western Ukrainian city of Lviv that Poland was already treating "several dozen" soldiers and was ready to receive more. "We are ready to receive at least 10,000 [soldiers] if necessary,"\par \par \uc2 13:09 Ukraine refugee figure now touches five million Nearly five million people have been forced to leave Ukraine since Russia launched its invasion on February 24, according to estimates by the \uc2 UN High Commissioner for Refugees\uc2  (\uc2 UNHCR\uc2 ), which come to confirm the worst forecasts put forward in the early stages of the conflict. More than 2.8 million refugees have already arrived in Poland alone, although in recent days local authorities have recorded a temporary reversal in the migratory flow, with more people returning to Ukraine than leaving, which could be due to temporary visits by Ukrainians to their country of origin.\par \par \uc2 13:00 Sanctions on Russia reopen the debate on the role of the dollar in non-Western countries, reports La Vanguardia journalist Andy Robinson. \uc2 NATO\uc2  ruled out the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of Russia's invasion, which began on February 24. Excluding the option of fighting against a nuclear power, the re-founded transatlantic alliance opted for a plan B: the so-called weaponization of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 12:49 Bulgaria is highlighting the value of its aid to Ukraine in the war with Russia. Bulgaria's Foreign Minister Teodora Genchovska has positively assessed the role her country is playing in the Ukrainian war after the meeting with her Ukrainian counterpart \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2 . "As a small country and according to our possibilities we try to help the Ukrainian people as much as possible," said the minister, who recalled the humanitarian aid, which since the beginning of the invasion amounts to 1.5 million euros, in addition to another 185,000 euros channeled through various NGOs. In addition, more than 91,000 Ukrainians have so far arrived in Bulgaria, the poorest country in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU), according to Genchovska.\par \par \uc2 12:41 Russia does not deserve sympathy and understanding, according to Kuleba. Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  said today from Bulgaria that he is "aware" of the pro-Russian sentiments of a part of the Ukrainian society, but stressed that today's Russia "destroys, murders, tortures and rapes" and therefore deserves neither sympathy nor understanding. "I am aware of Russia's role in the history of Bulgaria, as well as in the history of Ukraine," Kuleba told a press conference after meeting with his Bulgarian counterpart, Teodora Genchovska. "Historically Russia did some things that continue to live in the feelings of the peoples of these two countries," but today's Russia is a "different" one, he added.\par \par \uc2 12:34 Poland can take in 10,000 Ukrainian soldiers wounded by the war. The health system of Poland, a country bordering Ukraine and the main destination of people who decide to leave the former Soviet republic because of the war unleashed by Russia, has the capacity to care for at least 10,000 wounded Ukrainian soldiers, Polish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki said on Tuesday. The Polish leader assured reporters during a visit to the western Ukrainian city of Lviv that Poland is already treating "several dozen" soldiers and is ready to take in more. "We are ready to take in at least 10,000, if necessary [...] We are doing everything possible to take in and treat all wounded soldiers from Ukraine," he detailed.\par \par \uc2 12:27 Russia rules out using nuclear weapons in the war with Ukraine. Moscow has assured on Tuesday that it will only use conventional weapons in the conflict that has been going on in Ukraine since February 24. This was confirmed by the Russian Foreign Minister, \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2 , in response to a question about the possible use of nuclear weapons in the war in an interview granted to India Today. This is one of the strongest comments made by the head of Russian diplomacy on this issue. However, it should be kept in mind that Lavrov is not the ultimate military decision-maker.\par \par \uc2 12:20 Killing with God's forgiveness, chronicle of the special envoy of La Vanguardia in Kyiv, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s. Kyiv and Moscow. In both cities, capitals of Ukraine and Russia respectively, the faith is strained and the commandments are distorted in the midst of war. On the one hand, Father Lorenzo Zhivchik acknowledged in St. Michael's Cathedral, seat of the Ukrainian Patriarchate, that "killing is not a sin when we kill to defend the homeland, to protect ourselves from evil". In parallel, Kirill I, the Patriarch of the Russian Orthodox Church, thinks the same from the dark side of faith. In his case he looks favorably on Putin's war and has not condemned the death of civilians in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 12:13 Russian troops will take control of the Azovstal steelworks in Mariupol, according to a Chechen leader. \uc2 Ramzan Kadyrov\uc2 , leader of the Russian region of Chechnya and ally of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , has assured that Russian troops will take control of the Azovstal plant in the port of Mariupol on Tuesday. It should be recalled that a group of Ukrainian defenders is holding out at this facility. Russia has given an ultimatum to these fighters, also foreigners, to surrender and hand over their weapons before noon, guaranteeing them that they will be allowed to stay alive. "Today, with the help of the Almighty, we will take Azovstal completely," Kadyrov has said in an audio message on his Telegram channel.\par \par \uc2 12:05 Russia accuses Western countries of wanting to prolong the war in Ukraine. The United States and other Western countries are doing everything possible to prolong the "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine, according to the Kremlin. This was assured on Tuesday by Russian Defense Minister \uc2 Sergei Shoigu\uc2  in statements quoted by the Russian news agency TASS. "The growing volume of foreign arms supplies clearly demonstrates their intentions to provoke the Kiev regime to fight to the end in Ukraine," Shoigu said, according to the same source.\par \par \uc2 11:56 Access to the \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  (\uc2 HRW\uc2 ) portal in Russia restricted. Russia's communications regulator has restricted access to the website of the New York-based human rights group \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  (\uc2 HRW\uc2 ), state news agency TASS has detailed. The move is related to \uc2 HRW\uc2 's publication of what \uc2 Roskomnadzor\uc2 , the watchdog, considered false information about the behavior of the Russian military in Ukraine, TASS has reported.\par \par \uc2 11:49 Lavrov confirms that Russia has started the next phase of the military campaign in Ukraine. Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  confirmed on Tuesday that they have already started the next phase of the country's military campaign on Ukrainian territory, which began last February 24. This campaign is focused on the "complete liberation" of the Donetsk and Luhansk regions. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine is aimed, as already announced, at the complete liberation of the Donetsk and Luhansk republics. This \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue, the next phase of this special \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is beginning," he has detailed in statements to the India Today channel and broadcast by the Foreign Ministry.\par \par \uc2 11:42 Sanchez will travel to Kyiv to meet with Zelenski and reopen the Spanish embassy, reports the journalist of the Politics section of La Vanguardia, Juan Carlos Merino. The Moncloa has confirmed to La Vanguardia that the President of the Government will travel to Kyiv "very soon" to attend the reopening of the Spanish embassy in the Ukrainian capital and to meet with President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 , among other meetings that are planned. No exact date of the trip has been detailed. "No details can be given for security reasons," government sources justify.\par \par \uc2 11:34 Russia will denounce the freezing of its international reserves due to the war in Ukraine. Western countries froze Russia's international reserves in response to the invasion of Ukraine, launched last February 24. Moscow has expressed its opinion on this issue, which considers this measure to be unprecedented and will therefore denounce it in court. This has been assured by the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  (BCR), \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 . "This is an unprecedented freezing of gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, we will prepare lawsuits, we are preparing to file them. Because it is unprecedented that, on a global scale, the gold and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of such a large country are frozen," she has maintained at a meeting in the Duma. Nabiullina has pointed out that, due to this measure, the Russian monetary entity has had to introduce \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. "If we had gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, there would be no need for such draconian measures for capital movement," she has insisted.\par \par \uc2 11:26 Russian gas flow to Europe via Ukraine falls. Russian gas deliveries to Europe via Ukraine have dropped Tuesday morning, in line with customer requests, while flows to Germany via Nord Stream 1 have remained stable and increased eastward to Poland from Germany via the Yamal pipeline between Russia and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Daily nominations of Russian gas deliveries to Slovakia via Ukraine have fallen on Tuesday, according to data from Slovak operator TSO Eustream. Nominations through the Velke Kapusany border point stood at around 398,774 megawatt hours (MWh) per day on Tuesday, down from 420,099 MWh per day on Monday, the data showed.\par \par \uc2 11:14 ???? Sanchez will travel to Ukraine and meet with Zelenski. The President of the Government, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, will travel to Kyiv in the coming days and will meet with his Ukrainian counterpart, \uc2 Volod\u237\'00\'EDmir Zelenski\uc2 , and the Prime Minister of this country. The meeting between the two leaders will take place at a time when Spain is considering, like other European countries, reopening its embassy in the Ukrainian capital, which was closed following the escalation of violence in the war and after evacuating the Spanish residents in that country who requested them. The decision to reopen the Spanish embassy in the capital of the former Soviet republic corresponds to the "commitment" of the Government with the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 11:10 Russia starts the battle for Donbass in eastern Ukraine. Russian forces are attempting Tuesday to break through Ukrainian defenses along almost the entire front line in the east of the former Soviet republic, beginning what Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  has dubbed the "battle for the Donbass." "A very large part of the entire Russian army is now concentrated in this offensive," the Ukrainian leader said in a video released early this morning. "No matter how many Russian troops they send there, we will fight. We will defend ourselves," he added. On the other hand, the chief of staff of the Ukrainian president, \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2 , has assured citizens that his forces will be able to contain the offensive in "the second phase of the war". "Believe in our army, it is very strong," he said.\par \par \uc2 11:00 Sanctions on Russia reopen the debate on the role of the dollar in non-Western countries, reports La Vanguardia journalist Andy Robinson. \uc2 NATO\uc2  ruled out the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of Russia's invasion, which began on February 24. Excluding the option of fighting a nuclear power, the re-founded transatlantic alliance opted for a plan B: the so-called weaponization -transformation into a weapon- of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 10:52 \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  suspends production in Russia as a result of the war in Ukraine. Automaker Stellantis on Tuesday announced the suspension of production at its Russian plant due to logistical difficulties and sanctions imposed on Moscow. The company has a van manufacturing plant in Kaluga, some 201 kilometers southeast of Moscow, in co-ownership with Japanese automaker \uc2 Mitsubishi\uc2 . This plant already halted production earlier this month as a result of the conflict between Russia and Ukraine. "Given the rapid daily increase in cross-sanctions and logistical difficulties, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has suspended its manufacturing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kaluga to ensure full compliance with all cross-sanctions and to protect its employees," \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said in a statement. The world's fourth-largest automaker, which produces and sells the \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , Citroen, \uc2 Opel\uc2 , Jeep and \uc2 Fiat\uc2  brands in Russia, has only 1% of the country's car market. 10:44 Putin decorates the soldiers Ukraine accuses of the Bucha massacres, reports La Vanguardia's correspondent in Moscow, Gonzalo Aragon\u233\'00\'E9s. The massacre in Bucha, a town near Kyiv where a large number of civilian corpses were found with signs of having been executed, is again in the news. This past Monday the President of Russia, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , has decorated the unit of the Russian army that Ukraine points out as the perpetrator of the massacre. In a decree signed on Monday, the head of the Kremlin awarded the honorary name of "Guards" to the 64th Motorized Infantry Brigade. "For their enormous heroism and bravery, the endurance and courage shown by the brigade's personnel in protecting the Fatherland and the interests of the State in armed conflicts, I decide: to award the 64th Motorized Infantry Brigade with the honorary title of 'Guards'", the text details.\par \par \uc2 10:37 Russia gives another ultimatum to Ukrainian forces in Mariupol to surrender. Moscow has given another ultimatum to Ukrainian forces still holding out in the port city of Mariupol, heavily besieged since the first days of the war in Ukraine. These troops are holding out mainly at the Azovstal steelworks in the town's port. Russia is giving them until noon to lay down their arms in exchange for saving their lives. "Taking into account the catastrophic situation that has developed at the Azovstal metallurgical plant, as well as being guided by purely humane principles, the Russian Armed Forces once again offer militants of nationalist battalions and foreign mercenaries to stop hostilities and lay down their arms as of 12:00 (09.00 GMT)," the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  said. "All those who lay down their arms are guaranteed the preservation of life," said the head of the Russian Federation's National Defense Control Center, Colonel General \uc2 Mikhail Mizintsev\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 10:27 Ukrainian forces are holding out in Mariupol, one of Russia's targets in the war in Ukraine. The port city of Mariupol, besieged by the Russian army since the first days of the invasion, has not yet fallen under full control of Moscow despite heavy shelling. This has been assured by the deputy mayor of the town, Petro Andriushchenko, through his Telegram account. "The occupation troops not only drop bombs on the Azovstal plant (which is still defended by Ukrainian soldiers), but chaotically shell the city's residential quarters, where they only recently allowed civilians to return," the municipal aide noted.\par \par \uc2 10:19 Kyiv and other major Ukrainian cities come under fire again for the third day in a row . The Ukrainian capital and its metropolitan area continue to be targeted by Russia in the Ukrainian war. Likewise, Kharkiv, Dnipro and Mikolaiv, near Odessa, have also been targeted by Russian missiles in the last hours, reports La Vanguardia's special envoy in the former Soviet republic, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s. In these cities the Ukrainian defenses manage to keep the invading forces at bay, but the cost for the civilian population is higher every day, as many have been without water and electricity for weeks and with hardly any food.\par \par \uc2 10:12 Major cities suffer more Russian attacks in the last hours of war in Ukraine. In the last hours, several Ukrainian cities have been victims of attacks and shelling by Russian troops. One of them is Kreminna, in the Luhansk region, where four people have been killed and another injured when Russian soldiers opened fire on their vehicle. These people were trying to leave the village. The advance in Kreminna brings the Russians closer to the city of Sloviansk, which is considered a key target in Putin's offensive and which was seized by pro-Russian fighters in 2014, reports La Vanguardia's special envoy in the country, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s. On the other hand, Russian troops have already seized Izium, which is located on a road north of Sloviansk, and are ready to advance into the city from the north and east.\par \par \uc2 10:04 Greece seizes a Russian oil tanker on the island of Evia over the war in Ukraine. Greece has seized a Russian oil tanker off the island of Evia as part of sanctions imposed on Moscow by the international community due to the war in Ukraine. This has been confirmed by an official of the Greek Ministry of Maritime Transport. The Russian-flagged Pegas, with 19 Russian crew members on board, has been seized near the coastal town of Karystos in Evia. "It has been seized as part of EU sanctions," this same public worker has said. A coast guard official has said the ship had been seized, but not its oil cargo.\par \par \uc2 09:53 Denmark will temporarily increase natural gas production because of the war in Ukraine. Denmark's government has assured Tuesday that it will temporarily increase natural gas production from its North Sea fields, but planned to phase out natural gas in the long term to become independent of Russian supplies. "We will increase natural gas production in the North Sea for a limited period of time," Prime Minister \uc2 Mette Frederiksen\uc2  told reporters during a briefing on Tuesday. "We are convinced that it is better to produce gas in the North Sea than to buy it from (Russian President) \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ," Frederiksen has said.\par \par \uc2 09:43 Pro-Russian forces try to take control of Azovstal. Moscow-backed separatist forces are trying to seize control of the Azovstal metallurgical plant in the port city of Mariupol, RIA news agency details quoting separatist spokesman \uc2 Eduard Basurin\uc2 . The aim of the pro-Russians in this locality is to "liberate" the facility as quickly as possible, RIA quoted another separatist, \uc2 Denis Pushilin\uc2 , as saying.\par \par \uc2 09:33 Russia on Tuesday denounced an attack in a village in the Belgorod region. Russian authorities have denounced in recent hours the attack on a small village in the Belgorod region, near the country's border with Ukraine, and caused one wounded. According to the governor of the region, Vyacheslav Gladkov, the attack has been carried out from the Ukrainian territory. "A local woman was wounded," the head of state said via Telegram, where he added that the incident also caused damage. The governor added that he will personally go to the site to assess the damage.\par \par \uc2 09:23 New Zealand imposes sanctions against 18 Russian banks and financial institutions. The New Zealand Executive announced Tuesday the imposition of new sanctions against 18 Russian banks and financial institutions as part of the response to the invasion of Ukraine ordered by Moscow and specifically by its president, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . "These large institutions represent approximately 80% of Russia's total banking assets," New Zealand's Foreign Minister \uc2 Nanaia Mahuta\uc2  detailed through a statement. The measures affect in total three Russian government financial institutions, as well as 15 banks, including those linked to the oligarchs, military and those responsible for the annexation of Crimea, the note has added. Among them are the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Russian Federation\uc2 , the Black Sea Development and Reconstruction Bank, the \uc2 Moscow Credit Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2 , the \uc2 Russian Direct Investment Fund\uc2 , the \uc2 Russian Agricultural Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and the Industrial Savings Bank.\par \par \uc2 09:10 No agreement on opening new humanitarian corridors today in Ukraine. Ukraine has failed to reach an agreement with Russia on opening new humanitarian corridors this Tuesday to evacuate civilian population from some of the localities most besieged by Russian troops' attacks, Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Iryna Vereshchuk\uc2  has confirmed. "Today, April 19, unfortunately there are no humanitarian corridors," the Ukrainian leader wrote via \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , from where she also assured that intensive shelling has continued in the Donbass region in eastern Ukraine. Efforts to create a safe passage for civilians to leave the besieged southern city of Mariupol have repeatedly failed, with each side blaming the other. Russia has denied targeting civilians.\par \par \uc2 09:00 UK does not want to exchange Medvedchuk for two British fighters. Britain will not seek to help Russia, a senior minister has disclosed when asked about the possibility of exchanging pro-Russian politician detained by Ukrainian authorities \uc2 Viktor Medvedchuk\uc2  for two British fighters who were captured in Ukraine by Russian forces. The Britons appeared Monday on Russian state television and asked to be exchanged for Medvedchuk, a Ukrainian ally of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  who is being held by Ukrainian authorities.\par \par \uc2 08:50 France assures that it is working on an embargo on Russian oil at the European level. French Finance Minister \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2  assured on Tuesday that an embargo on Russian oil is being worked on at the \uc2 European Union\uc2  level and added that French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , looks favorably on such a measure. "I hope that in the coming weeks we will convince our European partners to stop importing Russian oil," Le Maire said in statements to the radio station Europe 1.\par \par \uc2 08:41 More Russian attacks on Ukrainian cities. Ukraine suffered this past Monday one of the worst air strikes since the war started last February 24. The main cities of the country, such as Kharkiv and Lviv, in geographically opposite places, were targeted by Russian shells. As reported by the special envoy of La Vanguardia in Kyiv, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s, a rain of missiles left dozens of civilian victims. Seven people died in the town of Lviv, in the west and a few kilometers from the border with the EU, and another two in Kharkiv, close to the border with Russia. The shelling began at dawn and continued throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 08:30 Street battles in Kreminna, according to a Ukrainian military official. A Ukrainian military official has assured in the last hours that street battles have started and that evacuation from the town of Kreminna is impossible. That is one of only two places where Ukrainians said the Russians have managed to break through Monday along a front that stretches for hundreds of miles. Luhansk regional military administrator \uc2 Serhiy Haidai\uc2  said the city was attacked with heavy artillery overnight, setting fire to seven residential buildings, and that the Olympus sports complex was also hit. Also, according to Haidai, the Russians have taken control of the city after "razing everything to the ground," so his men have withdrawn to regroup and continue fighting. "It just doesn't make sense to stand in one place, die for everyone, without causing significant damage to the enemy," he has detailed.\par \par \uc2 08:19 U.S. warns that Russia is sending reinforcements in the Donbass. The Pentagon has warned in the last hours that Russia is sending reinforcements of material and soldiers to the Donbass region to launch a major offensive to take control of this area of eastern Ukraine. The Defense Department spokesman, John Kirby, has assured in a press conference that there is an influx of artillery equipment, helicopter support and elements to facilitate Russian command and control \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Donbass. "We believe they have reinforced the number of their battalion tactical groups in eastern and southern" Ukraine, Kirby said.\par \par \uc2 08:12 Russia decorates the military unit that Ukraine holds responsible for the Bucha massacre. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has condemned the military unit that Kyiv blames for the massacre in Bucha, a town near the capital where a large number of civilian victims were found with signs of having been executed. Putin has justified this decoration for "heroism and courage of the masses", but without mentioning Russia's war in Ukraine. The decree signed by Putin has awarded the 64th Motorized Infantry Brigade with the honorary title of Guards, which was identified by the Ukrainian authorities as the perpetrator of alleged war crimes.\par \par \uc2 08:00 Russia destroys weapons storage near Lviv. Russian troops continue to attack key points in western Ukraine, despite their intentions to concentrate on the Donbass area in the east of the former Soviet republic. In recent hours, the Russian military has claimed to have destroyed a facility in the Lviv region that contained weapons shipped to Ukraine from the United States and other countries, according to Interfax. Pentagon spokesman John Kirby assured a few hours ago in statements to the media that he could not confirm such an attack.\par \par \uc2 07:52 Spain plans to reopen its embassy in Kyiv soon. The war between Russia and Ukraine continues at a delicate moment, when the Ukrainian authorities consider that Moscow has started the definitive offensive on Donbass to take control of the area. However, several European countries are considering reopening their embassies in Kyiv, including Spain. This was stated by the President of the Government, Pedro Sanchez, in declarations to Antena 3. It should be noted that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  reopened its mission in the Ukrainian capital just over a week ago after Russian troops withdrew from the region, although the capital has been attacked again in recent days.\par \par \uc2 07:44 U. S. wants to send back diplomats in Ukraine. Washington has raised its desire to send back US diplomats in Ukraine as soon as possible, although it has not yet set a specific date. This was detailed by \uc2 US State Department\uc2  spokesman \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 . "When the security situation permits, and not a second later, I can assure you that we will have a restored diplomatic presence on the ground in Ukraine," Price said.\par \par \uc2 07:32 EU plans solidarity fund for Ukraine reconstruction. The \uc2 EU\uc2  bloc is looking for ways to pay for the reconstruction of Ukraine after the war with Russia. The EU-27 plan to set up a solidarity trust fund to finance Ukraine's reconstruction, as member states were told they should pay the lion's share of the costs. The issues of post-war reconstruction and possible reparations are expected to focus talks in the coming weeks, as senior officials from Brussels and Washington plan to discuss ways to financially support Kyiv and ensure that the huge amount of money is not wasted.\par \par \uc2 07:20 Killing with God's forgiveness, chronicled by La Vanguardia's special envoy in Kyiv, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s. Kyiv and Moscow. In both cities, capitals of Ukraine and Russia respectively, the faith is strained and the commandments are distorted in the middle of a war. On the one hand, Father Lorenzo Zhivchik acknowledged in St. Michael's Cathedral, seat of the Ukrainian Patriarchate, that "killing is not a sin when we kill to defend the homeland, to protect ourselves from evil". In parallel, Kirill I, the Patriarch of the Russian Orthodox Church, thinks the same from the dark side of faith. In his case he looks favorably on Putin's war and has not condemned the killing of civilians in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 07:11 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  plans to meet with Ukraine's Prime Minister Denys Shmyhal. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  contemplates holding a meeting with Ukrainian Prime Minister Denys Shmyhal during this week's major meetings of world economic leaders in Washington, but she will try to avoid most contact with theRussian officials who will attend telematically. Russia's invasion of Ukraine, and how world powers should handle spillover effects on economies, including food insecurity, will take center stage at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  spring meetings, which run through Friday and will be both face-to-face and virtual. Yellen is expected to use this week's meetings to work with allies on efforts to increase economic pressure on Russia and mitigate spillovers, call for the implementation of a global minimum tax agreement and address food security issues.\par \par \uc2 07:00 Kyiv tries to protect its monuments from the ravages of Russian invasion. Ukraine's cultural heritage is being damaged because of attacks by Russian troops. Even the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's High Representative for Foreign Affairs, \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , has assured that the Russian troops are doing it "on purpose". Faced with this situation, and in order to protect their monuments from Russian shelling and fire, the Ukrainian authorities have chosen to cover them with sandbags.\par \par \uc2 06:52 EU accuses Russia of purposefully destroying Ukraine's cultural heritage. The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's High Representative for Foreign Affairs, \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , has recently warned that Russia is "purposefully" destroying cultural heritage in Ukraine during its invasion. "Objects of cultural heritage are destroyed on purpose in Russia's war against Ukraine," the leader of European diplomacy has pointed out through his official \uc2 Twitter\uc2  profile, in a message in which he has also stressed that it is "an important part of European heritage." For all these reasons, he assured that the \uc2 EU\uc2  "supports artists, cultural professionals and cultural organizations affected by the war".\par \par \uc2 06:45 Russian offensive in the Donbass, chronicle of the special envoy of La Vanguardia in Kyiv, Xavier Mas de Xax\u224\'00\'E0s. The Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volod\u237\'00\'EDmir Zelenski\uc2 , informed last night that Russia "has begun the great battle for the Donbass", announcing a new turn in the evolution of the war in the heart of the former Soviet republic. Zelensky said so in a video message broadcast on the Ukrinform news channel, in which he warned that the Ukrainian soldiers "will fight" and "will not give up" any territory in this great offensive of the Russian army to control the entire east of the country.\par \par \uc2 06:37 Ukraine attacks a Russian locality near the border. Ukrainian forces have attacked the Russian locality of Golovchino, close to the border between the two countries, resulting in one person wounded. This was reported by the governor of the Russian province of Belgorod, Vyacheslav Gladkov, via Telegram. At first it was not clear whether this attack was carried out with artillery, mortars, missiles or whether it was an air strike. If this information is confirmed, it would be a new Ukrainian attack on Russian soil, after the one by helicopters against a fuel depot in Belgorod.\par \par \uc2 06:28 Ukrainian army in Mariupol asks the Pope for help to evacuate civilians. The port city of Mariupol continues to represent Ukrainian resistance to the Russian invasion. However, the situation is becoming increasingly complex and more and more voices are calling for new humanitarian corridors to evacuate civilians. In the last hours, Ukrainian troops have even asked the Pope for help to evacuate civilians. The commander of the 36th Ukrainian Marine Brigade, Sergiy Volyna, has written a letter to the Pontiff asking him to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to save the exhausted civilian population of the city", as detailed by the Ukrainian ambassador to the Holy See, Andrii Yurash, on his social networks.\par \par \uc2 06:18 Zelensky claims that Russia has started "the great battle" for Donbass. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  reported last night that Russia has already started "the great battle for Donbass," referring to the major offensive to control eastern Ukraine. "We can confirm that Russian troops have started that battle," the Ukrainian leader assured in a video message broadcast by Ukrinform. Zelensky then added that Ukrainian soldiers "will battle" and "will not give up" any of the country's territory. "No matter how many Russian troops are deployed: we will fight," he stressed.\par \par \uc2 06:10 A total of 2,072 civilians killed, 169 of them children, in war in Ukraine. The \uc2 UN\uc2  has confirmed in the last hours the death of 2,072 civilians, including 169 of them children, since the beginning of the war in Ukraine. The civilian victims who have been injured number 2,818, according to the data that the \uc2 UN\uc2  agency has been able to corroborate. However, according to data published Monday by the Ukrainian Prosecutor General's office on its Telegram account, the number of children killed in Ukraine since the Russians invaded the country has reached 205.\par \par \uc2 06:00 Ukraine urges Russia to open a humanitarian corridor from Mariupol. Ukrainian authorities have urged Russia to open a safe humanitarian corridor with Mariupol, where an estimated 100,000 civilians remain, in addition to Ukrainian troops holding out in the besieged port city. "The situation in Mariupol has worsened. I call on the military-political leaders of the Russian Federation to open a humanitarian corridor from Mariupol to Berdyansk," Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Iryna Vereshchuk\uc2  has called on.\par \par \uc2 05:52 White House insists "no plans" for Biden's trip to Ukraine. U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has no upcoming trip to Ukraine on his agenda to travel to Ukraine, the White House has insisted in recent hours. This follows a request by Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  for the Democratic leader to travel to the former Soviet republic to see first-hand the damage of the Russian invasion. "There are no plans for President Biden to travel to Ukraine," White House spokeswoman \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  insisted at a press conference.\par \par \uc2 05:45 Russia-Ukraine war | Latest news on the conflict and Putin, live. Good morning and welcome to the minute-by-minute coverage of everything related to the Russia-Ukraine war. Kyiv has already filled in the form to be recognized as an applicant country to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , according to the Ukrinform portal, which cites sources close to the country's president, \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2 . This followed the steps and deadlines set by the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , who recalls the recent visit of the German politician to the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220419ei4j009oq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
