\par\par \b \uc2 GRAPHIC-The game is afoot: central banks ramp up inflation fight\b0\par\par\uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par 15:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 14 (Reuters) - Central banks are racing to get on top of surging inflation, with New Zealand and Canada delivering aggressive half-point rate hikes this week and the \uc2 ECB\uc2  on Thursday sticking with plans to dial back stimulus this year. But policymakers must also, while watching inflation, guard against the fallout from the war in Ukraine. The hit to consumer confidence from higher energy prices and disrupted supply chains might well darken the economic outlook, constraining monetary tightening. Here is a look at where policymakers stand on the path out of the pandemic-era stimulus, ranked in terms of hawkishness.\par \par \uc2 1) NORWAY\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  hiked rates by 25 basis points to 0.75% on March 24 and announced plans to hike at a faster pace than previously planned.\par \par \uc2 Including the March move, the bank now plans eight hikes to bring rates to 2.50% by end-2023, three more than projected in March, and two more than anticipated in March by a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 2) NEW ZEALAND\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  on Wednesday reinforced its position as one of the world's most hawkish central banks.\par \par \uc2 It raised the cash rate by 50 bps to 1.5%, the biggest rise in two decades and the fourth hike in the current cycle, and maintained its forecast for rates to peak around 3.35% by end-2023.\par \par \uc2 3) CANADA\par \par \uc2 Having kicked off its rate-rise cycle in March, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  raised key rates on Wednesday by 50 bps to 1%, its biggest single move in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 From next week, the \uc2 BoC\uc2  will also let maturing bonds roll off its balance sheet. \uc2 BoC\uc2  Governor \uc2 Tiff Macklem\uc2  reckons rates are still far below neutral levels, estimated between 2%-3%. Markets expect rates will hit 2.5% by year-end.\par \par \uc2 4) BRITAIN\par \par \uc2 With inflation at a 30-year high, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is under pressure to tighten policy further after three hikes since December.\par \par \uc2 Markets are all but certain it will raise its benchmark by 25 bps to 1% on May 5, and then to 2%-2.25% by end-2022.\par \par \uc2 However, with soaring energy prices threatening growth, the \uc2 BoE\uc2  has softened its language on the need for further tightening .\par \par \uc2 5) UNITED STATES\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised rates by a quarter point in March to a 0.25%-0.5% range and has flagged a half-point move on May 4. It is also discussing trimming its asset portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Inflation, which hit a 40-year high at 8.5%, may be starting to peak, but is expected to remain above the Fed's 2% target at least through 2023.\par \par \uc2 6) AUSTRALIA\par \par \uc2 Markets' campaign to get the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  to pivot hawkish succeeded on April 5, when it finally opened the door for its 0.1% cash rate to rise this year.\par \par \uc2 Data later this month is expected to show annual inflation at 3.2%, while a survey published on April 12 showed robust business conditions with the jobless rate possibly dropping below 4% this year for the first time since the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 Futures price a 25 bps hike in June and imply a cumulative 200 bps of tightening by year-end.\par \par \uc2 7) EURO ZONE\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is among the most cautious of major central banks. On Thursday, it stuck to plans to slowly unwind extraordinary stimulus, weighing worries over record-high inflation against the risk that the war in Ukraine could drive the euro zone economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 Economists still expect the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to hike rates later this year and markets price around 60 bps of tightening by year-end.\par \par \uc2 8) SWEDEN\par \par \uc2 Sweden's 6.1% inflation, the highest since 1991, may leave the dovish \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  no choice but turn into a hawk.\par \par \uc2 The bank will have to rethink its policy approach at its next meeting on April 28, deputy governor Martin Floden said last week. Governor \uc2 Stefan Ingves\uc2 , whose vote in February was the decider in keeping balance sheet plans unchanged this year, has made similar comments recently.\par \par \uc2 Current \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  guidance still sees a 2024 hike; markets see rates rising from zero to 1% by November..\par \par \uc2 9) SWITZERLAND\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  remains in the dovish camp but with 2022 inflation projected at an above-target 2.1%, it is paying closer attention to prices.\par \par \uc2 Flight to safety sparked by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  conflict has forced the SNB to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boostin Swiss sight deposits by 8 billion Swiss francs ($8.58 billion) in the past three weeks.\par \par \uc2 10)JAPAN\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan\uc2  remains the holdout dove.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  has warned the recent inflation spike, driven by import costs, could hurt the economy, stressing the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's resolve to keep monetary policy ultra-loose.\par \par \uc2 With policy tightening a long way off, traders have ramped up bets on a weaker yen, driving it to two-decade lows against the dollar and eliciting a rare warning from its finance minister.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.9329 Swiss francs)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Sujata Rao, Saikat Chatterjee, Yoruk Bahceli and Dhara Ranasinghe; Editing by Tomasz Janowski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-14T14:30:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220414ei4e02gw3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s