\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine - Russia war, live today | Latest news on the bombings\b0\par\par\uc2 7,184 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The key points of the day\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief will go to Moscow to push for a truce in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 - Russia has declared its readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 - More than 4,200 civilians evacuated through humanitarian corridors on Saturday in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 15:11 Seven buses set to take some 500 civilians out of Mariupol this Sunday Seven buses will attempt to leave the besieged Ukrainian city of Mariupol this coming Sunday with some 500 civilians inside as the Red Cross tries again to enter the city with humanitarian aid after the failure of its first raid on Friday. The buses are part of a civilian initiative announced on Telegram and, if successful, will move residents to Zaporiyia, while some 100,000 people still await their turn in the siege-ravaged city.\par \par \uc2 15:02 Ukraine demands "devastating new sanctions" against Russia after denouncing Bucha "massacre" Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dimitro Kuleba on Sunday accused Russian forces of orchestrating a "massacre" in Bucha and called on the \uc2 G7\uc2  to issue a series of "devastating new sanctions" against Moscow, with immediate effect, and estimated "hundreds" dead as Russian troops passed through the recently liberated areas near Kiev. "The massacre of Bucha has been deliberate. The aim of the Russians is to eliminate as many Ukrainians as they can. We have to stop them and kick them out," he said via his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 14:52 Russia will only export food to friendly countries in rubles or in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Russia will only export food and crops to "friendly countries" in rubles or in their national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , RIA news agency has reported Sunday, citing \uc2 Dmitry Medvedev\uc2 , deputy secretary of \uc2 Russia's Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 14:43 \uc2 UN\uc2  brings to 1,325 civilians killed in war The United Nations Office for Human Rights has reported that at least 1,325 civilians have died since the start of the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Among these dead, 120 are children, as indicated by the \uc2 UN\uc2 , which has clarified that these are the verified victims but that the real figure may be much higher. As for civilians wounded in the conflict, the \uc2 UN\uc2  has confirmed 2,017, including 168 children. The actual number of casualties "could be considerably much higher" as there is only partial information from some of the areas of major hostilities, including localities besieged by Russian troops such as Mariupol, Volnovaja, Izium or Irpin.\par \par \uc2 14:34 Ukraine says Bucha "massacre" was deliberate and calls for sanctions Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  has accused Russia of carrying out a "massacre" in the town of Bucha outside Kiev and called on the \uc2 G7\uc2  to impose new sanctions on Moscow. "We are still gathering and searching for bodies, but the number has already exceeded a hundred. Dead bodies are in the streets. They killed civilians while staying there and when leaving these villages and towns," the minister has posted on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 14:25 Explosions heard in Russian town of Belgorod near Ukraine border Two explosions were heard in the Russian town of Belgorod near the Ukrainian border on Sunday, two witnesses told Reuters, days after Russian authorities accused Ukrainian forces of attacking a fuel depot there. The cause of the explosions was not immediately clear. One witness said the explosions were so powerful that they shook the windows of his house in Belgorod.\par \par \uc2 14:16 Russia fires missiles near Odessa as evacuation of Mariupol is reattempted Ukraine claims Sunday that missiles are falling near the port of Odessa in southern Ukraine. Russia claimed this morning that it had destroyed an oil refinery used by the Ukrainian army, while attempts to evacuate civilians from the devastated city of Mariupol were expected to continue. For its part, the Odessa city council has said that "critical infrastructure facilities" have been hit by missiles, although for the moment, no casualties have been reported.\par \par \uc2 14:07 Twelve Spaniards have joined the Foreign Legion fighting the Russians in Ukraine At least a dozen Spanish citizens are already in Ukraine, enrolled (or with the intention of enrolling) in the International Legion for Territorial Defense created by the Government of \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  against the Russian invasion, according to the figures handled by the Spanish security forces. Four are Catalans, eight from the rest of Spain. Their names and lives are known and they are and will be under the radar the day they return, because of the military baggage they will have acquired in the war. If they return.\par \par \uc2 13:58 Theater as a shelter during the war A Ukrainian theater company born in a World War II bomb shelter, keeps alive the art of drama in Kyiv, as an antidote against dehumanization in the midst of war aggression by Russian troops. And when the anti-aircraft sirens sound, they welcome the whole neighborhood into their lair. The legend of the theater tells that this armored basement of a building in the Shevchenko district was used by the Gestapo for interrogations, but for the last eight years it has been the headquarters of the Ukrainian company ProEnglish Theater, the only one that performs plays in English.\par \par \uc2 13:49 Ukraine calls for ICC mission after at least 280 bodies found Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  has called for an \uc2 International Criminal Court (ICC) \uc2 mission to be sent to the Kiev district of Bucha after several civilian bodies were found. Kuleba has asked the ICC and other international organizations to go to Bucha and the other areas north of the capital that have been under Russian control until Friday to collect evidence of possible war crimes.\par \par \uc2 13:40 European Council President calls for more sanctions against Russia after "atrocities" uncovered near Kyiv European Council President \uc2 Charles Michel\uc2  on Sunday accused Russia of committing "atrocities" in the Kyiv region and called for more sanctions against Moscow. "Shocked by disturbing images of atrocities committed by the Russian army in the liberated Kyiv region," Michel wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , with the keywords #BuchaMassacre."\par \par \uc2 13:31 Ukraine assures that Russian troops have shelled OdessaTheUkrainian authorities have assured this Sunday that several missiles launched by Russian troops have hit the city of Odessa and damaged a refinery, without it being known yet if any casualties have been recorded.This was announced by the city council of the city on its Telegram channel, where they have asked citizens not to open the windows of their homes and not to interfere with the work of the relevant services.\par \par \uc2 13:22 Ukrainians in the rear, the report of the special envoy of La Vanguardia to Chop, Maria-Paz Lopez. The rearguard is a place where the war is far away but it is breathed everywhere. In Chop, a town of 8,800 inhabitants on the border with Hungary and an important railway junction from where refugee trains leave for Hungary and Slovakia, the convoys are no longer so full of passengers. As the fighting stalls, women and men over the age of 60 - the only adults allowed to leave the country - who fled from their bombed-out towns to these safer regions in the west are now choosing not to leave Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 13:13 Donetsk separatists accuse Ukraine of the deaths of 72 civilians since the start of the Russian invasion Authorities in the Moscow-friendly self-proclaimed Donetsk People's Republic have accused Ukrainian forces of the deaths of 72 civilians since the start of the Russian invasion of the country on February 24. "During the 46 days of the escalation, 72 civilians were killed," according to a message posted by the Joint Center for Control and Coordination on its Telegram channel and picked up by TASS news agency.\par \par \uc2 13:00 Russia says it is too early for more talks with Ukraine Russia's chief negotiator in talks with Ukraine says it is too early to talk about a meeting between the presidents of the two countries. \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 , who led the Russian delegation at Tuesday's talks in Istanbul, Turkey, said "there is still a lot of work to be done" to finalize a draft agreement before Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Ukrainian President Zelensky can meet.\par \par \uc2 12:55 Ukrainian Army announces it has taken full control of the border with Belarus The Ukrainian Army announced today that its troops have taken full control of the border with Belarus, as well as the area around the Chernobyl power plant. "Today, April 3, units of the Armed Forces of Ukraine took control of the Pripyat district and the area of the State Border of Ukraine with the Republic of Belarus," they announced in a statement posted on their official \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 12:46 EU pledges new sanctions after accusing Russia of committing "atrocities" in the Ukrainian town of Bucha European Council President \uc2 Charles Michel\uc2  has pledged new \uc2 European Union\uc2  sanctions after accusing the Russian Army of committing "atrocities" in the recently liberated Ukrainian town of Bucha. "Appalled by the shocking images of atrocities committed by the Russian Army in the liberated region of Kiev," Michel has made known in a message on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , accompanied by the hashtag "Massacre in Bucha."\par \par \uc2 12:37 Seven buses set to take some 500 civilians out of Mariupol this Sunday Seven buses will try to leave the besieged Ukrainian city of Mariupol this coming Sunday with some 500 civilians inside as the Red Cross tries again to enter the city with humanitarian aid after the failure of its first raid on Friday. The buses are part of a civilian initiative announced on Telegram and, if successful, will move residents to Zaporiyia, while some 100,000 people still await their turn in the siege-ravaged city.\par \par \uc2 12:28 Poland opens the door to hosting US nuclear weapons on its territory Polish Deputy Prime Minister Jaros?aw Kaczy?ski said Sunday that Poland would be willing to host US nuclear warheads, as well as advocating reinforcing the country's troops on \uc2 NATO\uc2 's eastern flank. "Basically, it would make sense to expand the nuclear presence on the eastern flank," said the president of the Law and Justice Party (PiS) in an interview with the Sunday edition of the German daily Die Welt, although he clarified that the initiative would have to come from Washington. Kaczy?ski has proposed increasing the US troop presence in Europe from 100,000 to 150,000, of which half should be permanently stationed in the Eastern European countries on the borders with Russia.\par \par \uc2 12:19 Russian lawmakers will propose measures to punish the implementation of sanctions in Russia "My colleagues from the State Duma and I have finished the work and on Monday we will introduce amendments to the Criminal Code for the implementation of restrictive measures (sanctions) imposed by foreign states on the territory of the Russian Federation," senior Russian lawmaker \uc2 Andrei Klishas\uc2  wrote on his Telegram channel. "We expect prompt consideration of the amendments by the State Duma." Although, Klishas has not specified how Russia would identify or punish those who implemented the sanctions. Russian lawmakers passed amendments to the code in early March that make the dissemination of "false" information a crime punishable by fines or imprisonment. They imposed fines also for those carrying out public calls for sanctions against Russia.\par \par \uc2 12:08 Russia increases pressure in the Donbas and gives Kyiv a respite This Saturday Russian troops continued to advance in the Donbas, eastern Ukraine, a place where the Ukrainian Army is withstanding the onslaught of Russian artillery and aviation, whose goal is to conquer the entire territory of the Donetsk and Lugansk regions, while Kyiv manages to breathe with some tranquility after more than a month of siege. "Russian forces are massing in Donbas, towards Kharkov, and are preparing for even more powerful attacks," Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  said in a video message broadcast online. Since early yesterday, pro-Russian militias from Donetsk partially seized the locality of Novobakhtumovka, located north of the regional capital, after clashing with units of the 25th airborne brigade of the Ukrainian Army, the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  reported in its morning briefing. The spokesman of the Russian military entity, Igor Konashenkov, pointed out that the militias would have caused up to 40 casualties to the Ukrainian troops, besides destroying a tank platoon, two motorized infantry platoons, and a mortar battery.\par \par \uc2 11:57 Colombia shows its support to Zelenski before the "vile attack" on Ukraine The President of Colombia, Ivan Duque, had a telephone conversation with his Ukrainian counterpart, \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 , to whom he expressed his country's support before Russia's "vile and disproportionate attack" on Ukraine, and highlighted the humanitarian aid provided to the Ukrainian people. The call, which lasted about 20 minutes, took place during Duque's visit to La Uribe, in the department of \uc2 Meta\uc2  (center), from where he said it was "a moving call". Duque said at an event that Zelenski "has shown the world his courage" and is "an example in the defense of the sovereignty of his people", while insisting on the "condemnation" of Russia's attacks. Ukraine "has been the victim of a vile and disproportionate attack, of a systematic violation of international humanitarian law and of atrocious acts such as the bombing of maternity hospitals and the destruction of its infrastructure", lamented the Colombian president.\par \par \uc2 11:47 Ukraine alleges Russian troops killed photojournalist Maxim Levin The Ukrainian Prosecutor's Office on Saturday accused Russian forces of having shot dead Ukrainian photojournalist Maxim 'Max' Levin, missing since the middle of last month near the capital, Kyiv. The journalist's body was found in the village of Guta Mezhyhirska, Vishgorod municipality. "According to preliminary information, Maxim Levin was unarmed and was killed by servicemen of the Russian Armed Forces with two shots from small arms," according to the statement picked up by the Ukrainian news portal Vgolos. The Prosecutor's Office has opened a criminal case against Russia for violating the laws on armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 11:40 Russia reorganizes its troops La Vanguardia's special envoy in Kyiv, Joaquin Luna explains how the latest developments of the Russian invasion in Ukraine are being experienced, with siren songs about a possible withdrawal sounding loudly. "Russian troops are not withdrawing, they are repositioning. Russia has repeatedly lied about its intentions," summarized yesterday the secretary general of the Atlantic Alliance, \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 , to express the mistrust, shared by Kyiv, about a supposed military de-escalation in the Kyiv and Chernobyl areas.\par \par \uc2 11:31 Russia fires missiles near Odessa as evacuation of Mariupol is reattempted Ukraine claims Sunday that missiles are falling near the port of Odessa in southern Ukraine. Russia claimed this morning that it had destroyed an oil refinery used by the Ukrainian army, while attempts to evacuate civilians from the devastated city of Mariupol were expected to continue. For its part, the Odessa city council has said that "critical infrastructure facilities" have been hit by missiles, although for the moment, no casualties have been reported. \uc2 Russia's Defense Ministry\uc2  has detailed that its military's strikes have destroyed an oil refinery and three fuel storage facilities near Odessa. It has further explained that the facilities have been used to supply Ukrainian troops near the town of Mykolaiv.\par \par \uc2 11:23 Orb\u225\'00\'E1n appeals to Hungarians to vote with Ukraine war as background Hungary's ultranationalist Prime Minister \uc2 Viktor Orb\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2  has appealed Sunday to vote in legislative elections, in which he is seeking a fourth term, claiming they are crucial as the choice is between "war or peace." Orb\u225\'00\'E1n presents himself as the guarantor ofthe country's stability and accuses the opposition, which appears to have a chance of winning for the first time since 2010, of wanting to involve Hungary in the war by showing solidarity with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 , whose country is being invaded by Russia. "It is the war of two big countries and it is a dangerous thing. We should not get involved. Our political rivals do not consider the seriousness of the situation and want to take steps with which they would involve the country in the war. That would be tragic for Hungary," Orb\u225\'00\'E1n said after voting at a polling station in a residential area of Budapest.\par \par \uc2 11:14 Ukraine claims to have expelled all Russian forces from Kyiv "Irpin, Bucha, Gostomel and the entire Kiev region have been liberated from the invader". This was confirmed on Saturday by Ukrainian Deputy Defense Minister Anna Maliar announcing that there are no more Russian forces in the administrative region surrounding the Ukrainian capital Kyiv. In the last few hours, Ukrainian troops have seized some thirty towns in the region, according to presidential spokesman Oleksii Arestovich, who warned however that "tough battles lie ahead" in the south and east of the country, according to Ukrainian public television. "Let us have no illusions. There is still heavy fighting in the south, for Mariupol, for the east of Ukraine," the spokesman explained. These Ukrainian advances would fit in with Moscow's declaration that it would desist from its offensive in the Kyiv and Chernobyl region to concentrate on the east of the country. Thus, Ukrainian sources quoted by the DPA news agency speak of Russian shelling of factories and industrial zones in Mariupol, Kharkov and Chernobyl.\par \par \uc2 11:05 Ukrainian ambassador to Germany accuses President Steinmeier of maintaining a "web of contacts" with Russia Ukraine's ambassador to Germany, Andri Melnik, on Sunday once again accused Berlin of having exhibited a timid response to the Russian invasion of his country after pointing to German President \uc2 Frank-Walter Steinmeier\uc2  for maintaining a "web of contacts" in Russia that has made a more forceful reaction impossible. "For Steinmeier, relations with Russia were and remain fundamental, even sacred, no matter what," Melnik told 'Der Tagesspiegel' in an interview. "He seems to share the idea" of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  "that Ukrainians are not really a people," he added. Melnik also said that the Russian invasion "does not play an important role" for the German president in his consideration of his relations with Moscow, and that the backing of practically the entire German Council of Ministers is conspicuous by its absence, with one notable exception. The ambassador has spoken about the co-chairman of the Greens and German vice-chancellor assuring that: "Only \uc2 Robert Habeck\uc2  answers my messages".\par \par \uc2 10:57 Greece to start humanitarian aid in Odessa this Sunday Greek Foreign Minister \uc2 Nikos Dendias\uc2  will land in Odessa today, Sunday, at the head of a humanitarian aid mission in the southern Ukrainian port city, the Foreign Ministry has confirmed. The humanitarian aid will be delivered to the city's municipal authorities. The minister also plans to establish a continuous Greek aid distribution mechanism and reopen the Greek consulate in the city, the Foreign Ministry said in a statement. At least 10 Greek people have been killed and several injured since Russia began targeting Mariupol. According to the Greek government, more than 150 Greek citizens, boat crew and ethnic Greeks have been evacuated from the region.\par \par \uc2 10:46 Johnson congratulates Zelensky on the retreat of Russian forces in several areas of Ukraine British Prime Minister Boris Johnson has congratulated Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  on the retreat of Russian forces in several areas of Ukraine, the official Downing Street residence reported Sunday. The prime minister's official office has detailed that this Saturday in a telephone conversation, Johnson acknowledged the immense suffering of civilians and "enormous challenges" still in Ukraine. The head of the \uc2 British government\uc2  also updated Zelenski on a recent donor conference convened by the UK with the participation of 35 countries, while pledging continued defensive support. In their conversation, both leaders agreed on the importance of increasing sanctions to maintain economic pressure on Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as long as Russian troops remain on Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 10:38 A film director and documentary filmmaker has died in Mariupol, according to Lithuanian media Lithuanian film director and documentary filmmaker Mantas Kvedarivicius died Saturday while trying to leave the Ukrainian city of Mariupol Lithuanian media reported Sunday. Kvedarivicius was in Ukraine in order to "capture the brutal reality of war," according to the Lithuanian news portal Delfi and died after his car was hit by a rocket. The 45-year-old director's death was announced by his Russian-Lithuanian colleague Vitalijus Manskis, who wrote on his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account that Kvedarivicius died "with the camera in his hands". The Lithuanian was known precisely for "Mariupolis" (2016), a documentary that focused on the experiences of the inhabitants of the port city to reflect the conflict in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 10:31 Kyiv denounces that the towns left by the Russians are full of mines, reports La Vanguardia's special envoy in Kyiv, Joaquin Luna. Ukraine claims to have recovered 30 towns in the region of the capital and Chernobyl, thanks to the "rapid withdrawal" of the invaders, as certified by the \uc2 British Ministry of Defense\uc2 , a reliable source. The bad news is that all these villages are mined, another demonstration that the Ottawa Treaty of 1997 was a toast to the sun in view of the fact that Russia, China and the USA have still not signed it. And in Bucha, on the outskirts of Kyiv, the picture was bleak, with at least twenty corpses in the unpopulated streets, some with their hands tied and in civilian clothes.\par \par \uc2 10:22 Eurogroup resumes on Monday its economic analysis on the impact of the war in Ukraine The Ministers of Economy and Finance of the Eurozone (Eurogroup) will resume on Monday their deliberations on the impact of the war in Ukraine, knowing that it will lead to lower than expected economic growth and higher inflation due to energy prices and supply problems. The Nineteen face a new meeting, which will be attended by the Vice President for Economic Affairs, Nadia Calvi\u241\'00\'F1o, with the assumption that Russian inflation will lead to an expansion of European economies "significantly lower" and a few days after knowing the inflation data for March, which in the eurozone soared to 7.5%. "The eurozone is likely to be very resilient this year, but growth will be significantly lower than anticipated and the war is causing a long-standing rise in inflation," summed up a senior European official ahead of the Eurogroup meeting.\par \par \uc2 10:13 Russian forces maintain blockade in the Black Sea The latest intelligence from the \uc2 British Ministry of Defense\uc2  indicates that Russian naval forces are maintaining a blockade of the Ukrainian coast in the Black Sea and Sea of Azov, preventing resupply from Ukraine by sea. Russia still retains the capability to attempt an amphibious landing, but such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have its risks due to the time Ukrainian forces have had to prepare, Sunday's report explains. In addition, the report states that mines that may be in the Black Sea pose a risk to maritime activity, although the origin of those mines is still unclear, their presence is almost certain due to Russian naval activity in the area and demonstrates how the Russian invasion of Ukraine affects civilian interests.\par \par \uc2 10:02 Putin and Zelenski to meet soon, Ukrainian chief negotiator says The head of the Ukrainian negotiating group, David Arahamiya, has reported that negotiations between Ukraine and Russia are sufficiently advanced that a face-to-face meeting between Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his Ukrainian counterpart, \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 , may take place soon. "The Russian team confirmed our thesis that the draft agreement is sufficiently developed to hold direct consultations between the two leaders, the presidents of Ukraine and Russia," Arahamiya wrote on his official Telegram channel. According to Arahamiya, Putin himself said on Friday in a telephone conversation with Turkish President Tayip Erdogan that "he is ready for such a meeting to be organized in the near future." "Neither the date nor the place is closed yet, but it is very likely that it will take place in Istanbul or Ankara, both in Turkey," the parliamentary spokesman of Zelenski's "People's Servant" party has pointed out. Arahamiya explained that in the talks held so far, Russia accepts, in the absence of putting it in writing, "almost all" of Ukraine's positions on its territorial integrity and sovereignty, except for its claims to Crimea, annexed by Moscow in 2014.\par \par \uc2 09:49 Russia claims that Ukraine is making slow progress in fulfilling the agreements The head of the Russian negotiating group, \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 , has criticized the "optimism" of his Ukrainian counterpart, David Arakhamia. He has stated that the Ukrainian authorities are making very slow progress in the fulfillment of the political agreements reached at the Russia-Ukraine negotiations held in Istanbul. "Regarding the preparations for drafting the agreement for a summit between Putin and Zelensky, I unfortunately do not share Arakhamia's optimism. We still have a lot of work ahead of us," he told Russia's Interfax news agency on Sunday. According to Medinsky, Ukraine's military and diplomatic services "are marching with a big delay even in fulfilling the agreements already reached at the political level." "Arakhamia said that Russia accepts the Ukrainian position with the exception of Crimea," the Ukrainian peninsula illegally annexed by Moscow in 2014. He also recalled that "previously agreed issues, specifically the neutral, non-aligned and non-nuclear status of Ukraine, the ban on foreign bases, on the presence of foreign troops or any kind of missile systems," among other points, "were precisely the demands put forward by Russia since 2014." "We see that the Ukrainian side now has a more realistic approach to this," he noted, although he stressed that "Russia's position on Crimea and the Donbas is unchanged."\par \par \uc2 09:40 Theater as a shelter during the war A Ukrainian theater company, born in a World War II bomb shelter, keeps alive the art of drama in Kyiv, as an antidote against dehumanization in the midst of the war aggression of Russian troops. And when the anti-aircraft sirens sound, they welcome the whole neighborhood into their lair. The legend of the theater tells that this armored basement of a building in the Shevchenko district was used by the Gestapo for interrogations, but for the last eight years it has been the headquarters of the Ukrainian company ProEnglish Theater, the only one that performs plays in English. Now, the space has regained its original function and has permanently sheltered, since the first day of the invasion, some twenty members of the company, who also decided to take refuge in art to calm the anxiety and uncertainty of an armed offensive against the capital.\par \par \uc2 09:31 Twelve Spaniards have joined the Foreign Legion fighting the Russians in Ukraine At least a dozen Spanish citizens are already in Ukraine, enrolled (or with the intention of enrolling) in the International Legion for Territorial Defense created by the Government of \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  against the Russian invasion, according to the figures handled by the Spanish security forces. Four are Catalans, eight from the rest of Spain. Their names and lives are known and they are and will be under the radar the day they return, because of the military baggage they will have acquired in the war. If they return. For now, 50 Georgians, between 60 and 70 French, 40 Dutch, about 600 Canadians (Canada has a huge community of Ukrainian origin), about 150 British, at least a hundred Americans and about thirty Croatians have traveled to the wheat fields, according to estimates published so far.\par \par \uc2 09:23 Ukrainians in the rear, the report of the special envoy of La Vanguardia to Chop, Maria-Paz Lopez. The rearguard is a place where war is far away but it is breathed everywhere. In Chop, a town of 8,800 inhabitants on the border with Hungary and an important railway junction from where refugee trains leave for Hungary and Slovakia, the convoys are no longer so full of passengers. As the fighting stalls, women and men over the age of 60 - the only adults allowed to leave the country - who fled from their bombed-out towns to these safer regions in the west are now choosing not to leave Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 09:13 Lithuania abandons Russian gas imports \uc2 Lithuania's Energy Ministry\uc2  announced Saturday that the country has been operating without Russian gas imports since the beginning of the month with the intention of achieving "full energy independence" from Russia in response to "Russian energy blackmail" and the invasion of Ukraine. Data from Lithuanian gas transmission system operator \uc2 Amber Grid\uc2  has confirmed this. It shows that on April 2 the import of Russian gas for Lithuania's needs through the Lithuanian-Belarusian interconnection has been equal to zero megawatts (MWh), the Ministry has reported in a statement. "We are the first \uc2 EU\uc2  country among \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's supplier countries to become independent from Russian gas supply, and this is the result of a coherent process of several years' energy policies and timely infrastructure decisions," \uc2 Lithuanian Energy\uc2  Minister \uc2 Dainius Kreivys\uc2  has assured.\par \par \uc2 09:04 Zelenski will speak before the Congress of Deputies this Tuesday The President of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 , will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by videoconference in the Congress of Deputies this Tuesday before the President of the Government, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez; the President of the Lower House, Meritxell Batet, and deputies and senators, in a week where another conflict, that of Western Sahara, will once again be the focus of parliamentary debates. Zelenski's act will last about half an hour and will be similar to the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  he has had before other parliaments. This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes at a time when Turkey has reiterated its offer to organize a meeting between the presidents of Russia and Ukraine to promote peace negotiations. Both the President of the Congress and the Chief Executive will address a few words to the Ukrainian leader in a session in which senators are also participating, so the plenary session of control of the Government in the Upper House will begin around 17:15 hours.\par \par \uc2 08:55 Ukraine claims no casualties in attack on Odessa refinery This morning's missile attack on a refinery in the southern Ukrainian city of Odessa has so far caused no casualties, Ukrainian Army Southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  Command Colonel Vladislav Nazarov said. In a video broadcast by the official Telegram channel of the Odessa City Council, Nazarov has detailed that the attack has been against a "critical infrastructure" of the city. Several missiles hit the refinery in the Ukrainian city of Odessa at around 6 a.m. local time (04.00 GMT), whose fuel tanks are on fire. The colonel explained that firefighters are on the scene trying to control the flares caused by the missiles, which are coming from several fuel tanks where, from time to time, explosions occur when their contents catch fire.\par \par \uc2 08:46 \uc2 HRW\uc2  accuses Russia of committing war crimes in different Ukrainian regions \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  (\uc2 HRW\uc2 ) said Sunday that it has documented several cases showing that Russian military forces have committed war crimes in the occupied areas of Kharkov, Chernobyl and Kyiv regions. These include one case of repeated rape; two cases of summary execution, one of six men, the other of one man; and other cases of unlawful violence and threats against civilians between February 27 and March 14, 2022. On the other hand, soldiers were also allegedly involved in looting civilian goods, including food, clothing and firewood. "The cases we documented amount to deliberate and unspeakable cruelty and violence against Ukrainian civilians," said \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  Europe and Central Asia director \uc2 Hugh Williamson\uc2 , who believes that all war crimes should be investigated.\par \par \uc2 08:37 Polish Prime Minister regrets that sanctions against Russia are not working Polish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki has publicly regretted that economic sanctions against Russia are not working. Morawiecki has demanded a tightening of the measures to curb the strengthening of the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , theruble. The Polish politician has called for immediate seizure of property and assets of both the Russian government and its oligarchs. "There's the new money. That's what I've been saying for weeks: hundreds of billions of dollars in European banks, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the accounts of the oligarchs. Freezing for the sake of freezing is not going to do anything. The seizure of these properties is necessary to contribute to the reconstruction of Ukraine," he told journalists.\par \par \uc2 08:29 Russia plans to resort to barter to avoid sanctions in its trade with Morocco Russia and Morocco can minimize the impact of Western sanctions against Moscow on their trade exchanges by resorting to barter or using \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar and the euro, Russian Ambassador to Rabat Valeryan Shuvaev has explained. "It is possible to discuss barter \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as to seek solutions for mutual settlements using \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar and the euro, or national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Shuvaev said.\par \par \uc2 08:22 \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief to Moscow to push for truce in Ukraine \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief \uc2 Martin Griffiths\uc2  of Britain will travel to Moscow on Sunday to try to push for a truce in Ukraine, \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary-General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  said on Friday. Griffiths plans to visit Kyiv later to discuss with the Ukrainian authorities the initiative for a humanitarian cease-fire that Guterres himself raised for the first time this week.\par \par \uc2 08:13 Ukraine accuses Russia of leaving mines "all over the territory" Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  warned his people Saturday that retreating Russian forces are creating "a complete mess" outside the capital as they leave mines "all over the territory," including around houses and corpses. Zelensky, on the other hand, has criticized Europe for "reacting in silence" to the humanitarian catastrophe in the southern Ukrainian city of Mariupol caused by Russian shelling and siege. According to the Ukrainian president, an estimated 160,000 citizens have been living for more than a month without basic services in Mariupol, a port on the Sea of Azov which has become the main target of Russian troops in Ukraine because of its strategic position between the Crimean peninsula and the Dombass.\par \par \uc2 08:05 Russia denounces that the Ukrainian Army carries out shelling in the Chernobyl region The head of the Russian National Defense Control Center, Colonel \uc2 Mikhail Mizintsev\uc2 , denounced on Saturday that the Ukrainian Army carries out shelling in the locality of Navoaya Basan, in the Chernobyl region. Mizintsev warned that the Ukrainian Armed Forces shelled the settlement for more than two hours, causing during the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  "numerous casualties among local residents". The Russian official also warned that mines are being ordered from Kyiv to be laid in the discharge locks of a hydroelectric power plant in the town of Aleksandrovskaya in the Nikolaev region.\par \par \uc2 07:57 \uc2 UN\uc2  brings to 1,325 civilians killed in war The UN Office for Human Rights has reported that at least 1,325 civilians have died since the beginning of the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Among these dead, 120 are children, as indicated by the \uc2 UN\uc2 , which has clarified that these are the verified victims but that the real figure may be much higher. As for civilians wounded in the conflict, the \uc2 UN\uc2  has confirmed 2,017, including 168 children. The actual number of casualties "could be considerably much higher" as there is only partial information from some of the areas of major hostilities, including localities besieged by Russian troops such as Mariupol, Volnovaja, Izium or Irpin.\par \par \uc2 07:47 Russia attacks with missiles a refinery in Odessa Several missiles have hit this morning a refinery in the Ukrainian city of Odessa, whose fuel tanks are on fire, without knowing yet if there have been any casualties in the attack. According to Efe news agency, the missiles hit this infrastructure located in the north of the city, next to its port, where an oil pipeline from Russia arrives. The City Council of the city has informed through \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of the attack: "Odessa was attacked from the air", that "some missiles were shot down by the air defense" and that "fires have been reported in some areas". The explosions (at least six of varying intensity) have been felt kilometers from the site and from the refinery complex three columns of black smoke can be seen from all over the city.\par \par \uc2 07:36 Ukraine claims to have recaptured 30 villages around Kyiv The Ukrainian government said Saturday that the country's military forces have recaptured more than thirty villages in the region surrounding the capital, Kiev. Presidential spokesman Oleksii Arestovich has reported these advances and that "tough battles lie ahead" in the south and east of the country, according to Ukrainian public television. "Let us have no illusions. There is still heavy fighting in the south, for Mariupol, for the east of Ukraine," the spokesman explained. Besides, Arestovich stressed that Ukrainian troops are being able to hold the front line in the east of the country. Ukrainian forces have taken control of such localities as Bucha or Brovari and also Russian forces have withdrawn from the Antonov airport in Gostomel, where they have been entrenched for several weeks, reported the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 07:28 More than 200 arrested in Russia during protests against the war in Ukraine More than 200 people have been arrested in Russia for protest actions against the offensive in Ukraine, announced the NGO OVD-Info, which specializes in monitoring arrests in the country. "More than 178 people were arrested in 15 Russian cities," the organization said in a statement. In Moscow, a sit-in against Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  was planned in the morning in Zariadi\u233\'00\'E9 Park near the Kremlin. Shortly afterwards, police began arresting people sitting on park benches in the middle of a snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 07:19 Ukraine blames Russia for manhandling and murdering civilians in Bucha One of Ukraine's presidential aides, \uc2 Mykhailo Podolyak\uc2 , on Saturday accused Russia of manhandling and murdering civilians found lifeless by Ukrainian troops after recapturing the town of Bucha, northwest of Kyiv. "The bodies of people with their hands tied, who have been shot dead by Russian soldiers, lay on the streets. These people were not in the army. They had no weapons. They posed no threat. How many more cases are happening right now in the occupied territories," Podolyak has questioned on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account. Podolyak has shared an image where several bodies are shown on the ground, one of them can be seen with his hands tied behind his back. The presidential aide has argued that Russia "is waging a war to destroy the Ukrainian people", and has used the message on social networks to ask Western allies for more weapons for the protection of Ukrainian citizens.\par \par \uc2 07:08 Russia assures readiness to evacuate foreigners from the city of Mariupol The head of the Russian National Defense Control Center, Colonel General \uc2 Mikhail Mizintsev\uc2 , has assured on Sunday that Russia is ready to assist in the evacuation of foreign citizens who are in the besieged city of Mariupol, southeastern Ukraine, according to the Russian agency TASS. "In accordance with the request of Turkish President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , Russia will provide assistance in the evacuation of foreign citizens held hostage by nationalists in Mariupol," Mizintsev explained. The colonel-general has maintained that Russia is carrying out "purposeful and coordinated work" to evacuate civilians and foreigners from dangerous regions of Ukraine. "To this end, we are in constant interaction with the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 , the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), the \uc2 International Committee of the Red Cross\uc2  and other international organizations," he stressed. He also added that the Russian Armed Forces would open a humanitarian corridor from Mariupol to the city of Berdiansk, 80 kilometers to the west, for evacuation of foreign citizens.\par \par \uc2 07:00 Pope warns of the risk of "an extended cold war" Pope Francis has warned in Malta of "the childish and destructive aggressiveness that threatens us" in reference to Russia's invasion of Ukraine and the risk of it becoming "an extended cold war that can suffocate the lives of entire peoples and generations." The Pontiff lamented that from Eastern Europe, "where the light once shone, the darkness of war has arrived", before adding: "We thought that the invasions of other countries, the brutal fighting in the streets and the atomic threats were dark memories of a distant past".\par \par \uc2 06:53 More than 4,200 civilians evacuated through humanitarian corridors on Saturday in Ukraine A total of 4,217 civilians have been evacuated on Saturday through the humanitarian corridors set up, including 1,263 from the city of Mariupol, which has been besieged by Russian forces for weeks. The Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister, Irina Vereshchuk, reported these figures and explained that the evacuees from Mariupol, also from Berdiansk, have already arrived in the city of Zaporiya, many of them using their own vehicles.\par \par \uc2 06:45 About 300 people buried "in mass graves" in the Ukrainian town of Bucha, according to the mayor Anatoly Fedorouk, mayor of the Ukrainian town of Bucha, northeast of Kiev, has assured that they have found the corpses of about 300 people in a mass grave. The town was retaken by Ukrainian troops. Earlier, a journalist from the AFP news agency confirmed that there were at least 20 bodies scattered on a street in the locality. One of the corpses had its hands tied, the reporter said. They were wearing civilian clothes.\par \par \uc2 06:38 Russia declares readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine Russian Presidential Spokesman Dmitry Peskov on Saturday expressed Russia's readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine and reproached Kyiv for vetoing Belarus as the host of these contacts. "We would love to continue negotiations in Belarus, but the Ukrainians do not want to. We have to recognize that the most important thing is to look for a place where we can meet with Ukrainian negotiators and, most importantly, that they continue either in Istanbul or elsewhere," Peskov said in an interview with Belarusian Belarus-24 television. Peskov said that "Ukraine is a very difficult country for us." "In the current situation it is a country that is hostile," he has noted. "It is also not a friendly country for Belarus," he has stated in reference to Kiev's support for the 2020-2021 mobilizations of the Belarusian opposition.\par \par \uc2 06:30 Russia - Ukraine war | Latest news from the conflict and negotiations, live | Good morning and welcome to the minute-by-minute coverage of everything related to the Russia - Ukraine war and its consequences on an international scale. Ukraine has assured that it has expelled Russian forces from the entire Kyiv region. Ukrainian Deputy Defense Minister Anna Maliar announced Saturday that there are no longer Russian forces in the administrative region surrounding the Ukrainian capital Kyiv. "Irpin, Bucha, Gostomel and the entire Kyiv region have been liberated from the invader," Maliar posted on her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page. In the last few hours Ukrainian troops have seized about thirty towns in the region, according to presidential spokesman Oleksii Arestovich, who warned however that "tough battles lie ahead" in the south and east of the country, as reported by Ukrainian public television.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220404ei43009b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
