\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Ukraine - Russia war, live | Latest news on the bombings and Putin\b0\par\par\uc2 3,530 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The key points of the day\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief will go to Moscow to push for a truce in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 - Russia has declared its readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 - More than 4,200 civilians evacuated through humanitarian corridors during the day in Ukraine on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 10:13 Russian forces maintain blockade in the Black Sea The latest intelligence from the \uc2 British Ministry of Defense\uc2  indicates that Russian naval forces are maintaining a blockade of the Ukrainian coast in the Black Sea and Sea of Azov, preventing resupply from Ukraine by sea. Russia still retains the capability to attempt an amphibious landing, but such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have its risks due to the time Ukrainian forces have had to prepare, Sunday's report explains. In addition, the report states that mines that may be in the Black Sea pose a risk to maritime activity, although the origin of those mines is still unclear, their presence is almost certain due to Russian naval activity in the area and demonstrates how the Russian invasion of Ukraine affects civilian interests.\par \par \uc2 10:02 Putin and Zelenski to meet soon, Ukrainian chief negotiator says The head of the Ukrainian negotiating group, David Arahamiya, has reported that negotiations between Ukraine and Russia are sufficiently advanced that a face-to-face meeting between Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his Ukrainian counterpart, \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 , may take place soon. "The Russian team confirmed our thesis that the draft agreement is sufficiently developed to hold direct consultations between the two leaders, the presidents of Ukraine and Russia," Arahamiya wrote on his official Telegram channel. According to Arahamiya, Putin himself said on Friday in a telephone conversation with Turkish President Tayip Erdogan that "he is ready for such a meeting to be organized in the near future." "Neither the date nor the place is closed yet, but it is very likely that it will take place in Istanbul or Ankara, both in Turkey," the parliamentary spokesman of Zelenski's "People's Servant" party has pointed out. Arahamiya explained that in the talks held so far, Russia accepts, in the absence of putting it in writing, "almost all" of Ukraine's positions on its territorial integrity and sovereignty, except for its claims to Crimea, annexed by Moscow in 2014.\par \par \uc2 09:49 Russia claims that Ukraine is making slow progress in fulfilling the agreements The head of the Russian negotiating group, \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 , has criticized the "optimism" of his Ukrainian counterpart, David Arakhamia. He has stated that the Ukrainian authorities are making very slow progress in the fulfillment of the political agreements reached at the Russia-Ukraine negotiations held in Istanbul. "Regarding the preparations for drafting the agreement for a summit between Putin and Zelensky, I unfortunately do not share Arakhamia's optimism. We still have a lot of work ahead of us," he told Russia's Interfax news agency on Sunday. According to Medinsky, Ukraine's military and diplomatic services "are marching with a big delay even in fulfilling the agreements already reached at the political level." "Arakhamia said that Russia accepts the Ukrainian position with the exception of Crimea," the Ukrainian peninsula illegally annexed by Moscow in 2014. He also recalled that "previously agreed issues, specifically the neutral, non-aligned and non-nuclear status of Ukraine, the ban on foreign bases, on the presence of foreign troops or any kind of missile systems," among other points, "were precisely the demands put forward by Russia since 2014." "We see that the Ukrainian side now has a more realistic approach to this," he noted, although he stressed that "Russia's position on Crimea and the Donbas is unchanged."\par \par \uc2 09:40 Theater as a shelter during the war A Ukrainian theater company, born in a World War II bomb shelter, keeps alive the art of drama in Kyiv, as an antidote against dehumanization in the midst of the war aggression of Russian troops. And when the anti-aircraft sirens sound, they welcome the whole neighborhood into their lair. The legend of the theater tells that this armored basement of a building in the Shevchenko district was used by the Gestapo for interrogations, but for the last eight years it has been the headquarters of the Ukrainian company ProEnglish Theater, the only one that performs plays in English. Now, the space has recovered its original function and has permanently sheltered, since the first day of the invasion, some twenty members of the company, who also decided to take refuge in art to calm the anxiety and uncertainty of an armed offensive against the capital.\par \par \uc2 09:31 Twelve Spaniards have joined the Foreign Legion fighting the Russians in Ukraine At least a dozen Spanish citizens are already in Ukraine, enrolled (or with the intention of enrolling) in the International Legion for Territorial Defense created by the Government of \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2  against the Russian invasion, according to the figures handled by the Spanish security forces. Four are Catalans, eight from the rest of Spain. Their names and lives are known and they are and will be under the radar the day they return, because of the military baggage they will have acquired in the war. If they return. For now, 50 Georgians, between 60 and 70 French, 40 Dutch, about 600 Canadians (Canada has a huge community of Ukrainian origin), about 150 British, at least a hundred Americans and about thirty Croatians have traveled to the wheat fields, according to estimates published so far.\par \par \uc2 09:23 Ukrainians in the rear, the report of the special envoy of La Vanguardia to Chop, Maria-Paz Lopez. The rearguard is a place where war is far away but it is breathed everywhere. In Chop, a town of 8,800 inhabitants on the Hungarian border and an important railway junction from where refugee trains depart to Hungary and Slovakia, the convoys no longer leave loaded with passengers. As the fighting stalls, women and men over the age of 60 - the only adults allowed to leave the country - who fled from their bombed-out towns to these safer regions in the west are now choosing not to leave Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 09:13 Lithuania abandons Russian gas imports \uc2 Lithuania's Energy Ministry\uc2  announced Saturday that the country has been operating without Russian gas imports since the beginning of the month with the intention of achieving "full energy independence" from Russia in response to "Russian energy blackmail" and the invasion of Ukraine. Data from Lithuanian gas transmission system operator \uc2 Amber Grid\uc2  has confirmed this. It shows that on April 2 the import of Russian gas for Lithuania's needs through the Lithuanian-Belarusian interconnection has been equal to zero megawatts (MWh), the Ministry has reported in a statement. "We are the first \uc2 EU\uc2  country among \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's supplier countries to become independent from Russian gas supply, and this is the result of a coherent process of several years' energy policies and timely infrastructure decisions," \uc2 Lithuanian Energy\uc2  Minister \uc2 Dainius Kreivys\uc2  has assured.\par \par \uc2 09:04 Zelenski will speak before the Congress of Deputies this Tuesday The President of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\uc2 , will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by videoconference in the Congress of Deputies this Tuesday before the President of the Government, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez; the President of the Lower House, Meritxell Batet, and deputies and senators, in a week where another conflict, that of Western Sahara, will once again be the focus of parliamentary debates. Zelenski's act will last about half an hour and will be similar to the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  he has had before other parliaments. This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes at a time when Turkey has reiterated its offer to organize a meeting between the presidents of Russia and Ukraine to promote peace negotiations. Both the President of the Congress and the Chief Executive will address a few words to the Ukrainian leader in a session in which senators are also participating, so the plenary session of control of the Government in the Upper House will begin around 17:15 hours.\par \par \uc2 08:55 Ukraine claims no casualties in attack on Odessa refinery This morning's missile attack on a refinery in the southern Ukrainian city of Odessa has so far caused no casualties, Ukrainian Army Southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  Command Colonel Vladislav Nazarov said. In a video broadcast by the official Telegram channel of the Odessa City Council, Nazarov has detailed that the attack has been against a "critical infrastructure" of the city. Several missiles hit the refinery in the Ukrainian city of Odessa at around 6 a.m. local time (04.00 GMT), whose fuel tanks are on fire. The colonel explained that firefighters are on the scene trying to control the flares caused by the missiles, which are coming from several fuel tanks where, from time to time, explosions occur when their contents catch fire.\par \par \uc2 08:46 \uc2 HRW\uc2  accuses Russia of committing war crimes in different Ukrainian regions \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  (\uc2 HRW\uc2 ) said Sunday that it has documented several cases showing that Russian military forces have committed war crimes in the occupied areas of Kharkov, Chernobyl and Kyiv regions. These include one case of repeated rape; two cases of summary execution, one of six men, the other of one man; and other cases of unlawful violence and threats against civilians between February 27 and March 14, 2022. On the other hand, soldiers were also allegedly involved in looting civilian goods, including food, clothing and firewood. "The cases we documented amount to deliberate and unspeakable cruelty and violence against Ukrainian civilians," said \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  Europe and Central Asia director \uc2 Hugh Williamson\uc2 , who believes that all war crimes should be investigated.\par \par \uc2 08:37 Polish Prime Minister regrets that sanctions against Russia are not working Polish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki has publicly regretted that economic sanctions against Russia are not working. Morawiecki has demanded a tightening of measures to curb the strengthening of the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble. The Polish politician has called for the immediate seizure of property and assets of both the Russian government and its oligarchs. "There's the new money. That's what I've been saying for weeks: hundreds of billions of dollars in European banks, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the accounts of the oligarchs. Freezing for the sake of freezing is not going to do anything. The seizure of these properties is necessary to contribute to the reconstruction of Ukraine," he told journalists.\par \par \uc2 08:29 Russia plans to resort to barter to avoid sanctions in its trade with Morocco Russia and Morocco can minimize the impact of Western sanctions against Moscow on their trade exchanges by resorting to barter or using \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar and the euro, Russian Ambassador to Rabat Valeryan Shuvaev has explained. "It is possible to discuss barter \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as to seek solutions for mutual settlements using \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar and the euro, or national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Shuvaev said.\par \par \uc2 08:22 \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief to Moscow to push for truce in Ukraine \uc2 UN\uc2  humanitarian chief \uc2 Martin Griffiths\uc2  of Britain will travel to Moscow on Sunday to try to push for a truce in Ukraine, \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary-General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  said on Friday. Griffiths plans to visit Kyiv later to discuss with the Ukrainian authorities the initiative for a humanitarian cease-fire that Guterres himself raised for the first time this week.\par \par \uc2 08:13 Ukraine accuses Russia of leaving mines "all over the territory" Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  warned his people Saturday that retreating Russian forces are creating "a complete mess" outside the capital as they leave mines "all over the territory," including around houses and corpses. Zelensky, on the other hand, has criticized Europe for "reacting in silence" to the humanitarian catastrophe in the southern Ukrainian city of Mariupol caused by Russian shelling and siege. According to the Ukrainian president, an estimated 160,000 citizens have been living for more than a month without basic services in Mariupol, a port on the Sea of Azov which has become the main target of Russian troops in Ukraine because of its strategic position between the Crimean peninsula and the Dombass.\par \par \uc2 08:05 Russia denounces that the Ukrainian Army carries out shelling in the Chernobyl region The head of the Russian National Defense Control Center, Colonel \uc2 Mikhail Mizintsev\uc2 , denounced on Saturday that the Ukrainian Army carries out shelling in the locality of Navoaya Basan, in the Chernobyl region. Mizintsev warned that the Ukrainian Armed Forces shelled the settlement for more than two hours, causing during the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  "numerous casualties among local residents". The Russian official also warned that mines are being ordered from Kyiv to be laid in the discharge locks of a hydroelectric power plant in the town of Aleksandrovskaya in the Nikolaev region.\par \par \uc2 07:57 \uc2 UN\uc2  brings to 1,325 civilians killed in war The UN Office for Human Rights has reported that at least 1,325 civilians have died since the beginning of the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Among these dead, 120 are children, as indicated by the \uc2 UN\uc2 , which has clarified that these are the verified victims but that the real figure may be much higher. As for civilians wounded in the conflict, the \uc2 UN\uc2  has confirmed 2,017, including 168 children. The actual number of casualties "could be considerably much higher" as there is only partial information from some of the areas of major hostilities, including localities besieged by Russian troops such as Mariupol, Volnovaja, Izium or Irpin.\par \par \uc2 07:47 A refinery in Odessa has been attacked with missiles Several missiles have hit this morning a refinery in the Ukrainian city of Odessa, whose fuel tanks are on fire, it is not yet known if there have been any casualties in the attack. According to Efe news agency, the missiles hit this infrastructure located in the north of the city, next to its port, where an oil pipeline from Russia arrives. The City Council of the city has informed through \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of the attack: "Odessa was attacked from the air", that "some missiles were shot down by the air defense" and that "fires have been reported in some areas". The explosions (at least six of varying intensity) have been felt kilometers from the site and from the refinery complex three columns of black smoke can be seen from all over the city.\par \par \uc2 07:36 Ukraine claims to have recaptured 30 villages around Kyiv The Ukrainian government said Saturday that the country's military forces have recaptured more than thirty villages in the region surrounding the capital, Kiev. Presidential spokesman Oleksii Arestovich has reported these advances and that "tough battles lie ahead" in the south and east of the country, according to Ukrainian public television. "Let us have no illusions. There is still heavy fighting in the south, for Mariupol, for the east of Ukraine," the spokesman explained. Besides, Arestovich stressed that Ukrainian troops are being able to hold the front line in the east of the country. Ukrainian forces have taken control of such localities as Bucha or Brovari and also Russian forces have withdrawn from the Antonov airport in Gostomel, where they have been entrenched for several weeks, reported the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 07:28 More than 200 arrested in Russia during protests against the war in Ukraine More than 200 people have been arrested in Russia for protest actions against the offensive in Ukraine, announced the NGO OVD-Info, which specializes in monitoring arrests in the country. "More than 178 people were arrested in 15 Russian cities," the organization said in a statement. In Moscow, a sit-in against Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  was planned in the morning in Zariadi\u233\'00\'E9 Park near the Kremlin. Shortly afterwards, police began arresting people sitting on park benches in the middle of a snowstorm.\par \par \uc2 07:19 Ukraine blames Russia for manhandling and murdering civilians in Bucha One of the advisers to the Ukrainian Presidency, \uc2 Mykhailo Podolyak\uc2 , on Saturday accused Russia of manhandling and murdering civilians found lifeless by Ukrainian troops after recapturing the town of Bucha, northwest of Kyiv. "Bodies of people with their hands tied, who have been shot dead by Russian soldiers, lay in the streets. These people were not in the army. They had no weapons. They posed no threat. How many more cases are happening right now in the occupied territories," Podolyak has questioned on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account. Podolyak has shared an image where several bodies are shown on the ground, one of them can be seen with his hands tied behind his back. The presidential aide has argued that Russia "is waging a war to destroy the Ukrainian people", and has used the message on social networks to ask Western allies for more weapons for the protection of Ukrainian citizens.\par \par \uc2 07:08 Russia assures readiness to evacuate foreigners from the city of Mariupol The head of the Russian National Defense Control Center, Colonel General \uc2 Mikhail Mizintsev\uc2 , has assured on Sunday that Russia is ready to assist in the evacuation of foreign citizens who are in the besieged city of Mariupol, southeastern Ukraine, according to the Russian agency TASS. "In accordance with the request of Turkish President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , Russia will provide assistance in the evacuation of foreign citizens held hostage by nationalists in Mariupol," Mizintsev explained. The colonel-general has maintained that Russia is carrying out "purposeful and coordinated work" to evacuate civilians and foreigners from dangerous regions of Ukraine. "To this end, we are in constant interaction with the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 , the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), the \uc2 International Committee of the Red Cross\uc2  and other international organizations," he stressed. He also added that the Russian Armed Forces would open a humanitarian corridor from Mariupol to the city of Berdiansk, 80 kilometers to the west, for evacuation of foreign citizens.\par \par \uc2 07:00 Pope warns of risk of "an extended cold war" Pope Francis has warned in Malta of "the childish and destructive aggressiveness that threatens us" in reference to Russia's invasion of Ukraine and the risk of it becoming "an extended cold war that can suffocate the lives of entire peoples and generations." The Pontiff lamented that from Eastern Europe, "where the light once shone, the darkness of war has arrived", before adding: "We thought that the invasions of other countries, the brutal fighting in the streets and the atomic threats were dark memories of a distant past".\par \par \uc2 06:53 More than 4,200 civilians evacuated through humanitarian corridors on Saturday in Ukraine A total of 4,217 civilians have been evacuated on Saturday through the humanitarian corridors set up, including 1,263 from the city of Mariupol, which has been besieged by Russian forces for weeks. The Ukrainian Deputy Prime Minister, Irina Vereshchuk, reported these figures and explained that the evacuees from Mariupol, also from Berdiansk, have already arrived in the city of Zaporiya, many of them using their own vehicles.\par \par \uc2 06:45 About 300 people buried "in mass graves" in the Ukrainian town of Bucha, according to the mayor Anatoly Fedorouk, mayor of the Ukrainian town of Bucha, northeast of Kiev, has assured that they have found the corpses of about 300 people in a mass grave. The town was retaken by Ukrainian troops. Earlier, a journalist from the AFP news agency confirmed that there were at least 20 bodies scattered on a street in the locality. One of the corpses had its hands tied, the reporter said. They were wearing civilian clothes.\par \par \uc2 06:38 Russia declares readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine Russian Presidential Spokesman Dmitry Peskov on Saturday expressed Russia's readiness to continue negotiations with Ukraine and reproached Kyiv for vetoing Belarus as the host of these contacts. "We would love to continue negotiations in Belarus, but the Ukrainians do not want to. We have to recognize that the most important thing is to look for a place where we can meet with Ukrainian negotiators and, most importantly, that they continue either in Istanbul or elsewhere," Peskov said in an interview with Belarusian Belarus-24 television. Peskov said that "Ukraine is a very difficult country for us." "In the current situation it is a country that is hostile," he has noted. "It is also not a friendly country for Belarus," he has stated in reference to Kiev's support for the 2020-2021 mobilizations of the Belarusian opposition.\par \par \uc2 06:30 Russia - Ukraine war | Latest news from the conflict and negotiations, live | Good morning and welcome to the minute-by-minute coverage of everything related to the Russia - Ukraine war and its consequences on an international scale. Ukraine has assured that it has expelled Russian forces from the entire Kyiv region. Ukrainian Deputy Defense Minister Anna Maliar announced on Saturday that there are no longer Russian forces in the administrative region surrounding the Ukrainian capital Kyiv. "Irpin, Bucha, Gostomel and the entire Kyiv region have been liberated from the invader," Maliar posted on her \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page. In the last few hours Ukrainian troops have seized about thirty towns in the region, according to presidential spokesman Oleksii Arestovich, who warned however that "tough battles lie ahead" in the south and east of the country, as reported by Ukrainian public television.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220403ei430053y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
