\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Cuts Turkey LC Rtg To 'B+', Affrms 'B+' FC Rtg; Otlk Neg\b0\par\par\uc2 4,620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1 Apr 2022 16:12 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Cuts Turkey LC Rtg To 'B+', Affrms 'B+' FC Rtg; Otlk Neg\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- The fallout of the Russia-Ukraine military conflict, including rising \par food and energy prices, will further weaken Turkey's already tenuous balance \par of payments and exacerbate inflation. The latter is on course to average 55% \par in 2022, the highest level of all the sovereigns we rate. \par      -- While we expect Turkey's net general government debt to amount to 34% \par of GDP by the end of 2022, preserving some fiscal space, the government's \par foreign and domestic borrowing costs have increased, and the key fiscal risk \par remains the underlying health of Turkey's recently recapitalized state banks. \par      -- The government's balance sheet and the broader economy have become \par increasingly dollarized, with over 50% of resident deposits now denominated in \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par      -- We lowered our unsolicited long-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (LC) rating on \par Turkey to 'B+' from 'BB -' and affirmed our unsolicited long-term foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (FC) rating at 'B+'. The outlook remains negative. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On April 1, 2022, S&P Global Ratings lowered its unsolicited long-term local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating on Turkey to 'B+' from 'BB -'. At the same \par time, we affirmed the unsolicited long-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating at 'B+' \par and the unsolicited foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  short-term ratings at 'B'. The \par outlook remains negative. \par  \par We also lowered Turkey's transfer and convertibility assessment to 'B+' from \par 'BB -' and the unsolicited national scale ratings to 'trAA-/trA-1+' from \par 'trAA+/trA-1+'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The negative outlook reflects what we view to be rising balance-of-payments \par and financial stability risks over the next 12 months amid the economic \par fallout from Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , global monetary \par tightening, and possible policy missteps in the run-up to Turkey's \par parliamentary and presidential elections in mid-2023. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if the adverse economic spillover from either the \par Russia-Ukraine conflict or global monetary tightening became more significant \par for Turkey, weakening its already vulnerable balance of payments and \par complicating the task of controlling inflation. We could also lower the \par ratings if policy missteps, for example, ahead of the elections next year, \par further undermined exchange-rate stability and worsened the inflation outlook. \par This would heighten the risk of banking system distress and thereby imply \par potential contingent liabilities for the government. Weakened asset quality \par following several rounds of large-scale credit stimulus in recent years could \par also weigh on the banking system in such a scenario, particularly state-owned \par banks that have seen their balance sheets expand more rapidly in recent years. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could revise the outlook to stable if we observed sustained and enhanced \par predictability of public policy and effectiveness of monetary policy while \par Turkey's balance-of-payments position strengthened, particularly the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) reserves. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The lowering of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating reflects what we now view as similar \par credit risks characterizing Turkey's foreign- and local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt. A \par differentiation between foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings is a comparatively \par rare phenomenon, with only 12 sovereigns benefiting from uplift for the local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating among all 137 sovereigns we rate. In our view, the conditions \par for such uplift are no longer present in Turkey's case. \par  \par We typically differentiate between the local and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings for \par sovereigns that can set interest rates and manage their local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par independently, that is, without reference to its external value. In our view, \par this is not the case in Turkey at present, as the multiple tools that it \par deployed previously effectively aimed at influencing the exchange rate, \par including foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the introduction of the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -protected deposit scheme late last year. We also note that Turkey's \par interest expenditure on local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government debt constitutes about 70% \par of total interest expenditure. We consider that, in a downside case, this \par could reduce the government's flexibility to accord a higher priority to \par servicing its local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  obligations, thus eroding the credit-risk \par distinction between the two types of debt. \par  \par In parallel, Turkey's economy has been persistently dollarizing in recent \par years. Resident deposits in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amounted to 55% of the total at \par the end of 2021, up from 39% at end-2017. If precious-metal deposits are \par included, this rises to about 60%. Meanwhile, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  composition of \par government debt has also been shifting in favor of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt at 66% at the end of 2021, up from under 40% at \par end-2017. \par  \par Our affirmation of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating primarily reflects our view \par that Turkey has some fiscal headroom in case of need. At 36% of GDP at \par end-2021, net general government debt looks favorable vis-a-vis other emerging \par markets such as Brazil or South Africa. We expect Turkey's net general \par government debt to stabilize at close to 32% of GDP over the medium term. \par Balance-of-payments risks, however, remain elevated, and public debt could \par increase further in the event of a more significant depreciation of the \par Turkish lira, or if the government provided more extensive support to the \par banking sector in a downside scenario. We consider that the global economic \par effects of Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February 2022, as well \par as the tightening monetary policy of central banks in developed markets, \par present additional balance-of-payments risks. \par  \par Overall, our ratings on Turkey remain supported by the country's diversified \par economy and resilient and adaptive private sector, which, in the past, has \par weathered external shocks, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility, and frequent changes in \par economic policy. Contained net general government debt, which we forecast will \par amount to 34% of GDP by the end of 2022, also supports the ratings. \par  \par The ratings on Turkey are constrained by its vulnerable balance-of-payments \par position and the limited effectiveness of its monetary policy. The ratings are \par also restricted by what we view as weak institutional arrangements. We see \par limited checks and balances between government bodies, with power concentrated \par in the hands of the executive branch, which renders policy responses difficult \par to predict. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Turkey's growth prospects have weakened in \par the aftermath of Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par      -- We project economic growth in Turkey of 2.4% in 2022, less than our \par 3.7% forecast at the end of 2021. \par      -- Turkey's institutional arrangements are weak, with limited checks and \par balances in place. \par      -- We think that economic policy uncertainty will remain elevated in the \par run-up to the 2023 general election, with possible additional stimulus \par measures at the expense of longer-term economic stability. \par In our view, Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February 2022 presents \par downside risks to global growth and we now expect weaker economic performance \par for European emerging markets, including Turkey. Last year, Turkey's growth \par outcome exceeded our previous forecasts, reaching 11%. Around half of this \par growth is attributable to a carryover effect due to a lower base stemming from \par the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic and the quick recovery in the latter part \par of 2020. However, quarterly growth was still very strong, with consumption and \par exports performing particularly well. In contrast, we expect a sharp slowdown \par to follow in 2022. \par  \par Turkey remains exposed to the fallout from the Russia-Ukraine conflict across \par several dimensions. Specifically: \par      -- Turkey's important tourism sector, which generates earnings in foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has in the past depended significantly on arrivals from Russia and \par to a lesser degree Ukraine. In 2021, arrivals from Russia constituted 19% of \par all foreign visitors to Turkey (16% pre-pandemic in 2019), while Ukraine had a \par share of 8% (3% in 2019). There is likely to be a significant reduction in \par these tourism flows in 2022 due to the direct impact of the military conflict \par in the case of Ukraine, and the large economic impact of sanctions, as well as \par disruption to transport and payment systems in Russia. In the case of tourists \par from Russia, this could be partially mitigated by the fact that Turkey is \par currently one of few destinations with direct flights from Russia still in \par place, as well as visa-free travel. Stronger European tourism thanks to an \par improved pandemic situation and a competitive lira could also provide support, \par but we consider the overall risks to be on the downside. \par      -- Russia and Ukraine's share of Turkish goods exports is more modest, at \par a combined 4% in 2020. However, given the scale of recessions (we expect a \par real GDP contraction of 8.5% in Russia and 22% in Ukraine this year), \par logistical challenges, and supply chain disruptions, this could still \par materially dampen Turkish exports, particularly taking into account \par second-round effects on European economies. \par      -- Turkey remains an energy importer and therefore significantly higher \par global prices for oil and gas will complicate the task of bringing inflation \par under control, with a likely adverse effect on domestic demand dynamics. \par Inflation rose to 54% year on year in February 2022, the highest level in 20 \par years. \par Consequently, we now forecast real growth of 2.4% in 2022, compared with our \par previous forecast of 3.7%. We expect a significant slowdown in exports and \par consumption growth, while we project that real investment will contract by \par 3.5% owing to high uncertainty and dwindling global growth momentum. \par  \par The future direction of government policy also remains highly uncertain. The \par political pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues after it made a series of \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2022 16:12 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Cuts Turkey LC Rtg To 'B+', -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par interest rate reductions in the latter half of 2021 against a background of \par soaring inflation. We also think that policy missteps could occur in the \par run-up to the 2023 general elections. Recent opinion polls suggest declining \par popular support for the ruling Justice and Development Party (Adalet ve \par Kalkinma Partisi; AKP), with the pandemic, high inflation in food prices, and \par a hit to real incomes likely to be contributing factors. In that context, \par additional policy-stimulus measures--for example, to boost the availability or \par reduce the price of credit--cannot be ruled out in 2022, even though Turkey \par still faces lingering imbalances from previous such measures, including lower \par useable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and higher inflation. \par  \par We consider that Turkey's broader institutional arrangements remain weak and \par continue to constrain the sovereign credit ratings. In the June 2018 \par presidential and parliamentary elections, the president and the AKP-led \par alliance secured a victory that marked the final chapter in Turkey's \par transition to an executive presidential system. We see limited checks and \par balances between government bodies. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Economic imbalances persist, with high \par inflation and weak balance of payments \par      -- We forecast that Turkey's inflation will average 55% in 2022, the \par highest level of all the sovereigns we rate. \par      -- Net general government debt should remain about 32% of GDP through \par 2025, still leaving some fiscal policy space. \par      -- Turkey's balance-of-payments position is weak, while its monetary \par policy flexibility is limited, underpinning the risk of a disorderly external \par adjustment. \par In our view, over the past year, Turkey's economic policy has become \par increasingly unpredictable and less conventional, presenting economic and \par balance-of-payments risks. Over the second half of 2021, and under political \par pressure, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  persistently lowered the policy rate (one-week repo \par rate) to 14% from 19%, even with inflation hitting multi-year highs and \par reaching 54% in February 2022, the highest rate of all the sovereigns we rate. \par In turn, this contributed to a significant disorderly depreciation of the \par exchange rate toward the end of 2021, similar in magnitude to that during the \par August 2018 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis in Turkey. \par  \par Although the authorities managed to arrest the slide in the lira through sales \par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves and the introduction of the so-called \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -protected deposit \par scheme, we consider that Turkey's balance-of-payments position remains \par fundamentally weak and is a key constraint for the sovereign ratings. The \par country has consistently run a current account deficit in the past, except in \par 2019, when imports contracted following the impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis in \par 2018. Following a reduction in the current account deficit to 1.8% of GDP in \par 2021 from almost 5.0% of GDP in 2020, the current account appears to be \par deteriorating again. In January 2022, the current account deficit totaled $7 \par billion, which in nominal U.S. dollar terms is almost half the entire deficit \par for 2021. \par  \par We consider that Turkey's longstanding structural external vulnerabilities are \par aggravated by what we view as an end to a period of ultra-loose monetary \par policy by developed-market central banks, including the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \par Additionally, the likely loss of tourist inflows from Russia and Ukraine and \par higher oil and gas prices following the onset of conflict in Ukraine could \par weaken Turkey's balance of payments as the country remains a net energy \par importer. \par  \par In parallel, the amount of external debt maturing over the next 12 months \par remains significant as of January 2022, at $174 billion, or 25% of GDP, around \par half of which pertains to the banking sector. At the same time, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves are weak. As of mid-March 2022, they amounted to $109 \par billion (16% of GDP). However, excluding the institution's foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par obligations to domestic residents, useable reserves--which represent the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's effective capacity to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --remained much lower, at $22 \par billion (3% of GDP) at the end of 2021. \par  \par We forecast that Turkey's current account deficit will widen to 3.6% of GDP in \par 2022 as oil prices are significantly higher, while tourism inflows will suffer \par from the impact of Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and goods exports \par might take a hit if there is a more pronounced slowdown of the global economy. \par There are downside risks to our forecasts, but the key reason for not \par projecting an even higher current account deficit is that we expect the \par availability of and appetite for external funding for a more sizable shortfall \par to be more limited in the future, including because of global monetary \par tightening. Given the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's limited capacity to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we expect \par the lira to correct itself further in such a scenario, in turn putting a lid \par on import growth, rather than the current account deficit continuing to expand \par unchecked. \par  \par Positively, available data so far suggest that confidence in Turkey's \par externally leveraged banking sector endures, with households choosing to keep \par their savings in the banks rather than withdraw them. Additionally, Turkish \par banks managed to roll over their sizable foreign debt coming due during \par previous episodes of financial market stress. \par  \par Nevertheless, financial stability risks are elevated, in our view. These \par could, in turn, present a contingent liability risk to the government if the \par government had to rescue a bank in a downside case, either because of a loss \par of confidence among domestic depositors, or if foreign creditors' appetite for \par rolling over Turkish banks' foreign debt were to reduce. The government has \par already contributed capital to public banks several times, with the latest \par capitalization worth 0.5% of GDP taking place in March 2022. We think that in \par a hypothetical scenario of a loss in banking sector confidence, the government \par may be called upon to contribute significantly higher amounts of equity and \par loans to the banks. \par  \par Banks' asset quality could also face further pressure, since about 38% of \par loans were denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as of December 2021, effectively \par making this debt more expensive to service as the lira depreciates. Risks to \par loan-book quality are particularly pertinent for public banks, in our view, \par since they have been heavily involved in episodes of rapid credit expansion at \par low rates in the past, raising questions about the borrowers' subsequent \par ability to repay these lines of credit. The other risk to Turkey's banks stems \par from rising global interest rates and a worsening of market conditions for the \par refinancing of external debt. We estimate the financial sector's short-term \par external debt by remaining maturity at about $83 billion or 12% of GDP as of \par end-January 2022. \par  \par The rapid depreciation of the lira also contributes directly to a rise in net \par general government debt. At the moment, about 66% of government debt is \par denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a marked shift from under 40% before 2018. An \par additional source of vulnerability for the government's balance sheet stems \par from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -protected lira deposits under a scheme that the authorities \par introduced at the end of 2021 as a measure to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Under \par this scheme, the government has committed to compensate deposit holders for \par the difference between the rate of exchange-rate depreciation and the interest \par earned on the Turkish lira deposits. Essentially, on top of its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk \par stemming from the debt denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the government has now \par assumed contingent liability risks to compensate private-sector depositors. We \par expect the government to account for the compensation directly in the budget. \par The total amount of such deposits currently stands at about $38 billion (5.5% \par of GDP), with around half of that amount being sponsored by the Treasury (that \par is, the Treasury is liable for compensation payments on that half) and the \par rest by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par That said, we consider that Turkey still has some fiscal headroom. At 34% of \par GDP, net general government debt looks favorable vis-a-vis some other emerging \par markets such as Brazil or South Africa. In our base case, we forecast that the \par general government deficit will widen to 4.2% of GDP in 2022 from an estimated \par 3.1% in 2021, driven by additional expenditure to offset the impact of higher \par energy and food prices, as well as potential additional spending ahead of the \par elections in 2023. \par  \par Positively, Turkey maintains a liquidity buffer that could help cover upcoming \par debt maturities. We estimate that liquidity buffer at about 6% of GDP at the \par end of 2021. The authorities also aim to reduce the foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  portion \par of domestic borrowing, while the average maturity of new domestic borrowing \par has increased from 37 months in 2020 to 55 months in 2021. \par  \par In our view, inflation will accelerate in the coming months, and we have \par revised our annual average inflation forecast for 2022 up to 55%. In monthly \par year-on-year terms, reported consumer price inflation could peak at over 60% \par in the coming months. Producer price inflation is even higher, reaching 105% \par year on year in February 2022. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, March 8, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, March 3, 2022 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2022 16:12 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Cuts Turkey LC Rtg To 'B+', -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment Update: February 2022, Feb. \par 25, 2022 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2022: Despite Stabilization, The \par Pandemic Threatens The Recovery, Jan. 27, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 13, 2021; a free interactive version \par is available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assess