\par\par \b \uc2 S&PGR Lowers Turkey's LC Rating to 'B+' -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par 22:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1646 ET - S&P Global Ratings says it's cutting its long-term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on Turkey to 'B+' from 'BB-,' adding its outlook was negative. S&P said fallout from the Russia-Ukraine military conflict will weaken Turkey's balance of payments and add to inflation, which is expected to average 55% this year. The nation's growth prospects have weakened, S&P said, adding it now expects growth of 2.4% in 2022, below the 3.7% forecast at the end of last year. "The negative outlook reflects what we view to be rising balance-of-payments and financial stability risks over the next 12 months amid the economic fallout from Russia's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , global monetary tightening, and possible policy missteps in the run-up to Turkey's parliamentary and presidential elections in mid-2023," S&P says. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1638 ET - Mexican stocks continue their rally with the benchmark IPC index closing up 0.1% to a record 56609. The peso is little changed from yesterday at 19.87 against the US dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports February remittances up 23% from a year earlier to $3.9B, bringing the amount for the first two months of the year to $7.8B, up 21%. "Remittances will likely maintain a strong pace in the short-term as the US economy has been quite resilient to recent headwinds," Banorte says. "More caution may be warranted if the Fed's hiking cycle is more aggressive than expected, which could hurt dynamism in the second half of the year." (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1625 ET - The WSJ Dollar Index gains 0.2% on the day and would end the week down 0.1% at current levels, Dow Jones Market Data says. Another solid gain in US payrolls didn't move the dollar much, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  ' Jonathan Petersen says, and yield gaps moving slightly in favor of other G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week left them little changed against USD. Petersen says "the lack of substantial monetary policy divergence over the next few years may limit the extent to which the dollar will strengthen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term." But he still thinks the dollar could edge higher in this tightening cycle once emphasis shifts from how fast to how far central banks will tighten, given that the US is better placed than most major economies to tighten above its "neutral rate" in this cycle. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1619 ET - Treasury yields rise with the two-year closing higher than the 10-year for the first time since August 2019. The curve inversion has often been followed by recessions in the past and investors fear the Fed reaction to rampant inflation could end up causing economic output to contract. Another strong jobs report this morning confirmed Chair Powell's assessment that the labor market is overheating, a potential reason to raise rates faster, although the data showed wage inflation losing steam. The two-year yield rose 0.131 percentage point to 2.430% this week, while the 10-year declined 0.117 p.p. to 2.374%. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1440 ET - Purchasing managers indexes for Mexico rose in March for a second consecutive month, suggesting an improvement in activity toward the end of 1Q. S&P Global's Mexico PMI [formerly \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  ] rose to 49.2 from 48 in February, IMEF's manufacturing index was up 1.2 points at 52.3, and the Inegi/Bank of Mexico index rose 0.55 points to 53.6. "Although the economic situation is still complicated, greater mobility and the opening of businesses as Covid infections fell suggest a good start to the year," says IMEF, whose services index rose 1.7 points to 52.8. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1402 ET - Brazil will produce a bigger corn crop than previously expected in the 2021-2022 season after the outlook for productivity in the country's winter crop improved, according to agricultural consultancy Safras & Mercado. The country will produce a total of 118.2M metric tons from Brazil's summer and winter crops and from the crop in the country's northern and northeastern states, compared with the previous forecast of 115.7M tons, Safras says. The consultancy raises its estimate for the summer crop, which is being harvested now, to 21.2M tons from 20.3M tons after the drought that hit Brazil's southern state had a slightly less negative impact than expected. Safras also raises its forecast for the winter crop, to 84.6M tons from 83.3M tons, based on the outlook for excellent productivity in some states. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1305 ET - The US dollar strengthens 0.2% against the euro and 0.7% against the yen. The WSJ Dollar Index gains 0.3%. G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reversed some of the strong moves of previous two to three weeks, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says. War headlines are mixed, the US yield curve inverted, eurozone inflation is persistent and the \uc2 ECB\uc2  is normalizing, the firm says. "We have been bearish on JPY but are turning tactically neutral with some risk of a pullback in USD/JPY after fiscal year end. But USD/JPY still supported by fundamentals and could test/break 125 into Q3." USD/JPY currently at 122.56. \uc2 BofA\uc2  is also bearish on the euro for the rest of the year, and is bullish USD. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1205 ET - Trucking industry payrolls fell by 4,900 jobs last month as freight demand showed signs of cooling. The decline breaks a 22-month string of jobs growth for trucking companies and contrasts with strong employment gains in March in the larger US economy. The national jobs growth reported by the Labor Department was boosted by big increases in service-sector employment, while goods-producing businesses added 60,000 jobs. Consumer-spending numbers released this week also showed a heavy tilt to services such as travel and restaurants over the goods purchasing that triggers freight shipments. Other logistics hiring is growing, with couriers and messengers adding 6,700 jobs last month and the warehousing and storage sector boosting payrolls by 4,300 jobs. (lydia.oneal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1145 ET - Economists polled in March by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  raised their median year-end inflation estimate to 5.8% from 4.68% in February, with core CPI seen ending the year at 5.47% versus 4.62% in the previous survey. The 37 analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lift its overnight interest-rate target to 8% this year from the current 6.5%, an increase from the median 7.25% estimate in the February survey. The GDP growth forecast for 2022 fell to 1.8% from 2%, with estimates ranging from 1% to 2.5%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1130 ET - There are still reasons to be optimistic about the U.S. manufacturing sector in the long run, but growth is likely to cool in the short-term, \uc2 Regions Financial Corporation\uc2  says. March's ISM survey data, which signal a slowdown in growth, don't reflect yet the recent wave of shutdowns of major manufacturing and shipping hubs across China, and supply-chain bottlenecks and shipping constraints are set to intensify, the U.S. bank says. "While this does not mean the end of the expansion in the manufacturing sector is at hand, it does mean that the pace of that expansion is likely to slow further in the months to come," it says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1109 ET - U.S. construction spending increased 0.5% in February compared with the previous month, easing from an upwardly revised 1.6% in January, data from the Commerce Department show. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expected a 1% rise in February. Outlays on residential construction increased 1.1% on month, while spending on new, single family homes rose by 2.5% compared with the previous month, the data show. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 ET - European bank stocks look attractive at their current valuation levels, and interest-rate hikes offer upside, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  analysts say. Despite the war in Ukraine, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should raise rates once in 2022 and twice in 2023, CS says. This should be particularly beneficial for domestic banks in Spain, more so than Italian ones, CS says, replacing \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  with \uc2 CaixaBank\uc2  on its top-five list of stock picks. The Swiss bank sees strong capital returns as another sector strength. Its other top picks are Lloyds, \uc2 UBS\uc2  , \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  and \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . (cristina.roca@wsj.com; @_cristinaroca)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 01, 2022 16:46 ET (20:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220401ei41003hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
