\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Outlook On Estonia To Stable; Ratings Affirmed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 22:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 31 Mar 2022 16:06 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Outlook On Estonia To Stable; Ratings Affirmed\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Russia's military action against Ukraine will weigh on the Estonian \par economy. We estimate real GDP growth at 1.8% for 2022 versus 8.3% in 2021, and \par equivalent to average quarterly growth this year of negative 0.15%. \par      -- Although Estonia still has significant fiscal policy space, we believe \par the conflict will put upward pressure on refugee and defense spending, slowing \par the budgetary consolidation path. \par      -- The uncertainty regarding the conflict's duration means that the \par potential for an upgrade has decreased in the near term. \par      -- We revised our outlook to stable from positive and affirmed our \par 'AA-/A-1+' long- and short-term sovereign credit ratings on Estonia. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On March 31, 2022, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on Estonia to stable \par from positive. At the same time, we affirmed our 'AA-/A-1+' long- and short \par term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Estonia. \par  \par As a "sovereign rating" (as defined in EU CRA Regulation 1060/2009 "EU CRA \par Regulation"), the ratings on Estonia are subject to certain publication \par restrictions set out in Art 8a of the EU CRA Regulation, including publication \par in accordance with a pre-established calendar (see "Calendar Of 2022 EMEA \par Sovereign, Regional, And Local Government Rating Publication Dates," published \par Dec. 16, 2021, on RatingsDirect). Under the EU CRA Regulation, deviations from \par the announced calendar are allowed only in limited circumstances and must be \par accompanied by a detailed explanation of the reasons for the deviation. In \par this case, the reason for the deviation is the risk of macroeconomic fallout \par due to Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The next scheduled \par publication on the sovereign ratings on Estonia will be on Aug. 19, 2022. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our expectations that the Estonian economy can \par weather temporarily reduced growth and higher spending pressures without any \par significant deterioration of its credit metrics over the next two years. The \par conflict in Ukraine will lead to a recession and potentially higher inflation \par for the Estonian economy, in our view. However, the fallout on public finances \par should prove manageable; we project that gross government debt should remain \par below 18% of GDP over the next three years. Beyond then, \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   transfers and \par Estonia's strong macroeconomic fundamentals will support a return to solid \par growth. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if the impact on Estonia from the conflict were \par more significant than we expect, or if the conflict escalated beyond Russia \par and Ukraine. This would imply economic growth and external accounts falling \par significantly short of our projections, and potentially leading to protracted \par fiscal support measures that weaken public finances much more than we \par anticipate. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could raise the ratings if Estonia's economy returns to its pre-conflict \par path. Under this scenario, Estonia's high-value-added services sector would \par post renewed productivity gains over the next few years, boosting incomes, and \par leading to an improved economic assessment. We could also raise the ratings if \par external position metrics exceeded our expectations; for example, if there \par were persistently high current account surpluses leading to a net external \par asset position for Estonia (excluding foreign direct investment [FDI] \par liabilities). \par  \par RATIONALE \par We expect that the indirect effects of Russia's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will push Estonia's small open economy into recession, while \par redoubling inflationary pressure, already notable throughout second-half 2021. \par As a result, we have revised down our real GDP growth rate projections for \par Estonia to 1.8% in 2022 from an estimate of 3.5% before the conflict (and \par versus GDP growth of 8.3% in 2021). This projection is equivalent to an \par average decline of negative 0.15% quarter over quarter for 2022. At the same \par time, the conflict is driving up hydrocarbon and other commodity prices, with \par evidence of a renewed broadening of inflationary pressure. Inflation touched \par 11.6% year over year in February 2022 (with wage increases averaging just \par under 7%), and is likely to remain elevated throughout 2022, weighing on \par households' disposable incomes and business confidence in what continues to be \par a very tight labor market. \par  \par Given the significant uncertainty and risk that the conflict entails, we \par believe upside potential on the rating has decreased in the near term, leading \par us to revise our outlook to stable from positive. \par  \par In our view, the conflict's effects on Estonia's economy will stem from \par worsening supply chain bottlenecks, leading to trade disruptions; rising \par commodity and energy prices pushing up inflation and eroding household and \par business spending capacity; and weakening external demand. At the same time, \par although Estonia's direct exposure to Russia has decreased significantly over \par the past decade, it remains relatively sizable. Exports to Russia, Ukraine, \par and Belarus combined represent about 5% (about half of national origin) of \par total exports, while imports represent close to 15%. More than half of imports \par of petroleum-related products come from Russia, making some sectors--for \par example, transportation--particularly vulnerable to trade disruptions. \par  \par Estonia is one of the few producers globally of oil shale, which covers 70% of \par domestic energy requirements. This enables Estonia to be largely \par self-sufficient in energy, albeit at the cost of rising carbon dioxide \par emissions and significant levels of hazardous waste connected to oil shale \par combustion and refining. Close to one-tenth of the EUR969 million in Next \par Generation EU grants for Estonia are targeted at reducing the economy's \par reliance on oil shale in electricity generation. Investments in green \par transition, digitalization, and real estate will support growth in 2022-2025, \par largely funded by inflows of EU funds. The slowdown in global demand and \par economic activity among Estonia's key trading will hurt the country's small \par and open economy and reduce exports. However, we consider overall risks to \par Estonia's external position low. The country has a solid external position, \par thanks to small surpluses, which have aided consecutive net external \par deleveraging over the past few years. \par  \par We believe spending pressure will rise as a consequence of the Russia-Ukraine \par conflict via, for example, already announced defense spending of EUR600 \par million until 2025; compensation to households for rising energy bills; and \par costs to cater for the large inflow of refugees. This could delay Estonia's \par consolidation efforts post pandemic, in our view. Nevertheless, Estonia's \par public finances remain robust, despite the temporary increase in spending \par induced by the COVID-19 pandemic and the ongoing conflict. We predict that net \par debt will rise slowly over the next few years, but stay below 10% of GDP, \par which is exceptionally low by global standards. \par  \par In our view, external security risks for Estonia, which borders Russia, have \par risen since the conflict's outbreak. However, Estonia has been a member of \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NATO\f28 \uc2 \f2   since 2004 and we believe this reduces the risk of the conflict \par escalating beyond Ukraine's borders. \f28 \uc2 \f2 NATO\f28 \uc2 \f2   has repeatedly confirmed its \par commitment to Article 5, stating that if one member was subject to an armed \par attack, the other members would provide support. Since the start of the \par military conflict, \f28 \uc2 \f2 NATO\f28 \uc2 \f2   troops in Estonia have doubled. That said, other \par security risks, including to cyber security, are on the rise. \par  \par The ratings remain supported by the established institutional set-up and a \par track record of strong fiscal and economic policies. Further strengths are \par Estonia's history of modest fiscal deficits and low public debt. As a eurozone \par member, Estonia benefits from access to the monetary union's deep capital \par markets and monetary policy support from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  . The \par ratings are constrained by Estonia's moderate income levels compared with \par those of similarly rated peers. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Environmental, Social, And Governance Principles In \par Credit Ratings, Oct. 10, 2021 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Principles Of Credit Ratings, Feb. 16, 2011 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings Score Snapshot, March 8, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, March 3, 2022 \par      -- Estonia, Feb. 21, 2022 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2020 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, April 12, 2021 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 31 Mar 2022 16:06 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Outlook On Estonia -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352 }}}\uc2 \f2  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par www.capitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at www.standardandpoors.com. Use the \par Ratings search box located in the left column.  Alternatively, call one of the \par following S&P Global Ratings numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; \par London Press Office (44) 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) \par 69-33-999-225; or Stockholm (46) 8-440-5914 \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Johanna Melinder, Stockholm +  46 84 40 5926; \par                         johanna.melinder@spglobal.com \par Secondary Contact: Niklas Steinert, Frankfurt +  49 693 399 9248; \par                    niklas.steinert@spglobal.com \par Additional Contact: Sovereign and IPF EMEA; \par                     SOVIPF@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par The article with statistical information is available in full \par at www.spglobal.com. Registration is free. To access the full article, \par select "Ratings Actions" in the left navigation under Ratings R