\par\par \b \uc2 Top News Today: Stocks Move Lower; Biden to Tap Oil Reserves in Bid to Tame High Fuel Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 2,746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARKET WRAPS\par \par \uc2 STOCKS: U.S. stocks fell to cap a quarter in which Federal Reserve monetary tightening and the Russian invasion of Ukraine have weighed on sentiment. The S&P 500 posted its first quarterly loss in two years.\par \par \uc2 TREASURYS: Treasury prices rose, pulling down yields, even after data showed the Fed's preferred inflation gauge jumping again.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The dollar strengthened against the euro but weakened against the yen.\par \par \uc2 COMMODITIES: Oil prices settled sharply lower with the U.S. announcing its largest-ever release from the nation's crude reserves and the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  and its allies sticking to a previously agreed plan to raise output in May.\par \par \uc2 HEADLINES\par \par \uc2 U.S. Inflation Rises to 40-Year Peak in February by Fed's Preferred Measure\par \par \uc2 Inflation reached a new 40-year peak in February, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's preferred gauge, buoyed by supply problems and strong U.S. consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department said Thursday that its personal-consumption-expenditures price index climbed 6.4% in February from a year ago, faster than the 6% increase for the year that ended in January. The February rise was the fastest since 1982.\par \par \uc2 Biden to Draw Down Oil Reserves in Bid to Ease Gas Prices\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON-President Biden will tap up to 180 million barrels of government oil reserves to help tamp down near-record high fuel prices, an unprecedented government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into oil markets following Russia's invasion of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In remarks from the White House Thursday, Mr. Biden framed high energy prices as a wartime issue that requires a robust and wide-ranging response. The oil release-about 1 million barrels a day for six months, starting in May-would be the biggest-ever drawdown from the country's emergency stockpile of roughly 568 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said he would also invoke the Defense Production Act, a Korean War-era national security mobilization law, to boost domestic output of minerals used in batteries for electric vehicles and other clean-energy technology.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Probes Meeting Between Activision CEO and Option Buyer\par \par \uc2 Authorities investigating timely trading in \uc2 Activision Blizzard Inc\uc2 . securities are looking at at least one meeting between the videogame firm's chief executive and one of three traders days before they placed a large bet on \uc2 Activision\uc2  shares, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Activision\uc2  CEO \uc2 Bobby Kotick\uc2  met with Alexander von Furstenberg in the week before Mr. von Furstenberg and media moguls \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  and David Geffen bought options to purchase \uc2 Activision\uc2  shares at $40 each on Jan. 14. The options trade, which has generated an unrealized profit of about $59 million, was arranged days before \uc2 Activision\uc2  agreed to be acquired for $95 a share by \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 ., The Wall Street Journal has reported.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is investigating whether the options trade violated insider-trading laws, the people familiar with the matter said. The \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  is separately conducting a civil insider-trading investigation, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Following Fed's Lead, Banks Shift Forecasts to Big Rate Increases\par \par \uc2 After key \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials opened the door to raising interest rates by more than the customary quarter-percentage point, several of Wall Street's biggest banks now believe the increases will come in jumbo increments.\par \par \uc2 Major banks in recent days have predicted some combination of half-percentage point rate rises by the Fed throughout the year. The Fed usually moves its federal-funds rate target by quarter-percentage point increments, but faced with surging inflation bringing the worst price pressures in 40 years, economists believe aggressive action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now likely.\par \par \uc2 The Fed lifted the federal-funds rate earlier this month for the first time since 2018, to a range of between 0.25% and 0.50%. The Fed's next meeting is scheduled for May 3-4, and it is likely to result in a half-percentage point increase, with more such increases later in the year, in the view of many large banks.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  Posts Higher Sales, Powered by Covid-19 Vaccine, Testing Demand\par \par \uc2 Walgreens Boots Alliance Inc\uc2 . posted higher sales for the most recent quarter as demand for Covid-19 tests and vaccines continued to drive growth.\par \par \uc2 Executives said Thursday they are strategizing how the company's drugstores can continue playing a key role in providing diagnostic and other health services beyond the current pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "Our pharmacists and our technicians are more than capable, as we've shown during the pandemic, of delivering these kinds of services," said \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  President \uc2 John Standley\uc2 . "That's a pretty big game changer if we can gain access to that market and in terms of how we grow our business and in terms of the value we can provide in the communities we serve."\par \par \uc2 Bain Capital\uc2  Looks at Possible Takeover Bid for \uc2 Toshiba\par \par \uc2 TOKYO-Bain Capital said it was examining a possible bid for \uc2 Toshiba Corp\uc2 ., and \uc2 Toshiba\uc2 's top shareholder pledged its support to Bain, making initial steps toward fulfilling foreign shareholders' desire to take the Japanese conglomerate private.\par \par \uc2 Bain, a U.S.-based private-equity firm, said it wanted to hold "careful and sincere" discussions with \uc2 Toshiba\uc2 's management, the Japanese government and banks over the possible deal. It cautioned that no decisions have been made.\par \par \uc2 "There are many issues that need to be addressed before delisting \uc2 Toshiba\uc2 ," Bain said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Mortgage Rates Surge to Highest Since 2018\par \par \uc2 The average rate for a 30-year fixed-rate home loan jumped to 4.67%, mortgage-finance giant \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  said Thursday, marking the weekly figure's highest reading since December 2018.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year fixed rate rose from 4.42% a week ago, continuing a steady rise that has pushed home-loan rates within sight of 5% for the first time in four years. This year's surge in mortgage rates was hardly unforeseen, given the record lows reached in the pandemic period and concerns about high U.S. inflation readings, but it has unfolded faster than expected. At the beginning of the year, the average rate on America's most popular home loan was 3.22%.\par \par \uc2 Inflation Weighed on Consumer Spending Growth in February\par \par \uc2 Consumer spending growth, a key engine of the economy, slowed sharply in February, as the Omicron surge of Covid-19 eased and inflation accelerated amid Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 U.S. households boosted their sending at a seasonally adjusted 0.2% pace in February from the month before, down from a revised 2.7% rate in January, when spending rebounded from an Omicron-related dip in December, the Commerce Department said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 SEC Tells Exchanges to Treat Customer Crypto Holdings as Liabilities\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON-Publicly traded cryptocurrency exchanges would have to report their customers' digital tokens as liabilities on their balance sheets under guidance released Thursday by the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The guidelines are designed to introduce consistency to the way crypto-trading platforms account for their holdings of assets like bitcoin on behalf of customers, SEC officials said.\par \par \uc2 WWE\uc2  Expands Reach to Traditional TV Shows-and Yes, They're About Wrestling\par \par \uc2 World Wrestling Entertainment Inc\uc2 . is starting to produce fictionalized TV shows, part of an effort to generate more revenue outside of the wrestling ring and capitalize on streaming services' growing hunger for fresh content.\par \par \uc2 The company, which runs the world's most famous wrestling league, said it has two scripted TV series in the works: a Spanish-language comedy about an aspiring female wrestler in Mexico and a drama about a fictional family that runs a wrestling company.\par \par \uc2 Colin Campbell, \uc2 WWE\uc2 's vice president of development, said the company expects the shows to help it reach new audiences and expand its relationships with media companies. \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALKING POINTS CFOs Find It Harder to Land Board Seats as Companies Seek Change By Nina Trentmann\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As company boards start to put the chaos of Covid-19 behind them, directors are focusing on new sets of issues, such as those related to environmental, social and governance matters or cybersecurity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That is spurring companies to fill their boardrooms with a wider range of experiences and skills, making it difficult for some CFOs to find a seat at the table with financial expertise alone. Slightly less than a quarter of chief financial officers-23%-at companies in the Fortune 500 hold board positions, down from a high of 32% in 2009, according to recruitment firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spencer Stuart\f28 \uc2 \f2 . This includes CFOs who sit on their companies' own boards.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Financial experts increasingly are competing with other skill sets like technology or human-capital expertise," said Joel von Ranson, who leads \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spencer Stuart\f28 \uc2 \f2 's global functional practices, adding that finance chiefs remain sought after to chair audit, compensation or governance committees.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With many boards undergoing a refreshment cycle, their size is growing, resulting in boards at S&P 500 companies having an average of 11.2 board members now, up from 11.1 in 2021 and 11.0 in 2020, according to ISS Corporate Solutions, a data and analytics provider. Meanwhile, the average tenure on a board has fallen to 7.7 years in 2021 from 8.7 years in 2011, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spencer Stuart\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are seeing a rise in demand for people with different backgrounds: racially diverse directors, people with cybersecurity experience, experience in how to deal with climate change, or in human-capital management," said Brianna Castro, a senior director of governance research at proxy advisory services company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glass, Lewis & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . A recent SEC proposal on new cyber disclosure could boost the demand for such experts further, Ms. Castro said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Marija Kramer\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of ISS Corporate Solutions, said that given the increase in sustainable investing and the rise of ESG corporate ratings, "it's no surprise we're seeing demand for prospective corporate directors with ESG credentials."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 31 Mar 2022 16:33 ET \par Top News Today: Stocks Move Lower; Biden to Tap -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These shifts mean CFOs have to communicate clearly to boards about what they can bring to the table. "It's about positioning yourself as a two-for: You bring the financial expertise, and you bring something else, " said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spencer Stuart\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Mr. von Ranson.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amy Butte, a former CFO of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said she is positioning herself as an audit chair for boards and an expert for initial public offerings. "This has helped with differentiating from others," she said, adding that "if you are a generalist, it is hard" to do that.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ms. Butte sits on several boards, including those of \f28 \uc2 \f2 DigitalOcean Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., a cloud infrastructure company; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bain Capital Specialty Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a business development firm; and BNP Paribas USA, the U.S. arm of the French bank. "It is really about the experience" that one brings to the company, Ms. Butte said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Read Full Article on NewsPlus\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ukrainians Who Sheltered in the Mariupol Theater Made It a Thriving Village By Brett Forrest in Odessa, Ukraine and Isabel Coles in Zaporizhzhia, Ukraine\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Russia laid siege to the Ukrainian port city of Mariupol, battering apartment blocks and killing civilians, more than 1,000 people took shelter inside the local performing-arts theater.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Evgeniya, a theater lighting technician, and her husband, Sergey, arrived on Feb. 25 and welcomed the first few dozen people. "Someone had to go and help," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A barrage from air, land and sea terrorized Mariupol's 450,000 residents, destroying most of the city's habitable buildings. The theater became a last-chance refuge, including for many who had once gone to see plays there and those left homeless. By then, the theater had no electricity, no food, no running water and just six bathrooms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Evgeniya and her husband, an actor, had worked at the theater for two decades and knew its offices and passageways, its strong walls and false ones. For nearly three weeks, their knowledge and gut instincts directed the daily lives of those crowding in the building.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We clutched at our heads," Evgeniya said. "This is madness. I panicked because I didn't know how to organize all these people."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chaos would transform into a mini-society created from scratch, according to interviews with more than a dozen people who stayed at or visited the Mariupol theater after the Russian invasion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At first, Evgeniya and Sergey admitted only women and children. The couple had brought their own kids thinking they might be there for some time. They soon opened the doors to everyone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Severed Internet services plunged Mariupol into an information blackout. People who emerged from the darkness of basements in the daylight hours, when the Russian shelling subsided, followed others to the theater, hoping for news about an organized exit from the city.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nadya Navka lived on the left bank of the Kalmius River, on the other side of town. She caught a ride to the city center with her husband and mother after shelling shattered the windows and doors of their home. She saw people streaming toward the theater and joined them. "If people gather there," she said, "then if there is any evacuation, it's going to be from there."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Read Full Article on NewsPlus \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 WEALTH MANAGEMENT How Much More Will You Spend in Retirement Than Expected? My Rule: $400 a Month\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 You should tell readers about your "$400 rule." Maybe they have some rules of their own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's my brother Stephen speaking. Yes, I have a "rule" about retirement and nest eggs. I don't pretend that it's anything as consequential as, say, the "4% rule." (That's the one tied to annual withdrawals from savings.) And my research, admittedly, involves a small sample. (Just me.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, I think it's interesting how this "guideline" (a better word) has worked in my retirement. What follows, then, is a brief explanation-and a request.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Actually, my guideline is borrowed. Several years ago, as I was approaching retirement from full-time work, a person-and I'm afraid I can't recall who it was, but I'm happy to give credit if you're reading this-told me: "You will spend about $400 each month in retirement above and beyond what you think you'll spend."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 I remember thinking: This sounds silly. Surely, I can craft an annual budget for retirement that doesn't miss the mark by almost $5,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And yet...more months than not, my wife and I find ourselves with a bill, or combination of bills, that total about $400.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Glenn Ruffenach \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expected Major Events for Friday\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 00:30/JPN: Mar Japan Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 05:00/JPN: Mar Auto sales\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 06:00/RUS: Mar Russian Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 07:45/ITA: Mar Italy Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 07:50/FRA: Mar France Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 07:55/GER: Mar Germany Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 08:00/ITA: Feb Foreign Trade non-EU\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 08:30/UK: 3Q Postcode Lending statistics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 08:30/UK: Mar CIPS / Markit Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:30/US: Mar U.S. Employment Report\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13:30/CAN: Mar Canada Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 13:45/US: Mar US Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14:00/US: Mar ISM Report On Business Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 14:00/US: Feb Construction Spending - Construction Put in Place\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15:00/US: Mar Global Manufacturing PMI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 20:00/US: Mar Domestic Auto Industry Sales\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All times in GMT. Powered by Kantar Media and Dow Jones. \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expected Earnings for Friday\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blade Air Mobility Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BLDE) is expe