\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The effective scope of economic artillery against Russia\b0\par\par\uc2 2,794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Effective range is the maximum distance at which a weapon can fire with sufficient accuracy to cause damage or casualties. In the face of Russia's bellicose attacks on Ukraine, the United States and its Western allies have chosen to use sanctions that have an effective range on the Russian economy with three objectives: 1) to isolate Russia from international trade, 2) to lock it out of the financial system, and 3) to generate a deep economic recession. On February 24, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  announced that it would take significant and unprecedented steps to "impose immediate costs and disrupt and degrade future activity; isolate Russia from international trade and finance; and degrade the Kremlin's future ability to project power."\par \par \uc2 Objective 1: Isolate Russia from international trade According to economists C. Syropoulos and Y. Yotov, about half of the economic sanctions imposed in history have partially achieved their stated objectives and about 35% fully achieved their goals. Never in history have equivalent sanctions been applied to an economy like Russia's, which is three times the size of Venezuela, seven times the size of Iran and 14 times the size of Cuba. However, in order to assess the effective scope of these measures, it is pertinent to analyze their potential impact on both Russia and the world. Russia is the world's 11th largest economy by GDP volume, equivalent to 1.4 times the size of Mexico. During 2019, its exports reached a value of 407 billion dollars, equivalent to 20% of its GDP. Mexico, being a smaller country, reached an export level 15% higher than that of Russia. * With data from 'The Observatory of Economic Complexity'. Additionally, Mexico has a higher Economic Complexity Index (ECI), which measures how diversified and complex the basket of exported goods is, determined by the knowledge and specialization required for its production. While the ECI for Russia is 0.58, it is 1.22 for Mexico and 0.98 for the G20. Russia's low complexity is due to its high dependence on energy, which represents 59% of its total exports. However, it is relevant to identify which goods are significant for the world. If Russian systemic goods are considered to be those with a presence of more than 5% in global exports, the following stand out: nickel and its manufactures; fertilizers; fuels and mineral oils; cereals; wood and wood products; iron and steel and rails. For whom is Russia important? Belarus is the country most dependent on Russian systemic goods, since 66% of its total imports of such goods are of Russian origin. This justifies its attitude as an ally in the conflict with Ukraine. It is relevant that the vast majority of countries highly dependent on Russian products voted against or abstained from the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  resolution deploring Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The only countries that, despite maintaining a high trade relationship with Russia, showed before the \uc2 UN\uc2  a stance against Russian war actions were Estonia, Latvia, Finland and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, if Russia were to keep as trading partners only those countries that supported it at the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 , it would lose $183 billion a year, equivalent to 45% of its total exports, considering only systemic goods. In contrast, the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) \uc2 member countries that have supported Ukraine, in some cases, are major importers of Russian systemic goods. Latvia stands out, with 30% of its total imports of such goods originating in Russia. Although Italy and Germany are not among the countries most dependent on Russian systemic goods, their dependence on imports of fuels and mineral oils from Russia is around 20%. Russia is the third largest oil producer in the world, behind the United States and Saudi Arabia. Therefore, when its oil exports are stopped or reduced, its availability decreases and its price increases in the short term, causing increases in the price of gasoline and its derivatives, as well as in transportation costs, which exerts greater inflationary pressures worldwide. However, current prices are making oil production profitable for some countries and fields that are currently facing losses and are considered unconventional sources of oil. The most likely candidates to take advantage of this opportunity in Europe and the Americas are Norway, the United States, Mexico, Venezuela and Brazil. The arrival of new entrants or increases in the production of participants already in the market will continue to contribute to a decrease in the price also during 2023, for that year, based on estimates by the \uc2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\uc2  (EIA), the price of Brent oil is expected to be US$89 per barrel, 15% lower than estimated for 2022. The replacement of Russian oil exports with oil from other sources, the transition to activities that do not depend on this fuel and investment in other types of energy will contribute to an adjustment in demand that will help lower the price of oil. On the other hand, replacing Russian natural gas production is more complex, given that its distribution depends on an installed logistics infrastructure of gas pipelines. To reduce this gap between demand and supply, European countries would need to resort to the use of nuclear, renewable, hydroelectric and coal energy. Despite this, the EIA estimates that this year it will reach $4.10 per million British thermal units, 94% more than in 2020, but only 1% more than that observed in 2021. As for Russian imports, the items on which Russia is most dependent from abroad are machinery and household appliances and electrical machinery. In that sense, the most important sanctions are related to technology transfer. In an interview with The New Yorker , Stephen Kotkin, a scholar of Russian history, exposed that "if the United States denies them software, equipment and products manufactured in the country affects almost all important technologies in the world, and has an objective and an enforceable mechanism to do so, it can damage this regime and create a technological desert."\par \par \uc2 Objective 2: Remove Russia from the global financial system While the world will perceive significant consequences of sanctions towards Russia in the short and medium term, countries with sufficient capacity to meet global demands, attracted by opportunity prices and space for new entrants, will be able to replace Russia's role in the markets and generate price stability and availability of goods. Although the trade restrictions Russia has faced have not directly targeted medical and pharmaceutical products, Russian producers in these industries have difficulties in financing their vaccine development projects, receiving international transfers for the purchase of their products and logistical complications in transporting them. It will be difficult for Russia to regain the economic dynamics it had without engaging in international trade. The first objective of pulling Russia out of international trade is proving successful and Russia will miss the world more than the world will miss Russia. The measures targeting the Russian financial system announced by the United States, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the rest of the allies are capable of unleashing financial chaos in Russia. As a result of the sanctions implemented, the ruble has lost more than a quarter of its value (equivalent to the exchange rate between the Mexican peso and the US dollar going from 20.9 to 26.4) and its usability outside Russia is low. Consequently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than doubled the interest rate to attract foreign investments and stop the fall of the ruble, going from 9.50% to 20.00%, only below Argentina (42.50%) and almost twice as high as Brazil (10.75%). When comparing the monetary policy exercised by Russia in recent months with different emerging countries, it can be seen how Western countries have been successful in their strategy of cornering the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by leaving the interest rate as their only monetary tool to face the chaos. Despite the fact that the Bank of Russia has around $630 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold reserves, which, in theory, would be more than enough to face any sanctions. Two days after Putin's attack, on February 26, governments allied with the West agreed to restrict Russia's access to its reserves, and subsequently, on March 11, denied it borrowing privileges in multilateral financial institutions. In June 2021, according to the Bank of Russia, about 65% of Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be located in Western countries and international organizations. Assuming the same proportions today, about 400 billion of Russia's reserves could be subject to sanctions. While this is a considerable amount and will make it difficult for the Bank to support the ruble, provide foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity to Russian banks and make or receive foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  payments to Russian companies, it remains a priority to stop access to the nearly $300 billion that the Russian government still has at its disposal thanks to the support of China, which has not joined the sanctions. * Note: The gray bars represent reserves not subject to sanctions, the only thing Russia can use. ** With data from the Bank of Russia.\par \par \uc2 Objective 3: Generate a deep economic recession in Russia Conclusions In addition to the complex monetary environment, Russian financial institutions were banned from accessing the global messaging system for financial transactions (SWIFT). The measure hinders Russia from collecting payment for its exports of gas, oil, minerals and wheat, and paying for its imports of other goods. On March 2, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  announced that it would ban seven Russian banks from the network as part of the sanctions package, including \uc2 VTB\uc2 , VEB and Otkritie. That would make it difficult for \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2 , the banking tool of gas company \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  and Russia's third-largest lender to receive payments. While the measure was not generalized for the rest of Russia's banks, it is a decisive move. "The fact that they are doing it, when they explicitly said a week ago that they were not going to do it, shows the seriousness of this effort," said Daniel Glaser, former undersecretary of the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 . In summary, the financial measures imposed on Russia have caused the ruble to lose its value and not to be accepted as a method of payment outside Russia; the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased its interest rate due to the fact that its international reserves are frozen and the main Russian banks are unable to receive and execute payments in the international market. According to multiple analysts, as a consequence of Western governments imposing trade and financial sanctions, Russia is heading for an economic recession. Prior to the announcement of the ban on Russian oil imports by the United States and the United Kingdom, economists at \uc2 JP Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 . estimated a 7% contraction in Russian GDP this year, the same figure projected by \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . For its part, Bloomber Economics forecast a fall of around 9%; however, it pointed out that the blockage of oil and gas exports would mean that the economy could contract by around 14% this year and, similarly, Robin Brooks, chief economist at the Institute of International Finance (IIF), estimated that, following the tightening of financial conditions, Russian GDP will fall by 15% by 2022. Now, according to Brooks, the forecast of average annual growth in 2022 of -15% for Russia compares the average level of GDP in 2022 with that of 2021, so the implied fall in GDP would be deeper, close to 30%. This would imply that Russia's GDP would rebound to a level similar to that of 2005. Not being enough, rating agencies \uc2 Fitch\uc2  and S&P downgraded Russia's sovereign rating, saying that a default is imminent, as sanctions and trade restrictions have undermined its willingness to repay debt. The further increase in sanctions and proposals that could limit energy trade increase the likelihood of a policy response from Russia that includes selective default on its sovereign debt obligations. If Russian GDP contracts 30% in 2022, the proportion of the Russian population in poverty (living on $5.5 or less per day) would double and return to 2009 levels. In other words, 4.4 million people would be added to the population in poverty, equivalent to the population of Panama. * Note: the estimate was made through a simple logarithmic regression to find the relationship between economic growth and the proportion of the population living on 5.5 dollars a day (PPP). With data from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 . In general, the above confirms what has been established in academic works such as that of O'Discroll (2017): "The imposition of economic sanctions slows economic growth and development, widens the poverty gap, exacerbates inequalities and negatively impacts other factors such as worsening the quality of life and increasing government repression on the citizenry". Economic sanctions imposed by Western countries have been successfully advanced. While Russia has not put a stop to Ukraine's attack, its economy is contracting by the day and, if the severity of the sanctions continues, it will only be a matter of time before Russia loses the ability to sustain its armed war. The goal of isolating Russia from global trade has not only put at risk its more than $400 billion in exports and the goods it buys from abroad, but has impacted the entire Russian domestic market. The increase in prices, the shortage of products and the cessation of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country by many private companies impact the entire economy as a whole. In addition to the above, the purchasing power has deteriorated, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost the recognition of the rest of the world and it is impossible to carry out transactions in the international market due to the measures aimed at removing Russia from the global financial system. The measures imposed on Russia have generated a catastrophe; it is estimated that its economy will contract up to 30% this year, reaching the goal of generating a deep recession in the country; however, the main affected will be the Russian population. The exodus of companies translates into a loss of jobs and a decrease in the quantity and variety of consumer products.\par \par \uc2 The cut in labor income for the newly unemployed, as well as the devaluation of the ruble and the increase in interest rates, will impact their quality of life and the lives of those who depend on that income. It is estimated that the Russian economic recession will lead to a doubling of the proportion of the population living in poverty, which, in the short, medium and long term, will have severe repercussions for the economy as a whole. Prior to the invasion of Ukraine, Putin's approval rating was 71%. However, growing protest sentiment and a further weakening of Russians' feelings that they have a say in the decisions being made in their country may lead to a cascade of disapproval. The analysis focused on Russia because of its scale, but a similar exercise could be conducted for Ukraine, where distortions are also being generated in its participation in global trade, directly impacting that country, its trading partners and those who depend on goods for which Ukraine is systemic. According to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , the Ukrainian economy could shrink by 35% if Russia's invasion turns into a prolonged conflict, so the economic impact of the armed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be similar to that of the West's economic artillery against Russia. Other countries in Europe and the rest of the world will have minor direct and indirect impacts, for some even new opportunities will arise. This war must be evaluated with metrics other than the traditional military metrics, since the sanctions are generating changes in economic variables such as the exchange rate, the price of goods, financial conditions, substitution of inputs for production, among others. In this case, we can conclude that the effective reach of the economic artillery used against Russia is fulfilling the three stated objectives, generating losses (especially for Russia, where they are exponentially greater), but also opportunities to reconfigure the global economy and take better advantage of trade alliances. Editor's notes: * Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Meade (@JoseAMeadeK) is a lawyer and holds a PhD in Economics. He was Secretary of State on five occasions. He currently carries out consulting and academic activities. * Emilio Fueyo (@efueyo) is an economist and political scientist. He has collaborated in federal agencies such as SRE, \uc2 SEDESOL\uc2  and SHCP. He is currently a founding partner of EMANT Consultores and specializes in economic, financial and public policy issues. * Paulina Peralta (@PaulinaPeralta_) is an economist and works as an Associate at EMANT Consultores, where she deals with economic analysis and public policy issues.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220331ei3v009sv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
