l humanitarian law," albeit a "common" tactic in war, lamented the \uc2 WHO\uc2 's top representative in Ukraine, Jarno Habicht, who emphasized that healthcare "is not and should never be a target."\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:55\par \par \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  calls on the world to demonstrate against Russian invasionThe president of Ukraine called on the citizens of the world to demonstrate against the Russian invasion of their country.\par \par \uc2 "Come with Ukrainian symbols to support Ukraine, to support freedom, to support life," he said in a video in which he spoke in English.\par \par \uc2 "Come to your squares, to your streets, be visible and listen," he added.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:38\par \par \uc2 Sanctions begin to affect the everyday life of Russians.Four weeks after the offensive in Ukraine began, the first concrete effects of Western sanctions and crackdown are beginning to be felt in the everyday life of Russians. Here are some examples.\par \par \uc2 Read more.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:21\par \par \uc2 U.S. reported that Russian soldiers are falling back on defensive positions outside Kiev Russian ground forces appear to be digging in and establishing defensive positions 15 to 20 kilometers outside Kiev after recording minimal or no advances toward the city center, a senior U.S. Defense official reported Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The official said it appears that forces are no longer attempting to advance toward the city center and, in some cases east of Kiev, Ukrainian soldiers have been able to push Russian troops away.\par \par \uc2 He added that Russian forces had been 20 to 30 kilometers east and northeast of the capital, but are now 55 kilometers away.\par \par \uc2 Instead of advancing, the official explained, Russian troops are committing more resources toward the eastern Donbas region, particularly in Luhansk and Donetsk.\par \par \uc2 The official said the U.S. has seen that Russia 