els, the president exchanged a brief greeting with the Belgian Prime Minister, \uc2 Alexander De Croo\uc2 , and with the three U.S. ambassadors based in that city, before heading to his hotel.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 18:20\par \par \uc2 Renault\uc2  announced "immediate" suspension of activity at Moscow plantThe French automaker announced the suspension of activities at its Moscow factory "as of this very day," after Ukraine called for an international boycott of the firm for remaining in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Renault\uc2  also said in a statement that it is examining options for its Russian subsidiary \uc2 AvtoVAZ\uc2 , adding that these decisions had forced it to revise downward its financial outlook for 2022.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 18:04\par \par \uc2 Russia bans Google News serviceRussia's Federal Service for Supervision of Communications, Information Technologies and Mass Media, or \uc2 Roskomnadzor\uc2 , banned the Google News service, The Kyiv Independent highlighted.\par \par \uc2 According to the information, they accuse \uc2 Google\uc2  of spreading "fake news" about Russia's war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 17:49\par \par \uc2 Russian factory forced to stop production of tanksUralvagonzavod, the historic tank and armored vehicle factory in Russia, was forced to stop production due to sanctions imposed on Moscow, as it is unable to obtain key components that are manufactured abroad.\par \par \uc2 This was stated by the General Staff of the Armed Forces of Ukraine and press sources in Ukraine, which, however, were not confirmed by Russia.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge industrial complex, located in Nizhny Tagil in the Urals region, which cannot but face significant difficulties due to the strict isolation to which the Russian Federation is subjected.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 17:33\par \par \uc2 Video: how the attack in which the Russian journalist was killed went downA group of journalists from Al Jazeera TV captured the moment of the bombing in which Oksana Baulina, a journalist from The Insider, Latvia, was killed.\par \par \uc2 It shows how they were broadcasting live from the same place, outside the shopping mall.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 17:16\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  is "concerned" about possible Russian cyber attacksFBI Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  said the agency is "concerned" about the possibility of Russian cyber attacks against critical U.S. infrastructure in the wake of the Russian invasion over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "The reason we're concerned is not just based on our longstanding understanding of how the Russians operate, but it's actually the product of specific investigative and surveillance work that we've all been doing together," Wray told an audience at the Detroit Economic Club.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 16:58\par \par \uc2 Russian journalist killed in Kiev after rocket hit a shopping mall Oksana Baulina, a journalist for The Insider , was killed by a rocket in Kiev. Photo courtesy of.\par \par \uc2 Oksana Baulina, journalist of The Insider media, from Latvia, "died under fire in Kiev," they explained on the website. "The journalist was filming the destruction that occurred after Russian troops shelled the Podolsk district of the capital," they continued.\par \par \uc2 Another civilian was also killed, they reported, and two other people accompanying her were injured and hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 "Before joining our media project, Oksana worked as a producer for the \uc2 Anti-Corruption Foundation\uc2 . After the organization was labeled as an extremist organization, she had to leave Russia in order to continue reporting on Russian government corruption for The Insider," they detailed.\par \par \uc2 "Oksana went to Ukraine as a correspondent, and managed to send several reports from Lviv and Kiev. The Insider expresses its deepest condolences to Oksana's family and friends," the statement concluded.Read more.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 16:41\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil closed above 120 dollarsA barrel of Brent North Sea crude for May delivery closed up 5.29% at $121.60 a barrel on Wednesday, boosted by the prospect of new sanctions against Russia and concerns about hydrocarbon supply.\par \par \uc2 In New York, the barrel of West Texas Intermediate (WTI), for May, climbed 5.17%, to end at $114.93.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 16:21\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  to deploy more troops to Bulgaria, Slovakia, Hungary and PolandThe 30 NATO heads of state or government are meeting Thursday in Brussels with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  as a guest via videoconference and with the priority mission of showing unity and announcing more military deployments on the eastern flank in the face of fears of Russia.\par \par \uc2 Read more.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 15:59\par \par \uc2 U.S. reiterates that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces committed war crimes in UkraineUkrainian rescuers with a woman and her baby in Mariupol. Both would later die. AP Photo.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Secretary of State, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , wrote a statement in which he reiterates that the Russian armed forces committed "war crimes" "Since launching his unprovoked and unjust war, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has unleashed unrelenting violence that has caused death and destruction throughout Ukraine," Blinken's letter begins.\par \par \uc2 In it, he asserts that there have been "numerous credible reports of indiscriminate attacks and deliberately targeted attacks against civilians, as well as other atrocities." "Many of the places attacked by Russian forces have been clearly identifiable as places for civilian use," he said and exemplified with the maternity hospital and a theater in Mariupol, which he said were marked with the word "????" - "children" in Russian.\par \par \uc2 Read more.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 15:42\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  estimates Russian soldiers killed in Ukraine at 7,000 to 15,000.Covered corpses in Ukraine. AP Photo.\par \par \uc2 In four weeks of fighting, the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  indicated that Russian soldiers killed between 7,000 to 15,000. The country's defenders put up much stronger resistance than expected and have denied Moscow a quick victory.\par \par \uc2 A senior \uc2 NATO\uc2  military official said the estimate is based on information from Ukrainian officials, what Russia has disclosed, and intelligence data compiled from open sources.\par \par \uc2 When Russia launched its invasion on Feb. 24, one of the biggest offensives in Europe since World War II, it threatened nuclear escalation if the West \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by then a rapid overthrow of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's democratic government seemed likely.\par \par \uc2 But four weeks into the fighting, Russia is bogged down in a slow military campaign, with untold numbers dead, no end in sight and its economy battered by various Western sanctions.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 15:23\par \par \uc2 Germany says Russian demand to pay for gas in rubles is a "breach of contract"That demand "constitutes a breach of contract," German Economy Minister \uc2 Robert Habeck\uc2  told a press conference in Berlin.? "We will now discuss with our European partners to decide how to respond to that demand," he added.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 14:48\par \par \uc2 The head of \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also wanted to resign when the war broke out, but Putin forbade her to do soElvira Nabiullina is the head of the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The highly respected head of \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 , tried to resign after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered the invasion of Ukraine, only to be told by the president to stay, according to four people with knowledge of the discussions.\par \par \uc2 Nominated for a new five-year term last week, Nabiullina's current views could not be known. She must manage the fallout from a war that quickly undid much of what she accomplished in the nine years since she took office. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 14:41\par \par \uc2 Sweden to send another 5,000 rocket launchers to UkraineThe Swedish government on Wednesday announced a decision to make a second shipment of armaments to Ukraine, after having authorized a first shipment worth 37.7 million euros on February 26.\par \par \uc2 "Sweden will double its contribution to the Ukrainian armed forces with 5,000 additional anti-tank weapons and mine clearance equipment. Another historic decision," Foreign Minister \uc2 Ann Linde\uc2  wrote on her \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 Defense Minister \uc2 Peter Hultqvist\uc2  also confirmed the news, in statements quoted by Swedish news agency TT, and said that "it is extremely important to continue to actively support Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 14:23\par \par \uc2 Russian troops reportedly attacked a train station in Donetsk regionThe front of the attacked train station in Donetsk. Photo: LB.ua\par \par \uc2 The chairman of the state railway transport administration in Ukraine on Wednesday denounced that Russian troops attacked a train station in Ocheretyne, Donetsk region. The head of \uc2 Ukrzaliznytsia\uc2 , Oleksandr Kamyshin, specified via Telegram that the Donetsk railroad was attacked but fortunately there were no casualties.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 14:05\par \par \uc2 In the midst of war, Russia bid to host the European Championship in 2028 or 2032Vladimir Putin during a mass rally in Moscow. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 Russia surprised the soccer world by announcing it will bid to host the European Championship, barely a month after invading neighboring Ukraine and having all its teams sidelined from international competitions due to the war.\par \par \uc2 The Russians will challenge a bid from the United Kingdom and Ireland, who thought they would have no rivals for the 2028 tournament until Turkey also threw its hat in the ring on Wednesday. The 2032 event, meanwhile, had Italy as a contender. Read more.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 13:44\par \par \uc2 Poland expels 45 Russian diplomats as "spies"Poland decided Wednesday to expel "45 Russian spies posing as diplomats," Interior Minister \uc2 Mariusz Kaminski\uc2  announced. "Consistently and decisively, we dismantled the network of Russian special services in our country," the minister said, on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Russian Ambassador to Poland Sergey Andreev confirmed these expulsions to the press and said that the individuals will leave the country within five days at the latest.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 13:34\par \par \uc2 Maria Laura Avignolo\par \par \uc2 Zelenski: "French companies must stop financing murder and rape".Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  during his speech in the French Parliament. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 This time it was in France. With his military shirt coloredUkrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  appeared by videoconference in front of French deputies and senators, who had gathered simultaneously at 3 p.m. on Wednesday, to listen to his message live from Kiev, under siege. He called for more weapons and more sanctions in the face of the illegal Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "For the first time in our history, our hemicycle will listen to the president of a country at war," stressed Senate President G\u233\'00\'E9rard Larcher. Earlier \uc2 Richard Ferrand\uc2 , his peer in the National Assembly, saluted "the courage of the Ukrainian people and the Ukrainian authorities". Read more here.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 13:25\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  again spoke about the Russian invasion of Ukraine: "I learned hatred for war from my grandfather"\uc2 .Pope Francis\uc2  during a general audience at the Vatican. Photo: ANSA\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  again referred to the Russian invasion of Ukraine and said that "hatred for war" he learned from his grandfather. "I can give a personal testimony. The hatred and anger at war I learned from my grandfather, who had made the battle of Piave in 1914 and passed on to me this anger at war because he told me about the sufferings of war and this is not learned from books, it is learned by passing it from grandparents to grandchildren," the pontiff said.\par \par \uc2 Francis said this at the usual Wednesday general audience at the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 13:11\par \par \uc2 Zelensky denounces that Russia hijacked a humanitarian convoy on its way to MariupolRussian forces hijacked a humanitarian convoy of 11 empty buses bound for Mariupol to rescue Ukrainians fleeing the city, taking the drivers of the vehicles and several emergency services operators "prisoner".\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  denounced Moscow's maneuver a few hours after his speech to the Italian Parliament and assures that Kiev is "doing everything possible to free" those detained by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's forces.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 12:52\par \par \uc2 For the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , "Putin has become the worst enemy of the Russian people"\uc2 .European Commission\uc2  President \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  during her speech in Brussels. Photo: EFE\par \par \uc2 The president of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , assured Wednesday that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has become "the worst enemy of the Russian people", whom he is suffocating economically, and pointed to him as responsible for future famines that could cause cereal shortages due to the war.\par \par \uc2 "Putin has also become the worst enemy of the Russian people," \uc2 Von der Leyen\uc2  told the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2 , who added that \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions against Moscow, ranging from freezing assets and reserves or expelling the vast majority of Russian banks from the SWFIT transfer system, are choking the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 12:31\par \par \uc2 Putin's top official resigns and leaves Russia over his rejection of the war in UkraineAnatoly Chubais was serving as a Russian climate envoy. Photo: Reuter\par \par \uc2 Russian climate envoy \uc2 Anatoly Chubais\uc2  resigned and left the country, citing his opposition to President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's war in Ukraine, according to two people familiar with the situation, becoming the highest-level official to break with the Kremlin over the invasion.\par \par \uc2 Chubais, 66, is one of the few economic reformers of the 1990s who remained in Putin's government and maintained close ties with Western officials. Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov did not immediately respond to a request for comment. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 12:14\par \par \uc2 Zelenski before the French Parliament: "Stopping the war is in your hands".Zelenski before the French Parliament. Photo: Reuter.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymir Zelensky\uc2  said in a message to France that "stopping the war is in your hands" and asked to convince Russia to seek peace and to support Kiev's desire to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I would like to ask you a question: how to stop the war, how to establish peace in Ukraine? Because most of the questions and answers are in your hands," he said in a video address to a solemn session of the two chambers of the French Parliament.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 11:48\par \par \uc2 Shocking photo of a girl in a hospital in Zaporiyia after being evacuated from MariupolA baby girl in a hospital in Zaporyia. Photo: Willian Keo/\uc2 Magnum Photos\par \par \uc2 A photo of a baby girl hospitalized in a pediatric hospital in Zaporiyia after being transferred from the devastated Mariupol went viral in the last hours.\par \par \uc2 The image was taken by William Keo for \uc2 Magnum Photos\uc2  and shows the baby girl on mechanical ventilation, in a bed next to a window that is covered with sandbags and cardboard.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 11:32\par \par \uc2 The story of the Russian soldier who gave up his tank in exchange for citizenship and 10,000 dollarsA Russian army tank that invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 In the midst of the cruel war between Russia and Ukraine stories like the one about the Russian soldier who handed over a tank to the military forces in Ukraine in exchange for the chance to receive a citizenship along with $10,000 emerge, officials confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Misha, short for Mikhail, voluntarily handed over a battle tank after calling the \uc2 Ukrainian Interior Ministry\uc2  with the clear intention of discussing an offer. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 11:16\par \par \uc2 Cara Anna\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's government is still standing and Russia does not control any of the major citiesA Ukrainian serviceman at a checkpoint in Kiev. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 One month into the war, still defiant. With its government still standing and its outnumbered troops fighting Russian forces in bloody deadlocks in multiple locations, Ukraine is scarred, wounded, mourning its dead, but far from defeated as it prepares for a second month of shelling, fighting, casualties and resistance.\par \par \uc2 When, on February 24, Russia unleashed its invasion force on Ukraine in the largest offensive in Europe since World War II and brandished the possibility of nuclear escalation if the West \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a lightning overthrow of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's democratically elected government seemed possible. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 11:10\par \par \uc2 Ukraine warns of consequences of Putin's decision to charge for Russian gas in rublesUkrainian presidential adviser Mikhail Podoliak warned on Wednesday about the consequences of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's government's decision to charge for Russian gas in rubles: "Russia opened an anti-EU front. Putin announced the transfer of gas payments to rubles and the rejection of dollars and euros , which will lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  failures and pressure on public opinion, second step for escalation," he specified on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 11:03\par \par \uc2 "With war all is lost, there is no victory in a war", \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 ' message23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 10:55\par \par \uc2 France proposes a global fund to cope with the lack of grain from Ukraine and Russia and warns of famineFrench President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  will propose to his European and \uc2 G7\uc2  partners the creation of an international fund to help the countries most affected by the interruption of grain exports from Ukraine and Russia, similar to the one created for the donation of vaccines against the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 The spokesman for the French government, Gabriel Attal, indicated on Wednesday that "the food crisis that is foreseen will, if no action is taken, provoke famine, humanitarian dramas and profound political changes", especially in countries of sub-Saharan Africa and the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 10:43\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  to give additional support to Ukraine in the face of "chemical and nuclear threats" from RussiaNATO Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  in Brussels. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  said Wednesday that at a summit of \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders the allies will agree to provide support to Ukraine in the face of "chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear threats."\par \par \uc2 "Tomorrow I expect the allies to agree to provide additional support, including cybersecurity assistance, as well as equipment to help Ukraine protect itself against chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear threats," the politician told a press conference ahead of Thursday's summit.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 10:25\par \par \uc2 Russia reportedly attacked two Ukrainian cities with white phosphorus bombsAn image of the white phosphorus bomb attack in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Irpin, Oleksandr Markushin, denounced that Russian forces attacked his town and Hostomel with white phosphorus bombs. The information was published by the Ukrainian media The Kyiv Independent. The use of such weapons against civilians is prohibited by the Geneva Conventions.\par \par \uc2 "Yesterday, March 22, late at night, Russian invaders used banned phosphorus in the northwestern suburbs of Kyiv," Irpin Mayor Oleksandr Markushyn stated on his Telegram channel.\par \par \uc2 "The approximate area where destruction was caused was Hostomel-Irpin," he added in his message, accompanied by photos that appear to show a cluster of sparks falling from the night sky.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 10:17\par \par \uc2 France calls on China to explicitly condemn Russia's invasion of UkraineFrench President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 . Photo: EFE\par \par \uc2 France on Wednesday called on China to explicitly express a condemnation of Russia's invasion of Ukraine and to bring its diplomatic weight to bear to get Russia to end that war.\par \par \uc2 "We want (China) to take an explicit position on the fact that it constitutes a serious violation of international law that deserves to be sanctioned," said sources in the Elys\u233\'00\'E9e on the eve of the one-month anniversary of the start of that conflict.\par \par \uc2 The French Presidency regretted that China abstained on the matter at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 10:05\par \par \uc2 Biden warns that a possible Russian chemical attack in Ukraine is a "real threat". \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the White House, before traveling to Europe. Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  warned Wednesday that a possible Russian chemical attack in Ukraine is a "real threat." He said this before leaving for Brussels, where he will participate in summits over the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 09:51\par \par \uc2 Putin to demand "unfriendly" countries to pay for Russian gas in rublesRussian President Vladimir Putin.Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said Wednesday that the country will refuse to pay for Russian gas in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the dollar and euro, and will charge supplies to "unfriendly" countries only in rubles.\par \par \uc2 "I have decided to implement as soon as possible a number of measures to transfer payments for our natural gas by the so-called unfriendly countries in rubles," he said at a meeting with members of the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 09:37\par \par \uc2 Zelensky calls for far-reaching reforms in the UNZelenski before the Japanese parliament. Photo: Reuter.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2  on Wednesday in a speech to the Japanese parliament called for deep reforms in the United Nations, noting that its instances failed to prevent the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 "Neither the United Nations nor the Security Council have worked. Reforms are needed," the Ukrainian leader said in a videoconference speech to Japan's parliamentarians.\par \par \uc2 "We need an instrument to preferentially ensure global security. Existing international organizations do not work in this regard. We must then develop a new tool that can really stop invasions," he said.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 09:28\par \par \uc2 Russia warns \uc2 NATO\uc2  about the consequences of an eventual deployment of peacekeepers in Ukraine Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2 . Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 Russia warned \uc2 NATO\uc2  on Wednesday over an eventual deployment of peacekeeping forces in Ukraine by pointing out that it would provoke military clashes between Russian and Atlantic Alliance troops.\par \par \uc2 It was the Russian Foreign Minister, \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2 , who warned of the consequences of such a hypothetical deployment that comes from the peacekeeping proposal of Poland, one of Kiev's main allies and destination of millions of refugees from the neighboring country.\par \par \uc2 Lavrov said that such an idea is a provocation and accused Poland of harboring territorial ambitions in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 09:15\par \par \uc2 Ukraine insists negotiations with Russia are "difficult" and that Kiev maintains its positions "on principle"Ukrainian chief negotiator Mikhail Podoliak acknowledged Wednesday that talks between Russia and Ukraine are "difficult" and that his country maintains "clear and principled positions."\par \par \uc2 "The talks are difficult because the Ukrainian side has clear and principled positions," Podoliak said, in written comments sent to journalists. A little earlier, Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  also called the negotiations "difficult," while accusing the Ukrainians of "constantly changing positions" and the United States of obstructing them.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 09:03\par \par \uc2 Italian prime minister says not helping Ukraine is like justifying Hitler and MussoliniBritish Prime Minister \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 . Photo: EFE.\par \par \uc2 Italian Prime Minister \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  on Wednesday justified sending arms to Ukraine to defend against Russia's invasion by explaining that not helping defenseless countries under attack would be like justifying aggressor nations and all autocrats, such as Adolf Hitler and \uc2 Benito Mussolini\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is how Draghi responded in the Chamber of Deputies after the debate that followed his appearance to explain Italy's position before the upcoming summits of the European Council, \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 G7\uc2  over the war in Ukraine following Russia's invasion.\par \par \uc2 Some MEPs questioned the sending of Italian military aid to Ukraine, to which Draghi replied that not helping countries that are attacked is to enter "a very slippery slope that leads us to justify all autocrats, starting with Hitler and Mussolini, all those who attacked defenseless countries."\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 08:58\par \par \uc2 Russia destroyed a bridge in Chernobyl that was used to evacuate civilian population Russian forces shelled and destroyed a bridge in Chernobyl, the governor of the region, Vyacheslav Chaus, said Wednesday. The destroyed bridge was used to allow the evacuation of civilians and the arrival of humanitarian aid. It crossed the Desna River and connected the city with the capital of the country, Kiev.\par \par \uc2 The Chernobyl authorities had warned in the last hours that the city had no water or electricity and called the situation a humanitarian disaster.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 08:50\par \par \uc2 Russian troops are "reorganizing" to continue "large-scale" offensives, they warnA Russian army military vehicle in Ukraine. Photo: Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 British Ministry of Defense\uc2  warned on Wednesday that Russian army troops in Ukraine are "reorganizing" in the north of the country before continuing their "large-scale" offensive \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The battlefield across northern Ukraine continues to be largely static with Russian forces likely undertaking a period of reorganization before continuing their large-scale offensive \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," highlights the report posted on the ministry's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 08:30\par \par \uc2 Russia claims there were prisoner swaps with Ukrainian forcesRussia and Ukraine have carried out two prisoner swaps, Foreign Ministry spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  said on Wednesday. "The \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  organizes daily humanitarian corridors and evacuation of civilians from (Ukrainian) localities. In addition, two prisoner exchanges have been carried out between Russia and Ukraine," she said.\par \par \uc2 Zakharova lashed out at the \uc2 European Union\uc2  for statements about Russia's use of Ukrainian refugees as "a new instrument of pressure."\par \par \uc2 "In reality, it is the Kiev authorities that prevent evacuation to our country and forcibly push refugees to the west. Many of them still try to get to Russia, where they have their relatives, but the European countries, mainly Poland, do not allow them to do so," he stressed.\par \par \uc2 23.03.2022\par \par \uc2 08:27\par \par \uc2 Russia continues shelling Kiev and attacks a shopping mall and company buildingsA shopping center attacked in Kiev. Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 The Kiev municipal government said today that Russian forces shelled the Ukrainian capital last night and this morning and damaged buildings in two neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 A shopping center, some private business buildings and skyscrapers were hit in the Sviatoshynskyi and Shevchenkivskyi neighborhoods, the Kiev government said.\par \par \uc2 Four people were injured, they added via the Telegram messaging platform, Europa Press news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220324ei3o004mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
