\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russia-Ukraine war live: Russian troops redouble attacks on Kiev, Ukraine calls for more help\b0\par\par\uc2 14,122 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 EN VIVONoticia enDesarrolloRussian forces on Wednesday attacked a 12-story apartment building\par \par \uc2 in central Kiev and set it on fire, amid renewed attacks on Ukraine's capital.16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 08:01\par \par \uc2 Ukraine rejects idea of neutrality modeled on Sweden or Austria Ukraine calls for "security guarantees" vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis Russia and rejects the idea of adopting "neutrality"\par \par \uc2 modeled on Sweden or Austria, the Ukrainian presidency announced Wednesday."Ukraine is in a direct war with Russia. Therefore, the model can only be 'Ukrainian' and only on the basis of solid security guarantees," said negotiator Mikhail Podoliak, in comments published by the office of President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 , specifying that signatories should commit to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in case of aggression against \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 07:47\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  admits its "responsibility" to keep war from spreading beyondUkraineNATO Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  acknowledged Wednesday that the alliance has a "responsibility" to ensure that the war does not spread beyond Ukraine and\par \par \uc2 admitted that the conflict will have "lasting" consequences for the security of allies. \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  attends a meeting of \uc2 NATO\uc2  defense ministers in Brussels, Belgium. Reuter photo.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 NATO\uc2  has a responsibility to ensure that this crisis does not escalate beyond Ukraine and that is also the reason why we have increased the (military) presence in the eastern part of the Alliance," Stoltenberg said alongside U.S. Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin ahead of today's \uc2 NATO\uc2  defense ministers' meeting in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 07:35\par \par \uc2 Real Madrid donates \u8364\'20\'AC1 million for displaced Ukrainian populationRealMadrid announced Wednesday that it will donate \u8364\'20\'AC1 million\par \par \uc2 to "contribute to alleviating the needs of the displaced population of Ukraine" due to Russia's invasion, as part of the "All with Ukraine" campaign it launched on March 5 through the club's Foundation.This money will go to the Red Cross, \uc2 UNHCR\uc2 , Cesal, \uc2 Salesian Missions\uc2  and Epicenter for children\par \par \uc2 . In addition, the club presided over by Florentino P\u233\'00\'E9rez has donated 13,000 items of warm clothing, sports and recreational material for the Red Cross and Cesal centers in Madrid to help welcome refugees in Spain.16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 07:10\par \par \uc2 Kremlin sees progress in negotiations, though not on all issuesRussiaand Ukraine made some progress on several of the issues they discuss in peace negotiations, though a rapprochement has not been achieved on all of them, the head of the Russian delegation, presidential adviser \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 , said Wednesday\par \par \uc2 .A photo provided by BelTA news agency shows the head of the Russian delegation, Russian presidential aide \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 . EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 "There is some progress on a number of positions\par \par \uc2 , but not on all of them. The positions of the parties have been defined, they are quite clear, we are moving forward slowly, respecting all the interests of the Russian Federation and its citizens," Medinsky said, according to Russia's Tass news agency.16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 05:45\par \par \uc2 Murakami to call for end of war during his radioprogramJapanese writer Haruki Murakami will call for peace in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 next Friday with a special "music to end the war" from the radio program he hosts on Tokyo FM." A war has started in Ukraine that no one wants\par \par \uc2 ," says the acclaimed writer, 73, in a statement picked up by the Japanese station that since 2018 has hosted his music program.For nearly an hour and through 10 songs with commentary, the writer will call for an end to the Russian invasion in Ukraine, put value on life and highlight the importance of peace, according to other details picked up by local media.\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 05:06\par \par \uc2 A residential building in Kiev was shelledA residential building in Kiev was shelled during the early morning hours by Russian troops. Reuters photo.\par \par \uc2 A 12-storey residential building in the Shevchenkivsky district of Kiev has been shelled in the early hours of the morning. Two people were injured and 37 were evacuated, according to the State Emergency Service.?Zaporizhzhia City Council Secretary Anatoliy Kurtev also confirmed explosions at the city's railway station.\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 04:20\par \par \uc2 Russia faces first round of payments, amid bankruptcy threatsRussiafaces a first exchange of bond payments Wednesday amid a threat of bankruptcy, having its assets abroad frozen\par \par \uc2 byinternational sanctions\par \par \uc2 .This Wednesday, Russia is due to repay $117 million\par \par \uc2 of two euro bonds, the first payment in a series due in March and April.But currently, in retaliation for the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, some $300 billion belonging to Russia's reserves are frozen in Western banks.?"The freezing of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts of the Bank of Russia and the Government can be regarded as the desire of foreign countries to provoke an artificial default\par \par \uc2 ," said Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2 . 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 03:50\par \par \uc2 Zelensky said that negotiations with Russia are now more "realistic "Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodomir Zelensky\uc2  said in a speech published early this morning that the positions in negotiations with Russia for a ceasefire are now more realistic\par \par \uc2 , but that time is still needed for decisions "to be in Ukraine's interest."In his latest message, made at the beginning of the twenty-first day of Russian invasion, Zelensky said that negotiations continue, that "it is difficult, but important" to negotiate and that "efforts are still needed" because "every war ends in an agreement."\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 03:15\par \par \uc2 Corpses pile up in the morgue in the Ukrainian city of MikolaivCorpsespile up in a morgue in Mikolaiv, Ukraine. Photo:AFP/BULENT KILIC)\par \par \uc2 In the courtyard of the forensic institute where the morgue is located in Mikolaiv, a Ukrainian city on the Black Sea, snow falls relentlessly on corpses wrapped in gray plastic bags\par \par \uc2 , waiting to be evacuated.In the dilapidated forensic premises of this city attacked by the Russians, other corpses lie on the floor for lack of space. The smell of death, mixed with that of disinfectant, is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 "I've never seen anything like it. We thought the worst thing that could happen to us here were car accidents," says Vladimir, one of the morgue employees.\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 02:31\par \par \uc2 90% of Ukrainians could be left in poverty if war spreads, warned UNOSIfthe war in Ukraine spreads, the country could lose 18 years of economic gains, with as much as 90 percent of the population living in poverty or at risk of falling into poverty in the next 12 months, according to estimates released Tuesday by the United Nations Development Organization (\uc2 UNDP\uc2 ) Program\par \par \uc2 .These projections, the first ever produced by \uc2 UNDP\uc2  on the impact of the conflict, indicate that over the next year nearly one-third of Ukrainians could move below the poverty\par \par \uc2 line, fourteen times more people than would have been expected without the war.In addition, another 62 percent of the country may be placed at high risk of falling into poverty.\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 01:55\par \par \uc2 Woman who barged into Russian TV show with anti-war banner finedTheRussian state TV station employee who interrupted a live news program to protest the war in Ukraine was released Tuesday and fined $270\par \par \uc2 , though she could still face a prison sentence.Marina Ovsyannikova told reporters that she was not allowed to sleep while in detention and was interrogated for 14 hours\par \par \uc2 .16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 01:23\par \par \uc2 U.S.Senate\uc2  calls for investigating Putin for war crimesTheU.S. Senate on Tuesday passed a resolution calling for an investigation of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and the Russian military for possible war crimes in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 . The resolution does not have the same force as a law, although it serves to support investigations that have been initiated by the Office of the Prosecutor of the International Criminal Court\par \par \uc2 (ICC) at the request of some 40 countries.The text of the resolution passed by the Senate condemns "war crimes, crimes against humanity and the systematic violation of human rights being committed by the Russian Armed Forces and military commanders, at the direction of President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 00:50\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson to meet with leaders of United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia to discuss Russian invasionThrougha press release, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson confirmed that he will meet this Wednesday with the leaders of the United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia to discuss the Russian invasion of Ukraine. According to the text, the meeting is expected to discuss "efforts to improve energy security and reduce volatility in energy and food prices."He will first meet with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Zayed in the UAE and then travel to Riyadh to meet with Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 16.03.2022\par \par \uc2 00:20\par \par \uc2 Biden to announce $800 million in aid for UkraineU.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will announce Wednesday that he will send $800 million in assistance to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 , a White House official said.The decision brings "the total (aid) total announced to $1 billion in the last week alone\par \par \uc2 ," the official said on condition of anonymity to the AFP news agency.On Saturday, the U.S. president had already authorized $200 million in additional military equipment for Ukraine, adding to the $350 million authorized on February 26.\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 23:50\par \par \uc2 Russian ships shelling shores near Odessa Russian ships in the Black Sea began shelling with rockets and artillery shells the shores near the city of Odessa, Ukraine's third-largest city and the country's main port, where sirens have already begun to sound. At the moment no attempts to land troops have been reported, \uc2 Anton Gerashchenko\uc2 , advisor to the Ukrainian Interior Minister, said on social networks.The targets of the shooting are bases of the Ukrainian armed forces and military infrastructure south of the city, in the Belgorod-Dnestrovsky area.\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 23:21\par \par \uc2 Zelensky appointed a new commander of the JointForcesUkrainian President \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky\uc2  appointed General Eduard Moskalov as the new commander of the Joint Forces of Ukraine (JFO). He replaces Oleksandr Pavlyuk and is in charge of leading the Kiev regional administration "to strengthen the defense of the capital", where several explosions\par \par \uc2 were heard during Tuesday night.15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 22:45\par \par \uc2 Air raid alert in several UkrainiancitiesUkrainian\par \par \uc2 mediawarn that in the wee hours of the morning Russian air raid alarms began to sound in several major cities and provinces. Sirens have been activated in Cherkasy, Dnipro, Lviv, Kyiv, Ivano-Frankivsk, Odessa, Vinnytsia, Kirovohrad and Khmelnytskyi Oblasts.?In addition CNN reports that, according to its team on the ground, loud explosions were heard in the suburbs of Kiev\par \par \uc2 .15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 21:05\par \par \uc2 The war as told by women journalists on the front linesClarissaWard in Kharkov, Ukraine. AP PhotoA\par \par \uc2 new generation is covering this war with women reporters well represented in TV, radio, newspaper and web media. Memorable reports illustrate both the skill and bravery of women journalists serving as eyewitnesses to Russia's brutal invasion of Ukraine and how their presence - hard-won after overcoming entrenched notions of why women shouldn't cover combat - has changed the nature of war journalism.They cover the tactics of war, but give equal weight to its consequences\par \par \uc2 . Read on.15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 20:46\par \par \uc2 Every minute, 55 Ukrainian childrenbecomerefugeesUkrainian\par \par \uc2 refugeesqueue outside the Porubne border crossing in Ukraine to enter Romania. EFE/ Marcel Gasc\u243\'00\'F3nAccording to\par \par \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , every minute that passes in the war, 55 Ukrainian children become refugees. The United Nations calls this refugee crisis the largest in Europe since World War II, surpassing the 2015 wave of Syrian \uc2 refugees.UNICEF\uc2  spokesman James Elder sounded the alarm: "It's 1.4 million children, although it's more than that because the last figures were 36 hours ago, so 1.4 million children in 18 days brings us to about 73,000. If we boil it down, we have 55 refugee children every minute, 55 per minute, that's almost one per second."?\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 20:10\par \par \uc2 ?The "heroine" who fought to the end Ukrainian lawmaker and former minister Inna Sovsun posted a photo of Olga Semydyanova, who died fighting Russian troops. She told that she was a mother of six children and that she kept her desire to defend the country until the end, "even when she knew that her battalion could not survive". He paid tribute to her with a heartfelt message: "She is a heroine of Ukraine. She is my heroine."\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 20:00\par \par \uc2 Isaac J. Martin\par \par \uc2 ?Odessa, the jewel of the Black Sea, is armored for the worstAsoldier patrols between an anti-tank barricade in the Ukrainian city of Odessa.Photo: EFEA\par \par \uc2 sepulchral silence fills what was once the city that did not sleep and its center is armored to preserve the so-called pearl of the Black Sea. The port city of Odessa is experiencing its most tense moments as it awaits the worst: the arrival of Russian troops to this city located in the southwest of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Barricades surround the streets of downtown Odessa\par \par \uc2 where only a few dozen people can be seen walking among the closed stores and only a few spaces are open. But what abound are the military.Andriy, a Ukrainian-Lebanese national, was a TV presenter and is now a soldier waiting in one of the avenues of the city, Ukraine's third largest. Continue reading15\par \par \uc2 .03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:52\par \par \uc2 ?From Kiev, Polish Prime Minister called for giving Ukraine the status of candidate to join theEUPolish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki insisted on the need to quickly give Ukraine the status of candidate to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (\uc2 EU\uc2 ) after the meeting held in Kiev with the country's President \uc2 Volodymir Zelenski\par \par \uc2 ."The \uc2 EU\uc2  must grant it that status as soon as possible,"\par \par \uc2 Morawiecki said,Inaddition, on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, he wrote: ? "Europe must understand that if it loses Ukraine will never be the same again. It will no longer be Europe. Rather it will be a defeated, humiliated and pathetic version of itself. I want a strong and determined Europe. "\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:42\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  agency downgrades global ratings of 31 Russian banksTheFitch agency downgraded the international issuer risk ratings (IDR) in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 31 Russian banks from "B" to "CC"\par \par \uc2 . According to a statement, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  took into account for its assessment the Russian presidential decree of March 5 that imposes "insurmountable obstacles"\par \par \uc2 to banks' ability to make scheduled debt payments to certain international creditors in their original \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."While the practical implementation of this decree remains unclear, we believe that the highly elevated risk is best reflected in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's "CC" rating definition, according to which some form of default appears likely," the document assures.\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:33\par \par \uc2 "Imagine it's four o'clock in the morning and your ears hear terrible explosions", Zelenski's story in front of Canada's parliamentVolodimirZelenski in front of \uc2 Canada's parliament\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The president gave a speech in front of the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  and parliament on Tuesday in which he told about the situation he is going through daily in his country."Imagine that it is four o'clock in the morning and your ears hear terrible explosions. And that your children hear them too. Let them hear missiles falling on the airport in Ottawa and other cities," he said, trying to generate empathy. He continued: "Imagine if they say to you, 'What's going on, daddy? Imagine finding the words to tell your children that the war started\par \par \uc2 ," he said. 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 19:04\par \par \uc2 UN\uc2  to vote on Russian "humanitarian" projectTheSecurity Council of the United Nations Organization will vote on the following day\par \par \uc2 .Thursday at Moscow's request a "humanitarian" resolution linked to the Russian "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine. This is a project proposed by the Russian authorities which, for the time being, does not seem likely to be approved.15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 18:43\par \par \uc2 We are providing almost $293 million to Ukraine in humanitarian assistance", says the US.Secretary Blinken assured this on his social networks: "The United States has provided nearly $293 million in humanitarian assistance to the people of Ukraine since February 24," he said.? "This allows humanitarian organizations to support those affected by Russia's brutal attack, as well as countries that are generously hosting refugees from Ukraine," he explained.\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 18:36\par \par \uc2 U.S. vows to hold Putin accountable for his "inhumane shelling of Ukraine."US Secretary of State, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  reaffirmed his commitment to the Ukrainian people and his condemnation of the actions of Russian troops, led by its President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I spoke with the Ukrainian Foreign Minister today. I reaffirmed the United States' continued support for the Ukrainian people and its commitment to holding Putin accountable for his unjustified and inhumane shelling of Ukraine," he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 15.03.2022\par \par \uc2 18:28\par \par \uc2 Syria, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's impunity laboratoryThe bodies of Syrian civilians found in a river, Aleppo, Syria.\par \par \uc2 In Syria, revolutionaries are celebrating this March 15 the eleventh anniversary of the start of the protest movement against Bashar al-Assad, while another war is raging on Ukrainian territory. Many observers agree that the origin of the Kremlin's attack in Ukraine lies on Syrian territory.\par \par \uc2 The impunity with which \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would have committed war crimes in Syria would have