\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Russia's economy, from BRIC to collapse: crisis, corporate flight and the concrete chance of becoming an unprecedented global pariah\b0\par\par\uc2 2,881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The invasion of Ukraine is the last link in a chain of missteps, miscalculations and negative twists and turns in the international situation that have diverted Russia from its attempt to recover the role of economic power it used to play in the past. The damage suffered by its economy due to the costs of the war, the political isolation in which it was placed and the sanctions it is the target of will not only be short term: it will take decades to recover.\par \par \uc2 Infobae consulted national and international specialists regarding the effect of the war conflict and the future that can be sketched for Russia in the economic and geopolitical planes that are configured from now on.\par \par \uc2 A truncated renaissance\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of the 21st century, and after the fall of the Berlin Wall, Russia had begun the slow path towards recovering its place in the world and positioned itself as one of the major emerging markets, forming together with Brazil, India, China and then South Africa, a bloc called BRICS called to dominate the global economy due to its large population, territorial extension, natural resources and very high Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth. But the years went by and that promise was not fulfilled: the countries that make up the bloc today navigate very different waters and in recent years certain economic differences began to emerge that were so marked that the ratings agency S&P came to pronounce the "death" of the group.\par \par \uc2 Subsequently, the global economic crisis of 2008 after the real estate and credit bubble that had been inflating in the United States, followed by the sanctions imposed by that country and by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in 2014, pushed Russia into recession and Putin's government relaunched its austerity policy.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the European country's economy began to suffer a series of ups and downs such as the fall in oil prices, the Coronavirus pandemic that hit the entire planet and, more recently, the invasion of Ukraine on February 24 this year.\par \par \uc2 This last action resulted in a counter-offensive by the West through strong economic sanctions that decimated the Russian economy with consequences that are still difficult to measure. The war adventure may be the coup de gr\u226\'00\'E2ce for the former power's attempt to return to being a leading player on the international scene.\par \par \uc2 The costs to come\par \par \uc2 Luis Palma Can\u233\'00\'E9, economist and expert in international finance, told this newspaper that Putin's "move" will have extremely negative consequences for Russia. In this sense, he warned: "With each passing day, Russia will be worse off. If this goes on for example for six months, the GDP may fall 20 or 25% without any doubt".\par \par \uc2 "The longer the war is delayed the more Russia will fall in economic terms. 65% of Russia's tax revenues come from energy exports and now they have a choked and isolated economy. The ruble is wrecked, the stock market is wrecked and they are technically in default. Any bonds maturing today they can't pay," he stressed.\par \par \uc2 "With every day that goes by, Russia is going to get worse. If this goes on for six months, for example, the GDP may fall 20 or 25% without any doubt" (Palma Can\u233\'00\'E9).\par \par \uc2 In outlining the diagnosis of Russia, Palme Can\u233\'00\'E9 considered that today the European country has a stifled economy and financially isolated from the world. "It can neither import nor export. The outlook is chaotic. There is going to be more inflation, more poverty and a fall in the level of activity. In this way, it is going to cease to be the power factor it represents at the moment", he analyzed.\par \par \uc2 Economic analyst Marcelo Elizondo emphasized the effects of the economic restrictions on Russia and estimated a thorny outlook for the country led by Putin for the future. For the specialist, due to its size and relevance in international trade, the country cannot afford to isolate itself for a prolonged period of time since it needs its buyers to finance itself and its suppliers to keep its economy going.\par \par \uc2 "Russia is an intermediate-sized economy, not one of the most powerful. Its GDP is $1.7 trillion. It is the 12th largest economy in the world. It is not a powerhouse. So it's more vulnerable economically than politically. That makes quite a bit of sense why the West has reacted on the economic side and not the military side," he told Infobae.\par \par \uc2 "Its exports are about $400 billion a year, so affecting them is a hard blow. While their imports are about $350 billion and they are also hit. This makes it difficult for companies to access inputs or capital goods to produce", he pointed out.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, Elizondo evaluated that the exodus of foreign companies in Russia as a result of the invasion of Ukraine may weaken the productive framework of the country in a much deeper way than the initial blow to the activity, due to the loss of key pieces of the gears that move different sectors and whose efficiency, knowledge and innovation capacity cannot be replaced overnight.\par \par \uc2 "The default, the non-functioning of the financial system, the 40% devaluation and the difficulty of access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obstruct, both internally and in Russia's relationship with the rest of the world, the functioning of its economy. This may lead first to a fall in GDP, then to a fall in employment and subsequently to a weakening of the functioning of the domestic value chains, which will slowly abort economic normality", he said.\par \par \uc2 An unprecedented pariah\par \par \uc2 Bombs are exploding in different parts of the Ukrainian territory and although the final offensive to put an end to the resistance is not yet on the near horizon, analysts are already wondering what Russia's position will be once the conflict is over.\par \par \uc2 "Never before have there been sanctions of such depth against one of the big five of the Security Council, made up of Russia, China, the United Kingdom, France and the United States" (Jaramillo).\par \par \uc2 Mauricio Jaramillo, a specialist in international relations and professor at the Universidad del Rosario, Colombia, told Infobae that there are two scenarios for the future of the Russian economy following the aggression against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "One is that they effectively become a kind of pariah, but it is difficult to foresee because never before have there been sanctions of such a depth against one of the big five of the Security Council, made up of Russia, China, the United Kingdom, France and the United States. There had been very tough sanctions on Iraq, Cuba, Venezuela, Syria and Burma, which had become pariahs. But we had never been in a situation where there was such a consensus to isolate one of the big players. That is one option, that it becomes a pariah and that these alternative systems of social, financial and monetary networks, on which Russia is betting with the support of China, fail", he said.\par \par \uc2 "The other scenario is that we return to a war by blocs, a 'cold war' in which they confront each other in third party territories, such as Syria," he added.\par \par \uc2 For Jaramillo, the Russian situation benefits states that can put stocks of goods that are in short supply on the market. "One could immediately think of Venezuela, the United States and Iran as some of the nations that can benefit from the Russian weakening. But I don't think that from a logistical point of view it would be easy to replace it as a supplier of energy and grains because this dependence between Russia and Europe has been going on for more than 30 years. You cannot change a chain of exchanges in the course of a few months," he said.\par \par \uc2 Lasting wounds\par \par \uc2 In turn, Gabriela Siller Pagaza, director of Economic Analysis at Banco Base de M\u233\'00\'E9xico told Infobae that although the war is not over yet, "there is still a wound on Russia because it invaded a country and somehow its assets will continue to be affected for a period of time".\par \par \uc2 "It is not going to be easy for companies to return to that country after Putin said he was going to nationalize the assets that left Russia. On the other hand, the ruble is not going to recover that fast," he considered.\par \par \uc2 "There remains a wound on Russia because it invaded a country and somehow its assets are going to remain affected for a period of time."(Siller Pagaza)\par \par \uc2 In line with the rest of the colleagues, Siller Pagaza assured that Russia is going to suffer the consequences of the invasion of Ukraine. "GDP is going to contract and there will also be an impact on its foreign trade numbers. It is very possible that Russia will experience a recession and then a long period of economic stagnation. There will be a wound left. I think there will be some kind of treaty or rules to prevent something like this from happening again," he told this newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brenda Estefan, a Mexican international analyst, told Infobae that what could affect Russia the most are the sanctions coming from the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , given that in the case of the United States the impact is more limited.\par \par \uc2 "The Russian economy is very concentrated in hydrocarbons. As Europe has a huge dependence, it has them tied up. In that context, the sanctions imposed are going to limit the export of technology to Russia and that is something they do need. Technological elements are important to continue with the extraction of their oil fields. In the medium term they will need technology and they will not be able to import it", he pointed out.\par \par \uc2 According to Estefan, Russia's relationship with China may moderate the impact on its economy, although he considered that this will not be enough. "Russia will have to have a relationship with other countries. We have to see what happens with Brazil, Argentina, Turkey, Israel, among others. These countries may continue to trade with Russia," he said.\par \par \uc2 He added: "Yes, it will become a country far away from most of the global markets. And also distant in terms of air connectivity. The Russian economy is going to become detached from the rest of the world. They are going to tighten their belts in a brutal way, as well as their incomes and sources of employment. That will happen as punishment for the invasion of Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Russia and an economy without a floor\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  won the presidential elections in March 2000 and, following his victory, began to turn around his country's economic policy after the dissolution of the Soviet Union. Gross Domestic Product began to grow, the ruble stabilized, inflation was brought under control and investment began to grow slowly.\par \par \uc2 Well into the 21st century, the European country made large debt repayments on \uc2 IMF\uc2  loans and managed to increase its international reserves. These were years in which Russia began to regain its place as an economic power and positioned itself as one of the major emerging markets, one of the booming BRICS.\par \par \uc2 "The Russian economy is going to take off from the rest of the world. They are going to tighten their belts in a brutal way and also their incomes and sources of employment" (Estefan).\par \par \uc2 However, the global economic crisis of 2008 after the bursting of the real estate and credit bubble that had been inflating in the United States, followed by the sanctions imposed by the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in 2014, pushed Russia into recession. Since then, the Russian economy began to suffer a series of ups and downs also as a consequence of falling oil prices.\par \par \uc2 For Pablo Kornblum, economist and doctor in international relations, Russia's role in the last time was to sustain a boost with the accumulation model of the beginning of this century based on an empowerment of hydrocarbon production and the revival of the war economy.\par \par \uc2 "Russia will be a pariah for many years to come in the \uc2 NATO\uc2  world, with clear short-term adverse economic and financial effects. What will not happen is an implosion as with the fall of the Soviet Union, which was due to expansion into the military technological area to the detriment of other areas of the economy where domestic needs were disregarded," the expert told this media outlet.\par \par \uc2 "Russia will be a pariah for many years for the \uc2 NATO\uc2  world, with clear short-term adverse economic and financial effects" (Kornblum).\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Gustavo Perego, director of the consulting firm Abeceb, said that what Russia will achieve with this process is to suffer a hard blow to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the international financial market. In that sense, he considered that Russia "will find it very difficult to operate in international trade because many countries will also deny the use of their ports as a secondary port to trade with Russia".\par \par \uc2 "There they have a problem because the whole scheme of international credit and financing to be able to negotiate letters of credit with Western banks will be complicated. Chinese banks are going to be the main players and that is already a complication from the beginning," he said.\par \par \uc2 The trigger for the collapse of the Russian economy\par \par \uc2 On February 24 of this year, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  took the plunge and concretized the invasion of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  through his "special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". The West refused to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with troops or its air forces, but the sanctions response was much harsher than could have been initially calculated,\par \par \uc2 The main measures range from the decision by Western countries to exclude Russia from the SWIFT (\uc2 Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication\uc2 ) system, the highly secure network that facilitates payments between 11,000 financial institutions in 200 countries, allowing billions of dollars in transactions around the world, to the decision by the United States to ban Russian oil and gas imports.\par \par \uc2 There were also new restrictions on the debt and equity of major Russian companies and entities. And additional total blocking sanctions on Russian elites and their families.\par \par \uc2 Some of the consequences so far were the fall of more than 50% of the Moscow stock exchange, the plunge of the ruble, which reached its historic low against the dollar after recovering more than 60% since the day before the beginning of the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the economic sanctions unleashed by the United States, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and other countries in response to the invasion of Ukraine have the financial system on the verge of collapse, international reserves frozen and Russian savers standing in long lines to recover their money. So much so that \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to resort to a measure like the one in force in Argentina since 2019 to try to limit foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases: a 30% commission was imposed on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in an attempt to support the Russian ruble.\par \par \uc2 In this context, inflation in Russia is expected to accelerate to 20% and its economy could contract by up to 8% this year, according to an independent survey of analysts requested by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Likewise, following the economic sanctions applied by the West, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  joined a growing group that warned about the risk of the country presided by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  defaulting on its debt obligations after its invasion of Ukraine, according to \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  news agency.\par \par \uc2 A Russian debt default is "no longer an unlikely event," \uc2 IMF\uc2  Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva told reporters on Thursday. "It's not that Russia doesn't have money, it can't use this money," she stressed.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  said this week that a bond default by Russia is "imminent" as a result of measures introduced since the war broke out.\par \par \uc2 However, the downfall of the Russian economy does not end there: since invading Ukraine, hundreds of companies have announced the abandonment of their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Russia. Some of the firms that stopped operating are: \uc2 Nike\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 BP\uc2 , \uc2 Shell\uc2 , \uc2 Equinor\uc2 , \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 , PWC, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , Toyota, \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 HP\uc2 , \uc2 Intel\uc2 , \uc2 Inditex\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , EA, \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Ikea\uc2 , \uc2 Oracle\uc2 , \uc2 Dell\uc2 , \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , \uc2 Airbus\uc2 , \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2 , Paramount, \uc2 WarnerMedia\uc2 , Universal and TikTok, among others from a long list of more than 300 companies.\par \par \uc2 These are transnational companies, with production chains that transcend borders and are unlikely to be replaced by new government-sponsored corporations hoping to fill the vacuum they leave behind.\par \par \uc2 Putin may have had in mind to restore Russia's old imperial glory when he ordered his troops to cross the border. But from the moment the first soldier set foot in Ukraine he set his country in the opposite direction. Closer and closer to being left out of the world.\par \par \uc2 Due to the Russian invasion of Ukraine, the BCRA relaxed the import restrictions for oil companies that it had implemented a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 : in the first two months of 2022, the Government has already doubled its spending on energy subsidies.\par \par \uc2 Stigliz once again supported his disciple Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n for the agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , but warned that "Argentina will inevitably experience shocks".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220313ei3d001ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
