\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Negotiations fail; Ukraine and Russia accuse each other for hospital attack\b0\par\par\uc2 2,473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  's government described the attack on the hospital in Mariupol as a "staged provocation" to stir up anti-Russian sentiment, leaving in the background the new failure in the negotiations between foreign ministers to define humanitarian corridors and cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 In response, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  asserted that this "genocidal" escalation shows the world that Ukraine is dealing with a terrorist state, as the accusations against it always turn out to be a foretaste of what they are planning.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  cast doubt on the bombing by disassociating itself from it. It explained that this was a plan to stir up anti-Russian sentiment, a fact for which three civilian deaths are reported, among them a girl, and at national level the figure amounts to 71 minors dead and 100 wounded, innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 Arguing that he has evidence, which he did not show, the spokesman of this Ministry, Igor Konashenkov, explained that this was internal and staged, since explosives were placed very close to the area, contradicting the Ukrainian version of the fall of projectiles which left a huge crater several meters deep. And he ratified that the targets of the "demilitarization \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " are the war infrastructure and not civilian areas.\par \par \uc2 This position was supported by the Russian embassy in Great Britain, which denounced on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  the use of influencers as victims, a publication that the social network deleted for being Russian propaganda.\par \par \uc2 Buildings, government offices and homes evidence the devastation wrought by enemy troops in the region.Photo:APSeparately, Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  asserted that the building was not a hospital but an extremist base. After talking with his counterpart Dimitro Kuleba in Turkey, he said that the devastated site was occupied by a radical Ukrainian battalion, so that there were no longer any patients, denying agency photos of evacuated pregnant women with visible wounds.\par \par \uc2 He reiterated that these versions only favor the interests of the West, which supports Ukraine, and reproached the handling of the conflict in the media; an issue that the Foreign Ministry spokeswoman, \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  , described as "information terrorism", since Ukrainian allies restrict the broadcasting of Russian channels and media.\par \par \uc2 Lavrov added that in yesterday's talks a cease-fire was not raised, as he reiterated that this discussion is not the same as in Belarus, so he said he was surprised that his counterpart criticized that there was no progress, when this issue was not even on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Zelenski disqualified Russia's position that it does not attack and sells itself as a promoter of peace; he affirmed that its allies are watching what the Russian troops are doing in their territory, where they prevent the evacuation of citizens and the arrival of humanitarian aid to besieged areas such as Mariupol and Volnovaja, while in one of the most affected areas, Kharkov and Donbas, the volley of projectiles persists. In this regard, he said that they are already preparing the reconstruction of the country in view of the siege for which, he insisted, those responsible will pay.\par \par \uc2 His Chancellor, Kuleba, backed him up by rejecting any indication of a possible use of biological weapons, questioning "the maniacal obsession with which several Russian officials fantasize", in view of the insistence that there are biological or chemical weapons in the invaded country.\par \par \uc2 Despite the clash of versions, both nations pointed out that there will be new safe corridors. Ukraine detailed that those interested will be able to leave Sumi and Mariupol through seven routes to Poltava, Zaporiyia, Volnovaja and up to Kiev; while the Kremlin said it will open specific spaces for refugees on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 Buildings, government offices and homes are evidence of the devastation wrought by enemy troops in the region.Photo:APSHowever, it was said that this plan is unilateral and will only lead to their territory. Some media even indicated that in order to facilitate the exits they will request lists of possible evacuees and the units used for that purpose. And it even boasted that it has already helped 187,000 people to leave Ukraine, although as of yesterday's cut-off the \uc2 United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) \uc2 only counts 82,000 internments in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Aday after this war crime, France, Canada and Germany joined the condemnations for the "atrocity" denounced by Zelenski\par \par \uc2 .French President and mediator, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  , called the launching of explosives clearly intended to kill civilians "unworthy acts of war", which goes against demands to end the war. And although he expressed his support for Ukraine, he commented that it is not the time for its accession to the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU). Separately, the German Chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  , once again reiterated his call for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Prime Minister of Canada, \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  , warned that Putin is making serious mistakes that will lead him to lose this war and he will also pay for each of the events provoked in the nation where multiple devastated areas are reported.\par \par \uc2 One of these is Mariupol, where the International Red Cross sees an "increasingly serious and desperate" humanitarian situation, as food and all the necessary supplies are already scarce, and the mayor Vadim Boichenko accused that shells are falling almost every half hour.\par \par \uc2 In the same vein, the Ukrainian Minister of Defense, Oleksi Reznikov, stated that in 15 days the enemy troops have killed more civilians than soldiers, without revealing the total number of casualties.\par \par \uc2 The fact: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  platforms have announced that they will allow the dissemination of messages calling for violence against Russia, only if they are attached to the context of the war.Putin mocks sanctions and says "we will overcome it"\par \par \uc2 15 days after the impact of the war and sanctions, the President of Russia, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , stated that the current difficulties are only a stumbling block that will be overcome, therefore, he called on the citizens to remain calm, since "there is no event that we cannot solve".During a meeting with his cabinet, he asserted that "in the end, everything will lead to an increase of our independence, self-sufficiency and sovereignty", minimizing the blow from the West with multiple restrictions and hostilities from governments, which continue to accumulate.\par \par \uc2 He maintained that, contrary to what some countries think, they will even benefit by strengthening them in the midst of the rejection of the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, which he insisted there was no alternative and he was forced to act due to the repeated violations of that government against its own citizens in Donbas, an area he recognized as a republic in direct affront against \uc2 Volodimir Zelenski\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This contrasts with those who exert pressure against him, since countries he called "hostile" are already suffering from the rise of products with "high prices, unprecedented inflation and reaching historic highs", for which they now blame them, and their territory, he says, will resist what he called "de facto economic warfare".\par \par \uc2 He ratified that the Kremlin will not compromise its sovereignty over economic gains, which he insisted will be short term, because in a crisis it is normal that "the demand for certain goods increases, but we have no doubt that we will solve these problems".\par \par \uc2 Although he admitted that the effects are already being felt in the country, Putin explained that in the past they have already overcome similar restrictions such as freezing assets and curbing trade exchanges, so this will not be an exception.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin leader added that he was already expecting these sanctions, as he said that, even if they had not made military incursions, some countries were already taking coercive measures against them, so they would have applied them anyway.\par \par \uc2 However, he emphasized that these are hostile and illegitimate acts, and minimized any attempt of the powers to weaken its economy, since it will manage to overcome this phase, of which he did not give a deadline or advance a possible end to the attacks on Ukraine, supported by its oil production, since he considered that its former partners will resent the blow more by running out of energy, as well as other materials such as railroad cars, turbines and more, a fact for which, he said, they are already asking their population to "tighten their belts".\par \par \uc2 He also explained that Russia will fulfill its energy obligations, since despite the break reported by the United States and Great Britain, it still supplies Europe and other places, guaranteeing gas transportation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It should be noted that the Kremlin has already announced restrictions on its own territory, in order to prevent capital outflows. In this regard, the Minister of Finance, \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  , recalled that these measures will reduce the impact to stabilize the financial climate and the nation will continue to pay its foreign debt, but no longer in dollars but in rubles.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, his Foreign Minister, \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  , pointed out that these sanctions, which he described as "political aggression", only make them see that they will no longer depend on Western governments or companies. He stated\par \par \uc2 that contrary to the blow they believe they will cause to the nation, they are giving it the opportunity to no longer depend on someone else, since returning to negotiate means giving the power to another government to violate or try to destroy its economy\par \par \uc2 .In declarations to the Russian media, he dismissed the claims that they will use energy supplies -oil and gas- as a weapon to condition governments, since, he insisted, they are not like the US.\par \par \uc2 Inview ofthe exit of many global companies, the Russian government threatened to take control of their assets, while the list of those that are suspending their participation in the region by joining the "economic war" is growing\par \par \uc2 .The Eurasian region said that it is contemplating changes to the law to take control of the companies that are temporarily leaving its territory or plan to suspend \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; therefore, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  referred "there are enough legal and market instruments for this", anticipating a freezing of assets in case they try to return by the time the military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  concludes.\par \par \uc2 After the withdrawal of transnationals such as \uc2 Coca Cola\uc2  , Pepsi, \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  , \uc2 Sony\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  , among others, the Ministry of Economy stated that they have the capacity to take control of foreign property, but the list continues to grow, since even companies that do not operate directly in their territory, but do participate in the supply chain, have announced a breakup, such as \uc2 Burger King\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 In view of the President's plans, the Prime Minister, \uc2 Mikhail Mishustin\uc2  , announced that they would modify the law for an external administration and thus transfer or nationalize such resources to sectors that will work, since they affirm that their population is not against the war, although protests persist in rejection of the conflict for which the neighboring nation invaded. Among the alternatives is a bankruptcy process or the nationalization of foreign organizations, and he pointed out that "foreign companies must understand that returning to the (Russian) market will be difficult".\par \par \uc2 Despite this plan, Putin said that they remain open to work with all partners who wish to do so by guaranteeing protection to investors who will remain in the region.\par \par \uc2 However, it was not specified whether such actions would be the government's response to the possibility of defaulting on its debt payments or some other existing loans.\par \par \uc2 Less growth due to Russia-Ukraine conflictDue tothe war conflict between Russia and Ukraine, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2  ) expects to cut its expectations for world economic growth, said \uc2 Kristalina Georgieva\uc2 , director of the world organization, assuring that Moscow's default is no longer an improbable event\par \par \uc2 .Georgieva pointed out that there is a "tragic impact of the war against Ukraine", so she assured that a significant contraction is in store for Russia. "We see the likely impact on our outlook for the global economy... Next month (during the spring boreal meetings) we will have a downward revision of our global growth projections," she added.\par \par \uc2 Russia's default puts pressure on that country's expansion and therefore on that of other nations, as he explained that it is not that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  's country does not have money to meet its obligations; but rather, it cannot use the money it has.\par \par \uc2 "The unprecedented sanctions against the nation will make it difficult for the country to convert its \uc2 IMF\uc2  reserve assets, known as special drawing rights, into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he stressed.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the economic sanctions Russia is facing due to the conflict, there is another one from the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  (BIS), which on Thursday suspended the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 According to agencies, the entity suspended the access of the banking establishment of the Russian Federation to "all BIS services" to the country, under the premise that this international organization is not "and will not be a way to avoid sanctions".\par \par \uc2 Its head of the Monetary and Economic Department, \uc2 Claudio Borio\uc2  , warned at the time that the war in Ukraine would complicate the task of the central banks, which were already in the process of modifying their monetary policy, preparing for an interest rate hike to face the increase in inflation.\par \par \uc2 Around 300,000 companiesin the United States and Europe, not counting Latin America, have been affected by the war between Russia and Ukraine, said Bertha Martinez Cisneros, coordinator of the International Logistics degree program at CETYS University Mexicali Campus\par \par \uc2 .She said that based on a study by the \uc2 International Chamber of Commerce (ICC) \uc2 , Ukraine has become a major supplier of essential metals and raw materials for global value chains, mainly in the production of semiconductors, automobiles and even medicines.\par \par \uc2 These industries have been severely affected by the interruption of commercial activities in this country, he said during the forum on "Semiconductor crisis, the future of the automotive and transportation industry", organized by CETYS.\par \par \uc2 Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez Cisneros explained that, to give an idea of the complexity of global supply chains, there are 2,100 U.S. companies and 1,200 European companies that have at least one direct supplier in Russia. In addition, more than 450 companies in the United States and 200 in Europe have first-tier suppliers in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "The numbers increase as we move from direct suppliers to indirect relationships (tier two or three). More than 15,100 companies in the United States and eight thousand 200 European companies have tier two suppliers based in these two countries and tier three we would be considering 190 thousand companies in the United States and 109 thousand companies in Europe; in total around 300 thousand companies would be affected in the United States and Europe, not counting Latin America", he added.\par \par \uc2 With information from Berenice Luna and Alina Archundia\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220311ei3b0049m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
