\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Hawkish Glapinski says Polish MPC resolute in inflation fight\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 17:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)               \par \par \uc2 By Anna Koper               \par \par \uc2 WARSAW, March 9 (Reuters) - Poland's Monetary Policy Council (MPC) is determined to fight a surge in inflation that will be fuelled by the war in Ukraine, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Wednesday, striking a hawkish tone a day after the bank raised rates more than expected.               \par \par \uc2 Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have taken a beating as a result of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, and this has combined with a surge in oil and gas prices to boost inflation that was already amongst the highest in Europe, piling pressure on central banks.               \par \par \uc2 "(The Monetary Policy Council) is determined to bring inflation to the target as soon as possible, and we will be consistent and tenacious in this," governor \uc2 Adam Glapinski\uc2  told a news conference.               \par \par \uc2 "I stand in front of you as a hawk... a hawk at the head of the hawks who form the Monetary Policy Council at the moment."               \par \par \uc2 Glapinski said the war in Ukraine could add two percentage points to Polish CPI, which is already running at its highest level in more than two decades, hitting 9.3% in January.               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2  (NBP) raised its main interest rate by 75 basis points to 3.5% on Tuesday. The hike was above analysts' estimates, but lower than some market participants had hoped for.               \par \par \uc2 "We are still in the rate-hike cycle... and we will remain in it. It is hard to say for how long," Glapinski said, adding he would now say the peak of rates would be higher than the 4.0-4.5% he has previously talked about.               \par \par \uc2 Glapinski also said the MPC was determined to strengthen the zloty, whose recent depreciation he labelled unjustified.               \par \par \uc2 After having softened more than 7% versus the euro between the start of Russia's invasion on Feb. 24 and Monday's close, the zloty, in common with regional peers, has rebounded strongly over the past two days.               \par \par \uc2 It gained around 1% during the course of Glapinski's news conference and was trading at 4.757 to the euro at 1607 GMT, a gain of 3.7% on the day.               \par \par \uc2 The NBP has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  war started to shore up the zloty, and Glapinski said it was ready to continue doing so.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-9T17:39:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220309ei3903hxe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
