\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 NATO\uc2  accuses Russia of using cluster bombs. Attack on nuclear power plant injures two. Wheat at 14-year highs\b0\par\par\uc2 2,936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 ago 34 min.15h00 Nuclear power plant attack eclipses good jobs data and knocks down Wall Street The escalation of the conflict in Ukraine, with the attack on Europe's largest nuclear power plant, has spooked investors and dwarfed the positive effect of the better-than-expected US job creation data. In the first moments of trading, Wall Street painted itself red with the Dow Jones falling 1.37% to 33,332.90 points, while the S&P 500 lost 1.25% to 4,309.08 points. The tech Nasdaq Composite falls 1.32% to 13,360.62 points.US employment data released before the markets opened showed the creation of 678,000 jobs in February, the highest since July, far above the 475,000 estimated by analysts. The unemployment rate retreated to 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 40 min ago.14h54 \uc2 NATO\uc2  accuses Russia of using cluster bom bs The \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary general says there is evidence that Russia is using cluster bombs in Ukraine and admits that Putin may move on to other countries such as Georgia or Bosnia-Herzegovina."We have seen the use of cluster bombs and we have reports of other types of bombs that may violate international law," \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  said at a press conference, quoted by agencies and the international press. At the end of an extraordinary meeting of the North Atlantic Council, where countries are represented by their heads of diplomacy, the official stated that \uc2 NATO\uc2  will not establish a no-fly zone over Ukraine to avoid Russian bombers, nor will it send its troops, promising other types of aid to Kiev and asking President Putin to immediately end the invasion.He warned, however, that the coming days will be worse, admitting that Russia may advance towards other countries."With cities under siege, schools, hospitals, and residential buildings bombed. Irresponsible actions around a nuclear plant last night and many civilians injured or killed... the coming days will likely be worse, with more deaths, more suffering, and more destruction," he said. "The Kremlin's ambition is to recreate a sphere of influence and deny other countries the right to choose their own path, and so ministers discussed the need to support partners who may be at risk, including Georgia and Bosnia-Herzegovina," \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 1:26 p.m. Stoxx 600 trading at near one-year lows The pan-European index is down 2.59 percent at this point, at 426.04 points, very close to a near one-year low. With all sectors trading with losses, the most significant drop belongs to the banking sector, which is down 4.63%, followed by the automotive sector, which is down 3.57%. The least significant drop in this sectors scenario belongs to the utilities sector, which is down 0.45%.The main European stock exchanges are trading on negative ground, as investors digest the developments linked to the conflict between Russia and Ukraine, and with fears linked to the attack on the Zaporizhia nuclear power plant. The Milan stock exchange is registering the most significant drop, of 4.53%, but several indices, such as the German DAX, the French CAC 40 or the Amsterdam stock exchange, are registering drops of more than 3%.\par \par \uc2 11:32 a.m. Russian parliament condemns "fake news" and considers sending to the army those arrested in anti-Russia protests The Russian parliament on Friday approved a prison sentence of up to 15 years for those who intentionally spread what they call "fake news" about the upcoming military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .The measure comes after several independent media outlets in the country criticized Moscow's invasion, which led to the closure of the Ekho Moskvy radio station and the Dozdh television channel. After the approval of the measure, which will come into effect tomorrow, the Znask website was also shut down.Bloomberg reports that the Russian parliament now wants to debate the sanctions to be applied to those arrested in "anti-Russian" protests. The proposal on the table is to send them to the military and deploy them in Donetsk and Luhansk, the two separatist regions in eastern Ukraine that President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  recognized last month as independent.\par \par \uc2 11:27 AM Wheat hits 14-year highs Wheat has reached its highest value since 2008 on growing fears of global shortages as the war in Ukraine affects more than 25 percent of global exports of the raw material that serves as the basis for foods such as bread, flours or pasta.Prices are heading in international markets for an appreciation of about 40 percent for the week, the biggest weekly gain ever. The agricultural commodity has been trading up since Russia invaded Ukraine last Thursday and the US and Europe imposed sanctions on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's regime. The armed conflict has forced the closure of major ports in Ukraine, as well as the disruption of logistics and transport links. The fighting also threatens work in the fields over the coming months.Trade with Russia has dried up mainly because buyers find the complexity of the sanctions difficult to navigate and refuse to raise insurance costs. Russia and Ukraine are also major producers of corn -- which has touched 2012 highs -- barley and sunflower oil. Soybean oil and palm oil have already reached record highs.\par \par \uc2 10:13 a.m. Attack on Zaporizhia power plant leaves two injured. Atomic Energy Agency says reactors are safe Ukraine: \uc2 IAEA\uc2  Director-General ready to go to Kiev to negotiate safety of nuclear power plants Loading video ... The director general of the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ) said today he is available to go to Ukraine to negotiate a solution to ensure the safety of nuclear facilities threatened by the war. The director of the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ), \uc2 Rafael Mariano Grossi\uc2 , advanced this Friday, during a press conference, that the Russian attack on the Zaporizhia nuclear power plant in Ukraine has caused two wounded, apparently security guards of the building.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IAEA\uc2  director general also assured that the reactors of the plant were not affected. "It is important to say that all the safety systems of the six reactors at the plant were not affected at all. There was no leakage of radioactive material," stressed \uc2 Rafael Mariano Grossi\uc2 , who added that "only the adjacent building of the plant was affected.\par \par \uc2 Despite everything, the head of the agency stressed how this situation "demonstrates the fragile situation we live in. This attack proves that this situation [the war] is not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 "We are willing to help. We live an abnormal normality that needs to be resolved," warned \uc2 Rafael Mariano Grossi\uc2 . The fire at the plant has since been extinguished, according to local authorities. The Russians are already taking over the facility.\par \par \uc2 10:08 a.m. Ukraine Sinks\par \par \uc2 Frigate to Avoid Russian Capture The Ukrainian navy followed government orders and sank the frigate Hetman Sagaidachny, which was under repair, to avoid capture by the Russians, Defense Minister Alexei Reznikov said in a statement Friday.\par \par \uc2 Pictures of the sunken ship near the port city of Mykolaiv have already made their way around the Internet and are being posted on social networks of various citizens. This move by the Ukrainian navy comes at a time when Russian troops are getting closer and closer to the main targets along the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 09:20 German Bunds return to negative territory The interest rates on ten-year sovereign debt are easing in the euro zone, with German bonds returning to negative territory\par \par \uc2 . The yield on ten-year German bunds - the benchmark for the European market - returned to negative territory for the second time this week, easing 4.9 basis points to -0.037%, the most significant relief in the entire eurozone\par \par \uc2 . Interest rates on Italian debt with the same maturity are also down 3.1 basis points, but still above 1%, specifically at 1.536%\par \par \uc2 . In the Iberian Peninsula, interest rates on Spanish and Portuguese 10-year bonds are falling below 1%, more specifically, in the case of Castilian debt, down 4.1 basis points to 0.915, while in Portuguese bonds with the same maturity, the yield is easing 4.2 basis points to 0.813%.\par \par \uc2 09:08 am Europe painted red. European equity funds with worst week ever Europe opened the session painted red, after feeling the threat of a nuclear disaster with a fire, now extinguished, in the block's largest power plant, based in the Zaporizhzhia region in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The European reference index, Stoxx 600, started the session losing 0.8% to 433.53 points, being pressured mainly by the auto (-3.74%) and banking sectors (-3.04%). Of the twenty sectors that make up the European benchmark, only utilities are in the green, up 0.64%.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx 600 suffered a 12% drop in February, penalized mainly by the invasion of Ukraine, ending up undoing the forecasts of the main investment houses that pointed out that European stocks would end the year with a better performance than US securities (due to the absence of interest rate hikes)\par \par \uc2 . In the remaining European markets, Paris registers the most expressive drop (-2.40%), followed by Milan (-1.88%), Frankfurt (-1.66%) and Amsterdam (-1.61%). The Spanish \uc2 IBEX\uc2  falls 0.45%, while the British FTSE slides 0.92%. In Europe, the PSI-20 has the slightest drop in Europe (-0.24%), after having opened the session in the green, mainly motivated by the gains of the EDP family.\par \par \uc2 For analysts, the crisis in Ukraine is leading investors to sell more and more shares as the conflict escalates."An event of this magnitude has financial consequences. Investors fear a worsening of inflation due to the war, so they have already started selling European assets," \uc2 Peter Oppenheimer\uc2 , chief strategist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  warned in a statement to Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 European equity funds recorded their highest-ever outflows in the week ending March 2, according to data compiled by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and cited by \uc2 Bloomberg\par \par \uc2 . 08:49 Oil heading for biggest weekly gain in nearly two years Oil is heading for the biggest weekly gain in nearly two years, according to data compiled by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 , due to the escalation in the conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 After yesterday touching below the $108 per barrel level, futures on West Texas Intermediate (WTI) - a reference for the North American market - scheduled for delivery in April are up 0.84% to $108.57 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Futures on North Sea Brent - a reference for European imports - scheduled for delivery in May are up 0.35% to $110.85 per barrel. Brent futures have risen 14% this week.\par \par \uc2 After "black gold" prices eased overnight, they have risen again as the bombing of the nuclear power plant in Ukraine, considered the largest nuclear power plant in Europe, took hold.\par \par \uc2 "We must understand that the threat of a larger-scale global energy crisis remains standing as the conflict in Ukraine ratchets up the tone," \uc2 Jeffrey Halley\uc2 , an analyst at Oanda Asia Pacific, warned in an interview with \uc2 Bloomberg\par \par \uc2 . The fact that Russian barrels are not reaching Europe is causing a sense of uncertainty in the market, with some investment houses such as \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  pointing the Brent crude price to $185 a barrel by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 08:49 Euro depreciates. Ruble at 2003 lows The euro falls 0.5% to $1.1011 in the early hours of Friday. The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had one of the worst performances in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the start of the war in Ukraine, plunging 3.7 percent in February, according to data compiled by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These results lead analysts to fear that the euro will fall at or below parity with the dollar later this year, a phenomenon whose probability has increased 9.6 percent since the start of the year, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported.\par \par \uc2 "The war in Ukraine and the Fed's interest rate hike are seriously hurting the euro\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If this war drags on there is a risk of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approaching parity with the dollar," Scotiabank strategist Qi Gao warned in an interview with Bloomberg TV.\par \par \uc2 Forits part, the Bloomberg dollar index - which compares the "green note" with 16 rival \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - is up 0.29% to 98.07 points. The dollar/ruble pair, meanwhile, continues to renew 2003 lows, losing 3.98% to 105.108 rubles\par \par \uc2 . 7:59 am Fire has been put out. Russia controls Europe's largest nuclear power plant Russian forces have occupied Europe's largest nuclear power plant, according to officials in Ukraine, cited by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 . After the overnight fire that followed a Russian attack overnight.\par \par \uc2 Europe woke up to Kiev's announcement that "Europe's largest nuclear power plant," based in Zaporizhzhia burned down early this morning following a Russian attack\par \par \uc2 . Theregion is currently one of the stages of Russia's war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The fire broke out moments after a Russian bombing raid on unit one\par \par \uc2 of the\par \par \uc2 nuclear power plant, a space of about 2,000 square meters.\par \par \uc2 After smoke from the fire began painting the Zaporizhzhia sky black, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  recorded a message via video, during which he stressed that "if there is an explosion it is the end of Europe."\par \par \uc2 The White House called on Russia to stop bombing in the region, after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spoke by phone with his Ukrainian counterpart. Meanwhile the fire has already been extinguished, according to a post by local authorities on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . The \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  also announced that the plant's "radioactivity levels remain stable."\par \par \uc2 07:48 Moscow Stock Exchange closed for fifth consecutive day The Moscow Stock Exchange will remain closed this Friday for the fifth consecutive day due to the impact of Western sanctions against Russian banks, sovereign debt and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2 . The Russian financial regulator said in a statement that the decision not to resume securities trading is due to fears of a collapse in major securities as a result of sanctions imposed following Russia's invasion of Ukraine.The Moscow stock exchange has been closed since Monday after the EU, the United States, Canada and other partners excluded some Russian banks from the SWIFT international interbank communication system, an unprecedented move to isolate the country from the global financial system.The ruble then registered a sharp drop. Before closing, the stock market fell 45% and major stocks lost more than 58%. The Bank of Russia had hoped that the measures taken to stabilize the market would succeed in reassuring Russian investors.Among other measures, it decided to allow banks hit by sanctions to use their accumulated capital reserve to continue operating and raised interest rates to 20%, while the government decided to use up to one trillion rubles (\u8364\'20\'AC8.9 billion) to the National Welfare Fund - fed by oil revenues and a kind of 'cushion' to use in times of crisis - to be used to buy shares in Russian companies.\par \par \uc2 07:44 Gold with a weekly gain of more than 2%. Palladium up 18% Fears over the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed) raising interest rates and the fire at Europe's largest nuclear power plant, intensifying investors' fears of an escalating war in Ukraine led precious metals to rise this morning.\par \par \uc2 Gold is up 0.10% to $1,937.26 an ounce. Since the start of the week the yellow metal has gained 2.7%, a move accompanied by palladium - whose 40% of world production is handled by Russia - which has appreciated 18%. Silver and platinum also followed this positive trend.\par \par \uc2 This week and before Congress, Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  confirmed the need to raise interest rates by 0.25 basis points. Also weighing on investor sentiment is Friday's fire at the Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant in Ukraine, which deepened fears of an escalation of the conflict in the region and a nuclear disaster in Europe.\par \par \uc2 "I expect the price of gold to continue on a very positive path. Investors are probably taking profits and looking for the next step to make more money in the gold market," explained Gavin Wendt, an analyst at MineLife Pty in an interview with \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 07:38 a.m. European futures point to sharp drop Fear of a nuclear threat is being reflected in market sentiment through futures trading, with European futures pointing to a sharp drop.\par \par \uc2 Futures on the Euro Stoxx 50 are down 1.9%, a movement accompanied by futures trading in other markets, such as Frankfurt (-1.7%) and London (-0.68).\par \par \uc2 "The news about the Russian bomber at what was classified as the largest nuclear power plant in Europe created a sense of fear in investors that was reflected in futures trading," explained Chamath Silva, an analyst at Australia's BetaShares, in an interview with Bloomberg TV.\par \par \uc2 In Asia, the falls at the end of the session were quite expressive, with the Nikkei falling 2.25%, the Topix sinking 1.96% while in Hong Kong, the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  fell 2.60% and Shanghai slid 0\par \par \uc2 .96%. 00:25 Fire in Europe's largest nuclear power plant after Russian attack A fire broke out at the Zaporizhzhya nuclear power plant, Europe's largest and which supplies one-third of the energy to Ukraine, after a Russian bombing, the local mayor indicated. \uc2 YouTube\uc2  footage of the fire was broadcast on a local channel.On his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  demanded that Russian forces immediately cease bombing and warned that if the plant explodes it will be "10 times worse than Chernobyl."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220304ei34006l0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
