\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 LIVE | Spain to send grenade launchers, ammunition and machine guns to Ukraine\b0\par\par\uc2 3,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 This Wednesday marks seven days since Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered a military invasion of Ukraine, which has claimed the lives of dozens of citizens. There are also reports of injuries, thousands of people displaced by the war and destruction.\par \par \uc2 The military offensive continues as the Ukrainian government waits for the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to accept the country as a member of the organization.\par \par \uc2 This is a minute-by-minute account of the seventh day of the Russian military invasion of Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 3:45 p.m. Russian missile breaks heating distribution pipeline in Kiev. "A major heating pipeline in Kiev was damaged as a result of the missile attack in the area of the central railway station. A part of the city could be deprived of heating tonight," Ukrainian Interior Minister's adviser Anton Gerashenko wrote on Telegram. The missile was intercepted by anti-aircraft defenses with a loud explosion and, falling, hit the pipeline.\par \par \uc2 3:20 p.m. Moscow admits death of Russian soldiers. The Moscow Defense Ministry admitted the death of 498 Russian soldiers in the war against Ukraine. Among the Ukrainians there are 2,870 dead and about 3,700 wounded.\par \par \uc2 Russia's largest bank announces its exit from the market due to sanctions and 9 other economic news around the world on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 02:40 p.m. Spain will send grenade launchers, ammunition and machine guns to Ukraine. Spain will send next Friday two planes with a first shipment of offensive military material to Ukraine, including 1,370 grenade launchers against tanks, 700,000 cartridges for rifles and machine guns, as well as light machine guns.\par \par \uc2 In a television interview, the Minister of Defense, Margarita Robles, has detailed that this first shipment will arrive in Poland, at a point very close to the Ukrainian border, in two planes.\par \par \uc2 Macron: Europe must spend more to become an independent power. The French president assured in a televised speech that Europe must spend more to be "an independent and sovereign power", because its "freedom is not guaranteed". Macron called for a definitive push for renewable energies and the construction of new nuclear reactors on the continent.\par \par \uc2 Next week's \uc2 European Union\uc2  summit in Versailles should begin to take decisions on these issues, he advanced. Macron assured that the invasion of Ukraine signals "a change of era", in which "democracy is not considered as an unquestionable regime", so Europe must take "historic decisions" in its defense.\par \par \uc2 02:10 p.m. Zelenski's chief of staff calls for more sanctions "from a bunker". \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2 , Ukrainian chief of staff, signs an op-ed in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  today written "from a bunker," in which he calls on the West for more economic sanctions and warns of Russia's imperialist intentions. "As I write, President Zelenski is at my side. Please help us show Putin the mistake he has made," the caption reads.\par \par \uc2 Yermak, who applauds civilians who have turned to arms and warns about Russian attacks on residential areas, reminds that there is a 64-kilometer military caravan heading to Kiev, so he also calls for anti-tank and anti-aircraft equipment, as well as ammunition.\par \par \uc2 01:50 p.m. The United States "will support diplomatic efforts" by Kiev to reach a cease-fire and the withdrawal of Russian forces from Ukraine, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "If there are diplomatic steps that we can take that the Ukrainian government believes would be helpful, we are prepared to take them, even as we continue to support Ukraine's ability to defend itself," he told a news conference in Washington.\par \par \uc2 01:30 p.m. Blinken will visit Poland, the Baltic countries and Moldova in support of Ukraine. U.S. chief diplomat \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  will travel in the coming days to Belgium, Poland, the Baltic countries and Moldova to reaffirm Washington's support for Ukraine in the wake of the Russian invasion, the State Department announced Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The trip, March 3-8, "continues extensive consultations and coordination with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies and European partners on the continuation of the Russian Federation's premeditated, unprovoked and unjustified war against Ukraine," State Department spokesman \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 01:00 p.m. The U.S. announced sanctions on 22 entities related to the Russian Defense sector and restrictions on technology exports that could support Russia's ability to refine oil.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the \uc2 U.S. Commerce Department\uc2  extended to Belarus the export controls it has imposed for Russia in order to "significantly degrade" Minsk's ability to support Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 U.S. accuses Russia at the \uc2 UN\uc2  of mobilizing banned weapons. "It appears that Russia is preparing to increase the brutality of its campaign against Ukraine," U.S. Ambassador to the \uc2 UN\uc2 Linda Thomas-Greenfield\uc2  told the General Assembly. "We have seen videos of Russian forces moving exceptionally lethal weaponry into Ukraine that has no place on the battlefield. That includes cluster and vacuum bombs and munitions, which are banned by the Geneva Convention," she asserted.\par \par \uc2 1:00 p.m. Russian fighter jets violated Swedish airspace. The Swedish Army reported that four Russian fighter jets violated Swedish airspace in the Baltic.\par \par \uc2 12:30 p.m. \uc2 European Commission\uc2  suspends scientific cooperation with Russia. In a statement, the European Commissioner for Innovation, Research, Culture and Education, Bulgarian \uc2 Mariya Gabriel\uc2 , indicated that she has instructed her collaborators "not to undertake any more cooperation projects" with Russia following that country's attack on neighboring Ukraine. Ongoing initiatives were suspended and outstanding payments were frozen. Gabriel also called for "maximum flexibility" with Ukrainian students in Erasmus+ programs" so that they can cancel or postpone scheduled activities.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine puts Russian casualties at more than 7,000. Since the beginning of the Russian invasion of Ukraine, more than 7,000 Russian soldiers have been killed, while hundreds have been taken prisoner, including senior officers, Oleksiy Arestovich, military adviser to Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 , said Wednesday. The first figure offered by the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  a short time earlier fell short of half a thousand dead.\par \par \uc2 12:00 p.m. Russia does not plan to use nuclear weapons under any circumstances. \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman \uc2 Maria Zakharova\uc2  stated that her country under no circumstances intends to press "the nuclear red button", following Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's recent order to put Russia's nuclear forces into "special service regime".\par \par \uc2 "We start from the point that this apocalyptic script will not be carried out under any pretext and under any conditions," he said in an interview to the Colombian channel W Radio, stressing that Moscow has made its position clear on the matter. "Apparently they confuse us with the United States, but Russia has never spoken" of using nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 11:50 a.m. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  adopted new economic sanctions against Belarus for its role in the Russian-initiated invasion against Ukraine. In an official note, the EU Council reported that it adopted restrictions "on trade in goods used in the production or manufacture of tobacco products, mineral fuels and bituminous substances." Earlier, sanctions had been announced against 22 high-ranking military officials.\par \par \uc2 11:30 a.m. \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  condemns Russian invasion of Ukraine. The \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  passed a resolution condemning the Russian invasion of Ukraine, with the support of 141 of the 193 \uc2 UN\uc2  member states. The text "deplores" the Russian aggression against Ukraine and "demands" that Moscow put an end to it and immediately and unconditionally withdraw its troops from the neighboring country.\par \par \uc2 The resolution, which is not binding, received only five votes against (Russia, Belarus, Syria, North Korea and Eritrea) and 35 abstentions, including Nicaragua and El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 11:00 a.m. A Ukrainian delegation is heading to Belarus for a second round of negotiations with Russia, which invaded Ukraine last week, the government in Kiev said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The Ukrainian delegation is heading to the venue of the negotiations," the Ukrainian presidency said.\par \par \uc2 Russia said the venue of the meeting is near the Belarusian-Polish border and that the agenda includes discussions on a cessation of hostilities.\par \par \uc2 10:57 a.m. Russia says it lost 498 soldiers in Ukraine, in first balance since the invasion. In the first casualty balance sheet published by the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2 , it admitted losing almost five hundred soldiers and more than a thousand wounded. "Unfortunately (...) we had losses. 498 Russian servicemen were killed (...) 1,597 of our comrades were wounded" during the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched on February 24, declared the army spokesman, Igor Konashenkov, in images broadcast by the Russian public television. Casualties among "Ukrainian military and nationalists" amounted to "2,870 dead and about 3,700 wounded," he said.\par \par \uc2 Germany uses its oil reserve to contain prices. "It is our contribution to the international concert. In times like these it is important to act with unity," declared Economy Minister Green \uc2 Robert Habeck\uc2 , according to the ministry.\par \par \uc2 The German contribution corresponds to the 5.4% that its oil consumption represents in relation to the total of the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2 , whose member countries agreed yesterday to release 60 million barrels, or 435,000 tons of crude. That is 3% of Germany's strategic reserve.\par \par \uc2 10:55 a.m. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  reiterated the "urgent need" to establish a safe corridor to facilitate the arrival of medical supplies. The director general of this organization, \uc2 Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus\uc2 , pointed out in a virtual press conference that the first shipment with sanitary material, transported from Dubai, will arrive tomorrow in Poland.\par \par \uc2 "There is some access to material, but given the evolution of the conflict, we fear a worsening of the situation," said \uc2 WHO\uc2 -Ukraine director Jarno Habicht, who focused concerns on the lack of oxygen and medicines and problems in carrying out the polio vaccination campaign.\par \par \uc2 German supermarkets exclude Russian products. From vodka to toothpaste, several supermarket chains in Germany have decided to exclude products imported from Russia, as a sign of solidarity with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 10:50 a.m. France vetoes cultural events and artists linked to the Russian government. The French Minister of Culture announced on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that her country has decided to suspend the holding of any new cultural events linked to official Russian institutions or Russian artists who are in favor of Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We stand in solidarity with hard-hit Ukrainian artists, cultural professionals and journalists. We do not forget Russian dissidents. Together, they keep democracy and culture alive every day, a ferment of peace and dialogue between peoples," he said. And he added that "this geopolitical crisis is accompanied by a war of information and propaganda".\par \par \uc2 Borrell offers cyber and anti-disinformation help to Moldova. "Disinformation has become a war in itself and is an active component of what is happening in Ukraine," EU High Representative for Foreign Policy \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2  said after meeting with Moldovan President \uc2 Maia Sandu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We stand ready to support Moldova in its efforts to guarantee the right to information for its citizens," said Borrell, who also offered cybersecurity support and welcomed the effort to receive refugees.\par \par \uc2 10:42 a.m. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  assured that a ban on Russian oil imports into the United States is not "off the table" in retaliation for the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Nothing is off the table," he told reporters when asked if oil could be the next target of unprecedented U.S. and Western sanctions against Moscow.\par \par \uc2 10:40 a.m. Ukraine's ambassador to the United Nations, Sergiy Kyslytsya, denounced that the Russian invasion is causing a "genocide" in his country before the rostrum of the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  that is to vote on a resolution condemning such an attack.\par \par \uc2 "It is already clear that Russia's goal is not just occupation. It is genocide," the ambassador said.\par \par \uc2 10:20 a.m. The \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  tasked a dozen prosecutors with prosecuting "corrupt Russian oligarchs" and anyone who violates Washington's sanctions against Moscow following the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "We will spare no effort to investigate, arrest and prosecute all those whose criminal acts enable the Russian government to continue this unjust war," U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 10:00 a.m. The \uc2 World Health Organization (WHO) \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  (WHO) expressed "concern" over reports of attacks on hospitals and health sites during the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "We are very concerned because of reports of attacks on health facilities," \uc2 WHO\uc2  Director-General \uc2 Tedros Ghebreyesus\uc2  said at an online press conference.\par \par \uc2 09:55 a.m. A draft resolution condemning the Russian invasion of Ukraine is being voted on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Much of the international community accuses \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's Russia of violating Article 2 of the UN Charter, which urges its members not to resort to threats or force to settle disputes.\par \par \uc2 09:50 a.m. Biden does not rule out sanctions against Russian oil imports.\par \par \uc2 Pope donates medical supplies to be sent to Ukraine. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  has donated medical material to be sent in the coming days to Ukraine to help a population that is suffering from Russia's invasion, informed the head of the Vatican limosneria, Polish Cardinal Konrad Krajewski. Through the limosneria, the organism in charge of carrying out charitable works on behalf of the Pope, Francis sent medical material to the Basilica of St. Sophia in Rome, a reference point for the Ukrainian community and from where for days now truckloads of food and subsistence items have been leaving for Ukraine. The papal almoner today personally took it upon himself to bring to the basilica "the Pope's help and closeness, taking up an appeal launched in recent days by the Ukrainian community in Italy asking for medical supplies," the Vatican explained.\par \par \uc2 09:00 a.m. Hundreds of citizens and employees of the Zaporozhie nuclear power plant, in the east of the country, prevented Wednesday with barricades and tankers the advance of a column of the Russian Army. "We explained to them (the Russian military) our position, that the Zaporozhie Nuclear Power Plant and the city are under secure protection. The residents will not let them take a single step in the city," the city's mayor Dmitry Orlov said, according to UNIAN agency.\par \par \uc2 Vodka and other Russian products are out of several German supermarkets. From vodka to toothpaste, several supermarket chains in Germany have decided to exclude products imported from Russia as a sign of solidarity with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Until now, the 'discounter' \uc2 Aldi Nord\uc2  only offered a single item from Russia: vodka in 0.70-liter bottle.\par \par \uc2 08:40 a.m. Belgium announced Wednesday the shipment of humanitarian aid supplies, such as tents, blankets and cold-adapted sanitary equipment, to Ukraine in solidarity with the Kiev authorities and the population suffering from Russia's military invasion. The shipment is to be channeled through the B-FAST emergency aid mechanism, an inter-ministerial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism for disasters abroad, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs said in a statement. Belgium is thus responding "to the request for assistance sent by Ukraine through the European Union Civil Protection Mechanism (EUCPM) following Russia's military invasion of Ukraine," the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Russia is keeping the stock market closed for the third day for the third consecutive day on fears of a plunge in major stocks due to heavy economic sanctions imposed by the West over Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Russia decided not to resume trading on March 2, 2022 on the Moscow Stock Exchange in the securities market section (...)" with some exceptions, it said in a statement. The exceptions concern direct orders with settlements in rubles, derivatives - except for derivatives market instruments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs section - precious metals and some contracts for futures in closing positions, among others.\par \par \uc2 08:20 a.m. The \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IAEA\uc2 ) ruled out Wednesday that Ukraine is developing nuclear weapons, as Russia alleges to justify, among other issues,its invasion of the neighboring country.\par \par \uc2 "For us this issue is very clear. We have no information that would call into question Ukraine's (nuclear) non-proliferation credentials," \uc2 IAEA\uc2  Director General \uc2 Rafael Grossi\uc2  told a press conference in Vienna. "We have no information about any detour of any kind of material, any undeclared material or activities aimed at the development of nuclear weapons" in Ukraine, the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  official said.\par \par \uc2 08:10 a.m. Ukrainian delegation for the Beijing Paralympic Games (March 4-13) has arrived safely in China despite the conflict in Ukraine, the \uc2 International Paralympic Committee (IPC) \uc2 International Paralympic Committee\uc2  (IPC) announced Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "I am happy to say that just a few hours ago, the Ukrainian team consisting of 20 athletes and 9 guides has arrived safely in Beijing" said at a press conference \uc2 Andrew Parsons\uc2 , president of the international organization.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine reports more than 2,000 civilians killed. More than 2,000 Ukrainian civilians have died since the beginning of the invasion launched by Russia on February 24, denounced Wednesday the State Emergency Service of Ukraine (DSNS, for its acronym in Ukrainian). "During the seven days of the war Russia has destroyed hundreds of transport infrastructure, housing, hospitals and kindergartens. In this time more than 2,000 Ukrainians have died, not counting our defenders," the DSNS noted on its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 08:00 a.m. The Spanish Prime Minister, Socialist Pedro Sanchez, announced Wednesday that his country will follow the lead of other nations and deliver "offensive military material" to "the Ukrainian resistance."\par \par \uc2 "As I see that there are (political) groups that question the commitment of the government of Spain" to Ukraine, "I also want to announce to them that Spain will deliver to the Ukrainian resistance offensive military material," Sanchez declared in the Congress of Deputies.\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson accuses Russia of "war crime". British Prime Minister Boris Johnson on Wednesday called Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a war criminal and urged \uc2 UN\uc2  member countries to condemn the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Johnson made the remarks in the House of Commons in London, where the Ukrainian ambassador was present and received a standing ovation from MPs.\par \par \uc2 07:56 a.m. Ukraine invites mothers of captured Russian soldiers to fetch them.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian military on Wednesday invited mothers of captured Russian soldiers to fetch them to Ukraine, on the seventh day of the invasion launched by Moscow during which it claims to have taken dozens of prisoners.\par \par \uc2 "It was decided to hand over the captured Russian soldiers to their mothers if they come to fetch them to Ukraine, to Kiev," the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Refugees. The number of Ukrainian refugees in neighboring countries rose by 200,000 people in 24 hours, according to the latest \uc2 UN\uc2  figures released Wednesday, bringing the total number to 874,000 since the start of the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine borders seven countries, Russia to the north and east, Belarus in the north, Poland and Slovakia to the west and Romania, Hungary and Moldova in the southwest.\par \par \uc2 07:40 a.m. The "consequences" of the war in Ukraine for the U.S. economy are "very uncertain," Jerome Powell, chairman of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Fed), said in a speech prepared for his appearance before the House of Representatives on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With prices rising at a pace not seen in four decades and oil above $100 a barrel, the Fed chief repeated that they are willing to raise interest rates to curb inflation. But "the economy is evolving in unexpected ways," he said, and "we will have to be nimble to respond." The Fed is due to decide how much to raise interest rates at its March 14-15 meeting.\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine triggers oil, gas, aluminum and nickel prices. Oil prices continued to soar Wednesday, natural gas and aluminum hit record highs and nickel hit a decade high, driven by the war in Ukraine, which fuels fears of energy and commodity supply disruption.\par \par \uc2 U.S. WTI crude rose to $111.50 a barrel, its highest price since 2013, while Brent North Sea crude hit $113.02 a barrel, a high since 2014.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220303ei320001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
