\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Tuesday, March 1, 2022 (04.00 GMT)\b0\par\par\uc2 1,986 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 ============== UKRAINE WAR ============== NEGOTIATION Russians and Ukrainians will resume negotiations at the Polish-Ukrainian border Moscow On the fifth day of the Russian military offensive against Ukraine, while armed actions continue and Russia is increasingly isolated by Western sanctions, the first meeting of negotiators from both countries held at the Belarusian-Ukrainian border gave a glimmer of hope, as both sides claimed to have found points of agreement that will allow them to sit down again in a few days. "We found some points on which we can forecast common positions, and the most important thing is that we agreed to continue the negotiation process," the head of the Russian delegation, presidential aide \uc2 Vladimir Medinsky\uc2 , told reporters. The next meeting will be held in a few days on the Polish-Belarusian border. There is a corresponding agreement," Medinski announced.\par \par \uc2  NEGOTIATION Zelenski says Ukraine received "some signals" in negotiation with Russia Kiev Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelenski said tonight that Ukraine has received "some signals" from Russia during the negotiation between representatives of the two countries on Monday, but it is still not the result he would like to have. "So far, we do not have the result we would like to have. Russia has expressed its points, we have expressed ours to end the war. We received some signals. When the delegation returns to Kiev, we will analyze what we have heard and then decide how to move to the second round," Zelenski said, commenting on the outcome of the meeting on the Ukrainian-Belarusian border. ICC ICC prosecutor asks to open investigation for war crimes in Ukraine The Hague The chief prosecutor of the \uc2 International Criminal Court (ICC)\uc2 , Karim Khan, announced today that he will ask the judges to open an investigation for war crimes and crimes against humanity "committed by any of the parties throughout the territory of Ukraine." The investigation, if authorized, will deal with crimes committed on the Crimean peninsula and in the self-proclaimed republics of Donetsk and Lugansk, as Khan's predecessor, \uc2 Fatou Bensouda\uc2 , advanced in December 2020, but the British jurist added that the inquiries will include crimes related to "the expansion of the conflict in recent days" in Ukraine. DIALOGUE Putin pledges to Macron to respect civilians in Ukraine Paris Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Monday pledged to French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  to suspend attacks on the Ukrainian population, homes and civilian infrastructure. Macron called Putin today, at the request of his Ukrainian counterpart, Volodymir Zelenski, to whom he formulated these requests, as well as that the security of the roads be guaranteed, especially that of the south of Kiev, reported a statement from the Elysee. UN West seeks to isolate Russia at \uc2 UN\uc2  over its invasion of Ukraine United Nations The \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  on Monday began an emergency special session on Ukraine, in which the West seeks to diplomatically isolate Russia by passing a condemnation of its invasion with the strongest possible backing. The General Assembly - the body in which the 193 member states of the United Nations sit - will vote later this week, probably on Wednesday, on a resolution similar to the one vetoed by Moscow in the Security Council last Friday. In the Assembly, no country has veto power; the text only needs two thirds of the chamber to go ahead and, although it is not binding, it will represent an important statement of the positions of the countries. On the other hand, the United States declared this Monday persona non grata to twelve diplomats of the Russian mission to the United Nations whom it accuses of "espionage" and gave them until March 7 to leave the country. EU Zelenski signs application for Ukraine's entry into the European Union Kiev Ukrainian President Volodymir Zelenski on Monday put his signature on the application for the country's entry into the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . "It is a historic moment," says a message from the Verkhovna Rada (the Ukrainian Parliament) on Telegram, where the Ukrainian leader can also be seen at the moment of signing the document. Also present at the signing of the document were Rada Speaker \uc2 Ruslan Stefanchuk\uc2  and Ukrainian Prime Minister Denys Shmyhal. The Swiss government today joined the US and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and announced today the freezing of the assets of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , his Prime Minister Mikhail Michoustine, his Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov and other members of his cabinet. These sanctions will take effect immediately and are a consequence of violations of international law for which Switzerland holds these individuals responsible. FINANCIAL SANCTIONS Russia suffers onslaught of Western sanctions with historic fall of the ruble Moscow Russia suffered this Monday the onslaught of the new Western sanctions, which target the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and include the expulsion of several banks from the SWIFT system, which resulted in the collapse of almost 30% of the ruble and forced Moscow to bring out its arsenal to ensure financial stability. Early this morning, the ruble plummeted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market against the dollar and the euro by almost 30%, a drop not seen since at least 1993 and 1994, respectively, something that the Kremlin's words or the urgent measures taken by the Bank of Russia could not change either. Putin today banned the transfer of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  abroad and ordered companies with economic activities abroad in the framework of foreign trade to convert 80% of their revenues into rubles, according to a decree signed Monday by the president to defend Russia from Western sanctions. TURKEY Turkey closes the Bosphorus to warships from all countries Istanbul The Turkish government announced today that it is closing the Bosphorus Strait to the passage of military vessels from all countries, although those ships based in the Black Sea, such as those from Russia or Ukraine, will still be able to return to their ports, in application of the Montreux Convention. "We have warned all countries, whether they border the Black Sea or not, that no warships should pass through the Bosporus and the Dardanelles," Turkish Foreign Minister Mevl\u252\'00\'FCt \u199\'00\'C7avusoglu told reporters after finishing a cabinet session, reports NTV channel. REFUGEES Some 500,000 Ukrainians have fled the Russian invasion to neighboring countries Przemysl (Poland)/Bucharest Thousands of Ukrainians continue to flee today to neighboring countries to the south and west, with Poland the main recipient of the more than 500,000 refugees counted by the United Nations since the Russian invasion began last Thursday. In fact, more than half of those refugees have arrived in Poland. To the city of Przemysl, on the border with Ukraine, trains arrive from Kiev, chartered by the Polish government, crowded with people, mostly women and children. A Polish soldier explained to Efe this morning that the flow of people is incessant LATIN AMERICA Latin American countries ask \uc2 UN\uc2  for help to evacuate citizens from Ukraine United Nations Eighteen Latin American and Caribbean countries asked the United Nations on Monday for help to evacuate their citizens who are in Ukraine. In a letter made public by the delegation of the Dominican Republic to the \uc2 UN\uc2 , the countries asked \uc2 UN\uc2  Secretary-General \uc2 Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres\uc2  to support efforts to evacuate their nationals and their families. The letter is signed by Antigua and Barbuda, Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Dominican Republic, Ecuador, El Salvador, Guatemala, Jamaica, Mexico, Panama, Paraguay, Peru, Trinidad and Tobago, Uruguay and the Dominican Republic. AIRSPACE Russia closes its airspace to 36 European countries and Canada Moscow Russia has closed its airspace to flights from 36 countries, including all \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries and Canada, in response to the same measure adopted by these states after the start of the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Federal Air Transport Agency\uc2  (\uc2 Rosaviatsia\uc2 ) reported today in a statement. "In accordance with the norms of international law, as a measure in response to the ban by European countries on flights of civilian aircraft of Russian and/or Russian-registered airlines, the restriction has been ordered for flights of airlines of 36 states," the agency says. USA Disney will not release its films in Russia because of the invasion of Ukraine Los Angeles (USA) \uc2 The Walt Disney Company\uc2  communicated on Monday that it will not release its new films in Russia because of the invasion of Ukraine. "Given the unprovoked invasion of Ukraine and the tragic humanitarian crisis, we will pause the release of our films in Russia, including \uc2 Pixar\uc2 's upcoming 'Turning Red,'" the company said in a statement. The company added that it "will make future business decisions" as the situation evolves and assured that it will provide humanitarian assistance through other NGO partners. ------------- ARGENTINA FIRE FIGHTS Rain mitigates fires in Argentina that devastated 900,000 hectares Buenos Aires The rainy weekend has contributed to mitigate most of the fires in the Argentine province of Corrientes (northeast), where the fire has devastated more than 900,000 hectares during the last weeks, official sources informed on Monday. According to the latest report of the Ministry of Environment, on Monday there were only two active fire outbreaks in the province, specifically in the towns of Corrientes and Santo Tom\u233\'00\'E9. Likewise, a total of four fires are "contained" and another five are "controlled", while six are already extinguished. USA CAPITOL The first trial against a US Capitol assailant begins in Washington The first trial against a US Capitol assailant began on Monday with the selection of the jury that will judge Guy Reffitt, a man from a far-right organization that went armed to the attack on the parliamentary precinct. Until now, all those convicted of storming the Capitol on January 6 last year to prevent the ratification of the electoral victory of the now president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , had reached plea bargains with the prosecution, which spared them the trial. MEXICO VIOLENCE Hitmen hide bodies of "shot" after funeral attack Morelia (Mexico) Authorities in the western Mexican state of Michoac\u225\'00\'E1n are looking for a group of hitmen who "apparently shot" a dozen people and hid their bodies after the funeral of the mother of an alleged drug trafficker in the town of San Jos\u233\'00\'E9 de Gracia. Adrian Lopez Solis, Attorney General of Michoacan, reported that there is evidence to confirm that among the possible victims is the alleged drug trafficker Alejandro G. B., alias "El Pelon", whose mother's funeral was held on a street in the downtown area of San Jose de Gracia, in the municipality of Marcos Castellanos. NORTH KOREA MISSILES Pionyang says latest test was to develop reconnaissance satellite Seoul North Korea reported today that the projectile it test-fired on Sunday was a test for the development of a reconnaissance satellite. "On February 27, the National Aerospace Development Administration (NADA) of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea (North Korea's official name) and the Academy of Defense Science conducted a major test in accordance with the procedure plan for the development of a reconnaissance satellite," the country's leading daily Rodong reported. UN ENVIRONMENT UN Assembly moves towards pact against plastic pollution Nairobi The 5th UN Environment Assembly (UNEA-5), the world's leading environmental decision-making body, today in Nairobi made good progress in pushing for the first global treaty against plastic pollution. "Things are looking good. We are very close to agreement on an internationally binding treaty to end plastic waste," said Norwegian Environment Minister \uc2 Espen Barth Eide\uc2 , whose country holds the Assembly's presidency. BURMA CRISIS Burmese military holding 80 children hostage, opposition says Bangkok Burmese military are holding 80 children hostage, all under 12 and some under 5, during an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the central Sagaing region, the opposition National Unity Government (NUG) said Monday. According to the NUG, the military junta that seized power in Burma a year ago began air strikes on Saturday on the village of Chin Pone to later assault the population. EFE cd.gcf\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220301ei310024q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
