\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Wall Street has a second negative month due to Ukraine invasion\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 New York, Feb 28 After a January marked by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's shift towards a more aggressive monetary policy, Wall Street closed this Monday its second negative month on the stock market due to the anticipation and geopolitical effect of the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Risk aversion led to significant cuts in the US stock market: the Dow Jones Industrials index fell by 3.5 %, the S&P 500 by 3.1 % and the Nasdaq index by 3.43 %. In Europe, the results were generally worse, as Frankfurt fell by 6.53 %, Paris by 4.86 %, Milan by 5.21 %, Madrid by 1.55 % and London by 0.08 %. The New York stock market seemed to bounce back at the beginning of the month, but the spirits were dampened by the escalation of tensions in Eastern Europe which led on February 24 to the order for military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Russian President, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Some analysts noted that the market then followed a "sell the rumor, buy the news" strategy that led to a surprising turnaround in the stock market, bouncing from the worst losses of the year to the best session on record since late 2020. However, the past few days have been dominated by volatility as the U.S. and its Western allies imposed tough economic sanctions on Moscow, with the latest day's focus on negotiations that have failed to bring a truce. The news of the war in Ukraine has added new worries on Wall Street due to the consequent increase in the price of raw materials and fuels, precisely when inflation is rising globally and in the US at a rate not seen in almost 40 years. Most sectors fell in February, especially communications (almost 5 %), non-essential goods (3 %) and industrial goods (-2.7 %), but following the economic punishment of Russia by the West, the financial sector also suffered, by almost 4 %. On the other hand, the energy sector has appreciated by almost 9 % in parallel with the rise in the price of crude oil, with notable gains for oil companies such as \uc2 Devon Energy\uc2  (17 %) and \uc2 Occidental Petroleum\uc2  (16 %), and more meager gains for the giants \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  (3 %) and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  (9 %). In the oil market, which was already very tight due to the growing global demand before the outbreak of the conflict in Eastern Europe, the fear of a reduction in the supply of Russian fuels has raised the barrel of Brent to 100.99 dollars and that of Texas to 95.72 dollars. Also noteworthy was the rise in the stock market, coinciding with the shipment of arms from \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries to Ukraine, of the specialized defense manufacturers \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  (up 11 % cumulatively), \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  (13 %) and \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  (18 %). In their flight to assets considered safe, investors have flocked to government bonds and slowed the rise in Treasury yields associated with the imminent interest rate hikes anticipated by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The 10-year yields are at 1.83%. Precious metals have also soared and the ounce of gold is above 1,900 dollars, while palladium, of which Russia and Ukraine are important suppliers and which is used to manufacture semiconductors, is touching 2,500 dollars. Cryptocurrencies came out of their slump today after several days of decline and the bitcoin jumped 10%, approaching $42,000, followed by other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as ethereum (7%) after Washington froze the assets of the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220228ei2s00bc3\par }\page {\par\fs