d cause damage to the world economy.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:30\par \par \uc2 Russia bans all UK-linked aircraft from its airspaceProtests around the world over the invasions in Ukraine. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 ?Russia banned all UK-linked aircraft from entering its airspace, following sanctions imposed by London on Russian airline Aeroflot after the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Airspace is banned from all aircraft "owned, leased or operated by a person linked to the UK" and those registered in that country, Russian air regulator Rosaviatsia said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:10\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President calls on European Union (EU) to intensify sanctions against Russia? "Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelensky called on the European Union (EU) to intensify sanctions against Russia in response to its invasion.\par \par \uc2 "The possibilities for sanctions have not yet been exhausted. The pressure on Russia must increase. That's what I said" to the president of the European Commission, Ursula von der Leyen, Zelenski said on Twitter\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:53\par \par \uc2 France offered help to the President of Ukraine because it believes he is in dangerProtests in different parts of the world against the invasion of Ukraine. Photo EFE.\par \par \uc2 French Foreign Minister Jean-Yves Le Drian assured Friday that the personal safety of Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski is in danger and offered his country's help "if necessary." "President Zelenski's security is a central element at the moment, we are in a position to help him if necessary. It is important that he keeps his post," said Le Drian, who said he was ready to "take the necessary measures" to preserve his integrity.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:50\par \par \uc2 Ukraine fights Russian tanks advancing towards KievThe Ukrainian Army fights in the locality of Ivankiv Russian tanks advancing in the direction of Kiev from the regions surrounding the former Chernobyl nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 "Russian tanks are advancing in the direction of Kiev, but in the vicinity of Ivankiv a fight broke out. Ukrainian forces blew up a bridge to stop the Russian column," stated the General Staff of the Armed Forces of Ukraine\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:44\par \par \uc2 International Criminal Court warned that it may investigate war crimes in UkraineThe International Criminal Court reminded the world on Friday that it is watching Russia's attack in Ukraine and could investigate any "acts of genocide" and "war crimes" observed there.\par \par \uc2 "I remind all parties conducting hostilities on the territory of Ukraine that my office may exercise its jurisdiction and investigate any acts of genocide, crimes against humanity or war crimes committed within the territory of Ukraine from February 20, 2014 onwards," prosecutor Karim Khan said.\par \par \uc2 He closed, "My office will continue to closely monitor the situation in Ukraine. In the independent and impartial exercise of its mandate, the Office of the Prosecutor remains fully committed to preventing atrocity crimes and ensuring that all those responsible for such crimes are held accountable."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:20\par \par \uc2 NATO meets urgently to prepare response to Russian invasionNATO leaders will hold an emergency meeting this Friday to prepare a response to Russia's invasion of Ukraine, allied sources confirmed to Europa Press.\par \par \uc2 After an emergency meeting of the Atlantic Council at ambassadorial level after activating Article 4 of the treaty, which provides for assistance to allies at the risk of attack, NATO, led by the United States , convened for this Friday the leaders by videoconference.\par \par \uc2 On the table will be the activation of NATO defense plans and the decision to strengthen the presence of the Atlantic Alliance on the eastern flank after the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:49\par \par \uc2 Dawn breaks in Kiev and an eerie calm prevailsAlthough the night curfew was officially lifted at 7 a.m., the city of Kiev remained eerily quiet and most residents chose to stay at home while many others already fled the Ukrainian capital after the start of the Russian offensive.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine's Defense Ministry reported that the country's airborne assault troops blew up a bridge over the Teteriv River in Ivankiv, some 50 kilometers north of Kiev, in an effort to prevent Russian forces from advancing on the country's capital.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:42\par \par \uc2 A picture of a deserted street in central Kiev after Russia advanced its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Ukraine. Photo: REUTERS/Antonio Bronic\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:38\par \par \uc2 UN secretary-general said the priority is "protection of civilians"UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres assured on Friday that the "protection" of civilians in Ukraine must be the number one priority.\par \par \uc2 In a message posted on his twitter account, Guterres noted that with "the rising death toll" in the Russian invasion of Ukrainian territory, "images of fear, anguish and terror are seen in every corner of Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 "The protection of civilians must be the number one priority. International humanitarian and human rights law must be respected," the UN official stressed.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:26\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:05\par \par \uc2 Russia's ambassador to Japan threatened "serious response" to sanctionsRussian Ambassador to Japan Mikhail Yurievich Galuzin on Friday threatened the Japanese Executive with "a serious response" from Moscow to the new Japanese sanctions imposed after the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The head of the Russian diplomatic mission in Japan made this statement on the same day that Japanese Prime Minister Fumio Kishida announced a new round of punitive measures against the neighboring country, including the freezing of funds of Russian financial institutions and individuals or the control of semiconductor exports.\par \par \uc2 "I have conveyed to the Japanese government that these actions will have a response, a serious response from our side," Galuzin said at a press conference held at the Foreign Correspondents' Club of Japan.\par \par \uc2 "There will be damage for both sides, both the one receiving the sanctions and the one imposing them," warned the ambassador, who declined to disclose further details about possible Russian retaliation.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:39\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President confirmed the missile attacksVolodimir Zelensky, President of Ukraine, confirmed that the missile attacks launched by Russia targeted both military and civilian sites.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he appreciated Ukraine's resistance: "Our boys and girls, all defenders of the country, did not allow the enemy to carry out the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan of the invasion on the first day."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:39\par \par \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky, president of Ukraine. Photo Presidential Press.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:15\par \par \uc2 New York offered to take in Ukrainian refugeesNew York Governor Kathy Hochul said Thursday that her state is ready to welcome Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 "New York is ready to welcome Ukrainian refugees," she said in a statement, in which she recalled that the state is home to the largest Ukrainian-origin population in theUnited States.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic governor added that her team is in contact with the federal government and that New Yorkers "will be ready to accept and support those seeking refuge."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 02