.French President Emmanuel Macron on Friday spoke with Vladimir Putin at the request of the Ukrainian president, who has been unable to contact the Russian leader since the conflict began.\par \par \uc2 Macron says he called Putin to ask him to stop military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the call was "frank, direct, quick.""It was also to ask him to discuss with President Zelensky, who had requested it, because he could not reach him," Macron told reporters after an EU summit in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 00:02\par \par \uc2 An "unidentified object" exploded in the sky over Kiev While announcing the Russian advance to the capital of Ukraine, an explosion of an "unidentified" object in the sky was recorded in the capital late at night. The explosion was aerial and its splinters illuminated the night and were recorded by media and viralized on social networks.The first information of the episode pointed to a Russian plane, intercepted by local anti-aircraft defenses, according to reports of the Ukrainian Ministry of Defense.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 23:37\par \par \uc2 Fear in Ukraine's capital: explosions are heard and the president denounces "infiltrators"As explosions are heard in Kiev, President Zelenski confirmed that he will stay in the capital, where curfew is in force.The president added tension and concern in the population by warning about "infiltrators" and Russian or pro-Russian "groups of saboteurs" in the city and asked citizens to be "vigilant" in a televised message.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 23:16\par \par \uc2 In photos: Kiev subway as a war shelter.Tracks, platforms, stations. The subway network of Kiev, Ukraine, is one of the missile shelters set up by the government for civilians against the possibility of bombing in the capital city.Photographers of international agencies portrayed the fear of people who left their homes to go to one of the points that the authorities in a campaign that began weeks before the Russian attack.\par \par \uc2 The subway of Kiev, capital of Ukraine, as a war shelter. Photo Vyacheslav Ratynskyi/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Sleeping in the Kiev subway, the official shelter option in the Ukrainian capital in the face of the Russian attack. Photo Emilio Morenatti/AP\par \par \uc2 Another dramatic image of refugees in the Kiev subway in Ukraine. Photo Zoya Shu/AP\par \par \uc2 The subway platforms in Kiev is one of the bomb shelters set by the Ukrainian government. Photo Emilio Morenatti/AP\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 22:50\par \par \uc2 Martin Goldbart\par \par \uc2 "All we want is to live in peace": desperate wish of Ukrainians in Argentina.Residents prepare to take the subway, in Kiev, Ukraine, Fears and tensions on the other side of the world. Photo Sergey Starostenko / Xinhua\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians in Argentina call for a march in Recoleta against the war. They will gather this Friday at Plaza Vicente L\u243\'00\'F3pez, in Paran\u225\'00\'E1 and Arenales.The distance, the uneasiness, the fears before the bombings and the community's criticism of Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez's link with Vladimir Putin, in this note.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 22:27\par \par \uc2 Japan's economic sanctions against Russia boosts the Tokyo Stock ExchangeThe Tokyo Stock Exchange started with a 1.38% rebound of its main indicator, the Nikkei, after the imposition of new sanctions by the G7 countries, including Japan, and the European Union on Russia after its invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese stock market day on Friday began with a marked upward trend, after closing Thursday with a drop of 1.81% and ending at its worst level in 15 months, on a day marked by the beginning of the attack by Russian troops in the east and other points of the Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei was spurred at the start of Friday by bargain hunting and the positive closing of Wall Street the day before, after the United States announced its new sanctions to economically isolate Moscow and the sending of U.S. troops to Germany to strengthen NATO's presence in Eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 Japan also announced Friday a new round of sanctions on Moscow that will affect semiconductor exports and Russian financial companies and individuals.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 22:09\par \par \uc2 Why Putin invaded UkraineThe analysis of Marcelo Cantelmi and Claudio Aliscioni, editors of Clar\u237\'00\'EDn' s International Politics on the conflict that keeps the world on edge.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 21:42\par \par \uc2 Anonymous declares "cyberwar" on RussiaThe hackers nucleated in the Anonymous organization today declared a "cyberwar" on Russia for its attack on Ukraine and claimed responsibility for the cyberattack against the Russian television station RT.\par \par \uc2 "Anonymous officially declares war on Russia; the collective hacked the website of Russian propaganda TV channel RT," the group said in a statement posted on its Twitter account.\par \par \uc2 The RT channel had reported that as of 5 p.m. (11 a.m. on theArgentina) its websites suffered cyber-attacks.\par \par \uc2 According to Sputnik news agency, the TV channel detailed that approximately 27% of the addresses from which the attacks were carried out are based in the United States. Find out more about the war on the web in this Clar\u237\'00\'EDn Technology article.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 21:20\par \par \uc2 "Who is willing to fight with us? I don't see anyone."Despite the announcements of the United States and the EU, President Zelenski of Ukraine said that they were left "alone" and, while he accused Putin of "killing civilians", he also pointed to the international community that promised him defenses and aid that do not seem to reach him.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president's questionings are known after Joe Biden announced more U.S. economic sanctions against Russian financial entities. The European Union pronounced itself in the same line, after a summit of its 27 leaders.However, for now, Biden reserved himself from applying the so-called "nuclear sanction", considered the maximum punishment. This option was to remove Russia from the Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication known as SWIFT, the international banking network. His Ukrainian counterpart complained about this. And he maintained, after hearing that Moscow - despite the military offensive - still wants to talk about Ukraine's neutral status so that it does not join NATO, that he is not afraid.\par \par \uc2 "We are not afraid to talk with Russia. We are not afraid to talk about security guarantees for our state. We are not afraid to talk about neutral status.But will we have security guarantees? Which countries will give them to us?", he wondered. Read more.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 21:08\par \par \uc2 100 thousand Ukrainians flee their homes and thousands cross the border after Russian invasion"It is clear that there were significant displacements inside the country and some movements at the borders," a spokeswoman for the UN High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR), Shabia Mantoo, told AFP about the conflict in Ukraine.To learn about the safeguards of neighboring countries in the face of the migrant flood read more here.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 20:35\par \par \uc2 Ezequiel Burgo\par \par \uc2 The economic impact of the war on the country and the world. 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 20:20\par \par \uc2 Nicolas Maduro condemned "the crisis" but blamed NATONicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro tweeted about the war and marked a pro-Russian stance of his regime."Venezuela rejects the aggravation of the crisis in Ukraine product of NATO' s breach of the Minsk agreements," he said.\par \par \uc2 In an accompanying statement, Maduro took sides: "The Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela, in accordance with its constitutional diplomacy of peace