Union reacted so far with sanctions to the Russian advance.\par \par \uc2 24.02.2022\par \par \uc2 18:19\par \par \uc2 Russian war plan: cyber army, three fronts and Desert Storm doctrine.As an irony of history, Vladimir Putin's war plan is based on the Scharzkopf doctrine, after a former US general who acted in \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Desert Storm in the Gulf War.\par \par \uc2 With the novelty of a cyber-army added to the troops, it aims at logistical destruction: attacking communication and command centers and supply channels before deepening the advance.\par \par \uc2 That is why the Russian rockets rained down in the early hours, which according to their calculations destroyed 83 military installations, among them 11 air bases, three command centers and a naval base. Also radio transmission stations, according to ANSA news agency.\par \par \uc2 By land, Russia took three directions. Tanks in the north from Belarus on their way to Kiev, taking control of the disused Chernobyl nuclear power plant and advancing to conquer the Antonov port of Hostomel, some 10 kilometers from the capital.\par \par \uc2 On the eastern front, the Russians advanced toward Kharkiv, with tanks entering near Sumy, where the most violent fighting was reported. Kiev spoke of "15 tanks destroyed" thanks to anti-tank rockets supplied from the United States.\par \par \uc2 Separatist militias from Lugansk are also advancing and, "thanks to the support of Russian artillery", have conquered Shchastia and Stanytsia Luhanska. To the south, militias from the other breakaway region, Donetsk, were advancing with some tanks towards the port of Mariupol, while Russian forces stationed in Crimea, assisted by the firepower of ships in the Black Sea, were breaking the Ukrainian defense on the Kherson lines, taking control of a hydroelectric power station.\par \par \uc2 The Russian is a pincer maneuver, aiming to bring together the armies deployed on the three war fronts.\par \par 