n that the United States still has the right and can impose on everyone its own rules of the world order, there are no other means left but blackmail, intimidation and threats," added the department headed by Sergey Lavrov.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:57\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis called for a day of "fasting and prayer for peace".Pope Francis on Wednesday launched an appeal to all parties involved in the conflict in Ukraine to avoid warlike actions and abide by international law. At the same time he called for a day of "fasting and prayer for peace" for next Wednesday, March 2.\par \par \uc2 "I have a great pain in my heart for the worsening of the situation in Ukraine, because despite the diplomatic efforts of recent weeks, increasingly alarming scenarios are opening up," said the Pontiff after the traditional Wednesday audience at the Vatican. More information here.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:47\par \par \uc2 Trump's unexpected praise for Putin amid escalating war in UkraineA photo from June 2019 when Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin greeted each other during a G20 meeting in Japan.\par \par \uc2 As Republican leaders in Congress press President Joe Biden to act more forcefully to condemn Vladimir Putin for sending troops to Ukraine, former Republican President Donald Trump and some of his prominent allies have praised the Russian leader's style of power. The Putin-driven crisis is causing a growing rift within the Republican Party On one side are traditional foreign policy hawks who have advocated a more confrontational stance with the Russian strongman and on the other, a "Trumpist" faction that has expressed some sympathy for the Russian's tactics.? "This is great. Putin declares a large part of Ukraine independent," Trump said on a conservative Podcast. "That's wonderful." (Source ANSA)\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:31\par \par \uc2 Putin accused Ukraine of genocide In his speech on Monday Putin accused Ukraine, a country without historical legitimacy according to him, of having a"neo-Nazi" regime, sponsored by the West, to exterminate Russian speakers in the Donbas.\par \par \uc2 "The so-called civilized world, of which our Western colleagues are the only representatives, prefers to turn a blind eye, as if these horrors did not exist, to the genocide suffered by four million people," he said, accusing Kiev of banning the Russian language.?Strictly speaking, Ukraine did not ban Russian, which is still widely spoken in large parts of the country, but adopted a law imposing Ukrainian in the public space and in the media.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:25\par \par \uc2 Half of Russians support the war Every second Russian, 50 percent, considers it right for their country to use military force against Ukraine to prevent the Kiev government from joining NATO, according to a poll published Wednesday by CNN.\par \par \uc2 The poll, conducted by the Savanta ComRes polling firm among citizens of Ukraine and Russia, concludes that only 25 percent of Russians believe it would be wrong to resort to the military against Ukraine and another 25 percent are not sure what is the best option to resolve the current crisis.\par \par \uc2 But the poll also found that more Russians think it would be wrong to try to forcibly "reunite" Russia and Ukraine, two countries with a long and complicated history of intertwined relations.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:10\par \par \uc2 "We are afraid of war, yes, but we are ready to face it." The demonstrations in Kiev. Photo EFE\par \par \uc2 Residents of the Ukrainian capital watch from afar and without altering their routine the possibility of a devastating conflict with Russia. But some rituals of the war rearguard not seen in years, returned overnight.\par \par \uc2 Since Tuesday, every hour on the hour, loud loudspeakers blare the national anthem "Ukraine is not dead" in the large Maidan square.What are they saying in the country's largest city about the news coming from the Russian border?\par \par \uc2 "We are afraid of war, yes, but we are ready to face it because it is a defensive war," says Anatoli Tarasenko, a 74-year-old pensioner who criticizes the government for "not having lifted a finger" to prevent after Crimea's annexation to Russia in 2014. ?\par \par \uc2 Protests in Kiev in front of the Russian embassy in the capital. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 Ksenia Balfy, a 38-year-old DJ, does not plan to have her Friday night show cancelled. For her, Putin's threats are "nothing new." "It is normal to be afraid, no one knows what will happen now, but we, we stay here and we will defend our country," the woman says.\par \par \uc2 Oleg Koras is 38 years old, he is a peddler and signed up for "territorial defense" as a reservist in case the war breaks out. "If bombs fall on our city, what do they want us to do, we will seek shelter. But then, we will know how to respond," he replied to the AFP agency.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:50\par \par \uc2 Humanitarian organizations fear for civilian casualties in the conflict UkraineThe current escalation of tension in Ukraine increases the risk of mass displacement in the region, warned from Geneva the Norwegian Refugee Council and the World Organization Against Torture (OMCT), which called for the inclusion of vulnerable groups in possible evacuation plans.\par \par \uc2 Children in specialized institutions, elderly in nursing homes or prisoners should be taken into account in eventual evacuations, OMCT claimed in a communiqu\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 "In case the armed conflict escalates, these people, numbering in the tens of thousands, would have no chance of reaching safe areas," warned the organization, who noted that since the outbreak of the conflict in Donbas in 2014 there have already been cases of attacks on penitentiary institutions.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:30\par \par \uc2 UN head questions Russia's decision to recognize Donetsk and LuganskUN Secretary-General Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres answers reporters' questions about the situation on the Ukrainian-Russian borders. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 ?Russia's decision to recognize the independence of the separatist Donetsk and Lugansk People's Republics constitutes a "violation of the territorial integrity" of Ukraine, UN Secretary-General Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres warned today, urging an immediate cessation of hostilities in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Let me be clear: the Russian Federation's decision to recognize the so-called 'independence' of certain areas of the Donetsk and Lugansk regions is a violation of the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine," Guterres said.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:02\par \par \uc2 China says U.S. "adding fuel to fire" in Ukraine-Russia crisis?China said the United States fuels the Ukraine-Russia conflict with its constant arms supplies to the Ukrainian military and sanctions against Moscow that solve nothing.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. has not stopped selling arms to Ukraine, increasing tension and creating panic," a Chinese Foreign Ministry spokeswoman, Hua Chunying, criticized to reporters in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine calls for state of emergency ?Russian armored vehicles at the railway station in Rostov region, Russia. Photo EFE.\par \par \uc2 ?The Ukrainian Security Council called for a state of emergency in the country, coinciding with a rise in fears of an imminent Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 "The Ukrainian Parliament must give its approval to this decision in the next 48 hours," said the secretary of the Security and National Defense Council, Oleksii Danilov.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220227ei2r001st\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
