sures that he is there "defending" his country against the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 "We are all here, our military is here, the citizens, the society, we are all here, defending our independence, our state," said Zelenski, dressed in an olive green military-style suit and accompanied among others by his prime minister, his chief of staff and a close advisor.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 14:37\par \par \uc2 Zelensky: the comedian who became president and tries to defend Ukraine from Putin's invasionUranian President Volodimyr Zelenski. Photo: Reuter\par \par \uc2 For a long time, Volodimyr Zelenski dragged the image of a former TV comedian elected president of Ukraine almost by chance, but in the wake of the crisis with Russia, he has become in the eyes of his compatriots a leader who knew how to stand firm and serene, oblivious to panic.\par \par \uc2 The 44-year-old came to the presidency in time to witness the worst standoff between the Kremlin and the Western powers since the end of the Cold War more than three decades ago.\par \par \uc2 He watched as Russia surrounded his country with more than 100,000 troops and launched an invasion of his country. In the midst of the offensive and fighting, Vladimir Putin called him a "drug addict" and a "Nazi". Read the full article here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 14:26\par \par \uc2 Maria Laura Avignolo\par \par \uc2 Who are the oligarchs sanctioned by Great Britain?Alfa Bank, one of the largest banks in Russia, Photo: EFE\par \par \uc2 The Russian oligarchs sanctioned by Britain, following Russia's attack on Ukraine, are directly linked to President Vladimir Putin, his financial and secret service interests.\par \par \uc2 His former son-in-law, Kiril Shamalov, is among them for his links to Sibur, the Russian petrochemical company.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions issued by the British treasury include freezing funds and barring officials of Russian banks and military companies from traveling or visiting Britain, following Prime Minister Boris Johnson's announcement in the House of Commons on Thursday. Their jets are not allowed to land or take off from the kingdom. Read the full story.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 14:07\par \par \uc2 Exodus of Ukrainians who want to escape the warWalking and dragging suitcases, thousands of Ukrainian families are fleeing on foot to Hungary to try to escape the war.\par \par \uc2 In Beregsurany, on the Hungarian-Ukrainian border, refugees try to escape Russian tanks.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:51\par \par \uc2 Marcelo Cantelmi\par \par \uc2 Did Xi Jinping call Vladimir Putin to force him to negotiate? Putin and Xi Jinping during the Russian president's visit to Beijing. Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping called Vladimir Putin and apparently persuaded him to start immediate negotiations to close the crisis. It is not clear what diplomatic tone the dialogue took. Everything seems to indicate that China is not willing to allow the crisis unleashed by its junior partner, Vladimir Putin with Ukraine, to continue escalating. Coincidences with the West?\par \par \uc2 Following the conversation between the two leaders, Moscow reported that it will seek some kind of negotiations with the embattled Ukrainian government, possibly to be held in Minsk, the capital of Belarus' Moscow satellite.\par \par \uc2 This is an interesting twist in the grave crisis scenario. According to Chinese media Putin defended the reasons why Russia launched "a special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Ukraine. Read the full article here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:20\par \par \uc2 The United States claims that Putin has already launched more than 200 missiles into Ukraine.One of the images of the Russian bombardment of Ukraine. Photo: AP\par \par \uc2 The United States assured that Russia has already launched more than 200 missiles into Ukraine, a Pentagon official was quoted by U.S. media as saying.\par \par \uc2 It was also reported that there are indications that an amphibious assault is underway west of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:08\par \par \uc2 Zelensky's ironic response to a comment by the Italian president.Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski. Photo: Reuter.\par \par \uc2 Amid global tension over Russia's invasion of Ukraine, President Zelenski responded ironically Friday to his Italian peer Mario Dragui, who complained that he was not available to talk on the phone.\par \par \uc2 According to Europa Press, during a presentation to Parliament, Dragui also explained to lawmakers that he had a phone call with his Ukrainian peer on the agenda for 9:30 a.m., which did not materialize "because President Zelenski was no longer available."\par \par \uc2 "Today at 10:30 at the entrances to Chernihiv, Hostomel and Melitopol there was heavy fighting. People died," reads the text that the Ukrainian leader posted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  this morning. He then adds, "Next time I will try to move the war schedule to talk to Mario Draghi at a specific time. In the meantime, Ukraine continues to fight for its people," he joked. Read the full note.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:48\par \par \uc2 Russia threatened Sweden and Finland with "military consequences". \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman Maria Zakharova.\par \par \uc2 At a press conference on Friday, \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman Maria Zakharova threatened Sweden and Finland with "military consequences" if they sought to join NATO.\par \par \uc2 "Finland and Sweden should not base their security on harming the security of other countries, and their joining NATO could have political and military consequences," Zakharova said.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:30\par \par \uc2 Dramatic moment when a Russian tank crushes a car in Kiev25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:14\par \par \uc2 Idafe Martin\par \par \uc2 Europe sanctions Putin but does not apply the option most feared by Moscow.Putin in the Kremlin. Photo:Reuter\par \par \uc2 The sanctions advanced by \uc2 European Union\uc2  leaders in the early hours of Thursday morning came with fine print that became known this Friday.\par \par \uc2 In addition to detailing these punishments, the European foreign ministers decided to include Russian President Vladimir Putin in the list of sanctioned persons. Until Thursday, it was argued that he was left out so as not to break all diplomatic bridges.\par \par \uc2 The agreement, reached between the 27 ambassadors of the member states, explains that both Putin and his chancellor Sergei Lavrov will see all their assets frozen on European territory, although they will not be prevented from setting foot on the continent. Read the complete note.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:00\par \par \uc2 Putin calls Zelensky's government "drug addicts and neo-Nazis" and urges Ukraine's military to seize powerVladimir Putin from the Kremlin. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 Russian President Vladimir Putin doubled down on his escalation against the Ukrainian government and called on the Ukrainian army to take control in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 "It will be easier for us to agree if you take power," Putin told members of the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 He also called Zelensky and the Ukrainian government "a gang of drug addicts and neo-Nazis," and said his army fights "terrorists" and "neo-Nazis."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:58\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  agreed to sanction Vladimir Putin and the Russian foreign ministerVladimir Putin on Friday in the Kremlin. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  (EU) countries reached an agreement Friday to sanction Russian President Vladimir Putin and his foreign minister, Sergey Lavrov, over Russia's offensive in Ukraine, concurring diplomatic sources told AFP.\par \par \uc2 According to these sources, the bloc would adopt a freeze on assets or property owned by Putin or Lavrov in Europe. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:43\par \par \uc2 Audio reveals how Putin's forces threatened and then killed 13 Ukrainian soldiersThe Snake Island that was seized by Russia, in the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 As Vladimir Putin's army was attacking different points in Ukraine, a group of Ukrainian border guards defending a Black Sea island received a message from Kremlin forces demanding that they surrender and avoid "bloodshed."\par \par \uc2 "This is a Russian warship. We suggest you lay down your arms and surrender to avoid bloodshed and senseless deaths. Otherwise, you will be shelled," threatened a Russian military officer in a radio dialogue with the Ukrainian base on Snake Island, according to an audio that went viral in recent hours.\par \par \uc2 In response, one of the guards who was defending the small island was blunt: "Go to hell".\par \par \uc2 After the brief exchange of words and their refusal to surrender, a group of 13 border soldiers died under Russian fire. Read the full story.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:29\par \par \uc2 Maria Laura Avignolo\par \par \uc2 Emmanuel Macron spoke again with Vladimir Putin to demand an end to the offensive.French President Emmanuel Macron. Photo credit: Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 After the failed negotiations between Emmanuel Macron and Russian President Vladimir Putin, the French head of state insisted again on Thursday night. He called his Russian peer again to demand that he stop the offensive in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Macron on Thursday called on Russia's president to "demand an immediate end" to the Russian army's offensive in Ukraine, the French presidency said.\par \par \uc2 "After speaking with the Ukrainian president, and in consultation with him, the president called Vladimir Putin to demand an immediate halt to Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recalling that Russia was exposing itself to massive sanctions," the Elys\u233\'00\'E9e said, whileMacron was taking part in an exceptional European summit in Brussels, where the leaders of the bloc are analyzing the reinforcement of sanctions against the Moscow government. Read the full note here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:23\par \par \uc2 "We are not satisfied with Argentina's response", warns Ukrainian embassy in Buenos AiresThe charge d'affaires of the Ukrainian Embassy in Argentina, Sergiy Nebrat.\par \par \uc2 The charge d'affaires of the Ukrainian Embassy in Argentina, Sergiy Nebrat, stated this Friday that in his country they are not "satisfied with Argentina's response" to the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 "We are not satisfied with Argentina's response. We wanted clearer signals," Nebrat stressed to the press in front of the Ukrainian Embassy building in Buenos Aires. Read more here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:12\par \par \uc2 Cafiero joined Pope Francis' request in the midst of the Russian invasion of UkraineChancellor Santiago Cafiero highlighted Pope Francis' gesture of going to the Russian embassy to the Vatican. He did so through Twitter, where he insisted that the call for peace "acquires a dramatic urgency in view of the tragic scenarios opened by the beginning of military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 "Inescapable call of @Pontifex_es for peace. We join his message and reiterate the call to the parties involved in the conflict in Ukraine to stop any action that causes suffering, destabilizes peaceful coexistence and discredits international law," wrote Cafiero.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:01\par \par \uc2 Mayor Kiev warns that the city entered "defensive phase".The mayor of Kiev and former world heavyweight boxing champion Vitali Klitschko warned Friday that the Ukrainian capital entered a "defensive phase" in the face of Russian attacks.\par \par \uc2 "We ask residents, in particular those living in the Obolon district, to avoid going out on the streets or stay in shelters during air strikes to avoid losses," he said at a press conference broadcast by the Kiev city hall.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 10:52\par \par \uc2 Journalist shows the aftermath of the Russian invasion of Ukraine from KievFrancesca Ebel is a correspondent for the AP news agency in the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 10:35\par \par \uc2 Russia-Ukraine War: Ten Keys to Understanding the EscalationUkrainian National Guard in Kiev in the midst of the Russian invasion. Photo: Reuter.\par \par \uc2 Kiev and other Ukrainian cities have been living since Thursday under the nightmare of Russian shelling. The world continues in a frantic race of meetings to try to find a diplomatic end to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 The West applies sanctions and President Vladimir Putin maintains his threat that any attempt to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have "consequences they have never seen".\par \par \uc2 The relationship between Russia and Ukraine, how it came to the current hostility and other keys to understand the conflict. Read the full article here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 10:15\par \par \uc2 Russia assures readiness to send delegation to Minsk to negotiate with UkraineRussian President Vladimir Putin at the Kremlin. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 Russia is ready to send a delegation to Minsk in Belarus to start negotiations with Ukraine, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Russian President Vladimir Putin, in response to the proposal (of his Ukrainian counterpart) Volodymir Zelensky, is ready to send to Minsk a delegation at the level of representatives of the Defense, Foreign and Presidential Administration Ministries for negotiations with the Ukrainian side," Peskov was quoted as saying by Interfax news agency.\par \par \uc2 The Russian presidential spokesman alluded to a recent statement by Zelensky in which the Ukrainian leader said he was ready to discuss the country's neutral status, thereby renouncing his aspiration to join NATO.\par \par \uc2 2022-02-25 9:50:03\par \par \uc2 Russia claims to have seized a strategic airport and cut off Kiev from the WestIn the background, smoke amid clashes in Hostomel, Ukraine. Photo: AFP.\par \par \uc2 The Russian military says it has taken control of a strategic airport on the outskirts of the Ukrainian capital and isolated Kiev from the West.\par \par \uc2 Hostomel airport has a long runway capable of accommodating heavy transport aircraft. Its fall would allow Russia to airlift troops directly to the outskirts of Kiev. Hostomel is just 7 kilometers (4 miles) northwest of Kiev.\par \par \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry spokesman Maj. Gen. Igor Konashenkov said Friday that Russian airborne forces used 200 helicopters to land at Hostomel.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:35\par \par \uc2 Formula 1 canceled the Sochi Grand Prix over Russia's invasion of Ukraine. An image of the Sochi Grand Prix in 2021. Photo: AP.\par \par \uc2 Formula 1 resolved Friday after a meeting with the International Automobile Federation (FIA) to cancel the Sochi Grand Prix, which was scheduled for Sept. 25, in the wake of Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 F1 and the FIA held a meeting with the teams that are carrying out a series of tests at the Montmel\u243\'00\'F3 circuit in Barcelona, with a view to the start of the competition, and it was decided to align with the European Union in its policy of sanctions and isolation of Russia.\par \par \uc2 "Formula One visits all countries around the world with the positive vision of bringing people together and bringing nations together. We are watching the situation in Ukraine unfold with sadness and shock. We hope for change and a peaceful resolution," the FIA said in an official statement.\par \par \uc2 It added: "We spoke with the FIA, together with the teams, about the position our sport is in and the conclusion is that it is impossible to bring the Russian Grand Prix to change under the current circumstances."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:21\par \par \uc2 From the European Union accuse Putin of behaving "like the Nazis".Russian President Vladimir Putin is behaving in Ukraine "like the Nazis" and is on the verge of committing genocide, a spokesman for the European Commission, Slovak Peter Stano, accused on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Russian leader talks about "preventing a genocide, which makes no sense, because he is committing one or is about to commit one. He talks about 'de-nazifying' Ukraine, but he behaves like the Nazis," the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Putin had justified the military offensive against targets in Ukraine with the need to eliminate Nazi-like elements in that country, who were carrying out a genocide against the Russian-speaking population.\par \par \uc2 In the last hours, Ukraine published through its official Twitter account a caricature of Hitler caressing Putin's face and together with that message it specified that it was not a meme but the reality they were living.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:10\par \par \uc2 Putin assured Xi Jinping that he is ready to negotiate with UkraineVladimir Putin with Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing. Photo: AFP\par \par \uc2 Russian President Vladimir Putin assured Chinese President Xi Jinping during a telephone conversation on Friday that he is ready to hold "high-level negotiations" with Ukraine following the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  he launched in that country.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Xi, quoted by state television CCTV, said he supports Kiev and Moscow"resolving their problems through negotiation", and that his country "respects the sovereignty and territorial integrity of states", although he emphasized that "it is important to respect the legitimate security concerns of all countries involved".\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:00\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis went to the Russian embassy to the Vatican Francis next to Putin at an audience they held in 2015. Photo: AP.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis visited the Russian embassy to the Vatican on Friday to express to Alexander Avdeev his concern over the attack on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Francis' gesture as he left the Vatican to visit the nearby Russian embassy, which is located on Via della Conciliazone, is part of the Holy See's attempt to call for dialogue and a return to the negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 Adveed works to prepare for the second meeting between Francis and Russian Orthodox Church Patriarch Kirill and keeps Francis constantly informed about the conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:53\par \par \uc2 "Let's sit down to negotiate", the Ukrainian president's request to Putin.Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky. Photo: Reuter.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky on Friday asked his Russian counterpart Vladimir Putin to sit down at the negotiating table to stop the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched the day before against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "I want to address once again the president of Russia. All over the territory of Ukraine there is fighting. Let's sit down at the negotiating table to stop the death of human beings," he said in a televised message.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:41\par \par \uc2 Maria Laura Avignolo\par \par \uc2 Russia-Ukraine war: an awkward situation for China, Vladimir Putin's key allyIsabel Hilton, CEO of China Dialogue and specialist on North Korea and China.\par \par \uc2 In Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the world is also looking to China. Xi Jinping's government declined to identify it as an "invasion" but called for "containment" to prevent the crisis from spiraling "out of control."\par \par \uc2 China-Russia relations are the best in 70 years. President Xi Jinping called Vladimir Putin "my best friend" when he opened, in his presence, the Winter Olympic Games in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 But it is obligatory to ask: did Russian President Vladimir Putin inform his best friend Xi Jinping of his intentions to invade Ukraine or threaten the West with a nuclear attack if he is removed from the system from which he benefited until before the sanctions?\par \par \uc2 Appearances indicate that China was surprised by this military offensive to invade Ukraine. It probably did not know that its action would sweep away Ukrainian sovereignty, which Beijing defended at the Munich Security Summit days earlier.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Isabel Hilton is a China specialist. There she studied and speaks fluent Mandarin and its dialects. Founder and CEO of China Dialogue, she is a journalist and visiting professor at the Lau Institute of King's College London. This was her dialogue with Clar\u237\'00\'EDn from London on China's role in this crisis. Read the full article here.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:27\par \par \uc2 Italy prepares for a possible energy crisis due to the warItalian Prime Minister Mario Draghi in parliament. Photo: EFE\par \par \uc2 Italian Prime Minister Mario Draghi announced Friday that his government is working to cope with a possible energy crisis after Russia's attack on Ukraine that even the reopening of coal-fired power plants is being studied.\par \par \uc2 "We hope that these plans will not be necessary, but we cannot be caught off guard," he warned while appearing in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 He further advanced that the emergency measures under consideration "include greater flexibility in gas consumption, suspensions in the industrial sector and rules on gas consumption in the thermoelectric sector, where there are also reduction measures."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:07\par \par \uc2 More than 1,800 detained in anti-war protests in RussiaMore than 1,800 people have been arbitrarily detained in Russia during protests against the war launched by the Russian government in Ukraine and it is unknown whether some have been released, the UN Human Rights Office said.\par \par \uc2 This is three times the number reported by the Main Directorate of the Russian Interior Ministry in recent hours.\par \par \uc2 "Being arrested for exercising the right to freedom of expression or assembly is an arbitrary deprivation of liberty and we call for the individuals concerned to be released," Human Rights Office spokeswoman Ravina Shamdasan said in Geneva\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 07:48\par \par \uc2 Marcelo Cantelmi\par \par \uc2 Opinion: Vladimir Putin in the time tunnel, threw the world into the pastA man looks at the wreckage of a downed military plane in Kiev. Photo EFE.\par \par \uc2 One criticism of today's China is that it has ignored a key command of the legendary Deng Xiao Ping founder of the modern and economically successful People's Republic.\par \par \uc2 That advice, in an almost poetic format and with tones of Machiavelli to the prince, recommended: "Face problems calmly, hide your abilities, buy time, never claim a position of leadership, lower your profile". That is how it should be, he warned, because there, in front of the wall "foreign troops are camped, stronger than ours". Read more.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 07:41\par \par \uc2 URGENT - Russia assures that it is ready to negotiate "if Ukraine lays down its arms". "We are ready to negotiate at any time, as soon as the Ukrainian armed forces hear our call and lay down their arms," Lavrov said at a press conference, a day after the start of the Russian offensive in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 07:28\par \par \uc2 Champions League: because of the war, the final will not be played in Russia and will be played on May 28 in ParisImage of the stadium in Paris where the Champions League final will be played. Photo AFP.\par \par \uc2 The Stade de France in Paris will host the Champions League final on May 28 instead of St. Petersburg, announced Friday UEFA, which has withdrawn the organization to this city in reaction to the invasion of Ukraine by the Russian army.\par \par \uc2 The stadium on the outskirts of Paris already hosted the Champions League final in 2006, in which Barcelona defeated Arsenal. Read more.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 07:13\par \par \uc2 Russia takes control of key canal to supply water to Crimea "Thanks to the use of ground regiments and airborne troops (...), Russian forces were able to reach the city of Kherson, which allowed the unblocking of the Northern Crimean canal and the restoration of water supply to the Crimean peninsula," the Russian military said in a statement Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the annexation of the peninsula in early 2014, Ukrainian authorities had cut off this canal, located in the Kherson region, which provided 85% of Crimea's water needs. Since then, Crimea has been facing severe water shortages, especially during summer drought periods.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:58\par \par \uc2 Leading monuments of the world lit up in Ukrainian colors.The image of the Colosseum in Ukrainian colors. Photo: AFP.\par \par \uc2 Cinquantenaire Park is illuminated in the color of the Ukrainian flag during emergency summit convened to deal with the Russia-Ukraine crisis. Photo: AFP.\par \par \uc2 The image of the European Commission building. Photo: AFP.\par \par \uc2 The image of Flinders Street station illuminated in yellow and blue in Melbourne. Photo: AFP.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:44\par \par \uc2 Gunfire near the government quarter of the Ukrainian capital Kiev.A man escapes the horror of war with his child in his arms Photo REUTERS/Radovan Stoklas.\par \par \uc2 Russia is taking its offensive in Ukraine to the gates of its capital, Kiev, after a series of air strikes on cities and military bases and entering the country from three flanks in an invasion that could rewrite the post-Cold War world security order.\par \par \uc2 The sound of explosions rocked Kiev before dawn and gunfire was later heard near the government quarter, as Western leaders called an emergency meeting and Ukraine's president appealed for international help to stop an attack that could topple his democratically elected government, inflict heavy casualties and cause damage to the global economy.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:30\par \par \uc2 Russia bans all UK-linked aircraft from its airspaceProtests around the world over the invasions in Ukraine. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 ?Russia banned all UK-linked aircraft from entering its airspace, following sanctions imposed by London on Russian airline Aeroflot after the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Airspace is banned from all aircraft "owned, leased or operated by a person linked to the UK" and those registered in that country, Russian air regulator Rosaviatsia said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 06:10\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President calls on European Union (EU) to intensify sanctions against Russia? "Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelensky called on the European Union (EU) to intensify sanctions against Russia in response to its invasion.\par \par \uc2 "The possibilities for sanctions have not yet been exhausted. The pressure on Russia must increase. That's what I said" to the president of the European Commission, Ursula von der Leyen, Zelenski said on Twitter\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:53\par \par \uc2 France offered help to the President of Ukraine because it believes he is in dangerProtests in different parts of the world against the invasion of Ukraine. Photo EFE.\par \par \uc2 French Foreign Minister Jean-Yves Le Drian assured Friday that the personal safety of Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski is in danger and offered his country's help "if necessary." "President Zelenski's security is a central element at the moment, we are in a position to help him if necessary. It is important that he keeps his post," said Le Drian, who said he was ready to "take the necessary measures" to preserve his integrity.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:50\par \par \uc2 Ukraine fights Russian tanks advancing towards KievThe Ukrainian army fights in the locality ofIvankiv to Russian tanks advancing in the direction of Kiev from the regions surrounding the former Chernobyl nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 "Russian tanks are advancing in the direction of Kiev, but in the vicinity of Ivankiv a fight broke out. Ukrainian forces blew up a bridge to stop the Russian column," stated the General Staff of the Armed Forces of Ukraine\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:44\par \par \uc2 International Criminal Court warned that it may investigate war crimes in UkraineThe International Criminal Court reminded the world on Friday that it is watching Russia's attack in Ukraine and could investigate any "acts of genocide" and "war crimes" observed there.\par \par \uc2 "I remind all parties conducting hostilities on the territory of Ukraine that my office may exercise its jurisdiction and investigate any acts of genocide, crimes against humanity or war crimes committed within the territory of Ukraine from February 20, 2014 onwards," prosecutor Karim Khan said.\par \par \uc2 He closed, "My office will continue to closely monitor the situation in Ukraine. In the independent and impartial exercise of its mandate, the Office of the Prosecutor remains fully committed to preventing atrocity crimes and ensuring that all those responsible for such crimes are held accountable."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 05:20\par \par \uc2 NATO meets urgently to prepare response to Russian invasionNATO leaders will hold an emergency meeting this Friday to prepare a response to Russia's invasion of Ukraine, allied sources confirmed to Europa Press.\par \par \uc2 After an emergency meeting of the Atlantic Council at ambassadorial level after activating Article 4 of the treaty, which provides for assistance to allies at the risk of attack, NATO, led by the United States , convened for this Friday the leaders by videoconference.\par \par \uc2 On the table will be the activation of NATO defense plans and the decision to strengthen the presence of the Atlantic Alliance on the eastern flank after the Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:49\par \par \uc2 Dawn breaks in Kiev and an eerie calm prevailsAlthough the night curfew was officially lifted at 7 a.m., the city of Kiev remained eerily quiet and most residents chose to stay at home while many others already fled the Ukrainian capital after the start of the Russian offensive.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine's Defense Ministry reported that the country's airborne assault troops blew up a bridge over the Teteriv River in Ivankiv, some 50 kilometers north of Kiev, in an effort to prevent Russian forces from advancing on the country's capital.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:42\par \par \uc2 A picture of a deserted street in central Kiev after Russia advanced its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Ukraine. Photo: REUTERS/Antonio Bronic\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:38\par \par \uc2 UN secretary-general said the priority is "protection of civilians"UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres assured on Friday that the "protection" of civilians in Ukraine must be the number one priority.\par \par \uc2 In a message posted on his twitter account, Guterres noted that with "the rising death toll" in the Russian invasion of Ukrainian territory, "images of fear, anguish and terror are seen in every corner of Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 "The protection of civilians must be the number one priority. International humanitarian and human rights law must be respected," the UN official stressed.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:26\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 04:05\par \par \uc2 Russia's ambassador to Japan threatened "serious response" to sanctionsRussian Ambassador to Japan Mikhail Yurievich Galuzin on Friday threatened the Japanese Executive with "a serious response" from Moscow to the new Japanese sanctions imposed after the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The head of the Russian diplomatic mission in Japan made this statement on the same day that Japanese Prime Minister Fumio Kishida announced a new round of punitive measures against the neighboring country, including the freezing of funds of Russian financial institutions and individuals or the control of semiconductor exports.\par \par \uc2 "I have conveyed to the Japanese government that these actions will have a response, a serious response from our side," Galuzin said at a press conference held at the Foreign Correspondents' Club of Japan.\par \par \uc2 "There will be damage for both sides, both the one receiving the sanctions and the one imposing them," warned the ambassador, who declined to disclose further details about possible Russian retaliation.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:39\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President confirmed the missile attacksVolodimir Zelensky, President of Ukraine, confirmed that the missile attacks launched by Russia targeted both military and civilian sites.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he appreciated Ukraine's resistance: "Our boys and girls, all defenders of the country, did not allow the enemy to carry out the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan of the invasion on the first day."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:39\par \par \uc2 Volodimir Zelensky, president of Ukraine. Photo Presidential Press.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 03:15\par \par \uc2 New York offered to take in Ukrainian refugeesNew York Governor Kathy Hochul said Thursday that her state is ready to welcome Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 "New York is ready to welcome Ukrainian refugees," she said in a statement, in which she recalled that the state is home to the largest Ukrainian-origin population in theUnited States.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic governor added that her team is in contact with the federal government and that New Yorkers "will be ready to accept and support those seeking refuge."\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 02:47\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian Chancellor compared Russia's attack to Hitler's Germany."The last time our capital experienced something like this was in 1941 when it was attacked by Nazi Germany. Ukraine defeated that evil and will defeat this one. Stop Putin. Isolate Russia. Cut all ties. Throw Russia out," Ukrainian Foreign Minister Dmytro Kuleba wrote on his official Twitter account. .\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 02:47\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 02:23\par \par \uc2 Denmark will receive refugees from UkraineDenmark's Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen confirmed Thursday at a press conference that her country will accept refugees fleeing Ukraine amid Russia's military onslaught.\par \par \uc2 "There will be internally displaced refugees. There will also be, of course, a lot of pressure on countries like Poland, but also Moldova and other countries. And then refugee flows can enter Europe, which will facilitate Denmark," Frederiksen explained.\par \par \uc2 However, he added that "it is still too early to put figures on how many refugees Denmark will take in."\par \par \uc2 According to the Danish news agency Ritzau, the immigration authorities have begun preparations to take in Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 01:53\par \par \uc2 European Union anticipated "massive and severe" sanctions against RussiaThrough a declaration presented at an emergency summit in Brussels, the leaders of the 27 countries of the European Union (EU) agreed on Thursday to adopt sanctions with "massive and severe" consequences against Russia, in response to its military offensive in Ukraine.Although details regarding the new sanctions were not yet known, the President of the European Commission, Ursula von der Leyen, specified at the end of the summit that they will focus on "five pillars": financial sector, energy, transport, export control and visa restrictions.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 01:14\par \par \uc2 Ukraine confirmed that the anti-defense system intercepted a Russian missile in KievUkrainian Deputy Interior Minister Yevhen Yenin attributed an explosion recorded Friday over Kiev to an action of the "Ukrainian anti-missile system that intercepted a (Russian) missile in the sky" over the capital.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Anton Gerashchenko, advisor to the head of Ukraine's Interior Ministry, said Friday that Kiev had been hit by "cruise or ballistic missiles."\par \par \uc2 Photos shared by Gerashchenko on Friday show a Kiev residential building on fire and firefighters responding at the scene as clouds of black smoke rise in the distance.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 01:04\par \par \uc2 Indian prime minister calls Putin and asks for "immediate cessation of violence".Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi "called for an immediate cessation of violence" in a call he held with Russian President Vladimir Putin, according to a statement issued by the Indian prime minister's office.\par \par \uc2 Modi "called for an immediate cessation of violence and called for concerted efforts by all parties to return to the path of diplomatic negotiations and dialogue," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 25.02.2022\par \par \uc2 00:51\par \par \uc2 Russia seized a small Ukrainian island in the Black Sea and killed 13 coastguardsmen Ukraine lost to Russia control of the small Snake Island in the Black Sea, about 45 kilometers off the coast of Romania, and 13 coastguardsmen who tried to defend it were killed by Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 "According to the information we have, the island of Serpents was seized" by Russian troops, the Ukrainian coast guard