whether or not to advance was in Putin's hands." He added: "They can go at any time."\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 16:57\par \par \uc2 The importance of the pipeline and why it was targeted by U.S. sanctionsThe 1,235-kilometer Nord Stream 2 was built with the aim of transporting Russian gas directly to Germany without passing through Ukraine, which deprived that country of an important source of income as it charged Russia a fee for letting gas pass through its territory.\par \par \uc2 Moscow opted for the construction of Nord Stream 2 after the so-called "gas wars" of 2006 and 2009, in which disagreements between Russia and Ukraine - then the main route for Russian gas - led to supply cuts and higher energy prices in Europe.\par \par \uc2 In this context, Biden announced this Wednesday sanctions against the company in charge of operating the pipeline and warned that it is part of the "first batch" of economic measures to be taken against Russia.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 16:41\par \par \uc2 Italy warns of growing risk of "large-scale Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine"Italian Foreign Minister Giorgio Di Maio on Wednesday raised the alarm of a growing risk of failure of efforts for a diplomatic solution to the crisis in Ukraine, informing Parliament that "the margins are shrinking day by day" to achieve a dialogue initiative to avoid a war.? "We condemn Moscow's decision to send troops to the territory of the two separatist republics", justifying them as "a contingent to keep the peace," Di Maio said.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 16:16\par \par \uc2 Biden announced sanctions against the gas pipeline linking Russia and GermanyBiden announced sanctions againstthe gas pipeline linking Russia and Germany. AP Photo\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Joe Biden on Wednesday announced sanctions against the company in charge of operating the Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline linking Russia to Germany.\par \par \uc2 "I have directed my administration to impose sanctions on Nord Stream 2 AG and its management," Biden said in a statement. ? "These measures are part of our first batch of sanctions in response to Russia's actions in Ukraine," he stressed.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 16:02\par \par \uc2 Amid escalation of war, the government receives Ukrainian representativeSergiy Nebrat, upon entering the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. Photo: Juan Asensio\par \par \uc2 The Charg\u233\'00\'E9 d'Affaires of Kiev, Sergiy Nebrat, was received this Wednesday at the Chancellery, where he will hold a meeting with Deputy Foreign Minister Pablo Tettamanti.As reported by Clar\u237\'00\'EDn, Foreign Minister Santiago Cafiero will not be present at the meeting. The Government argued that Nebrat is not an ambassador but a charg\u233\'00\'E9 d'affaires.?the last Ukrainian ambassador in Argentina returned to Kiev before the pandemic was declared and this post was not reinstated. Nebrat is the head of the mission.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 15:26\par \par \uc2 Russia allegedly deployed troops in eastern UkraineLatvia's Prime Minister Krisjanis Karins claimed Wednesday that Russian President Vladimir Putin has already deployed troops and tanks in the Donbass region of eastern Ukraine after recognizing its independence.\par \par \uc2 "According to the information we have, Putin is moving additional forces and tanks to the occupied territories of the Donbass. Crossing the sovereign territory of a neighboring country is an invasion," Karins stated without providing evidence in an interview with CNN. - this statement contradicts the statement of the Russian president, who assured that there are no soldiers of his country in Donbass at the moment.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 15:04\par \par \uc2 Shelling near Lugansk, one of the separatist territoriesDamage in the village of Vibrovka after a shelling near the pro-Russian-controlled territory. (Photo EFE)\par \par \uc2 A man looks into the hole left by a bomb in the village of Vibrovka, near the separatist-controlled town of Lugansk.\par \par \uc2 The village of Vibrovka is near Lugansk, one of the pro-Russian territories controlled by separatists, which was recognized as independent by Putin. (EFE photo)\par \par \uc2 The traces of a projectile near Lugansk (Photo EFE).\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 14:52\par \par \uc2 Ukraine introduces special measures in regions bordering Russia and Belarus.Ukraine has imposed special measures in the regions bordering Russia and Belarus and those with access to the seas, the Ukrainian State Border Guard Service (SEGF) reported Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The measures include limiting the movement of private vehicles and floating artifacts, flights of light aircraft and unmanned aerial vehicles, as well as restrictions on filming and photography of certain objects, according to a statement posted on the SEGF website.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's National Security and Defense Council has proposed that Parliament introduce a state of emergency in the country amid ongoing tensions with Russia.\par \par \uc2 If the Parliament approves the measure, the state of emergency will be introduced for 30 days throughout the country, except in the troubled Lugansk and Donetsk regions.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 14:30\par \par \uc2 U.S. defended sanctions on Russia: "We hit hard yesterday, and it was just a demonstration."Daleep Singh, deputy national security adviser. Photo EFE\par \par \uc2 The White House on Wednesday defended the "tough" economic sanctions imposed on Russia, while warning that they are "just a demonstration" of the "mounting costs" Moscow will face if it continues its aggressive actions in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Let me be really clear: we hit hard yesterday, and it was just a demonstration," Daleep Singh, White House deputy national security adviser, said in an appearance on CNN.\par \par \uc2 The official was commenting on the first direct economic measures announced yesterday by U.S. President Joe Biden against two major Russian financial institutions, VEB (one of Russia's main investment and development banks) and the military bank (PSB), considered fundamental for the Russian defense sector.\par \par \uc2 Among the additional measures, Biden's advisor mentioned controls on technology exports to Russia, which "can be applied at any time". And he indicated that Moscow "is already feeling the pain" from these sanctions, but specified that the objective "is not to maximize" the punishment.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:56\par \par \uc2 U.S. warns that a Russian invasion of Ukraine could displace 5 million peopleA Russian invasion of Ukraine could generate a "new refugee crisis" with up to five million people displaced, the US ambassador to the UN General Assembly in New York warned Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "If Russia continues down this path, it could - according to our estimates - create a new refugee crisis, one of the largest in the world today," said Ambassador Linda Thomas-Greenfield.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:30\par \par \uc2 Ukraine denounces cyber-attacks on government websitesUkraine's digital transformation minister says cyberattacks are affecting government websites and some banks in his country.\par \par \uc2 Mikhail Fedorov said Wednesday that the attacks targeted the websites of Parliament, the Cabinet and the Foreign Ministry.\par \par \uc2 He added that they also caused disruptions or delays on the sites of the Defense Ministry and Internal Affairs, which controls the police.\par \par \uc2 NATO blamed the recent cyber attacks in Ukraine on Russia's GRU military intelligence agency and warned that more attacks were likely as tensions over a possible Russian invasion of Ukraine increased.Russian cybercriminals are on the FBI's most wanted list.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putien 2006 at GRU headquarters.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 13:16\par \par \uc2 ???? Ukrainian soldier dies in shelling on the eastern frontA Ukrainian soldier was killed Wednesday in shelling near the front line with pro-Russian separatist territories in the east, the Armed Forces reported, at a time of growing fears of a Russian invasion of the country.\par \par \uc2 The military statement did not specify the place of the attack, limiting itself to say that the soldier "suffered fatal wounds" and that another serviceman was wounded.\par \par \uc2 Nine Ukrainian soldiers have been killed since the beginning of the year, six of them in the last four days, according to an AFP count based on official sources.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:56\par \par \uc2 Who are the leaders who support Putin in the world?Putin and Maduro at a meeting in 2017. AP Photo.\par \par \uc2 Nicolas Maduro, Daniel Ortega, Donald Trump, the presidents of Iran and Syria are some of them. The detail in this note.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:42\par \par \uc2 UN chief says world faces "a moment of danger".UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres said Wednesday during the opening of the organization's General Assembly that the world faces "a moment of danger" because of the crisis in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 For Guterres, moreover, "Russia's decision to recognize the so-called "independence" of the Donetsk and Lugansk regions - and subsequent actions - are violations of the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine and are incompatible with the principles of the UN Charter."\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:25\par \par \uc2 European Union convenes special summit on Thursday over Ukraine crisisEuropean Council President Charles Michel called for a special European Union (EU) summit in Brussels on Thursday to discuss the crisis between Ukraine and Russia, according to a letter released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "Russia's aggressive actions violate international law, and the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine. They also undermine European security," Michel said in the letter to the 27 heads of state and government invited to the special summit.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 12:16\par \par \uc2 Ukraine: "The fate of Europe is decided".Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelensky said today that "the fate of Europe is decided on the ground in Ukraine", therefore he proclaimed that "the time has come to react strongly" and called for "immediate security guarantees" from Western countries.?Zelensky announced that Ukraine confirms its desire to join the European Union and NATO despite the ultimatum launched on Tuesday by Russian President Vladimir Putin for Kiev to renounce its membership in the Atlantic Alliance.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:55\par \par \uc2 Who are the 5 people close to Vladimir Putin sanctioned by the United States?The U.S. Treasury Department released the names of the five people close to Russian President Vladimir Putin who were sanctioned by the U.S. "All property and interests of these five men are blocked," they said.\par \par \uc2 They are "powerful Russians from Putin's inner circle and family members", who are believed to be involved "in the kleptocracy of theRussian regime" (the interest of self-enrichment at the expense of others takes precedence).\par \par \uc2 According to the BBC, the five people sanctioned by the United States are: the director of the Federal Security System, Aleksandr Vasilievich Bortnikov, and his son Denis, who currently serves as vice-president of the Russian state financial institution VTB Bank Public Joint Stock Company. More information here.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:39\par \par \uc2 Deployment of Ukrainian soldiers on the hottest borderUkrainian soldiers on the border of the village Schastia in the pro-Russian Lugansk area, one of the hot borders of the conflict. AFP photo\par \par \uc2 The armed forces are ready to act in the face of any Russian advance (AFP Photo).\par \par \uc2 The military is deployed in the area that became a strategic point after Putin recognized two Ukrainian territories as independent. (AFP Photo)\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:21\par \par \uc2 Erdogan, President of Turkey: "Everyone talks but no one does anything". Faced with the crisis in Ukraine, "everyone talks but no one does anything," criticized Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan in a meeting with journalists published Wednesday by the Turkish daily H\u252\'00\'FCrriyet.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the results of the recent meetings of French President Emmanuel Macron and German Chancellor Olaf Scholz with Russian leader Vladimir Putin "are there for all to see" and that "no results have come out of the talks of U.S. President Joe Biden either". ?"Looking at the issue from a state or institutional angle, there is only NATO left," said Erdogan, who interrupted yesterday a tour of Africa to participate today in a videoconference with the leaders of the Atlantic Alliance, dedicated to the Ukrainian crisis.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:16\par \par \uc2 Ukraine calls for immediate "security guarantees" from Western countries and RussiaUkrainian President Volodimir Zelensky called for security guarantees from Western countries and Moscow.? "Ukraine needs clear and concrete security guarantees, immediately," from both Western countries and Russia, Zelensky told a press conference."I think Russia should be among the countries that will give us such guarantees," he insisted.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 11:05\par \par \uc2 Russia evacuates its embassy in Ukraine and tensions growCrosses in front of the Russian embassy in Ukraine. Photo Reuters\par \par \uc2 Russia began evacuating its embassy in Kiev, and Ukraine urged its citizens to leave Russia on Wednesday as the region braced for further confrontation after President Vladimir Putin received authorization to use military force outside his country and the West responded with sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Russians leave their embassy in Kiev. (AFP Photo)\par \par \uc2 Hopes of a diplomatic way out to avoid a devastating new war appeared to have faded and the United States and key European allies on Tuesday accused Moscow of crossing a red line by sending troops across Ukraine's border into separatist regions. Several described it as an invasion.\par \par \uc2 Russia began withdrawing staff from its diplomatic posts in Ukraine, state news agency Tass reported, a day after the Foreign Ministry announced an evacuation plan citing threats.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 10:55\par \par \uc2 Boris Johnson announces additional arms shipments to UkraineBritish Prime Minister Boris Johnson announced Wednesday that the UK will send more weapons to Ukraine for its defense in response to the imminent threat of a full-scale invasion by Russia.\par \par \uc2 "In light of Russia's increasingly threatening behavior, and in line with our previous support, the UK will shortly provide an additional package of military support to Ukraine," Johnson said at Prime Minister's Questions in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 10:27\par \par \uc2 ???? Russia pledges "strong" and "painful" response to US sanctionsRussia on Wednesday promised a "strong" and "painful" response to U.S. sanctions announced after Vladimir Putin recognized the independence of two Ukrainian separatist regions.\par \par \uc2 "Let there be no doubt: there will be a strong response to these sanctions, not necessarily symmetrical, but well-calculated and painful for the United States," the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 It noted that this is already the 101st round of sanctions against the country and argued that the US wants with these measures "to try to change Russia's course."\par \par \uc2 Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the obvious futility of the efforts made over the years to hinder the development of our economy, the United States is again taking advantage of restrictive instruments that are ineffective and counterproductive from the point of view of American interests," he said. ? "We affirm that in the arsenal of the US foreign policy, imprisoned by the stereotypes of a unipolar world with the false conviction that the United States still has the right and can impose on everyone its own rules of the world order, there are no other means left but blackmail, intimidation and threats," added the department headed by Sergey Lavrov.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:57\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis called for a day of "fasting and prayer for peace".Pope Francis on Wednesday launched an appeal to all parties involved in the conflict in Ukraine to avoid warlike actions and abide by international law. At the same time he called for a day of "fasting and prayer for peace" for next Wednesday, March 2.\par \par \uc2 "I have a great pain in my heart for the worsening of the situation in Ukraine, because despite the diplomatic efforts of recent weeks, increasingly alarming scenarios are opening up," said the Pontiff after the traditional Wednesday audience at the Vatican. More information here.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:47\par \par \uc2 Trump's unexpected praise for Putin amid escalating war in UkraineA photo from June 2019 when Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin greeted each other during a G20 meeting in Japan.\par \par \uc2 As Republican leaders in Congress press President Joe Biden to act more forcefully to condemn Vladimir Putin for sending troops to Ukraine, former Republican President Donald Trump and some of his prominent allies have praised the Russian leader's style of power. The Putin-driven crisis is causing a growing rift within the Republican Party On one side are traditional foreign policy hawks who have advocated a more confrontational stance with the Russian strongman and on the other, a "Trumpist" faction that has expressed some sympathy for the Russian's tactics.? "This is great. Putin declares a large part of Ukraine independent," Trump said on a conservative Podcast. "That's wonderful." (Source ANSA)\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:31\par \par \uc2 Putin accused Ukraine for genocide. In his speech on Monday Putin accused Ukraine, a country without historical legitimacy according to him, of having a"neo-Nazi" regime, sponsored by the West, to exterminate Russian speakers in the Donbas.\par \par \uc2 "The so-called civilized world, of which our Western colleagues are the only representatives, prefers to turn a blind eye, as if these horrors did not exist, to the genocide suffered by four million people," he said, accusing Kiev of banning the Russian language.?Strictly speaking, Ukraine did not ban Russian, which is still widely spoken in large parts of the country, but adopted a law imposing Ukrainian in the public space and in the media.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:25\par \par \uc2 Half of Russians support the war Every second Russian, 50 percent, considers it right for their country to use military force against Ukraine to prevent the Kiev government from joining NATO, according to a poll published Wednesday by CNN.\par \par \uc2 The poll, conducted by the Savanta ComRes polling firm among citizens of Ukraine and Russia, concludes that only 25 percent of Russians believe it would be wrong to resort to the military against Ukraine and another 25 percent are not sure what is the best option to resolve the current crisis.\par \par \uc2 But the poll also found that more Russians think it would be wrong to try to forcibly "reunite" Russia and Ukraine, two countries with a long and complicated history of intertwined relations.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 09:10\par \par \uc2 "We are afraid of war, yes, but we are ready to face it."The demonstrations in Kiev. Photo EFE\par \par \uc2 Residents of the Ukrainian capital watch from afar and without altering their routine the possibility of a devastating conflict with Russia. But some rituals of the war rearguard not seen in years, returned overnight.\par \par \uc2 Since Tuesday, every hour on the hour, loud loudspeakers blare the national anthem "Ukraine is not dead" in the large Maidan square.What are they saying in the country's largest city about the news coming from the Russian border?\par \par \uc2 "We are afraid of war, yes, but we are ready to face it because it is a defensive war," says Anatoli Tarasenko, a 74-year-old pensioner who criticizes the government for "not having lifted a finger" to prevent after Crimea's annexation to Russia in 2014. ?\par \par \uc2 Protests in Kiev in front of the Russian embassy in the capital. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 Ksenia Balfy, a 38-year-old DJ, does not plan to have her Friday night show cancelled. For her, Putin's threats are "nothing new." "It is normal to be afraid, no one knows what will happen now, but we, we stay here and we will defend our country," the woman says.\par \par \uc2 Oleg Koras is 38 years old, he is a peddler and signed up for "territorial defense" as a reservist in case the war breaks out. "If bombs fall on our city, what do they want us to do, we will seek shelter. But then, we will know how to respond," he replied to the AFP agency.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:50\par \par \uc2 Humanitarian organizations fear for civilian casualties in the conflict UkraineThe current escalation of tension in Ukraine increases the risk of mass displacement in the region, warned from Geneva the Norwegian Refugee Council and the World Organization Against Torture (OMCT), which called for the inclusion of vulnerable groups in possible evacuation plans.\par \par \uc2 Children in specialized institutions, elderly in nursing homes or prisoners should be taken into account in eventual evacuations, OMCT claimed in a communiqu\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 "In case the armed conflict escalates, these people, numbering in the tens of thousands, would have no chance of reaching safe areas," warned the organization, who noted that since the outbreak of the conflict in Donbas in 2014 there have already been cases of attacks on penitentiary institutions.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:30\par \par \uc2 UN head questions Russia's decision to recognize Donetsk and LuganskUN Secretary-General Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres answers reporters' questions about the situation on the Ukrainian-Russian borders. EFE photo.\par \par \uc2 ?Russia's decision to recognize the independence of the separatist Donetsk and Lugansk People's Republics constitutes a "violation of the territorial integrity" of Ukraine, UN Secretary-General Ant\u243\'00\'F3nio Guterres warned today, urging an immediate cessation of hostilities in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Let me be clear: the Russian Federation's decision to recognize the so-called 'independence' of certain areas of the Donetsk and Lugansk regions is a violation of the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine," Guterres said.\par \par \uc2 23.02.2022\par \par \uc2 08:02\par \par \uc2 China says U.S. "adding fuel to the fire" in Ukraine-Russia crisis?China said the United States fuels the Ukraine-Russia conflict with its constant arms supplies to the Ukrainian military and sanctions against Moscow that solve nothing.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. has not stopped selling arms to Ukraine, increasing tension and creating panic," a Chinese Foreign Ministry spokeswoman, Hua Chunying, criticized to reporters in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine calls for state of emergency ?Russian armored vehicles at the railway station in Rostov region, Russia. Photo EFE.\par \par \uc2 ?The Ukrainian Security Council called for a state of emergency in the country, coinciding with a rise in fears of an imminent Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 "The Ukrainian Parliament must give its approval to this decision in the next 48 hours," said the secretary of the Security and National Defense Council, Oleksii Danilov.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220227ei2r0055g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
