\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 What was the economic cost of Argentina's neutrality in the main war conflicts in Europe and what damages can be repeated?\b0\par\par\uc2 2,737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The great war conflicts that had their epicenter in Europe in the 20th Century left a deep imprint on the course of the Argentine economy. Will that impact be repeated with the war between Russia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which marked a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  orientation in the local economic policy, or will the change of global context towards a more globalized world -although hit by the Covid-19 pandemic- prevent thinking of a return to the unfinished paradigm of Aldo Ferrer's "Vivir con lo Nuestro" (Living with what is ours)?\par \par \uc2 Will there be a new false step in the "neutrality" that the country usually adopts in international conflicts, when the Government -which this year presides over the \uc2 United Nations Human Rights Council\uc2 - now needs the support of a group of key countries in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) board of directors to avoid an unmanageable crisis?\par \par \uc2 During World War I, as a product of its enormous and diverse number of immigrants from various European countries, the government of Hip\u243\'00\'F3lito Yrigoyen remained neutral but did not reduce its affinity with Great Britain, the country's main trading partner. However, for a country that was already suffering the full effects of the changes in trade and financial flows, that decade was devastating. The European war was perceived as a conflict between empires in the face of which Argentina felt no need to get involved, historian Alejandro G\u243\'00\'F3mez told Infobae.\par \par \uc2 "The upheavals that World War I generated in Argentina began before the war itself; already in 1913 the complicated situation in the Balkans affected the entire world capital market. The increase in the London bank rate prevented the country from financing its balance of payments deficit; the moment could not have been more inopportune, due to the bad harvests of 1913 and 1914\u8243\'20\'33, highlighted Pablo Gerchunoff and Lucas Llach in the book "El Ciclo de la Ilusi\u243\'00\'F3n y el Desencanto" (The Cycle of Illusion and Disenchantment). The four years of the conflict were very negative: GDP fell 10.4% in 1914, rose 0.5% in 1915, fell 2.9% in 1916 and another 8.1% in 1917. The monetary anemia, which triggered the crisis, took a long time to be cured.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, economist Dar\u237\'00\'EDo Judzik said that "although at international level, the Great Depression was the most severe macroeconomic blow of the 20th century, however, in Argentina the impact of the First World War was much more important. Economic historians who reconstructed the GDP per capita series find a dramatic fall between 1913 and 1917, around the First World War, while between 1929 and the first years of the 1930s there was a relevant but smaller fall".\par \par \uc2 Although at the international level, the Great Depression was the most severe macroeconomic blow of the 20th century, in Argentina, however, the impact of the First World War was much more important (Judzik).\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons for this difference is that in 1913 a process of strong growth in countries such as Argentina, based on international trade and foreign investment (mainly British), began to slow down. The world was culminating the first great globalization, which took place between the mid-nineteenth century and the First World War, with improvements in transportation and communications, a period in which huge foreign investments were arriving to Argentina, which was a very promising project. This began to slow down in 1913, shortly before the Great War.\par \par \uc2 After those anemic figures of 1914-1918, the country began to recover steadily, although with less convincing figures than before the war. "None of the countries that most resembled Argentina registered such high rates, whether one considers overall growth or per capita value; during the 1920s, Argentina grew more than the United States, Canada and Australia. By 1929, the country's total output had surpassed that of Australia, and was on its way to catching up with Canada.\par \par \uc2 This translated into greater social relaxation and fewer strikes," said Gerchunoff and Llach. On the other hand, for Arturo O'Connell, Argentina then suffered the "problems of an open economy". This problem was based on the fact that, as a producer of raw materials in a temperate zone, Argentina was a natural competitor of the central countries and, therefore, was more exposed than other nations to variations in trade and capital flows; in particular, the Argentine fiscal system depended excessively on taxes linked to foreign trade, an error that would be corrected in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, for Carlos D\u237\'00\'EDaz Alejandro, the growth prior to 1930 was caused precisely by the open character of the local economy. "Growth prior to 1930 was generated by exports, not because these and the associated capital inflows supplied a growing global demand (in the Keynesian sense), but because -and this is more important- because exports and capital inflows originated a more efficient allocation of resources than that which could have resulted from autarkic policies. In particular, the domestic cost of capital goods in an autarkic regime (in 1880, for example), was reduced to a level through market exports produced with the generous use of an input -land- whose economic value in an autarkic regime would be quite small".\par \par \uc2 In any case, as mentioned, the recovery was slow: it was not until 1923 that the GDP per capita of 1913 was reached. The rebound was reflected in an expansive fiscal policy, so typical of the various Argentine economic cycles: public spending rose from 8.5% to 13% of GDP between 1920 and 1929, when the deficit of the public accounts already amounted to 4% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Gerchunoff and Llach pointed out that Argentina's propensity to consume a lot and save little during times of bonanzas and optimism was also noticeable in the State. The practice of spending in excess of tax revenues had not been infrequent in the past. It had been a typical feature of Argentine finances to alternate between deficit cycles with growing debt and cycles of moderation in which obligations were cancelled.\par \par \uc2 Unlike many countries in the region or in other continents, there were no countercyclical measures to provide a "cushion" in lean times. Although in relative terms fiscal management during the Alvear administration was more predictable and orderly than under Yrigoyen, there was no tendency to save. "The total public debt increased 50%. For a country in expansion, this was not an unsustainable increase, but it was worrisome that the tendency to deficit was accentuated over the years. In 1927 the fiscal deficit was the highest ever recorded. It would be difficult to doubt the solvency of the Argentine State as long as the wind of the international economy was blowing in its favor and a certain control over the deficit was maintained. This was the case between 1924 and 1928, a period in which the Argentine State could comfortably finance itself abroad by taking American loans of close to USD 290 million", detailed Llach and Gerchunoff.\par \par \uc2 The problem, of course, arose when the international economic climate changed after the 1929 crisis: in two years, the public debt increased 35% and the tendency to fiscal imbalance, which had been hidden in the placid climate of Alvear's times, suddenly became a visible and pressing problem, the historians pointed out.\par \par \uc2 When the international economic climate changed after the 1929 crisis, in two years, the public debt increased 35% and the tendency to fiscal imbalance suddenly became a visible and pressing problem.\par \par \uc2 The economic growth of the decade was accompanied by higher indebtedness and lower savings. Debt payments were higher than the trade surplus, so more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows were required annually, O'Connell stressed, to avoid serious problems in the balance of payments and the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 Although Great Britain was the country's main trading partner, the United States in the 1920s became the main supplier of capital: in 1927, American capital installed in the country was 25 times greater than in 1909, basically directed more towards the industrial sector, infrastructure and transport, where British investments still predominated. However, despite the traditional revisionist reading of those years, these investments did not mean a greater foreignization of capital, since, as a percentage of the total, it fell from 41 to 34 between 1909 and 1927.\par \par \uc2 The country participated actively in international trade: in 1929 the value of Argentine products exchanged with the rest of the world was 3% of the total, despite having a population that represented 0.6% of the global population. Argentine exports had also grown more than the average: 57 against 15 between 1913 and 1929.\par \par \uc2 The impact of World War II\par \par \uc2 World War II consolidated the pattern of import substitution and increased state regulation that had begun in the interwar period in Argentina under conservative governments.\par \par \uc2 Historian Alejandro G\u243\'00\'F3mez commented to Infobae that "every time there is a war, governments spend more and then neither that spending nor the controls disappear. Besides, in the 1930s the idea of the welfare state prevailed, because the idea of the minimum state was attacked by communism on the left and by Nazism and fascism on the right".\par \par \uc2 Every time there is a war, governments spend more and then neither that spending nor the controls disappear (G\u243\'00\'F3mez).\par \par \uc2 In this sense, economist Miguel Kiguel told this media that "between 1946 and 1949 the State used a strong monetary and fiscal expansion and increased the control of its economy".\par \par \uc2 "On the external front, the Peronist administration expected that the recomposition of international trade would be slow and even interrupted by a third world war between the victorious powers. Therefore, it bet its chips on industry and the domestic market. The State guided the path of development with deliberate industrial policies, such as the nationalization of international trade and the protection of domestic industry through import restrictions. Domestic industry, especially light industry, grew to the point that it almost entirely supplied the domestic market", said Kiguel in his book "Argentina's Economic Crises".\par \par \uc2 According to Kiguel, "on the international front, the world did not support Argentina's plans: the Marshall Plan allowed the main European economies to recover quickly, but Argentina did not receive this help because of its poorly disguised sympathy for the Axis during almost the entire war".\par \par \uc2 On the international front, the world did not support Argentina's plans: the Marshall Plan allowed the main European economies to recover quickly, but Argentina did not receive this aid because of its poorly concealed sympathy for the Axis (Kiguel).\par \par \uc2 In this regard, Roberto Cort\u233\'00\'E9s Conde said in "La Econom\u237\'00\'EDa pol\u237\'00\'EDtica de la Argentina en el siglo XX" that "Per\u243\'00\'F3n did not have very precise ideas about the economy, but he shared those ideas that were common, after the crisis of 1930, in the post-war intellectual climate: the conviction that in the market something was failing and that State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an alternative, if not necessary, at least convenient". "The military had had the example of Germany, which had achieved a recovery in the 1930s, which was able to reconvert itself into a war power, and that of the planned economy of the Soviet Union, even if the regime seemed opprobrious to them."\par \par \uc2 "The autarkic and nationalist ideas were widely accepted because of the belief that in the post-war period international trade restrictions would continue. There were certain facts that were important: the autarchy that had existed de facto since the years of the Second World War and the inexistence of an international capital market," said Cort\u233\'00\'E9s Conde.\par \par \uc2 He also noted: "the enormous accumulation of trade surpluses, due to the restriction of imports during the war, created the impression that the accumulation of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was due to a great increase in wealth, when it was due to decapitalization".\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, "the enormous demand for food, translated into improved prices in the first two years after the war, also gave the impression of an increase in wealth and there was a pessimistic view about the possibilities of world trade to place Argentine exports", concluded Cort\u233\'00\'E9s Conde.\par \par \uc2 In turn, Llach and Gerchunoff observed that, with the Second War, "the economy felt the most profound change, as had happened with the two previous great international shocks, the First World War and the Great Depression".\par \par \uc2 "As the world conflict began in 1939 the post-Depression recovery was a fact. The national product, which had fallen at a rate of 2.5% between 1929 and 1933, was growing 4% per year between 1929 and 1939. But in the Argentine economy there were indelible traces of the crisis (such as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , exchange controls and regulatory boards) and the more solid presence of a national industry favored by those same institutions", they explained.\par \par \uc2 But the greatest impact "was linked, once again, to international trade. If the protectionist wave of the 1930s already made it impossible to return to pre-crisis conditions, World War II aggravated the situation further," the historians noted.\par \par \uc2 If the protectionist wave of the 1930s already made it impossible to return to pre-crisis conditions, the Second World War aggravated the situation even more (Gerchunoff and Llach).\par \par \uc2 "The Economic Action Plan proposed by (Federico) Pinedo in 1940 failed for political reasons but also because of its pessimistic forecasts, since the economy grew 3.6% per year for a five-year period, due to the effect of industry. However, growth was lower than in other Latin American countries", Gerchunoff-Llach argue.\par \par \uc2 And they add in the book El ciclo de la ilusi\u243\'00\'F3n y el desencanto: "The conjunction of the Third Position and the belief that the commercial difficulties of the immediate post-war period would be prolonged and could be aggravated by a new world conflict justified the government's option for economic autarky".\par \par \uc2 In this sense, they detail that "Argentina's commercial position at the end of the Second War was not comfortable, in spite of the trade surpluses that had been accumulating. In fact, the problem was the excessive surplus, since it was not the result of a great export performance but of the difficulties to obtain imports".\par \par \uc2 As a corollary, "after the Second World War, the economy began to show inflationary symptoms. From 1941 to 1945 the monetary base grew 15%, a high level compared to previous volumes. The whole world had high inflation during the war, but then it disappeared, while in Argentina it remained higher than in the most advanced countries. From the 1940s onwards, the paths of Argentine and North American inflation were definitively separated".\par \par \uc2 Part of this problem, as explained by Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Luis Machinea, Sebasti\u225\'00\'E1n Katz and Federico Grillo in "La econom\u237\'00\'EDa de Per\u243\'00\'F3n", was based on the fact that consolidated public spending went from an average of 16.2% of GDP in 1945-1946 to 22.5% in 1954-1955, with a peak of 29.7% in 1948.\par \par \uc2 After the Second World War, the economy began to show inflationary symptoms.\par \par \uc2 High public expenditure, low domestic savings and a bad reading of the international situation -reflected in a pretended historical "neutrality" of the country- seem to be the driving forces of Argentine economic policy in the aforementioned international conflicts.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, historian Alejandro G\u243\'00\'F3mez said that "Argentina was left out of many benefits because of its neutrality, not only during the Second World War conflict, but also afterwards with its famous Third Position. If Argentina now makes a clear statement against Russia, perhaps later on it will obtain financial relief, but if it maintains a lukewarm position it will be left aside when it has to ask for assistance from organizations such as the \uc2 IMF\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 With situations that can be compared between the wars of the 20th century and the current conflict: will there be time to avoid similar mistakes in the face of this new war scenario in 2022 and a local scenario of strong macroeconomic fragility?\par \par \uc2 CONTINUE READING:\par \par \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 : "We call on the Russian Federation to put an end to the actions undertaken".\par \par \uc2 Two historians analyze the invasion of Ukraine and the world wars: "Putin's speech is a copy of Hitler's".\par \par \uc2 Dollar, \uc2 IMF\uc2  and commodities: what could be the impact for Argentina of Russia's invasion of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine: the Government is worried about higher inflation pressure and a rise in gas costs\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220227ei2r002eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
