\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Russia to tap rainy-day fund as sanctions hit borrowing abilities\b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par 17:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, detail)\par \par \uc2 By Darya Korsunskaya and Andrey Ostroukh\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Russia will use money from its rainy-day fund this year to replace shortfalls caused by a forced reduction in state borrowing, which has grown more costly due to new Western sanctions, Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Following Russia's invasion of Ukraine, the United States, Britain and other Western nations have imposed new sanctions targeting Russian banks, sovereign debt and individuals.\par \par \uc2 Siluanov said Russia's economic and financial situation was stable in general but conceded that sanctions will change the country's borrowing plan, requiring it to tap the National Wealth Fund, which stood at $175 billion as of Feb. 1.\par \par \uc2 "Due to an increase in borrowing costs the issuing of state debt has been suspended. We are planning to reduce the amount of borrowing this year, will utilise NWF funds for it," Siluanov told a meeting with government officials.\par \par \uc2 As Russian missiles pounded Kyiv, authorities in Moscow were seeking ways to shield the economy from new penalties that sent the rouble to a record low, threatening living standards already hit by high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry said it will offer only a new series of OFZ government bonds and stop offering existing series of debt as the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  slapped restrictions on trading of Russian government debt.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  beefed up the banking sector with extra liquidity and started to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the rouble fell to all-time lows on the day Moscow sent its troops into \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it started this week for the first time since 2014, when Russia annexed Crimea from Ukraine, have already born fruit, \uc2 Vladimir Chistyukhin\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor, said at the same meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We have a serious safety margin from the financial stability point of view... The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to support banks with liquidity," Chistyukhin said.\par \par \uc2 Siluanov said swings on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were temporary and there was no immediate risk to financial stability. (Reporting by Andrey Ostroukh, Darya Korsunskaya, Elena Fabrichnaya and Alexander Marrow; Editing by David Gregorio) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-2-25T17:24:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220225ei2p0455i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
