\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks, Commodities Gyrate After Moscow Begins Attack -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,058 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Major indexes recover after invasion sends stocks lower and oil above $100 a barrel \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Hardika Singh, Anna Hirtenstein and Caitlin Ostroff \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fluctuated wildly Thursday, starting the day with a sharp drop and then recovering as investors piled into growth and technology stocks in the wake of Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell in the aftermath of the attacks on cities across Ukraine, while oil, gold and government notes rose. Investor optimism grew, however, after President Biden in an afternoon address announced new sanctions on Russian elites, state-owned enterprises and banks. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  later announced its own sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's "aggression in Ukraine will end up costing Russia dearly, economically and strategically," said Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 closed up 63.20 points, or 1.5%, to 4288.70. The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite finished ahead 436.10 points, or 3.3%, to 13473.59. The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 92.07 points, or 0.3%, to 33223.83.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble weakened to a record low, declining 4.5% against the dollar, while the MOEX benchmark for Russian stocks tumbled by about a third. The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and banned short selling. \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The country's stock exchange also said it was postponing activity.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, topped $100 a barrel for the first time since 2014, with the front-month contract settling up $2.24 per barrel, or 2.3%, to $99.08. Prices for its U.S. equivalent, West Texas Intermediate, also rose.\par \par \uc2 The invasion has heightened the pressure on a global economy already reeling from snarled supply chains and some of the highest inflation in years, with Europe likely to bear the brunt of the economic impact. Some investors, betting that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  won't act as aggressively to curb inflation at a time of heightened tensions, reopened their pandemic-era playbooks, piling into riskier assets in the second half of the trading session.\par \par \uc2 A pullback in the yield of the benchmark 10-year Treasury note Thursday added wind to the sails of growth companies. Yields settled at 1.969%, from 1.976% on Wednesday. Bond yields fall as prices rise.\par \par \uc2 "That makes people more comfortable buying growth stocks," said Gary Black, manager of the Future Fund Active exchange-traded fund. Mr. Black owns Alphabet and \uc2 Palo Alto Networks\uc2 , among other growth stocks.\par \par \uc2 Dramatic moves marked Thursday's trading. The Nasdaq Composite fell more than 3%, then closed up more than 3%, for the first time since November 2008, according to Dow Jones Market Data. The S&P 500 finished higher after falling at least 2.5% for only the 16th time in the past 15 years. The \uc2 Cboe\uc2  Volatility Index rose to the highest level in over 15 months.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500's information-technology and consumer-discretionary sectors were the biggest gainers, adding 3.5% and 2.5%, respectively. Netflix added $22.57, or 6.1%, to $390.03; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  gained 2.22, or 6.8%, to $34.98. Alphabet advanced $102.06, or 4%, to $2,653.82.\par \par \uc2 The Defiance Next Gen SPAC Derived exchange-traded fund, which tracks special-purpose acquisition companies, advanced $0.54, or 3.2%, to $17.40. Meme stocks also rose: \uc2 AMC Entertainment\uc2  jumped $1.95, or 12%, to $17.68, while \uc2 GameStop\uc2  climbed $9.71, or 8.5%, to $124.58.\par \par \uc2 Natural-gas futures in Europe surged 50%. Benchmark prices for aluminum and nickel, two metals of which Russia is a major producer, rose to their highest levels in about a decade. Wheat and corn futures advanced to multiyear highs. Russia and Ukraine are major grain producers.\par \par \uc2 "We haven't had any serious military conflict in Europe for a very long time, so there's no playbook for this," said \uc2 Gregory Perdon\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Arbuthnot Latham\uc2 . "A lot has changed since last night."\par \par \uc2 Gold, which like \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds typically rallies in times of stress, rose in price. The front month gold futures contract rose $15.90 per troy ounce, or 0.8%, to $1,925.10.\par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened, with the WSJ Dollar Index gaining 0.8%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered to be havens, such as the Japanese yen, also appreciated, while bitcoin recovered losses, trading up 2.2% to $38,395.65 from its 5 p.m. ET level on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Russian attack came as volatility in markets was already heightened because of looming changes in Fed monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Investors are building up cash buffers because of the market stress, said Florian Ielpo, head of macro at Lombard Odier Investment Managers. The company's multiasset funds currently hold the most cash since the market crash in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Markets are expecting a standard 0.25 percentage point increase at the Fed's meeting next month, rather than a more aggressive 0.5 percentage point increase. As of Thursday, market pricing showed the probability of a bigger rate increase fell to 13% from 34% the day before, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 "We might move past the geopolitical impact, but the market is still going to be concerned about the Fed's intentions to move rates higher," said \uc2 Lindsey Bell\uc2 , \uc2 Ally\uc2 's chief markets and money strategist.\par \par \uc2 In economic data, weekly U.S. jobless claims came in at 232,000, a decline from the previous week and in line with analysts' expectations in the midst of a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 Global markets fell. The pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 dropped about 3.3%. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index decreased over 3%. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average closed at its lowest level since November 2020.\par \par \uc2 -- Megumi Fujikawa, Will Horner and Quentin Webb contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Hardika Singh at hardika.singh@wsj.com, Anna Hirtenstein at anna.hirtenstein@wsj.com and Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (February 25, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 25, 2022 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220225ei2p000m0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
