\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 [Live] "Missile attacks on Kiev," official officials report.\b0\par\par\uc2 8,640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 A night view of Kiev after the mayor declared a curfew between 10 p. m. and 7 a. m. on February 24, 2022. In the early hours of the 25th, loud explosions were reported near the Ukrainian capital.Photo: Pierre CromInrecent hours, tensions around Ukraine rose after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered "military action" in the Donbas. Western leaders, such as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , condemned what happened, while warning that they will take action in response to what they consider "an unjustified attack". Meanwhile, explosions are reported from several Ukrainian cities and the country warns that it was attacked by Russian troops from Belarus. We tell you, minute by minute, what is happening in these tensions\par \par \uc2 .???????????? Before we begin, a bit of context: for weeks, Russia has deployed some 150,000 troops on the border with Ukraine and on Monday recognized the independence of two separatist territories: Donetsk and Lugansk. This stoked accusations that the Kremlin is preparing a large-scale attack against the neighboring country\par \par \uc2 .???????????? And a summary of what happened in the week:\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Germany suspended authorization of \uc2 Nord Stream 2.On\uc2  the same day, the Russian Senate authorized sending soldiers to separatist enclaves: The vote formally allowed Putin to send troops to the Donbas region of Ukraine.At the same time, the United States mobilized more troops to Eastern Europe.On Wednesday, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  formally announced sanctions on Russian leaders, as well as on banks and the defense sector. While Ukraine (prior to the Russian military action) announced a plan to mobilize its reservists and urged its citizens to leave Russia.Biden also announced sanctions against Russia on Wednesday: This first round of sanctions will prevent Russia from raising Western funds to repay sovereign debt. The US also said it would sanction the company in charge of operating the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline.Yesterday, Ukraine declared a state of national emergency, while neighboring countries are preparing for a possible migration wave. About 5 million people could be displaced, according to U.S. data.Russia also said on Wednesday that separatist regions in eastern Ukraine asked it for military backing. Hours later, Reuters reported that five explosions were heard in the city of Donetsk, while the U.S. said a full-scale invasion was imminent.10:40 p.m.: Border unit in Zaporizhia region targeted by missile attackTheUkrainian border post in Zaporizhzhya region was hit by a missile attack, the Ukrainian Border Guard Service reported. The attack, according to the announcement, occurred at 4:25 a. m. (Ukrainian local time) at the Primorsky Pasad border unit in the Zaporizhia region. According to reports, there are dead and wounded.\par \par \uc2 10:33 p.m.: Videos show explosion south of KievAlarge explosion occurred in the sky over the southern outskirts of Kiev, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports. Witnesses filmed burning debris falling over parts of the city. The videos appeared to show at least two surface-to-air missiles fired from the Ukrainian capital before the explosion.\par \par \uc2 10:14 p.m.: Russian plane shot down over KievTheUkrainian Interior Ministry said a Russian plane was shot down over Kiev. As read in The Guardian, \uc2 Anton Gerashchenko\uc2  , an adviser to Ukraine's interior minister, added a series of photos to his Telegram account, including what he said was a nine-story residential building on fire.\par \par \uc2 10:10 p.m.: Chinese embassy in Ukraine seeks to evacuate its citizensThediplomatic entity is organizing charter flights for Chinese citizens seeking to evacuate. There are about 6,000 Chinese citizens in Ukraine. The announcement came after a \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry\uc2  spokeswoman refused to call Russia's attack on Ukraine an "invasion," reports \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 9:57 p.m.: Loud explosions heard in Ukraine's capitalTwoloud explosions rang out early Friday (local Ukrainian time) in central Kiev on the second day of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, an AFP reporter noted\par \par \uc2 ."Attacks on Kiev with cruise or ballistic missiles resumed. I heard two loud explosions," Ukrainian Deputy Interior Minister Anton Herashchenko said on his Telegram account.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine's armed forces confirmed that additional weapons are being brought to Kiev, amid reports of explosions in the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 The press that is in Ukraine also reported that the explosions were heard. This was reported by \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reporter Emma Graham Harrison.\par \par \uc2 9:00 p.m.: The \uc2 European Union\uc2  announces the approval of another package of "massive and targeted sanctions" against RussiaThiswasannounced by the President of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2 , who assured, via \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , that "the approved package shows how united the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is". This package aims to affect the following areas:\par \par \uc2 Financial sanctions, targeting 70% of the Russian banking market and key state-owned enterprises, including in defense.Energy sector, a key economic area that particularly benefits the Russian state. The export ban will affect the oil sector, making it impossible for Russia to upgrade its refineries.Aviation, as the sale of aircraft and equipment to Russian airlines is prohibited. In addition, Russia's access to crucial technology, such as semiconductors or state-of-the-art software, is being limited.Visas: diplomats and related groups and businessmen will no longer have privileged access to the \uc2 European Union\uc2 ."8:18 p. m.: New Zealand, Russia and Japan announce sanctions againstRussiaAustralia\par \par \uc2 's Prime Minister \uc2 Scott Morrison\uc2  said he will impose more sanctions on the Russians. "Australia will impose further sanctions on the oligarchs, whose economic clout is of strategic importance in masking members of the Russian parliament who voted to authorize the use of Russian troops in Ukraine to invade it. We are also working with the United States to align with its new sanctions on television commercials, which are key accomplices in the aggressions."\par \par \uc2 For her part, New Zealand Prime Minister \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2  called for the immediate withdrawal of Russian forces in Ukraine, announced a travel ban for Russian officials and added that she is considering expelling the Russian ambassador. "New Zealand calls on Russia to do the right thing and immediately cease military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, and withdraw permanently to avoid catastrophic and pointless loss of innocent lives," she said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japan said it plans to limit semiconductor exports to Russia, while freezing the assets of some individuals and groups linked to Russia, and will stop issuing visas to certain Russians.\par \par \uc2 8:00 p.m.: Russian forces close in on Kiev, Ukrainian capitalAccording toThe \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , by the end of the first day of the invasion, Russian special forces and airborne troops were advancing toward the outskirts of Kiev. While it is not known exactly what Putin's ultimate goal is, U.S. officials believe that it may be to impose the replacement of President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 's government. Meanwhile, Ukrainians continue to move toward the western side of the country.\par \par \uc2 6:50 p.m.: WhiteHouse White Housepress secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  told a news conference that there are credible reports that personnel\par \par \uc2 at theChernobylfacility are being held hostage.\par \par \uc2 This illegal and dangerous hostage-taking, which could disrupt the routine civil service efforts needed to maintain and protect the waste facilities nu6\par \par \uc2 :00 p. .m: Volodimir Zelenski, president of Ukraine, gives a report on those killed in the Russian invasion"137 heroes, our citizens," lost their lives, Zelenski said in a video posted on the government's website, adding that 316 other Ukrainians were injured during the clashes\par \par \uc2 .In his remarks, he added: "I don't see anyone" to fight alongside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 5:39 p.m.: Ukraine decrees general mobilization over Russia'sinvasionThrough the official account of the General Staff of the Armed Forces of Ukraine it was made known that in connection "with the military aggression of the Russian Federation against Ukraine, and in order to ensure the defense of the state, maintain combat readiness and mobilization of the Armed Forces of Ukraine and other military formations, a general mobilization is announced."\par \par \uc2 The decree published on the website of Volodimir Zelenski, president of Ukraine, says the measure will affect persons subject to "compulsory military service and reservists" and will be in effect for 90 days in all regions of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 5:00 p.m.: Ukrainians take shelter, as aggressions continueTheGuardian reports that aggressions continue in Ukraine. For example, it is known that throughout the night (local time) a battle took place in the Ukrainian city of Sumy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, images of residents taking shelter in Kiev and Kharkiv metro stations are circulating on social networks, as reported by Beno\u238\'00\'EEt Vitkine, a reporter for the French newspaper Le Monde.\par \par \uc2 4:30 p.m.: \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  to vote on condemnation of RussiaTheUN resolution, which is in the hands of the 15-member Security Council, would condemn, "in the strongest possible terms, Russia's aggression, invasion and violation of Ukraine's sovereignty," a U.S. official said. It also "reaffirms Ukraine's sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity. And it requires the Russian Federation to withdraw its forces immediately, completely and unconditionally." A European diplomat at the \uc2 UN\uc2  also said the resolution would declare that "Russia can no longer encroach on its neighbors."\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reads that the resolution is likely to have more symbolic, than real, power, but the U.S. and its allies have said that the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and the council have a responsibility to act, and that a Russian veto would only demonstrate the country's isolation.\par \par \uc2 4:15 p.m.: \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  talks with Vladimir PutinTheFrench president spoke with his Russian counterpart, the Kremlin reported. The two had "a serious and frank exchange" on Ukraine, and Putin "gave a thorough explanation of the reasons and circumstances" surrounding the invasion, the statement issued from Moscow read.\par \par \uc2 "After speaking with the Ukrainian president, and in coordination with him, Macron called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to demand an immediate end to the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the French leader's office reported. In that dialogue, Macron reminded Putin that Russia was now exposed to "massive sanctions" by the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Macron is the fourth leader with whom \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has met after the attack on Ukraine, as in previous hours the Russian leader held meetings with the Prime Minister of Pakistan and with the leaders of Iran and India.\par \par \uc2 4:00 p.m.: \uc2 UN\uc2  warns of Ukrainians fleeing after invasionAbout100,000 people fled their homes in Ukraine and thousands sought refuge abroad after the Russian invasion, the \uc2 UN\uc2  said. "We think about 100,000 people fled their homes already and may have been displaced inside the country and that several thousand crossed international borders," Shabia Mantoo, a spokesman for the \uc2 UN High Commissioner for Refugees\uc2  (\uc2 UNHCR\uc2 ), told AFP.\par \par \uc2 3:47 p.m.: Explosions and a preliminary toll of wounded and dead reported from Ukraine"The Ukrainian city of Mariupol is under heavy fire with reports of hundreds of explosions. The city is one of the largest Ukrainian ports on the Sea of Azov. Taking Mariupol would help Russia secure a direct land route to Crimea, the Ukrainian peninsula annexed by Russia in 2014," so reports Kyiv Post on social media.\par \par \uc2 Journalists also reported additional explosions from the direction of Ukraine's Hostomel airport. "Big explosion from the direction of Hostomel airport just now. Bigger than the previous ones. One explosion, not several," reported reporter Jane Lytvynenko.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian Health Minister Oleh Lyashko reported that 57 people were killed and 169 injured. In addition, the deputy defense minister added that heavy shelling is still going on in the eastern Donetsk region.\par \par \uc2 3:28 p.m.: \uc2 European Union\uc2  warns of "massive and severe" sanctions against RussiaTheleaders of the 27 countries of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  agreed that "sanctions will cover Russia's financial, energy and transport sectors", according to the Declaration issued after the summit held in Brussels. The measures, which are not yet known in detail, contemplate export controls, visa issuance and the inclusion of Russian officials on the sanctioned list.\par \par \uc2 3:00 p.m.: \uc2 IMF\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  warn about the consequences of the war in UkraineClarifyingthat they will offer assistance to Ukraine, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  (\uc2 WB\uc2 ) warned about the repercussions that the Russian aggression in Ukraine may have on the global economy\par \par \uc2 .IMF\uc2  head Kristalina Georgieva said she was "deeply concerned" about the consequences of the fighting in Ukraine and warned in a tweet that the conflict "adds significant economic risk for the region and the world." As read by AFP, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is in the process of disbursing $2.2 billion in assistance to Ukraine under a loan program that will end in June.\par \par \uc2 For his part, David Malpass, president of the \uc2 WB\uc2 , noted that "the institution "is preparing options for major support to the people of Ukraine and the region, including immediate budget support."\par \par \uc2 2:52 p. m.: The \uc2 Inter-American Court of Human Rights\uc2  speaks of the central role of international lawThisis the call that the entity made from social networks: "The role of public international law is central and today's events demonstrate it. I make a special call to solve this conflict based on the law and the application of basic principles, such as that a State must respect the sovereignty of another."\par \par \uc2 2:00 p.m.: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ends his official statement after Russian aggression in UkraineAssertingthat what is happening today is the greatest threat to Europe and the world, and reaffirming that Ukraine has been an independent country for thirty years, the U.S. president said that the sanctions interposed by the allies show the union of almost two thirds of the countries in the world. "We are not doing this alone. We represent half of the world's economy."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. president also added that, in coordination with his allies, "we will limit Russian business in dollars, pounds and euros. We are attacking their finance, technology and industrial capabilities. The sanctions, he said, are intended to have "severe consequences" on the Russian economy in the long term.\par \par \uc2 According to Reuters, Biden assured that Putin would be a pariah on the international stage and that his actions would cost Russian civilians dearly.\par \par \uc2 Biden added: "Putin has much bigger ambitions than Ukraine. He wants, in fact, to re-establish the old Soviet Union. That's what this is all about and I think his ambitions are completely contrary to where the rest of the world has come."\par \par \uc2 1:31 p.m.: Germany says Putin "will try to wipe Ukraine off the map "\uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , Germany's chancellor, said that "we are witnessing the start of a war of a magnitude that we have not seen in Europe in more than 75 years" since the end of World War II. "This is an attempt to change with the use of force the borders in Europe, perhaps even to wipe an entire country off the map."\par \par \uc2 12:52 p.m.: Russians call first day of invasion a "success"All \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  assigned to the groups of troops of the Armed Forces of the Russian Federation for the day were successfully completed," said Defense Ministry spokesman Igor Konashenkov\par \par \uc2 .Thus, Russia said its military achieved its objectives on the first day of the invasion of Ukraine, despite warnings of harsh sanctions from Western countries.\par \par \uc2 12:35 p.m.: Russian forces reportedly seized Chernobyl"After heavy fighting in the Chernobyl area, Russian troops took control of the nuclear power plant in\par \par \uc2 the\par \par \uc2 area.trying to take Chernobyl. Our soldiers are sacrificing their lives so that the tragedy of 1986 will not happen again. This is a declaration of war against the whole of Europe".12:15 p. ...: Ukraine announced a toll of dead and wounded in the south of the country.At least 13 Ukrainian civilians and nine Ukrainian servicemen were killed on Thursday in the Kherson region (southern Ukraine), which is currently under partial control of Russian troops who invaded the country, regional authorities reported.\par \par \uc2 "Thirteen civilians, including two children, were killed and nine soldiers were killed in the attack on the Kherson region," the local government specified on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . The town of Guenichesk was seized by the Russians, it added.\par \par \uc2 12:10 p.m.: \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  unveils new package of sanctions against RussiaThe prime minister said the UK is announcing the biggest sanctions package Russia has ever seen, which includes the following measures:\par \par \uc2 Russian banks will be excluded from the UK financial system.Russian state-owned and private companies will be banned from raising money in the UK.The amount of money Russians can hold in British bank accounts will be limited.Sanctions will also apply to Belarus.Aeroflot will be banned in the UK.Export controls, similar to those imposed by the United States, will be implemented, meaning a ban on items such as semiconductors and other high-tech equipment.The export of dual-use items to Russia, which could eventually be used militarily, will also be banned.Before the House of Commons, Johnson described Putin as a "bloodied aggressor who believes in imperial conquest, always determined to attack his neighbor, no matter what we did." He added that "Putin will stand condemned in the eyes of the world and in the eyes of history. He will never be able to wipe the blood of Ukraine from his hands."\par \par \uc2 12:00 p.m.: Pakistan Prime Minister Imran Khan meets Putin.He is the first leader to meet with the Russian president after the attack on Ukraine. Khan arrived in Moscow yesterday on a two-day trip, amid rising tensions between the two countries, but when the military action had not yet taken place.\par \par \uc2 Some read this visit as "ill-timed". As read in The Guardian, Michael Kugelman of the Wilson Center in the United States said: "There is a risk that Khan's visit to Moscow will be perceived by many in the West as an indirect endorsement of Putin's decree on eastern Ukraine, a move that much of the world has condemned and that Pakistan itself is unlikely to support. Although Khan's visit was planned weeks ago and focuses on bilateral relations, the timing is striking and presents some problems of optics."\par \par \uc2 11:42 a.m.: \uc2 G7\uc2  ready to act if invasion causes "disruption" to energy supplies"We are closely monitoring global oil and gas market conditions," the \uc2 G7\uc2  said in a statement following a video conference to discuss the crisis sparked by the invasion.\par \par \uc2 The group said it supports "consistent and constructive engagements and coordination between major energy producers and consumers in the collective interest of a stable global energy supply."\par \par \uc2 11:27 a.m.: 'This is a threat to the global order': G7The \uc2 G7\uc2 , which groups advanced economies, on Thursday condemned the Russian invasion of Ukraine and assured that the attack constitutes "a serious threat to the international order, with ramifications beyond Europe." Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  "has placed himself on the wrong side of history," the \uc2 G7\uc2  said in a statement following a meeting to discuss the crisis.\par \par \uc2 11:22 a.m.: Russian forces seize a military airport near Kiev.Russian forces seized Thursday a military airport about forty kilometers from Kiev, the capital of Ukraine, said Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 , who assured that the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover it was already underway.\par \par \uc2 According to Zelenski, who was speaking in a video address, the incursion of Russian paratroopers in Gostomel, where the airport is located, was "blocked" and the Ukrainian forces "received the order to annihilate them".\par \par \uc2 10:20 a.m.: Curfew declared in KievKiev Mayor Vitaly Klitschko announced Thursday a curfew to protect the safety of the capital's residents. "The curfew will last from 10 p.m. to 7:00 a.m.\u8243\'20\'33, he added in a statement, specifying that public transport will not operate at those times but that metro stations will remain open to serve as a shelter in case of attacks.\par \par \uc2 10:09 a.m.: Fighting at Ukraine's Chernobyl power stationUkrainian authorities reported fighting near the nuclear waste repository.\par \par \uc2 According to an advisor to the \uc2 Ukrainian Interior Ministry\uc2 , Anton Guerashtshenko, the "occupants' troops entered from Belarus into the area of the Chernobyl power plant." "Members of the National Guard protecting the depot put up stubborn resistance," he said on the Telegram messaging network.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 9:55 a.m.: Biden to meet with G7.U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will brief the United States on the situation in Ukraine and next steps. According to him, this crisis will cause a "catastrophic loss of lives".\par \par \uc2 In a few moments, they will hold a virtual meeting with the leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2  (United Kingdom, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States). Prior to the appointment, Biden "convened a meeting of the National Security Council (...) to address the latest developments in Ukraine," the White House reported.\par \par \uc2 "The United States and its allies and partners will respond in a united and determined manner. The world will hold Russia accountable," Biden said in his first, after Russian missiles began falling on Ukraine. Biden also spoke by phone with his Ukrainian counterpart, Volodimir Zelenski, and said he had pledged to "provide support and assistance to Ukraine and the Ukrainian people."\par \par \uc2 9:28 a.m.: Fighting erupts at military base near KievThe Ukrainian military announced Thursday that there was fighting with Russian troops near Kiev for control of the Gostomel military airport.\par \par \uc2 "There is ongoing fighting for control of the Gostomel airport," located a few kilometers northwest of Kiev, Valery Zaloujnyi, speaking on behalf of the Ukrainian armed forces, announced in a message on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . AFP reporters saw helicopters flying at low altitude towards the city from the north.\par \par \uc2 8:46 a.m.: Russian authorities threaten those taking part in protests.The \uc2 Russian Interior Ministry\uc2 , the Prosecutor's Office and the \uc2 Russian Investigative Committee\uc2  warned their population against any protest actions. The Committee noted that people who take part in rallies about "the tense situation in foreign policy" or in clashes face prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Journalist Kevin Rothrock tweeted some images of the protests in Moscow. "They are arrested almost immediately, often as they leave their homes," Rothrock wrote.\par \par \uc2 8:32 a.m.: Four missiles were fired from Belarus.The Armed Forces of Ukraine reported that four ballistic missiles were fired from the territory of Belarus in the southwestern direction.\par \par \uc2 7:53 a.m.: 'Russia is invading on multiple fronts': former Ukrainian ministerAndriy Zagorodnyuk, Ukraine's former defense minister, told \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  that there are ground invasions on several fronts in the country. "They are quite close to the city of Kherson now. The situation is really critical there. They are also trying to surround Kharkiv. It seems that Kharkiv at the moment is the main target. They are also trying to get to Kiev using a battlefield group. The group left Belarus and was stopped," Zagorodnyuk commented.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 7:33 a.m.: Ukrainian military plane crashes near KievA Ukrainian military plane crashed Thursday near Kiev with 14 people on board, the state emergency situations service announced. The plane went down near the village of Trypillia, about 50 kilometers south of the Ukrainian capital, according to the same source.\par \par \uc2 ???????? 6:55 a.m. 6:55 a.m. Russian forces arrive in the Kiev region: border guardsRussian troops crossed the Vilcha border point in the Kiev region, 50 kilometers from the border with Belarus, the Ukrainian Border Guard Service reported today. "Russian troops violated the state border. The enemy's war equipment crossed through the Vilcha border point, about 150 kilometers north of the capital," the entity reported according to which the Ukrainian Army planted battle with the Russian Army.\par \par \uc2 6:39 a.m.: \uc2 NATO\uc2  activates defense plansThe \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  countries agreed to activate their defense plans, following Russia's offensive in Ukraine, announced Thursday the secretary general of the military alliance, \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 . During a press conference in Brussels, the official also confirmed that the transatlantic military alliance will hold a leaders' summit on Friday via videoconference in the wake of the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 6:29 a.m.: Multiple explosions were heard in Kiev."Confirmed by Ukrainian authorities. A large air assault \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Mi-8 helicopters at Antonov International Airport in Hostomel. Interior Ministry says Russia has taken control. Very dangerous; it's just 15 minutes west of a highway from the capital," wrote \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  correspondent Christioher Miller.\par \par \uc2 6:10 a.m.: Ukraine asks Turkey to close Russian access to the BosphorusUkraine asked Turkey to close the Bosphorus straits to Russian warships, which use the passages to enter the Black Sea, \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  reported. "We conveyed our official request to the Turkish side regarding the closure of the airspace, the Dardanelles and of the Bosporus for Russian ships," Ukraine's ambassador to Ankara, Vasyl Bodnar, said, according to Turkish media Hurriyet. Bodnar added that Ukraine is also asking Turkey to sanction Russian businesses.\par \par \uc2 ???? 5:30 a.m.: \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies invoke Article 4."Today we have held consultations under Article 4 of the Washington Treaty. We have decided, in accordance with our defensive planning to protect all Allies, to take additional measures to further strengthen deterrence and defense throughout the Alliance. Our measures are and will remain pre-emptive, proportionate, but not escalatory," the North Atlantic Council said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 4:59 a.m.: Separatists in eastern Ukraine are taking control of territories: RussiaThe Russian military announced Thursday that pro-Russian separatists in eastern Ukraine were making territorial gains against the Ukrainian army.\par \par \uc2 General Igor Konashenkov, a spokesman for the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2 , said in television remarks that the rebels advanced three kilometers in the Donetsk region and one and a half kilometers in Lugansk, and assured that civilians had "nothing to fear" because Russia has military targets.\par \par \uc2 4:46 a.m.: "At least five Ukrainian servicemen die in Russian invasion": Ukrainian governmentThe official number of Ukrainian servicemen killed in the Russian "military action" that began on Thursday rose to five, the Interior Ministry said. One air defense serviceman died in the Zaporiyia region (south), while four border guards were killed in several localities of the Kherson region (south), the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 ???????????? 4:40 a.m.: Ukraine breaks off diplomatic relations with Russia."We have severed diplomatic relations with Russia. For all those who have not yet lost their conscience in Russia, it is time to come out to protest against the war with Ukraine," President \uc2 Vladimir Zelensky\uc2  maintained on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 4:17 a.m.: Ruble plunges to record lowsRussia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bonds and stocks plunged, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years, Reuters reported. "To stabilize the situation on the financial market, the Bank of Russia decided to initiate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," the bank said Thursday on its \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 4:16 a.m.: Ukraine claims 50 Russians killed on eastern front.Ukraine's military announced Thursday that at least 50 Russian occupants in the Lugansk region were killed in the first hours "of the Russian invasion of the country."\par \par \uc2 "On February 24, about 50 Russian occupants were liquidated near the locality of Shchastia in eastern Ukraine," the army's General Staff said in a statement. Ukrainians use the term "Russian occupier" both to refer to a Russian soldier and to pro-Russian separatist fighters in the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 2:24 a.m.: \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , figurehead of diplomacy in this conflict, speaks out.The president, who wanted to assume the role of mediator between Russia and Ukraine, assured that "France strongly condemns Russia's decision to declare war on Ukraine. Russia must put an end to its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately. France stands in solidarity with Ukraine . It supports the Ukrainians and works with its partners and allies to end the war."\par \par \uc2 2:00 a.m.: Japanese prime minister speaks on repercussions of Russian attack on world order.Fumio Kishida said that "the Russian invasion shakes the foundations of the international order, which does not allow unilateral attacks to upset the status quo. We strongly condemn Russia. We will coordinate efforts with the international community and react quickly."\par \par \uc2 1:46 a.m.: \uc2 UN\uc2  chief asks Putin to stop conflict in the name of "humanity."As read by AFP, at the end of the emergency meeting of the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 , which coincided with Putin's announcement of the start of a military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of his troops in Ukraine, \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2 , \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary general, said that it is "the saddest day" he has had at the head of the organization.\par \par \uc2 "President Putin, in the name of humanity, bring the troops back to Russia. In the name of humanity, don't let what could be the worst war since the turn of the century start in Europe. Stop it now," he implored.\par \par \uc2 1:15 a.m.: Ukraine reports that five Russian planes and a helicopter were shot down.Via \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , the \uc2 Ukrainian Defense Ministry\uc2  reported that it shot down five Russian aircraft and one helicopter. The message reads, "According to the Joint Forces Command, today, February 24, five planes and one helicopter of the aggressor were shot down. The Joint Forces give a worthy rebuff to the Armed Forces of the Russian Federation. The military units are in their positions. The enemy is suffering losses. Keep calm and believe in the Ukrainian defenders - together we will win, glory to Ukraine!".\par \par \uc2 Now, the Russian military denied that one of its aircraft was shot down in the fighting over Ukraine during the morning. Meanwhile, as read by AFP, Russia claimed it destroyed Ukrainian air bases and anti-aircraft defense.\par \par \uc2 1:04 a.m.: Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski imposes martial law in the country.Ukrainian President Volodimir Zelenski imposed martial law in his country after Russia launched its military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country. Although he did not go into details, he suggests that military forces will have extraordinary powers in this situation of extreme alarm.\par \par \uc2 12:40 a.m.: Ukraine says it was attacked from Belarus."At around 5:00 a.m., Ukraine's state border in the area with the Russian Federation and the Republic of Belarus was attacked by Russian troops supported by Belarus," Ukraine's state border service said.\par \par \uc2 The entity added that attacks are being carried out in Luhansk, Sumy, Kharkiv, Chernihiv and Zhytomyr regions, areas on the eastern and northern borders of Ukraine. The attacks are targeting border units, border patrols and checkpoints with artillery, heavy equipment and small arms, the border service said. "In addition, the attack takes place from the side of the Autonomous Republic of Crimea," the service said.\par \par \uc2 In addition, CNN reads, the U.S. Defense Department is tracking the reported incursion of Belarusian troops into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 12:32 a.m.: Ukrainian and U.S. presidents speak by phone.Via \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Biden wrote: "President Zelenskyi reached out to me tonight and we just finished talking. I condemned this unprovoked and unjustified attack by Russian military forces. I briefed him on the steps we are taking to bring international condemnation, including tonight at the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In aWhite House statement reads, "The President of Ukraine asked me to call on world leaders to speak out clearly against President Putin's blatant aggression and to stand with the people of Ukraine. Tomorrow I will meet with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders and with our allies and partners we will impose severe sanctions on Russia. We will continue to provide support and assistance to Ukraine and the Ukrainian people."\par \par \uc2 12:16 a.m.: European leaders speak out.UK Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  spoke out in the face of the most recent events in Ukraine. Via \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he wrote: "I am appalled by the horrific events in Ukraine and have spoken to President Zelenskiy to discuss next steps. President Putin has chosen a path of bloodshed and destruction by launching this unprovoked attack on Ukraine. The UK and our allies will respond decisively."\par \par \uc2 For its part, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  said it will "hold Moscow accountable" for what commission chief \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  said was an "unjustified" attack on Ukraine . "In these dark hours, our thoughts are with Ukraine and with the innocent women, men and children facing this unprovoked attack and fearing for their lives," she said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . "We will hold the Kremlin accountable," he added.\par \par \uc2 ???????? Thursday, February 24 ????????12:00 a.m.: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  is issuing a speech.Zelenskiy said Russia has carried out missile strikes against Ukraine's infrastructure and Ukrainian border guards, with explosions heard in many cities across the country. He added that he spoke to the U.S. president and told Ukrainians to stay at home and remain calm: "The military is working. I will be with you all the time. Stay strong. We will win because we are Ukraine. Glory to Ukraine." According to Reuters, the prime minister assured that he will introduce martial law in all territories of the state.\par \par \uc2 11:45 p.m.: Ukrainian officials warn that Russian troops are landing in Odessa and Mariupol.Ukrainian officials say Russian troops have landed in Odessa while others are crossing the border into Kharkiv. \uc2 Ukraine's Interior Ministry\uc2  made the announcement on Telegram and added that the rocket attacks are targeting Ukrainian fighter jets at an airport outside Kiev. Ukraine's state emergency service says attacks were launched against 10 Ukrainian regions, mainly in the east and south of the country.\par \par \uc2 11:12 p.m.: Ukraine confirms Russia's "full-scale invasion".Ukrainian Foreign Minister \uc2 Dmytro Kuleba\uc2  confirmed the Russian "full-scale invasion" of Ukraine. "Putin has just launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine. Peaceful Ukrainian cities are on strike. This is a war of aggression. Ukraine will fight back and win. The world can and must stop Putin. The time to act is now."\par \par \uc2 This pronouncement was echoed by Ukrainian Deputy Interior Minister \uc2 Anton Gerashchenko\uc2 , who said via \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that "the invasion has begun. There have just been missiles at the military headquarters, airports, military warehouses, near Kyiv, Kharkiv, Dnieper. The shooting at the border is underway. As of this day, there is a new geopolitical reality in the world."\par \par \uc2 11:03 p.m.: At the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 , Ukraine and Russia exchange messages.Russia's representative to the \uc2 United Nations\uc2 , \uc2 Vasily Nebenzya\uc2 , justified Russia's military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Article 51 of the \uc2 UN\uc2  charter, which allows for "self-defense." The Ukrainian ambassador to the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  responded that the Russian president declared "a war against my country."\par \par \uc2 10:53 p.m.: "Putin chose a premeditated war": Biden on military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in UkraineU.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was quick to react to the "military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ordered by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in eastern Ukraine. According to the President, the Russian military forces have launched an "unprovoked and unjustified" attack which is being suffered by the Ukrainian people. He went on to say that with \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies he would "coordinate a strong and united response to deter any aggression against the alliance".\par \par \uc2 In addition, Biden assured that he will monitor the situation and that early tomorrow morning he will meet with his \uc2 G7\uc2  counterparts. He will then address the American people to announce the "future consequences that the United States and its allies will impose on Russia for this unnecessary act of aggression against Ukraine and global peace and security."\par \par \uc2 10:37 p.m.: Reports of explosions in Ukrainian cities after Putin's announcementShortly before the president's announcement, some Ukrainian cities began reporting explosions.\par \par \uc2 Around 4:00 a.m. Ukrainian time (9 p.m. Colombian time), explosions were reported in the city of Mariupol, in the east of the country.Around 5:00 a. m. (10 p. m. Colombian time), reports came from Kramatorsk.The explosions in Kramotorsk followed reports of bombs in Kharkiv, the country's second largest city.Explosions were also reported in Kiev, according to CNN's team in the area, but no further information has been received on this case.Odessa, a city in the east of the country, was the last city to report explosions.We will continue to report on reports of more explosions on Ukrainian territory as more information comes in.\par \par \uc2 10:00 p.m.: Putin announced "military action" in DonbasAccording to information read in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , in an address to the Russian people now taking place at the same time as the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  meeting is taking place, the Russian president said that:\par \par \uc2 Clashes between Ukrainian and Russian forces are "inevitable" and it was "only a matter of time."Further expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and its use of Ukrainian territory is unacceptable.The Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at "protecting people".Circumstances "demand decisive action by Russia."Putin is still speaking and what is known from his speech is that he wants to "demilitarize and denazify" Ukraine. He further warned that "in case of foreign interference, Russia will act immediately" and that the responsibility for the bloodshed will be in the hands of the "Ukrainian regime."\par \par \uc2 9:44 p.m.: \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2  spoke at the ongoing meeting of the \uc2 UN Security Council.At\uc2  the emergency meeting being advanced by the \uc2 UN\uc2  body, the Secretary General said, "If in fact an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being prepared, I have only one thing to say: Putin stop your troops from attacking Ukraine. Give peace a chance, too many people have already died."\par \par \uc2 In addition, Albania's representative, Ferit Hoxha, called the situation "senseless madness." "Russian wars have nothing to do with your security. This is a confrontation between Russia and international law," he added.\par \par \uc2 9:20 p.m.: Ukrainian airspace closed.Flights of civilian aircraft through Ukrainian airspace were suspended after a notice to airmen (Notam) was issued. Earlier, airports in the cities of Dnipro, Kharkiv and Zaporizhzhia were closed.\par \par \uc2 8:05 p. m.: Blinken expects 'full invasion' before the night is overU.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said he expects Russia to invade Ukraine before the end of the night [Eastern time], but still sees the possibility of "avoiding major aggression." Speaking to NBC News, he asserted, "Everything seems to be in place for Russia to engage in major aggression against Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Blinken also claimed to ABC News that Russia is "putting the finishing touches" on having forces in place for an "all-out invasion" and said the U.S. "has certainly seen the presence of Russian forces inside Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 7:37 p.m.: Five explosions reported in eastern UkraineAt least five explosions were heard in the city of Donetsk, a witness told Reuters. According to what is read in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 , after the explosions, four military trucks could be seen driving to the site. Earlier, the Kremlin said that two separatist regions in Ukraine had asked for Russia's help in responding to the Ukrainian army's "aggression."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the United States said a full-scale invasion is imminent and that Russian troops and separatist forces surrounding the country are in combat-ready positions. Pentagon spokesman John F Kirby assured reporters that "Russian forces continue to gather closer to the border. They are ready to go. They could strike at any time with significant military force."\par \par \uc2 7:20 p.m.: Press reports new cyber attack in Ukraine.Ukrainian media report that at least six government websites are down, given an alleged cyber attack. "This attack also hit the websites of the ministries of Infrastructure, \uc2 Strategic Industries\uc2  and Education," the Kyiv Independent newspaper reported. According to the publication, the websites of the Cabinet of Ministers, the ministries of Foreign Affairs and Agriculture, as well as that of the security service are down.\par \par \uc2 6:56 p.m.: \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  said that Russia had approved an offensive against Ukraine.The Ukrainian president finished a 10-minute speech in which he also assured that his Russian counterpart, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , had not responded to his invitation for talks. This was reported by Reuters when translating the speech Zelenskiy gave in Ukrainian and Russian.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president also warned that any outburst from Russia could "burn everything" and create a catastrophic crisis. Speaking in Russian, he asked Russians not to believe the narratives about Ukraine presented on the neighboring country's state television.\par \par \uc2 "I started a phone call with the president of the Russian Federation. Result: silence. This step could be the beginning of a big war on the European continent. The whole world is talking about what could happen at any moment. Any provocation. Any outburst, one that could burn everything."\par \par \uc2 5:50 p. .m.: Russian mercenaries are seeping into separatist-controlled region, European officials sayEuropean officials claimed that about 300 mercenaries, from the Russian paramilitary group Wagner, arrived in Donetsk and Lugansk. Intelligence services tracked them from Libya and Syria until they reached Crimea, from where they left for the separatist regions, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reads.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine requested a meeting with the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 5:00 p.m.: Ukrainian soldiers prepare in case of a Russian attack.According to reports by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Ukrainian troops stationed in separatist areas in the east of the country are ready in case a battle with Russia breaks out. In Zaitseve, in the Russian-backed separatist region of Lugansk, soldiers held their positions amid explosions felt on Wednesday. \uc2 Oleksiy Danilov\uc2 , head of Ukraine's National Defense and Security Council, said, "Our army is ready" to defend against an attack.\par \par \uc2 3:50 p.m.: Leaders of separatist regions ask Russia for help.According to information from Ifax, citing Russian government spokesman Dmitry Peskov, the leaders of two separatist regions in eastern Ukraine have asked Russia for help in the face of aggression by the Ukrainian army. Many experts point to these requests as the latest (possibly final) pretext Russia could use to launch an all-out attack on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 As read in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the Kremlin said Putin had received a request for "help in repelling aggression by Ukrainian armed forces and formations." Russian media have claimed that Ukraine is about to launch an offensive against Russian-backed separatist territories in eastern Ukraine, something the Kiev government denies it intends to do.\par \par \uc2 2:55 p.m.: Ukraine imposes a state of national emergency.Ukraine's parliament has just voted to approve a state of emergency, which will begin on February 24 and last for 30 days, with the possibility of extension. According to Ukrainian press reports, such as Kyiv Independent, the state of emergency may provide for:\par \par \uc2 increased protection and security of public ordercontrols of civilian identification documentsban on proteststemporary or permanent evacuation of people from dangerous areas and their accommodationprohibition of relocation of conscripts and reservists without prior noticeprohibition of producing and disseminating information that could "destabilize the situation".prohibition of amateur radio transmitting devices.It may also include:\par \par \uc2 curfewa special entry and exit regimeban on mass events"special rules" for the dissemination of online information.2:53 p.m.: Neighboring countries and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  prepare for a possible wave of migrantsSeveral European countries, along with stationed U.S. military personnel, are preparing to house more than 1 million refugees from Ukraine if Russia continues the invasion, the Washington Post reports.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Ambassador to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . \uc2 Linda Thomas-Greenfield\uc2  warned that the conflict could spark "a new refugee crisis" with "as many as 5 million" displaced people.\par \par \uc2 2:40 p.m.: Pentagon says Russian troops are prepared for an attack that could be imminentAs read in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the Pentagon said that 80% of the 190,000 Russian troops and separatist forces in or near Ukraine are now in combat-ready positions, and that a full-scale military assault is very likely imminent. "They are ready to go now if they get the order to do so," a senior Defense Department official told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials say Russian forces have been preparing for months for a large-scale attack and are now in a position to carry it out. That attack would likely include missile salvoes, air strikes, special forces \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an amphibious assault and ground troops, and could begin at any time, one official told the U.S. newspaper.\par \par \uc2 2:00 p.m.: Ukrainian conflict death toll risesThe town of Schastia, located on the line of contact with the separatist Luhansk People's Republic in Ukrainian-controlled territory, has been without power and water for several days after artillery cut off water and electricity supplies. Lines of people gather at ATMs and at the borders.\par \par \uc2 ???????????? 1:20 p. m.: Sanctions against \uc2 Nord Stream 2The\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration announced economic sanctions against the company building the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  gas pipeline. Why is this project so important to Putin?\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin needed approval from German regulators for this pipeline which, if \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would deliver 55 billion cubic meters of gas per year directly from Russia to \uc2 Europe.Gazprom\uc2 , the Russian state-owned company that owns the project, said its average export price was $280 per 1,000 cubic meters of gas in 2021, meaning the new pipeline could be worth more than $15 billion a year.The issue is key for the region, as with natural gas prices near an all-time high, many fear that further tension will result in serious repercussions to European consumers."The pipeline would allow Russia to send gas to Europe without going through Ukraine, meaning Moscow could put pressure on Kiev without the risk of Kiev cutting off the gas supply route in retaliation. Ukraine has lobbied furiously against the project, saying it undermines its national security," explained several Guardian reporters.1:18 p.m.: EU prepares to launch second package of sanctions against RussiaThe European Union warned that it is ready to issue more sanctions against Russia if that country's troops move beyond Ukraine's separatist regions. "If there are further Russian aggressions and further incursions into Ukrainian territory, we are ready to step up our response also in terms of sanctions," \uc2 European Commission\uc2  executive vice president \uc2 Valdis Dombrovskis\uc2  told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 12:58 p.m.: "Russia moved nearly 100% of its troops; readies for invasion": U.S. official."Russia has moved nearly 100 percent of forces to the location needed for an invasion, according to a senior U.S. defense official," Jack Detsch, a journalist for Foreign Policy, wrote on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 11:51 a.m.: "An invasion could displace 5 million people": US.U.S..A Russian invasion of Ukraine could generate a new refugee crisis, with up to five million people displaced, the U.S. ambassador to the Assam warned Wednesday????????????\par \par \uc2 11:03 a. m.: Ukraine suffers new cyberattackUkrainetoday suffered a new cyberattack, the third in a few weeks, against several banks and government and parliamentary websites, Digital Transformation Minister Mykhailo Fedorov reported today. "At approximately 4 p. m. (local time) began another massive DDoS (denial of service) attack on our state," he wrote on his official account on the Telegram social network.\par \par \uc2 11:00 a.m.: EU formally announces sanctions against RussiaTheCouncil of the European Union (EU), which brings together member states, on Wednesday formally adopted EU sanctions against Russia for recognizing the independence of Donetsk and Lugansk, which include a ban on senior Russian officials traveling to European territory, limit Moscow's access to European financial markets and contain a trade embargo on the separatist regions\par \par \uc2 .10:34 a.m.: Ukrainian soldier diesAUkrainian soldier was killed Wednesday in shelling near the front line with pro-Russian separatist territories in the east, the Armed Forces reported. The statement did not specify the location of the attack, merely noting that the soldier "suffered fatal wounds" and that another serviceman was wounded.\par \par \uc2 10:22 a.m.: EU convenes special summitThursdayEuropean Council President \uc2 Charles Michel\uc2  called for a special \uc2 European Union\uc2  summit in Brussels on Thursday to discuss the crisis between Ukraine and Russia, according to a letter released Wednesday\par \par \uc2 ."Russia's aggressive actions violate international law, and the territorial integrity and sovereignty of Ukraine. They also undermine European security," Michel said in the letter to the 27 heads of state and government invited to the summit.\par \par \uc2 9:00 a.m.: UK to send more weapons toUkraineBritish Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announced Wednesday that the UK will send more weapons to Ukraine for its defense. "In light of Russia's increasingly threatening behavior, and in line with our previous support, the UK will shortly provide an additional package of military support to Ukraine," Johnson said at Prime Minister's Questions in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 8:35 a.m.: Russia evacuates diplomatic staff from UkraineRussiaannounced Tuesday its intention to evacuate its diplomats from Ukraine, accusing local authorities of not doing enough to ensure their safety. This Wednesday, the Russian flag was no longer flying on the roof of the diplomatic building and several people were seen leaving the site with suitcases.\par \par \uc2 8:21 a.m.: Russia promises response after Western sanctions"Let there be no doubt: there will be a strong response to these sanctions, not necessarily symmetrical, but well-calculated and painful for the United States," the \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  said in a statement\par \par \uc2 .6:52 a.m.: \uc2 European Union\uc2  to adopt Russia sanctions todayDatafrom \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  shows that, if approved, the \uc2 EU\uc2  will impose travel bans and freeze the assets of 23 senior Russian officials. The bloc would also sanction 351 members of the Russian State Duma who voted in favor of recognizing the two territories. "\uc2 EU\uc2  officials are also drawing up measures to ban trade between the two Russian-controlled territories in eastern Ukraine," reads the British newspaper publication\par \par \uc2 .???????????? 6:02 a.m.: Ukraine readies for nationwide state of emergencyTheNational Security and Defense Council of Ukraine (NSCD) decided today to institute a state of emergency throughout the country in the face of Russian aggression, except in the Donetsk and Lugansk regions, which are already under a politico-military administration. The decision must now be ratified by the Verkhovna Rada, or Ukrainian Parliament, CSND Secretary Oleksiy Danylov told a press conference. "I will report it to the Rada today," Danilov said.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to this mechanism, the official explained, the authorities will be able to increase the protection of public order and strategic infrastructure, "limit traffic, intensify the verification of vehicles and documents of individuals," Danilov said. "These are preventive measures to preserve calm in the country, so that the economy continues to function," he added. The implementation of the state of emergency will not mostly affect the lives of Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 5:10 a.m.: Pope Francis speaks out on Ukraine"Despite the diplomatic efforts of recent weeks, we have before us an increasingly worrying situation," Francis said, following his weekly audience. "I ask all the parties involved to refrain from actions that could provoke even more suffering among the people," the pope added.\par \par \uc2 "I have great sorrow in my heart for the situation in Ukraine," the pope confessed, two days after Russia's recognition of the independence of pro-Russian separatist territories in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising fears of military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 4:49 a.m.: \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  announces mobilization of its reservistsTheUkrainian armed forces announced a plan to mobilize reservists starting Wednesday, due to fears of a military escalation with Russia. The mobilization covers reservists aged 18 to 60 for a maximum term of one year, the Ukrainian army specified on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Ukraine has about 200,000 reservists and 250,000 active military personnel in the armed\par \par \uc2 forces.???????????? 3:40 a.m.: Ukraine asks its citizens to leave RussiaTheUkrainian Foreign Ministry urged its citizens to leave Russia quickly, given the risk of a military offensive by Moscow. "The ministry recommends Ukrainian citizens to refrain from traveling to Russia and those who are already in Russia to immediately leave the territory," the ministry said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In developmentWith\par \par \uc2 information from Efe and Afp*????????????\par \par \uc2 Have you already heard about the latest news in the world? We invite you to check them out at El Espectador.\par \par \uc2 Receive alerts from GoogleNewsRelated topicsNewsthe worldThe world todayLatest news in the worldNews today