\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 How Russia has prepared to deal with Western sanctions and what real impact they may have on its economy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Reuters Russia has been preparing for this moment for years.\par \par \uc2 In 2014, when \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's troops entered Crimea, annexing part of Ukraine, a first round of international sanctions fell on the Kremlin. And that left Moscow with an important lesson.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Putin has developed better defense mechanisms, moving away from reliance on the dollar and trying to protect the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 However, a new package of restrictive measures were announced Tuesday by Western powers in reaction to Putin's decision to recognize as "independent republics" two pro-Russian territories in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is seen as a first step toward broader \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , in announcing a series of financial measures against Russia, considered that, with Putin's decision, the invasion of Ukraine had begun.\par \par \uc2 "Simply put, Russia has just announced that it is taking over a large part of Ukraine. In my opinion, it is establishing a reason to take more territory by force. This is the beginning of a Russian invasion of Ukraine," he said.\par \par \uc2 Biden indicated that he will hit with "full blockade sanctions" two large Russian financial institutions: a military bank (\uc2 Promsvyazbank\uc2 ) and VEB, the state bank in charge of supporting the development of the economy, managing state debt and pension funds.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. president also announced additional sanctions on Russia's sovereign debt, "which means that we are cutting off the Russian government from Western finances", he said.\par \par \uc2 Protesters in Berlin, Germany, called on Tuesday for sanctions against Russia. Getty Images Protesters in Berlin, Germany, called Tuesday for sanctions on Russia. "Russia will not be able to make money from the West and will not be able to trade its debt on our markets or on European markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 Previously, Berlin had announced the suspension of the approval process for the entry into \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  gas pipeline, which connects Germany and Russia, and the United Kingdom also advanced sanctions against Russian banks, politicians and entities.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2 , for its part, announced that it will sanction the 351 deputies of the Russian parliament who voted on Tuesday in favor of recognizing Donetsk and Luhansk as independent republics, as well as 27 individuals and legal entities.\par \par \uc2 As explained by the head of European diplomacy, \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , these are "individuals and entities that are playing a role in undermining or threatening the territorial integrity, sovereignty and independence of Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Both the U.S. and Europe promised more sanctions should Russia continue its escalation against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But how effective can these measures really be? Is Russia prepared to evade them?\par \par \uc2 A more prepared Russia Today's Russia is not economically the same Russia that annexed Crimea eight years ago\par \par \uc2 . Putin's government has over the years accumulated huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and trimmed its budgets to keep its economy and government services running, even under isolation.\par \par \uc2 It has thus reoriented trade and sought to replace Western imports in a way that makes it easier for it to evade sanctions.\par \par \uc2 To the point that, according to analysts, the Russian president is able to show that he can withstand sanctions for longer than the West assumes.\par \par \uc2 Russian armored vehicles in Russia's Rostov region near the border with Ukraine EPA Russian armored vehicles in Russia's Rostov region near the border with Ukraine On Monday, at a meeting with his Security Council, Putin asked several ministers how prepared Russia is to face sanctions, to which his officials replied that the West would hinder Russia in any way and that the country was ready for it\par \par \uc2 . As of January this year, the Russian government's international reserves in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold were at record levels, worth more than US$630\par \par \uc2 billion. That is the fourth highest amount of such reserves in the world and could be used to help prop up the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, for a considerable time.\par \par \uc2 Only about 16% of Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently held in dollars, down from 40% five years ago. About 13% is now held in Chinese renminbi.\par \par \uc2 While about half of Russia's total external bank assets and liabilities are in U.S. dollars, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 That's down from about 80% in 2002 and 70% in early 2014.\par \par \uc2 EPA\uc2  All of this is designed to protect Russia as much as possible from U.S.-led sanctions\par \par \uc2 . Reduced dependence There have also been other changes in the structure of the Russian economy\par \par \uc2 . Over time, the country has reduced its dependence on foreign loans and investment and has been actively seeking new business opportunities outside Western markets.\par \par \uc2 China plays a large part in that strategy.\par \par \uc2 Recently, Putin traveled to Beijing to participate in the opening of the Winter Olympics, while most Western governments desisted from sending their officials to the ceremony in a diplomatic boycott over human rights abuses in the Asian country.\par \par \uc2 And on Monday night, while most nations at the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  condemned Putin's decision to recognize the rebel-held areas of Donetsk and Luhansk as "independent republics," the Chinese government was among the few that did not join in the questioning and concentrated on calling for understanding "on all sides."\par \par \uc2 Other mechanisms The Moscow government has also taken the first steps to create its own international payment system, in case it is cut off from Swift, a global financial messaging service overseen by major Western central\par \par \uc2 banks. \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , head of European diplomacy, claims that "individuals and entities" threaten "the territorial integrity, sovereignty and independence of Ukraine." Getty Images \uc2 Josep Borrell\uc2 , head of European diplomacy, claims that "individuals and entities" threaten "the territorial integrity, sovereignty and independence of Ukraine." This has been one of the sanctions assessed by the West and has only been applied once in history, so it is unclear how it may impact Moscow or what alternatives it may find given its proximity to China.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin has also cut the size of its budget\par \par \uc2 , prioritizing stability over growth, which has meant that the Russian economy has grown at an average of less than 1% a year over the past decade, but may have become more self-sufficient in the process. "What Russia is doing, in effect, is building almost an alternative financial system to be able to withstand some of the impact of sanctions that the West might impose," says Rebecca Harding, chief executive of Coriolis Technologies.\par \par \uc2 "But there will be some short-term pain in all of this, and the vulnerability of the Russian system lies in the fact that it has a very thin network spread around the world," she adds.\par \par \uc2 BBC\uc2  Strategic interests Experts agree that an invasion of Ukraine could be a dangerous game for Moscow\par \par \uc2 . Sanctions on major Russian banks, particularly state-owned banks such as those sanctioned Tuesday by the U.S., would be damaging.\par \par \uc2 But Putin may be calculating that the US , UK and EU have slightly different strategic interests\par \par \uc2 to consider. Obviously, it is easier for some countries to impose sanctions on the Russian oil and gas industry than for others.\par \par \uc2 The EU, for example, gets 40% of its natural gas supply from Russia, while the UK gets about 3%.\par \par \uc2 EPA Germany's decision to suspend the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, therefore, is detrimental to Russia, but it will also have a direct impact on energy prices in Western Europe\par \par \uc2 . Targeting the oligarchs Another measure the West has focused on is to target high-powered individuals with great influence in the government for sanctions: the so-called oligarchs\par \par \uc2 . Putin is said to have no money or other assets abroad to his name (for obvious reasons), but a network of ultrarich sympathizers looks after him.\par \par \uc2 "There have been some sanctions against the oligarchs since 2014, but they haven't gone far enough. Change will only happen if they are much more targeted against them," says Professor Tomila Lankina of the \uc2 London School of Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 London is a particular focus, with its long-established network of front companies, properties and political influence.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK government\uc2  has now announced new sanctions against specific individuals, but anti-corruption group \uc2 Transparency International\uc2  says there is around \u163\'00\'A31.5 billion (about US$2.04 billion) of Russian money invested in London properties alone\par \par \uc2 , much of it funds held in tax havens. "Western governments are not only failing the Russian people by allowing this to happen, they are also failing their own people," Lankina asserts.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced strong sanctions with Russia on Tuesday. EPA U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced strong sanctions with Russia on Tuesday. The impact Western leaders have made it clear that the sanctions announced in recent days are only the first of several potential steps\par \par \uc2 . The pressure may increase considerably depending on what Putin's next step is.\par \par \uc2 But will it be enough to force Russia to change course?\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that sanctions can have an impact, but never has such a comprehensive package been imposed on an economy as large as Russia.\par \par \uc2 And to make it effective, the West would also have to pay the long-term consequences.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Biden himself said Tuesday that his administration is studying mechanisms to prevent sanctions on Russia from being reflected in the price of gasoline in the U.\par \par \uc2 S. " I want to limit the pain that the American people feel at the gas pump. This is fundamental to me," he said.\par \par \uc2 "As I said last week, standing up for freedom will have costs for us as well and here at home. We have to be honest about it. But as we do this, I will take strong steps to make sure that the pain of our sanctions is directed at the Russian economy, not ours," he added.\par \par \uc2 You can now receive notifications from BBC World. Download our app and turn them on so you don't miss our best content.\par \par \uc2 Do you already know our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel, subscribe!\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220223ei2n003sr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
