\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Severe Russia Sanctions Could Pressure Sovereign, Bank Ratings\b0\par\par\uc2 2,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2022\par 12:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 8 Feb 2022 06:19 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Severe Russia Sanctions Could Pressure Sovereign, Bank Ratings\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-08 February 2022: Severe sanctions on Russia could have negative rating implications for the sovereign and banks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2   says. For the sovereign, this would depend on their capacity to undermine macroeconomic and financial stability, or impede sovereign debt service payments, in line with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rating sensitivities. For banks, sanctions that impede the ability to execute foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (FC) payments could be ratings negative. Our base case is that new sanctions would not be sufficiently severe to warrant negative rating actions, but the risk of this scenario has become more significant in recent weeks. Russia's 'BBB'/Stable rating is constrained by existing sanctions and the prospect of additional sanctions weighing further on GDP growth, financing flexibility and prospects for structural improvements, which are credit weaknesses relative to peers. The probability of some form of new sanctions was high when we affirmed the rating in December. Tensions over Ukraine have increased the risk of conflict and more far-reaching sanctions, although our base case remains there is no full-scale invasion. Sanctions would likely be calibrated according to the extent of any Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is highly uncertain. Triggers could include contained military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or cyber-attacks. In the absence of conflict, more likely sanctions include preventing US investors transacting in secondary market sovereign debt, and additional sanctions on senior officials, government institutions and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2  's supporters' wealth. Alone, these would be unlikely to cause ratings pressure. Russia's strengthened macro-policy framework since 2015, the build-up of large external and fiscal buffers and reduced external exposures have enhanced resilience to shocks. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US secondary market sovereign debt sanctions would further constrain Russia's fiscal financing flexibility and foreign investment, while capital outflows and a weaker Russian rouble could precipitate tighter policy. However, this would not significantly affect macro-stability or buffers, which include a Federal Treasury cash reserve and National Wealth Fund assets totaling 16.5% of GDP, and the 'BBB' category's highest international reserves (17 months of CXP). Non-resident OFZ holdings have fallen to 20.5% of the total (below 3% of GDP); we forecast general government surpluses (average 1.3% of GDP in 2022-2023) and the banking sector can absorb more debt. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russia\f28 \uc2 \f2  could provide additional liquidity and address market volatility through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nevertheless, heightened risks around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the US draft Menendez Bill underline the potential in a downside scenario for measures with a larger sovereign credit impact, such as preventing systemic banks and corporates from transacting in US dollars or accessing the international payments system, or broad-based energy market sanctions disrupting exports. The biggest impact on banking sector creditworthiness would be if sanctions impaired large state-owned banks' ability to execute FC payments, particularly through banning dollar transactions. Exclusion from SWIFT's messaging system would also cause sizeable near-term disruption, given alternative systems have limited international participation. We do not expect such measures in our base case, partly because they would impede repayment of foreign creditors, and consider sanctions on less systemically important banks more likely. The rating impact on banks of such severe sanctions would depend on their formulation, especially how far payments to creditors could be channelled through alternative payment systems and other Russian banks, and exposures switched to other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or settled with Asian and European counterparties. Bank ratings would likely be placed on Rating Watch Negative if such sanctions were imposed, and could be downgraded if greater uncertainty over the ability and willingness to service debt persisted. If sanctions resulted in freezing banks' FC assets, Viability Ratings would also come under pressure. The impact on financial metrics is unlikely to result in downgrades given banks have generally large liquidity buffers to offset deposit outflows, foreign payment schedules are not onerous (reflecting adjustment to earlier sanctions) and potential regulatory forbearance, if needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rated Russian corporates are generally well positioned to cope with sanctions. Balance sheets are strong, including FC positions, and corporates with FC external debt typically benefit from export receipts. The biggest impact would be if sanctions restricted corporates' ability to access FC cash or draw credit lines, and depends on whether Russian banks could still transact in dollars and international banks could service export cash flow and facilitate FC cash accumulation. Lower-rated Eurobond corporate issuers with short-term maturities are most sensitive to such measures. Contact: Douglas Winslow Director, Sovereigns +44 20 3530 1721 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  30 North Colonnade London E14 5GN Anton Lopatin Senior Director, Financial Institutions -- Banks +7 495 956 7096 Nikolai Lukashevich Senior Director, Corporates +7 495 956 9968 Mark Brown Senior Director, Fitch Wire +44 20 3530 1588\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Media Relations: Peter Fitzpatrick, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1103, Email: peter.fitzpatrick@thefitchgroup.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anna Bykova, Moscow, Tel: +7 495 956 9903, Email: anna.bykova@fitchratings.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tahmina Pinnington-Mannan, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1128, Email: tahmina.p-mannan@thefitchgroup.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additional information is available on \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 All \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) credit ratings are subject to certain limitations and disclaimers. Please read these limitations and disclaimers by following this link: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/understandingcreditratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/understandingcreditratings }}}\uc2 \f2 . In addition, the following \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/rating-definitions-document"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/rating-definitions-document }}}\uc2 \f2  details \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rating definitions for each rating scale and rating categories, including definitions relating to default. Published ratings, criteria, and methodologies are available from this site at all times. \f28 \uc2