\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine Fails to Raise New Debt as Russian Invasion Threat Looms\b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2022\par 15:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s access to debt markets hit a bump this week in a sign that investors are getting jittery about the possibility of a Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 A government-bond auction failed on Tuesday to raise the targeted amount when investors balked at the interest rates being offered.\par \par \uc2 The government raised around 214 million hryvnia, equivalent to $7.5 million, mostly from the sale of six-month bills. None of the one- or 1.5-year bonds that were on offer sold. Investors bought some two-year debt and dollar bonds, but they raised less than $1 million, according to data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last week, a successful auction raised the equivalent of nearly $100 million. Some auctions targeted at international investors can raise hundreds of millions or even billions of dollars. Foreign ownership of Ukrainian bonds has dropped to 8%, from 10% before Russia\u8217\'20\'19s military buildup on Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s border, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Russia has massed more than 100,000 troops near Ukraine and began military exercises in neighboring Belarus in recent weeks. Foreign investors have sold out of Ukrainian andRussian assets, putting pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sending both countries\u8217\'20\'19 borrowing costs up.\par \par \uc2 A 10-year Ukrainian bond traded with a 9.5% yield on Tuesday, easing moderately after reaching above 11% in the last week of January. That was the highest level since the pandemic-induced market meltdown in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s economy is operating normally despite the tensions, but companies have reported that the psychological threat of war is weighing on business decisions. Many investors have frozen funding and put expansion plans on hold as they wait to see how the crisis evolves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation is still so fluid,\u8221\'20\'1D said Viktor Szabo, an investment director with a focus on emerging-market debt at Abrdn. \u8220\'20\'1CI would be more comfortable buying if they didn\u8217\'20\'19t have 100,000 troops next to the border awaiting instructions to move in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite the threat of war, Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s financial position is stronger than it has been in the past. The government ran a budget surplus in January and its international reserves reached $30.9 billion at the end of 2021. Its debt load is moderate, at 44% of gross domestic product in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUkraine started the year with a significant liquidity buffer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Yuriy Butsa, the country\u8217\'20\'19s commissioner for public-debt management. \u8220\'20\'1CIn order to mitigate the impact of geopolitical risk on the budget financing, we concentrate now on maximizing the support from our international partners to cover any potential financing gap.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has financial lifelines from the West. It received funding from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  late last year with a loan program extended through June. The \uc2 European Union\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s executive arm said last month that it is working to provide a new loan package worth 1.2 billion euros, equivalent to $1.36 billion, for Ukraine and would begin looking at an additional, longer-term lending package.\par \par \uc2 European officials said they hope to be able to disburse the first \u8364\'20\'AC600 million tranche in the first quarter of this year and believe the package could cover around half of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s foreign financing needs in 2022.\par \par \uc2 One of the issues with this week\u8217\'20\'19s auction was that the Ukrainian government was trying to sell the debt with yields that were below where their existing bonds were trading in the market, according to Timothy Ash, an emerging-market strategist at \uc2 BlueBay Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re signaling they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to borrow money at the market levels,\u8221\'20\'1D Mr. Ash said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. European officials said the country\u8217\'20\'19s reserves remain at a decent level but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can\u8217\'20\'19t continue unchecked over the long term.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened its local government-bond market to foreign investors two years ago to attract capital into the economy. The take-up has been modest. Ukraine restructured its sovereign debt in 2015 and has received several bailouts from the \uc2 IMF\uc2  since independence 30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine last successfully sold internationally marketed bonds denominated in dollars or euros in July 2021, raising $517 million, according to data from Dealogic. The next local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond auction is scheduled for Feb. 8.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anna Hirtenstein at anna.hirtenstein@wsj.com and Laurence Norman at laurence.norman@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 03, 2022 09:10 ET (14:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220203ei23002e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
