\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine elections won't derail inflation targeting,  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reforms:  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2018\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Dec 5 (Reuters) - Ukraine will press on with inflation targeting and plans to lift \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions despite entering a period of instability during elections next year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Yakiv Smoliy told Reuters on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBU) has enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if needed, and expects to replenish its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $19.2 billion with the expected disbursement of \uc2 IMF\uc2  loans in December, he said.\par \par \uc2 Smoliy was aiming to reassure investors as Ukraine gears up for two tightly contested election races in 2019, the presidential election in March and parliamentary polls in October, amid the threat of escalating conflict with Russia.\par \par \uc2 While it will miss its target to bring inflation below double digits in 2018, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set itself a target of around 5 percent by the end of 2019 that would allow it to lower its key rate, which is "high enough" at 18 percent, Smoliy said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to lift \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, many of which were imposed or tightened after the collapse of the hryvnia in 2014 following the annexation of Crimea and the war in the Donbass region. The hryvnia has fallen by more than 70 percent since 2014.\par \par \uc2 Planned reforms include lifting restrictions on Ukrainians opening bank accounts abroad and investing abroad without special permission from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and on foreign investors withdrawing dividends out of the country.\par \par \uc2 "The National Bank is out of politics," Smoliy said in an interview at the bank's headquarters.\par \par \uc2 "Of course, the election is a certain stress for citizens, a certain instability, but with the independence of the National Bank, we will continue to adhere to our policy in terms of macrofinancial stability."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also look at whether to scrap a requirement for exporters to sell half of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings "soon" he said, without giving a firm timeline.\par \par \uc2 Smoliy said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stay independent and not be pressured into printing money, which has happened in previous election cycles when governments courted voters with spending increases.\par \par \uc2 "The current liquidity on the market is balanced and cannot affect inflationary processes," he added.\par \par \uc2 As evidence of his credentials to shepherd the banking system through elections, Smoliy cited the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm market jitters after \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced martial law last week.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Petro Poroshenko\uc2  introduced martial law after Russia fired on three Ukrainian vessels in the Kerch Strait and captured their crews on Nov 25.\par \par \uc2 The NBU's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "turned out to be quite effective and on Thursday we realized that the trend of panic moods was broken," Smoliy said.\par \par \uc2 He added there was practically no outflow of deposits following the martial law announcement. "The banking system almost did not feel the introduction of martial law."\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2  LOANS BY CHRISTMAS\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and other foreign institutions have supported Ukraine's economy since the Crimea annexation, conditional on Kiev's pro-Western leadership implementing reforms and tackling corruption.\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2  loan disbursements have effectively been frozen since April 2017 as reforms slowed down, but Smoliy expects the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to release a loan tranche in the ten days before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 "Ukraine has fulfilled all prior actions. Only bureaucratic procedures are left," he said.\par \par \uc2 He did not specify what size tranche he expected but said it would help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raise its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $19.2 billion from $17.8 billion now.\par \par \uc2 As part of a clean-up of the banking system, the NBU helped forcibly nationalise Ukraine's biggest bank against the wishes of its biggest shareholders in 2016, before Smoliy took office.\par \par \uc2 The decision sparked hundred of lawsuits and on Tuesday a London court threw out a PrivatBank case against the former owners, potentially derailing Kiev's efforts to claw back money it says was lost to fraud.\par \par \uc2 PrivatBank said it would appeal the decision and Smoliy said the idea that Ukraine might have to return PrivatBank to its former owners was not under discussion.\par \par \uc2 "This is a long process, so far it is impossible to ascertain that someone has won and someone has lost," he said. (Reporting by Natalia Zinets and Matthias Williams; Editing by Hugh Lawson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-12-5T17:47:14.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020181205eec502v35\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picsc