\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch: Ukraine Bond Issue Is Positive but \uc2 IMF\uc2  Funding Still Key\b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2017\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2017, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 22092017\par \par \uc2 Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s return to the international bond market reduces refinancing risk and boosts reserves, supporting the country\u8217\'20\'19s sovereign credit profile, but official lenders (chiefly the \uc2 IMF\uc2 ) remain the cornerstone of both Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s external financing and its commitment to reform, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine (B-/Stable) raised USD3 billion of 7.375% 15-year bonds on Monday in its first international issue since the crisis triggered by Russia\u8217\'20\'19s military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2014 and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s subsequent debt restructuring. The issue attracted orders of USD9.5 billion from around 350 investors, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The strong demand highlights the progress the Ukrainian authorities have made in correcting economic imbalances and strengthening the country\u8217\'20\'19s macroeconomic policy framework. The deal reduces refinancing risk as a portion of the proceeds will be used to repurchase USD1.6 billion of notes maturing in 2019 and 2020. And it will further increase reserves, which had climbed to USD18 billion in August from USD15.4 billion in January, partly due to the latest \uc2 IMF\uc2  disbursement (USD1 billion following the conclusion of the programme\u8217\'20\'19s Third Review in April), the second instalment of the EU Macro-Financial Assistance Programme of EUR600 million, and sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  by residents.\par \par \uc2 Sustained bond market access would improve external financing flexibility, but until Ukraine has re-established a track record of issuance, multilateral and bilateral support will remain the key source of balance-of-payments and budget financing. Fitch does not anticipate a strong pick-up in FDI inflows in 2017-2018, leaving official disbursements (mostly from the \uc2 IMF\uc2 ) to provide the bulk of net external financing.\par \par \uc2 We believe that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  programme also underpins the confidence and reform momentum that supported Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s bond market return and helps ensure support from other official sector creditors. Further disbursements under the Extended Fund Facility will depend on the government\u8217\'20\'19s structural reform efforts. The government has indicated that it wants to move swiftly on pension and land sales, both key \uc2 IMF\uc2  reform benchmarks.\par \par \uc2 But reform fatigue and delays in execution present risks, particularly as the emphasis shifts towards introducing and sustaining politically and socially sensitive reforms such as gas tariff adjustments. Meeting deficit targets (2.5% of GDP in 2018 and 2.3% in 2019) will probably require additional measures due to spending pressures, notably from pension transfers and wages.\par \par \uc2 We think the government remains committed to reform, and the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has shown flexibility in its programme assessments (Ukraine completed just five of 14 structural benchmarks for the Third Review). However, maintaining momentum in areas such as privatisation and tackling corruption may prove challenging, as highlighted in recent comments by \uc2 IMF\uc2  official \uc2 David Lipton\uc2  to the Ukrainian press. The approach of the 2019 elections may also weigh on reform momentum.\par \par \uc2 Given the importance of multilateral support, our sovereign rating assessment is focused on the credibility and consistency of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s policy framework, sustained strengthening of external buffers, and progress of reforms intended to improve macroeconomic performance (most notably growth prospects) and to cement fiscal consolidation, in line with Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 IMF\uc2  programme targets.\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Erich Arispe\par \par \uc2 Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1753\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Limited\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade\par \par \uc2 London\par \par \uc2 E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Paul Gamble\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1623\par \par \uc2 Mark Brown\par \par \uc2 Senior Analyst, Fitch Wire\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1588\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020170925ed9p000jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
